// Authored cases, not generated from lesson titles or trace labels.
// Existing lesson sources support the mechanisms; values below are teaching fixtures.
export const BESPOKE_EXAMPLES = {
  "0651": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Weight the rate by notional</figcaption><p>Desk A has rate 100 with weight 1 and rate 200 with weight 3.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Weighted sum<br>100 × 1 + 200 × 3 = 700</div><div>Total weight<br>1 + 3 = 4</div><div>Weighted rate<br>700 ÷ 4 = 175 basis points</div></div><details><summary>Is the result the unweighted mean 150?</summary><p>No. The larger notional gives rate 200 more influence.</p></details><details><summary>Does counting one accepted row exclude the rejected row from the weighted rate?</summary><p>No. In the shown expression the rate uses both rows. Filtering that metric would require a separate rule.</p></details>`,
  "0652": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Shift within each instrument after sorting time</figcaption><p>Instrument x has rates 100 at time 1 and 102 at time 2. Instrument y has rate 200 at time 1.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>x, time 1<br>Previous null<br>Group mean 101</div><div>x, time 2<br>Previous 100<br>Group mean 101</div><div>y, time 1<br>Previous null<br>Group mean 200</div></div><details><summary>Does y inherit x's last rate as its previous value?</summary><p>No. The window is partitioned by instrument.</p></details><details><summary>Does the group mean use only earlier rows?</summary><p>No. It uses the instrument's full group here. Do not treat it as a point-in-time historical feature.</p></details>`,
  "0653": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep unmatched trades visible for reconciliation</figcaption><p>Trades contain instruments x and z with quantities 5 and 7. Reference data has x and y.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>x trade<br>Matches desk A</div><div>z trade<br>Desk null<br>Still retained</div><div>Anti join<br>Finds unmatched z</div></div><details><summary>Does the left join preserve total quantity in this validated fixture?</summary><p>Yes. It remains 12 with two trade rows.</p></details><details><summary>Why validate many-to-one cardinality?</summary><p>Duplicate reference keys could otherwise multiply trade rows and distort totals.</p></details>`,
  "0654": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use a prior quote only while it is fresh enough</figcaption><p>Quotes for x occur at times 10 and 14 with rates 100 and 104. Backward tolerance is 5 time units.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request at 12<br>Prior quote 10<br>Age 2 → rate 100</div><div>Request at 20<br>Prior quote 14<br>Age 6 → no match</div></div><details><summary>Why not use quote 14 for the request at 12?</summary><p>It is a future quote and would violate the backward point-in-time rule.</p></details><details><summary>Does no match mean the rate is zero?</summary><p>No. It is missing. Apply an explicit stale or missing-data policy.</p></details>`,
  "0655": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Time buckets and row-count windows group different things</figcaption><p>Quantities 1, 2 and 4 arrive at minutes 0, 1 and 2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Two-minute left-closed buckets<br>[0, 2): 1 + 2 = 3<br>[2, 4): 4</div><div>Last two rows<br>null, 1.5, 3.0</div></div><details><summary>Does the row at minute 2 belong in the first bucket?</summary><p>No. The right edge is excluded by the left-closed boundary.</p></details><details><summary>Does a two-row mean always mean the last two minutes?</summary><p>No. Row-count and time-duration windows differ when events are irregular.</p></details>`,
  "0656": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Exploding legs changes the number of rows</figcaption><p>RFQ a has legs [10, 20]. RFQ b has [30].</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>List sums<br>a: 30<br>b: 30</div><div>Exploded rows<br>a: 10<br>a: 20<br>b: 30</div><div>Row count<br>2 becomes 3</div></div><details><summary>Does summing each list require one output row per leg?</summary><p>No. The list sum keeps one result per original RFQ.</p></details><details><summary>What does the identity struct contain?</summary><p>The rfq and desk fields. Selecting its rfq field recovers a and b.</p></details>`,
  "0657": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Turn bid and ask columns into side rows</figcaption><p>The wide frame has a with bid 99 and ask 100, and b with bid 101 and ask 102.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Wide<br>2 RFQ rows</div><div>Unpivot bid and ask<br>4 side-price rows</div><div>Pivot by RFQ and side<br>Recover original values</div></div><details><summary>Does unpivot duplicate the economic quantity automatically?</summary><p>It changes representation and row count. Interpret measures according to their grain before aggregating.</p></details><details><summary>Why explicitly select rfq, bid and ask when comparing the restored frame?</summary><p>It makes the expected column order explicit for the equality check.</p></details>`,
  "0658": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Verify both stored values and the lazy aggregate</figcaption><p>The fixture writes RFQs a and b with quantities 10 and 20 to a temporary Parquet file.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Write and read<br>Assert frame equality</div><div>Lazy scan<br>Select quantity sum</div><div>Collected result<br>30</div></div><details><summary>Does a successful write alone prove the round trip preserved the frame?</summary><p>No. Read it back and compare values and schema.</p></details><details><summary>Does this tiny temporary-file test establish cloud ingestion performance?</summary><p>No. It only checks the local fixture's storage and query behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0659": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Filter rows and select only the result columns</figcaption><p>The source has IDs 1, 2 and 3 with quantities −1, 2 and 3, plus an unused string column.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Filter qty greater than 0<br>Keep IDs 2 and 3</div><div>Project result<br>id and qty × 2</div><div>Output<br>(2, 4), (3, 6)</div></div><details><summary>Does defining a lazy query execute the full result immediately?</summary><p>No. The fixture collects the result explicitly.</p></details><details><summary>Does this in-memory fixture prove Parquet I/O was reduced by pushdown?</summary><p>No. Inspect plans and measure an appropriate scan workload before making that claim.</p></details>`,
  "0660": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare sorted results across execution paths</figcaption><p>Desk A has quantities 1 and 2; desk B has 4. The query groups by desk.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Normal collection<br>A: 3, B: 4</div><div>Streaming collection<br>Compare same sorted result</div><div>Parquet sink<br>Read back and compare</div></div><details><summary>Does using the streaming engine prove this workload has constant memory use?</summary><p>No. Operator state, data shape and execution behavior still need measurement.</p></details><details><summary>Why sort before asserting equality?</summary><p>The test is about grouped values, not an assumed group output order.</p></details>`,
  "0661": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Prefer the native expression for a simple square</figcaption><p>The input column is [1, 2, null]. Compare multiplying the column by itself with a Python element callback.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Native expression<br>[1, 4, null]</div><div>Python callback fixture<br>[1, 4, null]</div><div>Equality check<br>Same result for this input</div></div><details><summary>Does equal output prove equal performance?</summary><p>No. The fixture establishes correctness equivalence, not timing. The callback may also produce a performance warning.</p></details><details><summary>Is a Python callback necessary for this transformation?</summary><p>No. Native column multiplication already expresses it.</p></details>`,
  "0662": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check totals and the empty schema contract</figcaption><p>The source has B:4 and A:1,2. The function groups and sorts by desk.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Expected rows<br>A:3, B:4</div><div>Conservation check<br>Source sum 7<br>Output sum 7</div><div>Empty input<br>Zero rows<br>Expected typed schema retained</div></div><details><summary>Does checking only the grand total prove the desk mapping is correct?</summary><p>No. Wrong groups can preserve a grand total. Compare the expected frame too.</p></details><details><summary>Is an empty result's schema irrelevant?</summary><p>No. Downstream consumers still depend on its column names and types.</p></details>`,
  "0663": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep units visible in the synthetic risk calculation</figcaption><p>The fixture multiplies nonnegative exposure cents by shock basis points and divides by 10,000 using integer floor division.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>100,000 cents × 25 bp<br>250 cents</div><div>200,000 cents × 10 bp<br>200 cents</div><div>Total<br>450 cents</div></div><details><summary>Does BUY versus SELL change this formula's sign?</summary><p>No. The shown calculation does not use side to assign a sign.</p></details><details><summary>Is this a complete instrument-pricing model?</summary><p>No. It is bounded synthetic arithmetic. Real pricing, rounding and exposure conventions need explicit contracts.</p></details>`,
  "0664": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">SQL and expressions can describe the same local aggregation</figcaption><p>The registered RFQ frame has A:1,2 and B:4.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Polars SQL<br>GROUP BY desk, SUM(qty)</div><div>Polars expressions<br>group_by and sum</div><div>Sorted comparison<br>A:3, B:4 in both</div></div><details><summary>Does SQLContext send this query to PostgreSQL?</summary><p>No. This fixture operates on the registered Polars frame.</p></details><details><summary>Does passing the comparison verify cloud credentials or service deployment?</summary><p>No. Neither is used by this example.</p></details>`,
  "0665": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Deduplicate RFQs before comparing desk totals with limits</figcaption><p>RFQ a changes from quantity 10 to 12. RFQs b and c have quantities 8 and 5. Only desk A has a known limit of 18.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Latest RFQs<br>a:12, b:8, c:5</div><div>Desk A<br>12 + 8 = 20<br>20 exceeds 18: breach</div><div>Desk B<br>5, missing limit<br>Unknown</div></div><details><summary>Should the earlier quantity 10 for a also be summed?</summary><p>No. That would double-count superseded state.</p></details><details><summary>Does a missing limit mean desk B is within its limit?</summary><p>No. The result is unknown. The total quantity remains 25 after grouping.</p></details>`,
  "0636": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use the disputed capacity assumption as the discussion point</figcaption><p>Fictional conflict: one partner expects autoscaling to absorb retries. Another has load evidence showing downstream overload.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Shared goal<br>Protect launch and availability</div><div>Disputed assumption<br>Can capacity absorb repeated work?</div><div>Proposed resolution<br>Bound retries and define degraded mode</div></div><details><summary>Should the story portray the partner as careless?</summary><p>No. Explain the competing priority and evidence neutrally.</p></details><details><summary>Can the sample's successful launch be claimed as personal experience?</summary><p>No. Use truthful decisions and outcomes from the learner's own work.</p></details>`,
  "0637": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Change the detail for the audience, not the result</figcaption><p>The fictional latency story has a recruiter version and an engineer version.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Recruiter<br>Context, decision, evidence</div><div>Engineer<br>Add retry mechanism and partial-result tradeoff</div><div>Both<br>Same ownership and measurement scope</div></div><details><summary>May the sample's 14s to 4.2s p99 change be reused as an actual achievement?</summary><p>No. It is fictional teaching data.</p></details><details><summary>What does omitting the partial-result tradeoff risk?</summary><p>It can imply latency improved without a product cost. Explain that some requests returned partial results.</p></details>`,
  "0638": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Different concerns need different evidence</figcaption><p>The fictional retry proposal involves product, payments and support.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Product: launch concern<br>Show degraded-mode demo</div><div>Payments: overload concern<br>Show load curve</div><div>Support: ambiguous outcomes<br>Show error workflow</div></div><details><summary>Does tailoring evidence mean changing the facts?</summary><p>No. Address each concern using the same truthful proposal and alternatives.</p></details><details><summary>When should escalation occur?</summary><p>Use the agreed risk threshold and decision owner, not surprise pressure.</p></details>`,
  "0639": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A clear decline must remain a real choice</figcaption><p>The sample proposal has visible terms, no false claims and a clear way to decline.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Initial proposal<br>Passes fixture checks</div><div>Add penalty for declining<br>Voluntary check fails</div><div>Add manufactured urgency<br>Proportionate check fails</div></div><details><summary>Does plain language make a coercive proposal acceptable?</summary><p>No. All the checks must pass; readability does not repair coercion.</p></details><details><summary>Should a material downside be hidden to increase acceptance?</summary><p>No. Informed choice requires truthful, visible terms.</p></details>`,
  "0640": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A failed demo should have a prepared evidence path</figcaption><p>The presentation plan includes a live demo, a recording and redacted screenshots.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Live path works<br>Show user outcome</div><div>Live path fails<br>Switch to labelled recording or local fallback</div><div>Continue discussion<br>Architecture, metric and tradeoff</div></div><details><summary>Does printing the preflight list verify the demo?</summary><p>No. Perform the checks and record their results.</p></details><details><summary>Should the fallback be presented as a live production result?</summary><p>No. Label the artifact and its actual conditions.</p></details>`,
  "0641": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Make one relevant request with an easy decline</figcaption><p>The fictional outreach references a genuine prior connection, one relevant case study and a request for a short conversation.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Context<br>Why this person</div><div>Evidence<br>One relevant artifact</div><div>Ask<br>Specific and optional</div></div><details><summary>May the fictional payments collaboration be copied as a real connection?</summary><p>No. Replace it with truthful context.</p></details><details><summary>Should this template drive automated mass messages?</summary><p>No. Personalize manually and respect the recipient's choice.</p></details>`,
  "0642": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Higher base and higher scenario total can belong to different offers</figcaption><p>All values are fictional same-currency units. A has base 100; B has base 108.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A<br>Recurring scenario 123<br>First-year scenario 127</div><div>B<br>Recurring scenario 118<br>First-year scenario 118</div></div><details><summary>Is A's 127 guaranteed recurring salary?</summary><p>No. It includes contingent bonus and equity assumptions plus one-time relocation.</p></details><details><summary>Should a counteroffer invent a competing offer?</summary><p>No. Use real alternatives and request revised conditions in writing.</p></details>`,
  "0643": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Unknown sponsorship status is a research item, not a yes</figcaption><p>The relocation tracker begins with an unchosen country and unresolved eligibility and employer requirements.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Choose destination<br>Identify exact official route</div><div>Verify current requirements<br>Record date and unresolved questions</div><div>Compare written offer<br>Include relocation conditions and risks</div></div><details><summary>Does filling the tracker establish legal eligibility?</summary><p>No. It organizes research and questions; qualified advice may be needed.</p></details><details><summary>Can old application-time research be assumed current at offer time?</summary><p>No. Recheck official sources before relying on it.</p></details>`,
  "0644": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Not observed is different from needs practice</figcaption><p>The synthetic mock notes three separate findings.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Empty input not tested<br>Targeted drill</div><div>Estimate derived with units<br>Recheck later without notes</div><div>No reflection question attempted<br>Choose a prompt that exposes it</div></div><details><summary>Does missing observation mean failure?</summary><p>No. It also does not establish mastery. Gather evidence with an appropriate prompt.</p></details><details><summary>Does the helper grade the learner automatically?</summary><p>No. A human supplies concrete observations; the helper organizes follow-up practice.</p></details>`,
  "0645": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep leading zeros by declaring an identifier as text</figcaption><p>The Polars frame contains RFQ IDs 0012 and 0013 with quantities 10 and 20.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>rfq_id: String<br>0012 and 0013 preserved</div><div>qty: Int64<br>Sum 30</div><div>Empty frame, same schema<br>0 rows and 2 columns</div></div><details><summary>Should an identifier be numeric just because it contains digits?</summary><p>No. Here the string schema preserves its representation, including leading zeros.</p></details><details><summary>Does an empty frame have to lose its column types?</summary><p>No. Construct it with the existing schema.</p></details>`,
  "0646": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Build gross before using it in a condition</figcaption><p>Rows have quantity and price pairs (2, 10) and (5, 20).</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First expression<br>Gross 20 and 100</div><div>Next expression<br>Gross at least 100 → review</div><div>Result bands<br>small, review</div></div><details><summary>Which columns does the numeric selector return?</summary><p>qty, price and gross. It excludes the string band.</p></details><details><summary>How many rows satisfy qty greater than 2 and price at most 20?</summary><p>One: the row with quantity 5 and price 20.</p></details>`,
  "0647": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A failed cast and missing input both produce null but have different causes</figcaption><p>Raw quantities are 10 as text, oops and a missing value. The cast is non-strict.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>10<br>Parsed integer 10</div><div>oops<br>Cast result null<br>Quarantine candidate</div><div>Missing input<br>Result null<br>Already missing</div></div><details><summary>How does the fixture identify the failed cast?</summary><p>The raw value is not null but the parsed value is null.</p></details><details><summary>Does fill_null replace NaN too?</summary><p>No. The fixture's NaN remains after null is filled with zero.</p></details>`,
  "0648": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Parse only the narrow RFQ grammar you agreed to accept</figcaption><p>The expression trims surrounding spaces, then matches side, integer quantity and three uppercase currency letters.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div> BUY 100 USD <br>BUY, 100, USD</div><div>SELL 20 EUR<br>SELL, 20, EUR</div><div>BUY lots USD<br>No match</div></div><details><summary>Does matching BUY 0 USD make it valid?</summary><p>No. The later quantity rule requires a value greater than zero.</p></details><details><summary>Does this regex prove the currency code is supported by the business?</summary><p>No. It validates the shown shape. Apply any supported-currency rules separately.</p></details>`,
  "0649": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two clock readings can identify the same instant</figcaption><p>The strings are 10:00 at UTC and 11:00 at offset +01:00 on 1 January 2026.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>10:00 +0000<br>10:00 UTC</div><div>11:00 +0100<br>10:00 UTC</div><div>New York display in fixture<br>05:00 for both</div></div><details><summary>How many distinct parsed instants are present?</summary><p>One. The offset-adjusted times are equal.</p></details><details><summary>Does converting the display time zone change the underlying instant?</summary><p>No. It changes the local representation.</p></details>`,
  "0650": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Break timestamp ties before keeping the latest row</figcaption><p>ID a has timestamp 10 twice: sequence 1 has quantity 5 and sequence 2 has quantity 7. ID b has quantity 3.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Sort a by timestamp then sequence<br>Sequence 2 comes last</div><div>Keep last per ID<br>a: 7<br>b: 3</div></div><details><summary>Why include sequence after timestamp?</summary><p>It gives the tied rows a deterministic order under this contract.</p></details><details><summary>Does applying the same operation again change these results?</summary><p>No. Each ID already has one selected row.</p></details>`,
  "0611": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Give each stopped run an honest outcome</figcaption><p>Run three deterministic agent fixtures separately.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Repeated tool request<br>Detect repetition and enforce budget</div><div>Forbidden action<br>Executor denies authority</div><div>Budget exhausted<br>Stop with explicit reason</div></div><details><summary>Can model prose override the executor's allowed tools?</summary><p>No. Application controls enforce authority and stopping.</p></details><details><summary>Should uncertain external outcome be labelled successful completion?</summary><p>No. Preserve uncertainty and trigger the defined reconciliation path.</p></details>`,
  "0612": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A higher average can hide a security regression</figcaption><p>Hypothetical dataset: 90 ordinary cases and 10 permission cases. Baseline passes 80 ordinary and all 10 permission cases. Candidate passes 90 ordinary but only 5 permission cases.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Baseline<br>90 of 100 overall<br>10 of 10 permission</div><div>Candidate<br>95 of 100 overall<br>5 of 10 permission</div></div><details><summary>Is the candidate automatically releasable because 95 exceeds 90?</summary><p>No. Critical permission failures must not disappear inside the average.</p></details><details><summary>When should release thresholds be chosen?</summary><p>Before inspecting candidate scores, with slice counts and an uncertainty policy.</p></details>`,
  "0613": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A fluent answer can be the grader's false positive</figcaption><p>A blinded fixture pairs a plausible unsupported answer with a terse correct answer.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Human-adjudicated rubric<br>Unsupported answer fails</div><div>Model grader<br>Compare its decisions</div><div>Reverse presentation order<br>Check sensitivity</div></div><details><summary>What if the judge passes the unsupported answer?</summary><p>Record a false positive and examine the rubric and evidence boundary.</p></details><details><summary>Does agreement between two models remove the need for calibration?</summary><p>No. They can share a blind spot.</p></details>`,
  "0614": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Repeated runs do not create new independent questions</figcaption><p>The benchmark repeats two cases five times. The candidate answers arithmetic correctly but returns Yes. when exact YES is required.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Simple slice<br>5 of 5 pass</div><div>Format slice<br>0 of 5 pass</div><div>Total<br>10 runs, 2 independent cases<br>Quality 0.5</div></div><details><summary>Does ten runs mean ten distinct tasks were covered?</summary><p>No. There are only two independent cases.</p></details><details><summary>Should a timed-out call's unknown cost be recorded as zero?</summary><p>No. The harness records unknown cost separately because a failed call may still be charged.</p></details>`,
  "0615": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Retrieved instructions cannot authorize a tool</figcaption><p>A harmless test document tells the assistant to change its allowed-tool policy.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Retrieved document<br>Untrusted data</div><div>Model proposes forbidden tool<br>Proposal is not authority</div><div>Executor<br>Reject unauthorized action</div></div><details><summary>Is telling the model to ignore such text the complete defense?</summary><p>No. Enforce authorization and secret boundaries outside the model.</p></details><details><summary>Can a retrieved document provide approval for an irreversible effect?</summary><p>No. Bind approval to the exact effect through trusted application state.</p></details>`,
  "0616": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A quality drop can occur without a model change</figcaption><p>The fixture keeps model M and prompt P fixed but replaces fresh retrieval data with stale version D2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Baseline<br>M, P, fresh D1</div><div>Candidate<br>M, P, stale D2</div><div>Trace comparison<br>Inspect retrieval evidence and affected slices</div></div><details><summary>Should the model be blamed solely because answer quality fell?</summary><p>No. Versioned stage evidence helps locate the changed component.</p></details><details><summary>Must all raw prompts be logged to investigate?</summary><p>No. Use necessary redacted evidence with access and retention controls.</p></details>`,
  "0617": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A supported task and an out-of-scope task need different handling</figcaption><p>The exercise defines a low-risk support assistant with explicit prohibited uses and escalation paths.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Supported support request<br>Apply task controls</div><div>Out-of-scope request<br>Follow boundary and escalation policy</div><div>Group-level evaluation<br>Compare errors with counts and uncertainty</div></div><details><summary>Does one technical control establish legal compliance?</summary><p>No. Mark jurisdiction-specific questions for qualified review.</p></details><details><summary>Can equal observed error rates in tiny samples prove fairness?</summary><p>No. State uncertainty, meaningful slices and user recourse instead of a blanket claim.</p></details>`,
  "0618": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Remove the AI proposal and inspect the remaining user need</figcaption><p>Hypothetical discovery: a user needs to locate the current approved policy. The proposed solution is a chatbot.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>User task<br>Find current approved policy</div><div>Existing workaround<br>Search or ask a colleague</div><div>Remove chatbot<br>Which requirement becomes unmet?</div></div><details><summary>Must the solution keep AI because it was the original idea?</summary><p>No. Keep it only where evidence supports the user's outcome.</p></details><details><summary>What should be marked as an assumption?</summary><p>Any unverified claim about the user, workaround or success measure. Separate it from interview observations.</p></details>`,
  "0619": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One flow must cross the real application boundaries</figcaption><p>The practice slice accepts one authenticated request, persists it and calls a bounded model adapter.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Frontend<br>Pending and error states</div><div>API and database<br>Authorization and stable request identity</div><div>Model adapter<br>Bounded call and explicit failure</div></div><details><summary>What if acceptance commits but the response is lost?</summary><p>Retry with the same operation identity and recover its outcome without repeating the intended effect.</p></details><details><summary>Do separate unconnected snippets establish an end-to-end implementation?</summary><p>No. Demonstrate the coherent path and label mocked services.</p></details>`,
  "0620": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Attach evidence to each operational claim</figcaption><p>The implemented slice claims it can reject unauthorized input, pass a quality gate and recover disposable data.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Authorization claim<br>Observed denial test</div><div>Quality claim<br>Versioned evaluation gate</div><div>Recovery claim<br>Restore timing and recovered invariants</div></div><details><summary>Does a completed checklist prove those claims?</summary><p>No. Keep the actual observations and artifact identities.</p></details><details><summary>Can rollback and restore be treated as the same exercise?</summary><p>No. They recover different parts of the system and can have different compatibility limits.</p></details>`,
  "0621": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Label a local experiment as a local experiment</figcaption><p>The portfolio contains a demo, architecture decision and synthetic failure test, but no production deployment.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Built personally<br>Name exact owned components</div><div>Measured locally<br>State fixture and conditions</div><div>Not deployed<br>State remaining production work</div></div><details><summary>Can the case study call synthetic users real customers?</summary><p>No. Keep scope and evidence truthful.</p></details><details><summary>What makes a failed design choice useful interview evidence?</summary><p>Explain the constraint, observation, decision and what changed. Do not invent an impact result.</p></details>`,
  "0622": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Map role requirements to existing evidence</figcaption><p>The fictional evidence map contains TypeScript, Python and RAG.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Full Stack AI role<br>3 of 4 must-have skills mapped<br>AWS gap</div><div>Backend AI role<br>1 of 4 mapped<br>FastAPI, PostgreSQL and eval gaps</div></div><details><summary>Does an unmapped skill prove the learner lacks it?</summary><p>No. It means this evidence map has no supporting item yet.</p></details><details><summary>Is the ratio a hiring probability?</summary><p>No. It is a simple planning signal, not a prediction of selection.</p></details>`,
  "0623": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A metric needs its conditions and ownership</figcaption><p>The fictional resume bullet claims duplicate effects fell from 1.8% to zero in failure tests.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Scope<br>Failure tests, not all production</div><div>Evidence needed<br>Sample size, conditions and report</div><div>Ownership<br>Transaction boundary and consumer work</div></div><details><summary>May this sample metric be copied into the learner's resume?</summary><p>No. Replace it with truthful personal evidence.</p></details><details><summary>Does zero observed failures guarantee zero future failures?</summary><p>No. State what the test measured and its limits.</p></details>`,
  "0624": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep profile claims consistent across documents</figcaption><p>The fictional profile features a product, architecture case study and evaluation report.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Headline<br>Target role and actual skills</div><div>Experience<br>Same dates and scope as resume</div><div>Featured evidence<br>Accessible, non-confidential artifacts</div></div><details><summary>Should visibility preferences be copied without review?</summary><p>No. Choose them deliberately for the user's situation.</p></details><details><summary>Does listing a skill establish professional experience with it?</summary><p>No. Use accurate scope and evidence rather than implied credentials.</p></details>`,
  "0625": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A pinned repository should be verifiable from a clean checkout</figcaption><p>The fictional portfolio pins a RAG product with an architecture note, evaluation and threat model.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>README<br>User problem and setup</div><div>Evidence<br>Tests and measurements</div><div>Limits<br>Personal scope and incomplete parts</div></div><details><summary>Is a screenshot sufficient to prove reproducible setup?</summary><p>No. Verify the documented path from a clean checkout.</p></details><details><summary>Should unfinished features be hidden?</summary><p>No. State what remains incomplete so the repository's claims match its behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0626": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read the actual hostname, not the familiar prefix</figcaption><p>The trusted hostname in the fixture is company.example.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>company.example/jobs/1<br>Matches expected host</div><div>company.example.evil.test<br>Different host</div><div>company.example@evil.test<br>Actual host is evil.test</div></div><details><summary>Does a matching hostname alone prove a genuine opportunity?</summary><p>No. It is one screening signal. Verify the employer and role independently.</p></details><details><summary>Should every external applicant-tracking domain be assumed fraudulent?</summary><p>No. Verify legitimate external domains through the employer rather than assuming either trust or fraud.</p></details>`,
  "0627": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Different posting IDs must not collapse into one application</figcaption><p>The fixture receives /jobs?id=1 and /jobs?id=2 from the same employer for similarly named roles.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>URL one<br>Query id=1</div><div>URL two<br>Query id=2</div><div>Dedupe identity<br>Preserve full normalized URL</div></div><details><summary>Would removing every query string be safe here?</summary><p>No. It would remove the distinguishing posting IDs.</p></details><details><summary>Does a dedupe match authorize automated submission?</summary><p>No. A human verifies the role, truthfulness and personalization before submitting.</p></details>`,
  "0628": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Shorten the introduction without changing its facts</figcaption><p>The rehearsal prepares 30-second, 90-second and five-minute versions of a truthful career narrative.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Short version<br>Current work and direction</div><div>Longer version<br>Add relevant personal evidence</div><div>Logistics<br>Known location, authorization and notice facts</div></div><details><summary>May the longer version claim broader ownership to sound senior?</summary><p>No. Detail can increase; factual scope must remain consistent.</p></details><details><summary>Should unknown work-authorization details be guessed?</summary><p>No. State known status accurately and identify what needs confirmation.</p></details>`,
  "0629": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Look for an earlier index before storing the current one</figcaption><p>For values [3, 3] and target 6, the map starts empty.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Index 0<br>Need 3, not found<br>Store 3 at 0</div><div>Index 1<br>Need 3, found at 0<br>Return [0, 1]</div></div><details><summary>What happens with only [3]?</summary><p>Return null. There is no distinct second index.</p></details><details><summary>Why not store before looking up?</summary><p>The lookup could find the current index and incorrectly reuse it. The invariant is that stored indices are earlier.</p></details>`,
  "0630": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count first, then apply an explicit tie rule</figcaption><p>Values [3, 1, 3, 2, 1, 3] produce counts 3→3, 1→2 and 2→1.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Sort by count descending<br>3, 1, 2</div><div>Take first two<br>[3, 1]</div><div>Equal counts<br>Lower numeric value first</div></div><details><summary>Does k=0 return the most frequent item?</summary><p>No. It returns an empty result.</p></details><details><summary>Is the complexity just O(n)?</summary><p>No. Sorting m distinct values adds O(m log m), giving O(n + m log m).</p></details>`,
  "0631": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Waiting for I/O differs from running a long callback</figcaption><p>The interview asks how Node.js handles concurrent I/O on one JavaScript thread.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Socket wait<br>OS facilities can track readiness</div><div>Ready callback<br>Runs on shared JavaScript thread</div><div>Long CPU callback<br>Delays other callbacks</div></div><details><summary>Does adding async move CPU work off the main thread?</summary><p>No. Async syntax does not provide that offload.</p></details><details><summary>How should pool saturation and event-loop blocking be distinguished?</summary><p>Compare loop delay and utilization, dependency timing and a controlled profile instead of guessing from latency alone.</p></details>`,
  "0632": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Choose the critical path before adding components</figcaption><p>Practice design: accept a job and later show its result. First clarify whether acknowledgment means durable acceptance or completed processing.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Contract<br>Define accepted versus complete</div><div>Critical write path<br>Trace state ownership</div><div>Failure table<br>Lost response and retry</div></div><details><summary>Should cache and queue choices come before this contract?</summary><p>No. Requirements and invariants determine which components are needed.</p></details><details><summary>When is a service split justified?</summary><p>After a stated constraint or measured bottleneck supports the added distributed cost.</p></details>`,
  "0633": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An AI target is a claim to test</figcaption><p>The fictional design targets p95 latency of 2,500 ms with a 6,000-token budget and 24 concurrent calls.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Targets<br>Hypothetical constraints</div><div>Evidence<br>Held-out tasks and offered load</div><div>Release decision<br>Quality, safety, latency and cost together</div></div><details><summary>Can those numbers be presented as measured achievements?</summary><p>No. Collect observations before making that claim.</p></details><details><summary>Should cost exclude retries and failed calls?</summary><p>No. Report cost per task, including retrieval, failures and fallback.</p></details>`,
  "0634": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A deadline request needs a risk decision</figcaption><p>Hypothetical interview: a manager asks to skip testing to meet a deadline.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Clarify<br>Customer impact and untested risk</div><div>Compare options<br>Reduce scope, stage exposure or change date</div><div>Recommend<br>Owned release with monitoring and rollback</div></div><details><summary>Should the answer promise zero risk?</summary><p>No. Make residual risk and acceptance explicit.</p></details><details><summary>Can this hypothetical answer be presented as a past incident?</summary><p>No. Keep scenario reasoning separate from personal history.</p></details>`,
  "0635": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep a story's evidence stable across rehearsal lengths</figcaption><p>The fictional queue-overload story includes an 18-minute recovery and zero observed duplicate effects.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>One-minute version<br>Decision and outcome</div><div>Three-minute version<br>Add actions and tradeoffs</div><div>Five-minute version<br>Add evidence and reflection</div></div><details><summary>May the sample numbers become the learner's own results?</summary><p>No. Replace the fiction with real personal evidence.</p></details><details><summary>What should remain identical across versions?</summary><p>Facts, ownership and measurement scope. Only the level of detail changes.</p></details>`,
  "0591": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A network chunk is not a complete event</figcaption><p>In the LF-only fixture, one event's JSON data arrives in two network chunks. Its blank-line terminator arrives with the second.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First chunk<br>Partial event<br>Keep buffered</div><div>Second chunk<br>Complete blank-line boundary</div><div>Parse and yield<br>Apply before saving resume cursor</div></div><details><summary>Should trailing text without a blank line be emitted at end of stream?</summary><p>No. This fixture does not treat it as a completed event.</p></details><details><summary>Does canceling the reader prove the provider stopped computing?</summary><p>No. Local consumption and upstream cancellation are separate. This is also not a general SSE parser.</p></details>`,
  "0592": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Clicking Approve submits a decision, not proof of authority</figcaption><p>A tool card shows a proposed call and its arguments.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Proposed<br>Show Approve and Reject</div><div>User chooses Approve<br>POST decision with call identity</div><div>Server<br>Reauthorize and bind exact intent</div></div><details><summary>May the UI itself grant permission to execute?</summary><p>No. The server must check actor, tenant, arguments, version and expiry.</p></details><details><summary>What if approve and reject requests race?</summary><p>The server must serialize conflicting decisions. Separate request keys alone do not settle the conflict.</p></details>`,
  "0593": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Resume from the last successfully applied event</figcaption><p>Practice stream S has sequences 1 through 5. The client applies through 3 and disconnects.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Saved cursor<br>S, after 3</div><div>Resume authorization<br>Check chat and stream ownership</div><div>Replay page<br>Read events after 3</div></div><details><summary>Should a duplicate POST create a new generation?</summary><p>Not for the same scoped identity and intent. The acceptance adapter must return the original message and stream IDs.</p></details><details><summary>Does one bounded replay page implement a complete live handoff?</summary><p>No. Handle pagination, expired cursors and the gap before tail subscription.</p></details>`,
  "0594": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Exact identifiers and paraphrases need different retrieval evidence</figcaption><p>Practice queries ask for error ZX-17 and for a document about ending a subscription using different wording.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>ZX-17<br>Rare exact text<br>Lexical signal useful</div><div>Paraphrased cancellation question<br>Little word overlap<br>Dense signal worth testing</div><div>Hybrid result<br>Explicit fusion and tie rules</div></div><details><summary>Can lexical and dense scores be added as if they shared one scale?</summary><p>Not automatically. Define and evaluate the combination rule.</p></details><details><summary>Does vector similarity prove a relevant result?</summary><p>No. Compare both methods against the same labelled queries.</p></details>`,
  "0595": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Re-ingesting a version should not duplicate its chunks</figcaption><p>Source D has version V1 and deterministic chunk IDs. It is ingested twice, then replaced by V2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First V1 ingestion<br>Create V1 chunks</div><div>Repeated V1<br>Same identities<br>No silent duplicates</div><div>V2 update<br>Publish new version<br>Retire stale chunks</div></div><details><summary>Does successful embedding generation finish ingestion?</summary><p>No. Index publication, permissions, provenance and stale-chunk cleanup still matter.</p></details><details><summary>Why retain source offsets?</summary><p>They connect retrieved chunks to the actual source passages used for citations.</p></details>`,
  "0596": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A high retrieval score cannot override permissions</figcaption><p>Hypothetical candidates: A costs 100 tokens and is allowed; B costs 100 and is forbidden; C duplicates A. Context budget is 150 tokens.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>B<br>Reject before model exposure</div><div>A and duplicate C<br>Remove redundant content</div><div>Context<br>A fits budget<br>Keep its provenance</div></div><details><summary>Can the reranker recover a needed document absent from all candidates?</summary><p>No. Candidate generation must retrieve it first.</p></details><details><summary>Is a citation valid merely because its ID exists?</summary><p>No. The cited evidence must actually support the associated claim.</p></details>`,
  "0597": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One relevant hit gives two different ratios</figcaption><p>Relevant documents are a and b. The returned top three are a, c and d.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Hits<br>Only a<br>1</div><div>Precision at 3<br>1 ÷ 3</div><div>Relevance recall at 3<br>1 ÷ 2</div></div><details><summary>Can reranking a, c and d retrieve missing b?</summary><p>No. It can only reorder the supplied set.</p></details><details><summary>Can perfect ANN recall coexist with zero relevance recall?</summary><p>Yes. Matching exact geometric neighbors does not prove those neighbors are relevant documents.</p></details>`,
  "0598": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Revocation must reach every answer path</figcaption><p>User A could read document D at indexing time. Access is later revoked.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Lexical and vector paths<br>Enforce current permission</div><div>Reranking and context<br>Do not expose D</div><div>Cached answer<br>Must not bypass revocation</div></div><details><summary>Does old permission metadata prove current access?</summary><p>No. Define freshness and revocation guarantees explicitly.</p></details><details><summary>Is deleting the source enough if stale chunks remain searchable?</summary><p>No. Handle tombstones, index lag and cached answers under the stated deletion contract.</p></details>`,
  "0599": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Filter the embedding space and tenant before returning passages</figcaption><p>The fixture has two demo-v1 React passages for tenant 7 and one Python passage for tenant 8. The query vector is [1, 0, 0].</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Tenant filter<br>7 only</div><div>Model filter<br>demo-v1 only</div><div>Cosine distance order<br>Exact [1, 0, 0] passage has distance 0</div></div><details><summary>May the tenant-8 note be returned because it is nearby?</summary><p>No. Similarity cannot override the filter and authorization contract.</p></details><details><summary>Does creating an HNSW index prove exact-neighbor recall?</summary><p>No. Compare results with an exact baseline under the same filters and metric.</p></details>`,
  "0600": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare candidate IDs with the exact neighbor set</figcaption><p>For the first query, exact top two are a and b. Suppose an approximate search returns a and c.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Expected IDs<br>a, b</div><div>Actual IDs<br>a, c</div><div>Neighbor recall<br>One overlap out of two<br>0.5</div></div><details><summary>Does the supplied candidate_search exercise a real approximate index?</summary><p>No. It currently calls exact search. Replace it with the index under test before drawing ANN conclusions.</p></details><details><summary>Is this ratio semantic relevance recall?</summary><p>No. It measures agreement with exact nearest neighbors in one fixed embedding space.</p></details>`,
  "0601": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A restore must preserve deletion as well as live data</figcaption><p>The fixture inserts point 1 for tenant a and point 2 for tenant b, then deletes point 2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before deletion<br>Points 1 and 2</div><div>After deletion<br>Point 2 must be absent</div><div>Snapshot restore check<br>Keep deletion and tenant filtering intact</div></div><details><summary>Is a snapshot listing enough to establish recovery?</summary><p>No. Restore and verify data, deleted-point absence and retrieval behavior.</p></details><details><summary>Can a tenant-a query return tenant-b payloads after maintenance?</summary><p>Not under the required isolation contract. Test the filtered path explicitly.</p></details>`,
  "0602": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A knowledge-product demo needs failure evidence</figcaption><p>The capstone changes one document, revokes one user's access, removes relevant evidence and simulates a provider timeout separately.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Changed source<br>Check freshness and citations</div><div>Revoked access<br>Check every retrieval path</div><div>Missing evidence or timeout<br>Check abstention and recovery</div></div><details><summary>Does a fluent answer establish groundedness?</summary><p>No. Grade its support against the supplied evidence.</p></details><details><summary>What should the portfolio report besides a demo?</summary><p>Stage-level metrics, labelled mocks, failure traces and recovery instructions.</p></details>`,
  "0603": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The scripted agent needs a tool step and an answer step</figcaption><p>The question asks about A-42. The scripted model first requests lookup_order, then answers from the observation.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Step 1<br>Tool returns packed</div><div>Step 2<br>Final answer: Order is packed.</div><div>Budget of one step<br>Exhausted before final answer</div></div><details><summary>Does max_steps=1 prevent the first tool from running?</summary><p>No. It permits that first step but leaves no second step for the final response.</p></details><details><summary>Does a step budget bound a blocked tool call?</summary><p>No. Add elapsed-time, size, spend and authorization controls.</p></details>`,
  "0604": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the goal when trimming context</figcaption><p>The fixture has a goal, one remembered preference and two recent observations.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Limit 4 items<br>Goal, preference, two observations</div><div>Limit 2 items<br>Goal and latest selected observation</div><div>Limit 1 item<br>Goal only</div></div><details><summary>Does the dictionary named durable_memory survive a process restart?</summary><p>No. It is a process-local stand-in, despite its name.</p></details><details><summary>Does four items guarantee a small token budget?</summary><p>No. Bound each value and total bytes or tokens separately.</p></details>`,
  "0605": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Choose invoke or stream for one workflow run</figcaption><p>The agent looks up A-42 and requests a structured answer with answer text and order_found.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Tool fixture<br>A-42: packed, expected Friday</div><div>Structured response<br>Answer and boolean field</div><div>Execution choice<br>invoke or stream</div></div><details><summary>Should invoke be followed by stream just to show the same run progressively?</summary><p>No. That starts the workflow again and can repeat costs or effects.</p></details><details><summary>Does the fixture lookup supply authenticated production order data?</summary><p>No. It is static practice data. Real access needs authorization and current records.</p></details>`,
  "0606": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Resume the approval node with an explicit boolean</figcaption><p>The graph pauses to ask whether to refund A-42.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Interrupt<br>Action awaits decision</div><div>Resume True<br>Route to execute<br>Status approved</div><div>Resume False<br>Route to cancel<br>Status rejected</div></div><details><summary>Would the string yes satisfy the fixture's approval check?</summary><p>No. It requires an actual boolean. Production also needs authenticated, intent-bound approval.</p></details><details><summary>Is code before interrupt guaranteed to run only once?</summary><p>No. The node restarts on resume. Keep effects safely placed and idempotent; the in-memory checkpointer is not durable storage.</p></details>`,
  "0607": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">List a tool before calling its bounded input contract</figcaption><p>The stdio dispatch fixture advertises explain_term with one string term, at most 80 characters.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>tools/list<br>Discover input schema</div><div>tools/call<br>term: idempotency</div><div>Fixture response<br>Return one glossary explanation</div></div><details><summary>May diagnostic messages be printed to stdout beside the protocol?</summary><p>No. Reserve stdout for protocol frames and use stderr for diagnostics.</p></details><details><summary>Is this dispatch lab a complete MCP server?</summary><p>No. Initialization enforcement, negotiation, cancellation and session ownership remain incomplete.</p></details>`,
  "0608": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A valid token for another audience is rejected</figcaption><p>The authenticated POST sketch expects audience https://mcp.example and scope lessons:read.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Untrusted Origin<br>Reject 403</div><div>Wrong token audience<br>Reject 401</div><div>Missing required scope<br>Reject 403</div></div><details><summary>Does knowing a session ID prove ownership?</summary><p>No. Load it under the authenticated subject and enforce session ownership.</p></details><details><summary>Does the ten-second abort signal prove an external mutation did not occur?</summary><p>No. Cancellation is cooperative and an effect may already have happened.</p></details>`,
  "0609": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Replay the same approved action after a lost completion record</figcaption><p>The fake external service commits action A, then the workflow crashes before saving completion.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Checkpoint<br>Approved action A</div><div>External effect<br>A completed</div><div>Recovery replay<br>Reuse A's idempotency identity</div></div><details><summary>Should recovery invent a new action key?</summary><p>No. That could create a second effect instead of recovering the first result.</p></details><details><summary>Does a durable checkpoint alone provide exactly-once external effects?</summary><p>No. The external service must support the required replay or reconciliation contract.</p></details>`,
  "0610": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A supervisor needs a policy for disagreement</figcaption><p>Two practice specialists process the same task. One says approve; the other says reject or times out.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Conflicting results<br>Validate and resolve</div><div>Missing result<br>Apply deadline and partial-failure policy</div><div>Compare baseline<br>Quality benefit versus added cost</div></div><details><summary>Does adding specialists automatically improve reliability?</summary><p>No. Measure quality against a simpler bounded worker on the same labelled tasks.</p></details><details><summary>Should the supervisor silently choose whichever answer arrived first?</summary><p>Only if that is the explicit validated contract. Otherwise handle disagreement and missing evidence deliberately.</p></details>`,
  "0566": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate namespaces still need explicit controls</figcaption><p>Fictional tenants A and B run in different namespaces. A tries to reach B and consume excessive resources.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Cross-tenant traffic<br>Network policy boundary</div><div>API access<br>RBAC boundary</div><div>Resource consumption<br>Quota and admission boundary</div></div><details><summary>Does the namespace name alone deny network access?</summary><p>No. Verify the controls required by the threat model.</p></details><details><summary>Are namespaces always sufficient for hostile workloads?</summary><p>No. The threat model may require stronger cluster or infrastructure separation.</p></details>`,
  "0567": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The worker can read Jobs, not create them</figcaption><p>The RoleBinding grants worker the job-reader Role in product.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Jobs and jobs/status<br>get, list, watch</div><div>Create or delete Job<br>Not granted here</div><div>Other namespaces<br>Not granted by this namespaced Role</div></div><details><summary>Does watch permission allow changing job status?</summary><p>No. Reading the status subresource is not permission to update it.</p></details><details><summary>Does this Role alone prove the worker has no other permissions?</summary><p>No. Inspect other bindings that may also grant authority.</p></details>`,
  "0568": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A writable memory mount still uses memory</figcaption><p>The Pod fragment runs UID 10001 with a read-only root and a 64 MiB memory-backed /tmp.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Root filesystem<br>Read-only</div><div>/tmp<br>Approved writable mount</div><div>Memory-backed bytes<br>Consume memory resources</div></div><details><summary>Does /tmp sizeLimit replace container memory limits?</summary><p>No. Configure and verify memory limits separately.</p></details><details><summary>Can any image run correctly under UID 10001?</summary><p>No. The image and mounted paths must support the configured identity and permissions.</p></details>`,
  "0569": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">CPU utilization is relative to the request</figcaption><p>Hypothetical target container: CPU request 200 millicores and use 130 millicores.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Utilization<br>130 ÷ 200 = 65%</div><div>Fixture target<br>65% average utilization</div><div>Replica bounds<br>3 to 30</div></div><details><summary>Is the 65% measured against the entire node's CPU?</summary><p>No. It is relative to resource requests.</p></details><details><summary>Can raising desired replicas create node capacity immediately?</summary><p>No. Observe pending Pods and scale-up delay. CPU can also miss an external-model bottleneck.</p></details>`,
  "0570": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A voluntary drain differs from a lost zone</figcaption><p>The tabletop has three spread replicas and a disruption budget.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Voluntary drain<br>Supported eviction checks budget</div><div>No replacement capacity<br>Maintenance can block</div><div>Involuntary zone loss<br>Budget cannot prevent outage</div></div><details><summary>Does a toleration force a Pod onto a tainted node?</summary><p>No. It permits placement; other scheduling rules still apply.</p></details><details><summary>Should a blocked drain be bypassed without analysis?</summary><p>No. Check capacity and availability before overriding a safety boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0571": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Connect a user failure to time-aligned platform evidence</figcaption><p>A test request fails while its destination Pod becomes unready.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request trace<br>ID and failure time</div><div>Service endpoints<br>Eligibility at that time</div><div>Pod and node evidence<br>Readiness, events and conditions</div></div><details><summary>Are Kubernetes events a permanent audit trail?</summary><p>No. Preserve necessary evidence through durable collection.</p></details><details><summary>Should each user ID become a metric label?</summary><p>No. Avoid high-cardinality or sensitive labels. Use appropriately protected correlation data.</p></details>`,
  "0572": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Inspect the previous container before restarting again</figcaption><p>A Pod repeatedly restarts. The diagnostic recipe reads status, description, previous logs and events.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Pod and owner<br>Identify workload</div><div>Previous logs<br>Evidence from prior container instance</div><div>Conditions and events<br>Choose the next diagnostic boundary</div></div><details><summary>Does every restart loop mean insufficient memory?</summary><p>No. Use termination and application evidence to distinguish causes.</p></details><details><summary>Should random restarts precede evidence collection?</summary><p>No. They can erase useful context without repairing the cause.</p></details>`,
  "0573": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A valid template can render the wrong selector</figcaption><p>A values change alters an application's selector in the rendered objects.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Values file<br>Small edit</div><div>Rendered manifests<br>Selector changes</div><div>Review consequence<br>Which Pods match now?</div></div><details><summary>Does valid YAML prove the workload wiring is correct?</summary><p>No. Review the rendered semantics, including hooks and lifecycle.</p></details><details><summary>Can a Helm rollback undo every external database effect?</summary><p>No. Data and external effects need their own recovery procedure.</p></details>`,
  "0574": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An emergency edit can conflict with reconciliation</figcaption><p>Git declares three replicas. An authorized sandbox operator temporarily changes the live object to four.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Git desired state<br>3 replicas</div><div>Manual live state<br>4 replicas</div><div>Reconciler<br>May restore declared 3</div></div><details><summary>How should a lasting emergency change be preserved?</summary><p>Reconcile it into the approved source and field-ownership process.</p></details><details><summary>Does creating a custom resource implement its behavior?</summary><p>No. A controller or operator must act on it. Its availability is another dependency.</p></details>`,
  "0575": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Control-plane backup and application data have different scopes</figcaption><p>The recovery inventory includes cluster objects, external database data and persistent volumes.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Control-plane backup<br>Cluster state scope</div><div>External database<br>Separate recovery path</div><div>Volumes<br>Backend-specific recovery</div></div><details><summary>Does restoring the control plane restore every data store?</summary><p>No. Inventory and test each dependency.</p></details><details><summary>Can upgrade order be copied from any older checklist?</summary><p>No. Read the exact target version-skew and API-removal requirements.</p></details>`,
  "0576": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Three failures probe different owners</figcaption><p>The capstone uses bounded, separate fault exercises.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Failed rollout<br>Deployment and readiness response</div><div>Unavailable dependency<br>Application failure handling</div><div>Resource pressure<br>Requests, limits and node behavior</div></div><details><summary>Does a green apply establish production readiness?</summary><p>No. It shows API acceptance, not the observed recovery outcomes.</p></details><details><summary>What must accompany fault-injection evidence?</summary><p>Record scope, ownership, expected versus observed results, cleanup and cost limits.</p></details>`,
  "0577": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Doubling a vector's length keeps its direction</figcaption><p>Compare vectors [1, 2] and [2, 4]. The second is twice the first.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Same direction<br>Cosine similarity 1</div><div>Perpendicular pair<br>[1, 0] and [0, 1]<br>Cosine similarity 0</div><div>Zero vector<br>No direction<br>Rejected</div></div><details><summary>What if you multiply one vector by a negative number?</summary><p>Its direction reverses. The positive-scaling argument no longer applies.</p></details><details><summary>Does cosine 1 prove two documents mean the same thing?</summary><p>No. It describes vector geometry, not verified semantic relevance.</p></details>`,
  "0578": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A step can overshoot the best weight</figcaption><p>The training points follow y=2x. At weight 0.5, the supplied gradient is −14.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Step size 0.1<br>New weight 0.5 − 0.1 × (−14)<br>1.9</div><div>Step size 1<br>New weight 14.5<br>Overshoots 2</div></div><details><summary>Which step reduces the fixture's loss?</summary><p>The smaller step moves close to weight 2. The larger step increases the loss.</p></details><details><summary>Does falling training loss prove generalization?</summary><p>No. Evaluate held-out behavior and choose regularization with validation data.</p></details>`,
  "0579": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">High accuracy can miss every rare positive</figcaption><p>The fixture has 99 negative labels and one positive. The classifier always predicts negative.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Correct predictions<br>99 of 100</div><div>Accuracy<br>99%</div><div>Positive recall<br>0 of 1<br>0%</div></div><details><summary>Does 99% accuracy establish a useful positive detector?</summary><p>No. It misses the only positive case.</p></details><details><summary>Should the held-out feature value 100 affect the training mean of [0, 2]?</summary><p>No. Fit preprocessing on training data only. The training mean is 1, not the leaked mean 34.</p></details>`,
  "0580": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Changing units changes the nearest neighbor</figcaption><p>The points are A=(1000, 0), B=(0, 10), and query=(900, 9).</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Raw coordinates<br>A is nearest</div><div>Divide first coordinate by 1000<br>Query becomes (0.9, 9)</div><div>Scaled coordinates<br>B is nearest</div></div><details><summary>Did the raw records change?</summary><p>No. The distance calculation changed how their features are weighted.</p></details><details><summary>Does nearest automatically mean best recommendation?</summary><p>No. Compare against held-out relevance labels and the intended ranking objective.</p></details>`,
  "0581": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The same text can use different token counts</figcaption><p>The tiny greedy vocabulary contains cat as one piece plus its individual letters and a space.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>With cat piece<br>cat, space, cat<br>3 tokens</div><div>Without cat piece<br>c, a, t, space, c, a, t<br>7 tokens</div></div><details><summary>Can character count alone determine production token use?</summary><p>No. Use the model's actual tokenizer and include message, tool and output budgets.</p></details><details><summary>Does a larger token ID mean stronger semantic meaning?</summary><p>No. IDs are vocabulary indices, not ordered semantic scores.</p></details>`,
  "0582": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The causal mask hides the later value</figcaption><p>The scalar head has values 10 and 20. Position zero can attend only to itself.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Position 0<br>Only value 10 visible<br>Output 10</div><div>Position 1<br>Scores 0 and 1<br>Weighted average favors 20</div><div>Change future value to 999<br>Position 0 still outputs 10</div></div><details><summary>Why would removing the mask be wrong for next-token training?</summary><p>The earlier position could use future information that is unavailable during generation.</p></details><details><summary>Is this scalar head a full transformer block?</summary><p>No. Real blocks also include projections, multiple heads, residual paths, normalization and a feed-forward network.</p></details>`,
  "0583": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Each input position predicts the following token</figcaption><p>The sequence is BOS, a, b, EOS.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>BOS predicts a</div><div>a predicts b</div><div>b predicts EOS</div></div><details><summary>What changes when correct-token probabilities rise from [0.5, 0.25, 0.5] to [0.75, 0.5, 0.75]?</summary><p>The average negative log likelihood falls.</p></details><details><summary>Does lower next-token loss prove safe tool use?</summary><p>No. Evaluate instruction following, factuality and tool safety with separate tasks.</p></details>`,
  "0584": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Lower temperature concentrates probability on the largest logit</figcaption><p>The logits are [2, 1, 0]. Compare temperatures 0.5 and 2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Temperature 0.5<br>More probability on first candidate</div><div>Temperature 2<br>Flatter distribution</div><div>Temperature 0<br>Rejected by this division formula</div></div><details><summary>How would greedy zero-temperature selection be implemented?</summary><p>Use a separate argmax rule with a defined tie policy.</p></details><details><summary>Does this probability example measure batching or KV-cache speed?</summary><p>No. Those have separate memory and throughput behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0585": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Opposing calibration errors can cancel in the average</figcaption><p>One synthetic band reports 90% confidence and another 10%. Each is correct on five of ten cases.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>High-confidence band<br>90% reported, 50% observed</div><div>Low-confidence band<br>10% reported, 50% observed</div><div>Combined average<br>50% reported, 50% observed</div></div><details><summary>Does the zero global gap prove calibration?</summary><p>No. Each band misses by 40 percentage points.</p></details><details><summary>Does articulate reasoning prove the answer is correct?</summary><p>No. Require verifiable outcomes, abstention rules and evidence with counts and uncertainty.</p></details>`,
  "0586": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A partial stream is not a completed answer</figcaption><p>A deterministic provider mock emits one text fragment and then fails. Another mock does not support images.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Partial text<br>Preserve incomplete status</div><div>Unsupported image input<br>Explicit capability error</div><div>Malformed usage<br>Do not invent token accounting</div></div><details><summary>Should the adapter turn every partial stream into success?</summary><p>No. Keep finish and error semantics visible to callers.</p></details><details><summary>Can a common interface promise every provider's capabilities?</summary><p>No. Normalize only the semantics actually shared, and expose required capabilities.</p></details>`,
  "0587": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Test a prompt against instruction-like retrieved text</figcaption><p>A held-out document contains a quoted instruction to ignore the task. Compare baseline and candidate prompts on the same cases.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Trusted task<br>Separate instructions</div><div>Retrieved quotation<br>Untrusted task data</div><div>Evaluation<br>Compare failures, regressions and token cost</div></div><details><summary>Do delimiters make the quotation a secure boundary?</summary><p>No. They help structure context but do not enforce authorization or tool restrictions.</p></details><details><summary>Is one pleasing answer enough to select the new prompt?</summary><p>No. Compare held-out results across relevant failure categories.</p></details>`,
  "0588": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Correct JSON can still request an unauthorized entity</figcaption><p>The fixture expects a small object containing an entity ID.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Broken JSON<br>Parser rejects</div><div>Wrong object shape<br>Schema rejects</div><div>Correct shape, forbidden ID<br>Authorization rejects</div></div><details><summary>Does schema compliance grant access to that entity?</summary><p>No. Validate business constraints and authority separately.</p></details><details><summary>Should parsing repair continue without a limit?</summary><p>No. Bound repair attempts and output size. Never let repaired model fields become trusted authority.</p></details>`,
  "0589": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A replay key must keep the same tool intent</figcaption><p>The fixture calls lookup_order for A-42 using req-1.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First call<br>Returns packed<br>One audit entry</div><div>Same call again<br>Returns copied saved result</div><div>req-1 with B-1<br>Changed intent<br>Rejected</div></div><details><summary>Can changing the returned dictionary corrupt the saved result?</summary><p>No. The fixture returns a deep copy.</p></details><details><summary>Does its in-memory dictionary provide durable production idempotency?</summary><p>No. Authentication, scoped durable receipts and effect coordination remain required.</p></details>`,
  "0590": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A retry must fit the remaining task budget</figcaption><p>Hypothetical task deadline: 10 seconds. The first attempt uses 9 seconds before a rate-limit error.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Total budget<br>10 seconds</div><div>Used<br>9 seconds</div><div>Remaining<br>1 second<br>Check before retry admission</div></div><details><summary>Should a retry needing at least 2 seconds start?</summary><p>No. It cannot fit this budget. Use the declared fallback or failure path.</p></details><details><summary>Can a matching question reuse another tenant's cached answer?</summary><p>No. Cache identity must preserve the required tenant and permission boundaries.</p></details>`,
  "0541": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">No shell does not mean no application</figcaption><p>A minimal-image workload is running, but an attempt to execute sh fails because the image has no shell.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Shell command fails<br>Missing diagnostic executable</div><div>Inspect workload<br>State, exit history, logs and events</div><div>Next step<br>Use approved host or debug-container evidence</div></div><details><summary>Does the failed shell command prove the application crashed?</summary><p>No. Those are different claims.</p></details><details><summary>Should diagnostic packages be installed into the production container?</summary><p>No. Preserve the artifact and use the approved debugging path.</p></details>`,
  "0542": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A moved tag points at different content</figcaption><p>In an isolated registry, test-tag initially points to digest A and later moves to B.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Pull test-tag before move<br>A</div><div>Pull test-tag after move<br>B</div><div>Pull recorded digest A<br>Requests original content</div></div><details><summary>Which identity connects promotion to earlier tests?</summary><p>The tested digest, with the relevant manifest and platform information.</p></details><details><summary>Can retention delete A just because no current release tag points to it?</summary><p>It may still be needed for rollback. Protect required digests before cleanup.</p></details>`,
  "0543": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One image reference can describe two platforms</figcaption><p>The build requests linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 and pushes a multi-platform result.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>amd64 output<br>Platform-specific image</div><div>arm64 output<br>Platform-specific image</div><div>Published reference<br>Inspect manifest and platform entries</div></div><details><summary>Does a successful amd64 run prove arm64 behavior?</summary><p>No. Verify the relevant platform output and runtime behavior separately.</p></details><details><summary>Do requested provenance and SBOM outputs replace a trust policy?</summary><p>No. Consumers still need to inspect and verify evidence against their policy.</p></details>`,
  "0544": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A signature needs a trusted identity</figcaption><p>The image exercise compares an unsigned artifact with one signed by an unknown builder.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Unsigned image<br>Missing required signature</div><div>Unknown signer<br>Signature may exist<br>Trust policy still fails</div><div>Admission decision<br>Expected identity and artifact binding</div></div><details><summary>Does any valid signature establish approval?</summary><p>No. Check who signed and what identity the policy trusts.</p></details><details><summary>Is a clean vulnerability scan permanent assurance?</summary><p>No. Findings change. Assign rebuild ownership and expiry for exceptions.</p></details>`,
  "0545": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Smaller transfer size does not prove lower runtime memory</figcaption><p>Compare two equivalent practice images: a baseline and one with fewer packaged tools.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Transfer measurement<br>Compressed image bytes</div><div>Runtime measurement<br>Memory under the same workload</div><div>Operational check<br>Can the team still diagnose failure?</div></div><details><summary>Can image size stand in for measured memory use?</summary><p>No. They measure different resources.</p></details><details><summary>Is removing the shell always an unqualified improvement?</summary><p>No. It changes diagnostic options. Keep an approved debugging plan.</p></details>`,
  "0546": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Starting Compose is only the first capstone check</figcaption><p>Run separate disposable exercises for container replacement, database interruption and backup restore.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Replace API<br>Check state ownership and startup</div><div>Interrupt database<br>Check bounded failure and recovery</div><div>Restore test data<br>Check usable recovered state</div></div><details><summary>Does compose up prove these recovery paths work?</summary><p>No. Record expected and observed behavior for each failure.</p></details><details><summary>Why change only one condition per run?</summary><p>It makes the observation easier to attribute to the intended failure.</p></details>`,
  "0547": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A controller restores the desired replica count</figcaption><p>The disposable Deployment requests two Pods. One owned Pod is deleted.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Desired<br>2 replicas</div><div>Observed after deletion<br>1 remaining</div><div>Controller reconciles<br>Creates replacement</div></div><details><summary>Does deleting an unowned standalone Pod imply the same replacement?</summary><p>No. This replacement follows the owning controller's desired-state loop.</p></details><details><summary>Does needing two processes alone justify a cluster?</summary><p>No. Weigh the application's needs against cluster operating costs.</p></details>`,
  "0548": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A reused name does not reuse object identity</figcaption><p>A test object named api has UID A. It is deleted and recreated with the same name.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Old object<br>Name api, UID A</div><div>New object<br>Name api, different UID</div><div>Concurrent update<br>Use current opaque resourceVersion</div></div><details><summary>Can resourceVersion be treated as an application timestamp?</summary><p>No. Treat it as opaque concurrency data.</p></details><details><summary>Must status reflect a changed spec immediately?</summary><p>No. Reconciliation is asynchronous; desired generation and observed status can differ temporarily.</p></details>`,
  "0549": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">API requests can fail before persistence</figcaption><p>The fixture identity can read a test object but cannot create it.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>No accepted identity<br>Authentication failure</div><div>Read-only identity creates<br>Authorization failure</div><div>Authorized creator, invalid object<br>Validation failure</div></div><details><summary>Does successful admission prove a Pod is running?</summary><p>No. Controllers and nodes still need to materialize the workload.</p></details><details><summary>Why record the rejection stage?</summary><p>It identifies the failed contract and the audit evidence needed to diagnose it.</p></details>`,
  "0550": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Deletion waits while finalizer cleanup is unfinished</figcaption><p>The paper controller owns external cleanup for a disposable object. Cleanup fails after deletion is requested.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Deletion requested<br>deletionTimestamp set</div><div>Finalizer remains<br>Cleanup pending</div><div>Controller retries<br>Needs idempotent cleanup</div></div><details><summary>Does the finalizer guarantee cleanup will succeed?</summary><p>No. It records an outstanding protocol obligation.</p></details><details><summary>Should the first response be to strip the finalizer?</summary><p>No. Restore cleanup or follow an explicit orphan-recovery procedure before manual intervention.</p></details>`,
  "0551": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An impossible request prevents startup</figcaption><p>Hypothetical test nodes each have 2 CPUs allocatable. A Pod requests 3 CPUs.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Requested<br>3 CPUs</div><div>Any eligible node<br>2 CPUs allocatable</div><div>Scheduling<br>No feasible placement</div></div><details><summary>Does low observed CPU use override the request?</summary><p>No. Scheduling accounts for requests and constraints, not just current use.</p></details><details><summary>Will lowering the request always make placement possible?</summary><p>No. An impossible node-affinity constraint can still keep the Pod pending.</p></details>`,
  "0552": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An unavailable image differs from an unavailable node</figcaption><p>Compare two tabletop failures for one desired Pod.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Image pull fails<br>Node may still report normally</div><div>Node heartbeat lost<br>Node availability is uncertain</div><div>Diagnosis<br>Check the owner and timing of status</div></div><details><summary>Does a desired Pod prove its process exists?</summary><p>No. Image retrieval, sandbox creation and runtime startup may not have completed.</p></details><details><summary>Can replacement be assumed instantaneous after heartbeat loss?</summary><p>No. Detection timing, scheduling and storage constraints affect recovery.</p></details>`,
  "0553": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two containers share the Pod network and declared volume</figcaption><p>A disposable Pod contains a server and client plus a shared emptyDir.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Client to server<br>Use localhost and server port</div><div>Shared marker<br>Exchange through emptyDir</div><div>Pod replacement<br>New identity and emptyDir lifetime</div></div><details><summary>Does sharing a Pod make every filesystem path shared?</summary><p>No. Share files through explicitly declared volumes.</p></details><details><summary>Does a container restart equal full Pod replacement?</summary><p>No. The sharing and storage lifetime differ. Track the Pod identity.</p></details>`,
  "0554": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Readiness failure need not restart the process</figcaption><p>The disposable app has separate readiness and liveness checks.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Readiness fails<br>Not eligible for normal service traffic</div><div>Liveness remains healthy<br>No liveness-triggered restart</div><div>Liveness later fails<br>Restart can follow configured thresholds</div></div><details><summary>Should every slow database response fail liveness?</summary><p>Not automatically. That can restart every instance without fixing the dependency.</p></details><details><summary>Does removing readiness immediately finish all in-flight work?</summary><p>No. Measure draining and enforce a bounded termination policy.</p></details>`,
  "0555": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Surge capacity supports a rollout with no planned unavailability</figcaption><p>The Deployment requests four replicas, maxSurge 1 and maxUnavailable 0.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Desired replicas<br>4</div><div>Rollout surge allowance<br>1 extra</div><div>New readiness requirement<br>10 seconds before availability counts</div></div><details><summary>Does maxUnavailable 0 prevent all outages?</summary><p>No. It constrains rollout behavior, not node failures or every application failure.</p></details><details><summary>Does exceeding the 300-second progress deadline automatically roll back?</summary><p>No. It reports stalled progress. Rollback needs an explicit response.</p></details>`,
  "0556": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Stable identity does not copy database contents</figcaption><p>A StatefulSet replica has an ordinal name and its own persistent-volume claim.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Replace Pod<br>Stable role identity</div><div>Preserve claim<br>Reattach eligible storage</div><div>Lose storage failure domain<br>Need database recovery design</div></div><details><summary>Does the StatefulSet implement database replication?</summary><p>No. Define replication and quorum rules separately.</p></details><details><summary>Can deleting test Pods be assumed to delete or retain every claim?</summary><p>No. Inspect the configured claim and storage lifecycle before cleanup.</p></details>`,
  "0557": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A DaemonSet follows node eligibility</figcaption><p>Paper fixture: nodes A and B match the selector. Node C does not.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A and B<br>Eligible for agent</div><div>C<br>Excluded by selector</div><div>Add an untolerated taint to B<br>Re-evaluate eligibility</div></div><details><summary>Is a DaemonSet simply a Deployment fixed at three replicas?</summary><p>No. Its desired placement follows eligible nodes.</p></details><details><summary>Why bound an agent's permissions and resource requests?</summary><p>A broken node-local agent can affect many hosts at once, especially when privileged.</p></details>`,
  "0558": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A retry can repeat an already completed effect</figcaption><p>Job attempt A records effect E, then crashes before normal completion is observed.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A<br>Effect E committed</div><div>Completion not observed<br>Retry permitted</div><div>Attempt B<br>Must recognize E's stable identity</div></div><details><summary>Does the Kubernetes Job guarantee one business effect?</summary><p>No. Application idempotency must protect repeated attempts.</p></details><details><summary>Does an execution deadline undo E?</summary><p>No. It bounds execution, not previously committed effects.</p></details>`,
  "0559": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Service port 80 forwards to target port 8000</figcaption><p>The ClusterIP Service selects Pods labeled app=evidence-api.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Client<br>Service port 80</div><div>Selector<br>app=evidence-api</div><div>Selected endpoints<br>Target port 8000</div></div><details><summary>Does a Pod labeled app=other match this Service?</summary><p>No. Inspect EndpointSlices to confirm the actual selected endpoints.</p></details><details><summary>Does creating this ClusterIP Service create a public load balancer?</summary><p>No. Its type is ClusterIP.</p></details>`,
  "0560": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A routing object needs a working controller</figcaption><p>The exercise creates a route without a matching controller, then separately tests a wrong backend port.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Route specification exists<br>No controller to implement it</div><div>Controller exists, wrong port<br>Backend path still fails</div><div>Evidence<br>Status, listener, Service and ready endpoints</div></div><details><summary>Does API acceptance prove packets are forwarded?</summary><p>No. Verify the responsible implementation and status.</p></details><details><summary>Can fixing TLS alone repair a mismatched backend port?</summary><p>No. Trace host matching and the backend connection separately.</p></details>`,
  "0561": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A short name can trigger several DNS attempts</figcaption><p>Compare a nonexistent short name with an absolute name ending in a dot inside a disposable Pod.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Short name<br>Search domains may be tried</div><div>Absolute trailing-dot name<br>No search-domain expansion</div><div>After resolution<br>Endpoint connection remains a separate check</div></div><details><summary>Does a resolved address prove the service is reachable?</summary><p>No. Routing, policy and endpoint readiness can still fail.</p></details><details><summary>Why inspect the actual resolver configuration?</summary><p>Search paths, ndots, caching and application resolver behavior affect the observed attempts.</p></details>`,
  "0562": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The policy permits database traffic but omits DNS</figcaption><p>The policy selects API Pods in product. It permits ingress from web Pods in product and egress to Pods in data on TCP 5432.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>web → api:8000<br>Listed ingress path</div><div>api → data namespace:5432<br>Listed egress path</div><div>DNS lookup<br>No resolver rule in this policy</div></div><details><summary>Does the egress peer select only the database Pod?</summary><p>No. It selects any Pod in the data namespace on that port. Add a pod selector in the same peer to narrow it.</p></details><details><summary>Does this policy override every other matching allow?</summary><p>No. Matching NetworkPolicy rules add together, and enforcement requires a supporting CNI.</p></details>`,
  "0563": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A changed ConfigMap does not rewrite a running process environment</figcaption><p>The fixture exposes the same harmless setting as a mounted file and an environment variable.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>ConfigMap updated<br>New desired value</div><div>Projected file<br>Can update after propagation</div><div>Existing process environment<br>Does not automatically reload</div></div><details><summary>Does a changed file guarantee the application uses its new value?</summary><p>No. The application needs suitable reread or reload behavior.</p></details><details><summary>Does base64 encoding make a Secret encrypted?</summary><p>No. Encoding and encryption are different. Review storage and access protection separately.</p></details>`,
  "0564": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Requests and limits answer different resource questions</figcaption><p>The container requests 250 millicores and 256 MiB. Its limits are one CPU and 512 MiB.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Scheduling request<br>0.25 CPU, 256 MiB</div><div>Runtime limits<br>1 CPU, 512 MiB</div><div>Diagnosis<br>Inspect throttling, termination state and node pressure</div></div><details><summary>Is the CPU request the same as its maximum allowance?</summary><p>No. The request and limit have different roles.</p></details><details><summary>Can one usage snapshot distinguish OOM from eviction?</summary><p>No. Inspect status, events and node conditions.</p></details>`,
  "0565": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A claim can exist before usable storage exists</figcaption><p>The paper fixture has a pending PVC and a Pod constrained to a zone incompatible with the storage.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>PVC<br>Requests storage</div><div>Provisioning and binding<br>Must satisfy class and topology</div><div>Pod use<br>Needs attachable, usable volume</div></div><details><summary>Does the PVC object prove a filesystem is attached?</summary><p>No. Check provisioning, binding, topology and attachment.</p></details><details><summary>Are access modes a complete application authorization system?</summary><p>No. They describe storage access capabilities. Application permissions remain separate.</p></details>`,
  "0516": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Promote the bytes that passed testing</figcaption><p>Candidate A has digest D1. Rebuilding the same source after a base dependency changes produces candidate B with digest D2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Test evidence<br>Applies to D1</div><div>Rebuilt candidate<br>D2 differs from D1</div><div>Promotion<br>Use the tested object<br>Or verify the new object</div></div><details><summary>Does the same version label transfer test evidence to D2?</summary><p>No. The artifact changed.</p></details><details><summary>Should rollback rebuild an old source tag with today's dependencies?</summary><p>No. Retain the tested artifact and compatible configuration needed for rollback.</p></details>`,
  "0517": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate configuration before accepting work</figcaption><p>Hypothetical contract: timeout_ms must be between 100 and 5,000. A required credential comes from the secret path.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>timeout_ms = −1<br>Invalid configuration</div><div>Credential missing<br>Required startup state absent</div><div>Valid reload<br>Publish one consistent configuration view</div></div><details><summary>Should startup print the credential to explain a failure?</summary><p>No. Report the missing or invalid setting without exposing secret values.</p></details><details><summary>Can two related flags be changed independently during active requests without review?</summary><p>That can expose inconsistent state. Define atomic reload, rollback and ownership rules.</p></details>`,
  "0518": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Every traffic increase needs new evidence</figcaption><p>The release sketch starts the candidate at 5%, then proposes 25% and 100%.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>5% traffic<br>Observe availability, latency and task success</div><div>25% traffic<br>Repeat observation gate</div><div>100% traffic<br>Only after the next gate passes</div></div><details><summary>Does one good ten-minute window validate every later traffic level?</summary><p>No. Load and sample mix change. Each promotion needs fresh evidence.</p></details><details><summary>What if the telemetry is stale?</summary><p>Do not treat it as a passing gate. Define freshness, minimum samples and abort ownership before rollout.</p></details>`,
  "0519": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Backfill while both application versions still work</figcaption><p>The migration adds display_name alongside name. New writers populate both before the backfill begins.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Expand<br>Nullable display_name</div><div>Backfill<br>Up to 1,000 unlocked rows per batch</div><div>Verify, switch reads<br>Contract only after compatibility window</div></div><details><summary>Does an empty SKIP LOCKED batch prove there are no missing values?</summary><p>No. Other transactions may hold the remaining rows. Verify completion separately.</p></details><details><summary>Why defer dropping name?</summary><p>Old code may still need it during rollback. A schema change can invalidate application rollback.</p></details>`,
  "0520": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Define what the availability numerator counts</figcaption><p>The teaching rule treats every non-5xx response as good, including 4xx responses.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>HTTP 200<br>Counted good</div><div>HTTP 403<br>Also counted good here</div><div>HTTP 503<br>Counted bad</div></div><details><summary>Does this ratio measure whether every user task succeeded?</summary><p>No. It measures the chosen response predicate and misses requests that never reach the instrumented server.</p></details><details><summary>What does no traffic imply?</summary><p>The ratio is undefined. It does not prove availability. Handle missing and low-volume data explicitly.</p></details>`,
  "0521": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A rollback may leave the database queue overloaded</figcaption><p>Tabletop incident: checkout errors rise after deployment. The database remains saturated after the application version is rolled back.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Initial mitigation<br>Roll back application</div><div>New evidence<br>Queued work still saturates database</div><div>Next decision<br>Reduce impact and verify recovery</div></div><details><summary>Should the incident close because rollback completed?</summary><p>No. Confirm the user journey has recovered.</p></details><details><summary>What belongs in the shared decision log?</summary><p>Timestamped observations, hypotheses, actions and owners. Keep uncertain causes distinct from observed facts.</p></details>`,
  "0522": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Lower admission to drain the queue</figcaption><p>The simplified model starts empty. Arrivals are 120 jobs per second and processing capacity is 100.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First 60 seconds<br>20 added each second<br>Backlog 1,200</div><div>Admission drops to 90<br>Drain 10 each second</div><div>Clear existing backlog<br>120 seconds under fixed-rate assumptions</div></div><details><summary>Does admission of exactly 100 drain the backlog?</summary><p>No. In this simplified model it stops growth but leaves the backlog unchanged.</p></details><details><summary>Can these means alone set a safe queue deadline?</summary><p>No. Include bursts, service-time variation and autoscaler delay.</p></details>`,
  "0523": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Apply the reviewed plan, then interpret the next result</figcaption><p>The recipe saves reviewed.plan, exposes its JSON for restricted review and applies that saved plan.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Plan<br>Potentially sensitive artifact</div><div>Review and approve<br>Apply exact saved plan</div><div>Next detailed-exitcode plan<br>0: no changes<br>1: error<br>2: proposed changes</div></div><details><summary>Does exit code 2 mean Terraform crashed?</summary><p>No. With this option it means changes are proposed.</p></details><details><summary>May plan JSON be committed as ordinary interview evidence?</summary><p>No. It can contain plaintext secrets. Restrict access and share only carefully redacted excerpts.</p></details>`,
  "0524": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare a template with a portal using the team's task</figcaption><p>The fictional team needs three days for its first deployment. Compare two proposed remedies.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Documented template<br>Small intervention</div><div>New portal<br>More product and support work</div><div>Comparison<br>Completion time, adoption and recovery</div></div><details><summary>Does the portal win merely because it has more features?</summary><p>No. Choose the smallest intervention that removes the demonstrated delay.</p></details><details><summary>What if a team needs an unsupported deployment?</summary><p>Define an owned escape hatch instead of trapping the team in the standard path.</p></details>`,
  "0525": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Three upload inputs test three different controls</figcaption><p>Use harmless fixtures instead of malicious files.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Oversized body<br>Bounded input control</div><div>Wrong content type<br>Content validation</div><div>Another tenant's file ID<br>Object authorization</div></div><details><summary>Does passing the size check prove the caller owns the file?</summary><p>No. Each control protects a different boundary.</p></details><details><summary>Does a green policy check certify the whole feature?</summary><p>No. Keep response ownership, vulnerability handling and expiring exceptions explicit.</p></details>`,
  "0526": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Rollback requires a compatible state</figcaption><p>The capstone contrasts a failed health gate with a schema change old code cannot read.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Failed health gate<br>Retained compatible artifact<br>Rollback may be valid</div><div>Incompatible schema<br>Old code cannot read state<br>Repair or roll forward may be needed</div></div><details><summary>Does retaining the old image guarantee safe rollback?</summary><p>No. Configuration and data compatibility also matter.</p></details><details><summary>What links a deployed service to its test evidence?</summary><p>Record source revision, tested artifact digest, environment configuration and approval together.</p></details>`,
  "0527": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The local client may talk to a remote daemon</figcaption><p>The experiment records the selected Docker context, then points a disposable context at an unavailable endpoint.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Client executable exists<br>Local CLI can start</div><div>Daemon endpoint unavailable<br>Server request fails</div><div>Diagnosis<br>Separate client installation from daemon reachability</div></div><details><summary>Does a working client prove containers run on the laptop?</summary><p>No. Locate the selected daemon before reasoning about host files.</p></details><details><summary>What should the experiment restore?</summary><p>The original context. Record endpoint metadata without exposing credentials.</p></details>`,
  "0528": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A restart keeps the original writable layer</figcaption><p>Container A writes a marker. Container B is newly created from the same image digest.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Restart A<br>Its marker remains</div><div>Create B<br>Does not inherit A's layer</div><div>Shared image digest<br>Same base content, separate container state</div></div><details><summary>Was the marker added to the image automatically?</summary><p>No. It was written into A's container layer.</p></details><details><summary>Does a tag identify fixed bytes forever?</summary><p>No. Tags can move. A content digest identifies the image content.</p></details>`,
  "0529": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Build arguments do not automatically become runtime variables</figcaption><p>The exercise declares BUILD_LABEL as ARG and APP_MODE=demo as ENV, then runs with APP_MODE=changed.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Build input<br>BUILD_LABEL</div><div>Runtime environment<br>APP_MODE becomes changed</div><div>Working directory<br>/work</div></div><details><summary>Will BUILD_LABEL necessarily exist in the running process environment?</summary><p>No. ARG does not automatically create a runtime environment variable.</p></details><details><summary>Does EXPOSE publish the application port on the host?</summary><p>No. It documents a port. Port publishing is a separate runtime configuration.</p></details>`,
  "0530": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Copy changing source after stable dependency inputs</figcaption><p>The build copies the dependency file and installs dependencies before copying application source.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Change source only<br>Earlier dependency inputs unchanged</div><div>Change dependency file<br>Dependency step invalidated</div><div>Build context<br>Exclude secrets and irrelevant files</div></div><details><summary>Why not copy the entire project before the dependency step?</summary><p>A source-only change could invalidate that earlier step unnecessarily.</p></details><details><summary>Does a cache hit prove dependencies are trustworthy or current?</summary><p>No. Cache reuse and supply-chain verification are separate concerns.</p></details>`,
  "0531": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Only selected build outputs cross into the runtime stage</figcaption><p>The build stage creates wheels in /wheels. The runtime stage copies those wheels, installs them and copies the application.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Build stage<br>Create wheels</div><div>Explicit cross-stage copy<br>/wheels → /wheels</div><div>Runtime stage<br>Install wheels<br>Run as app user</div></div><details><summary>Does the runtime stage automatically inherit every build-stage file?</summary><p>No. Cross-stage content enters through explicit copies.</p></details><details><summary>Does this Dockerfile remove the copied wheel files after installation?</summary><p>No. The shown commands retain them. Do not claim the image is minimal merely because it uses two stages.</p></details>`,
  "0532": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Run arguments replace CMD, not the entrypoint</figcaption><p>The image entrypoint is python -m app. CMD supplies host and port defaults.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Normal run<br>python -m app<br>Default host and port arguments</div><div>Run with --port 9000<br>python -m app --port 9000</div></div><details><summary>Are the original host arguments automatically retained?</summary><p>No. The supplied run arguments replace CMD as a whole.</p></details><details><summary>Does exec form alone guarantee graceful shutdown?</summary><p>No. The application still needs termination handling and bounded cleanup.</p></details>`,
  "0533": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Signal receipt happens before cleanup completion</figcaption><p>Hypothetical stop exercise: the grace period is 10 seconds and cleanup needs 15.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Time 0<br>Process receives termination signal</div><div>Time 10<br>Grace period exhausted</div><div>Needed cleanup time<br>15 seconds<br>Cannot assume completion</div></div><details><summary>Does a log saying signal received prove all work was flushed?</summary><p>No. Record completed cleanup separately.</p></details><details><summary>Does making Python PID 1 automatically reap every arbitrary child?</summary><p>No. Child handling and any init helper need an explicit design.</p></details>`,
  "0534": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Container controls protect different boundaries</figcaption><p>The disposable inspection compares process visibility, resource limits and one denied operation.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Namespaces<br>Separate views</div><div>Cgroups<br>Control resource use</div><div>Capabilities and security policy<br>Restrict operations</div></div><details><summary>Does a separate process view imply a separate kernel?</summary><p>No. Containers share the host kernel in this model.</p></details><details><summary>Should a denied operation be retried with privileged mode to finish the exercise?</summary><p>No. The denial is the intended evidence. Preserve the configured isolation boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0535": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Non-root execution and a rootless daemon are different</figcaption><p>The run command uses UID 10001, a read-only root filesystem and a 64 MiB temporary /tmp mount.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Container identity<br>UID 10001</div><div>Writable exception<br>Bounded /tmp</div><div>Daemon mode<br>Inspect SecurityOptions separately</div></div><details><summary>Does UID 10001 prove the daemon is rootless?</summary><p>No. Inspect the selected daemon's configuration.</p></details><details><summary>Do requested CPU and memory flags prove enforcement?</summary><p>No. Verify actual enforcement and host support, especially in rootless setups.</p></details>`,
  "0536": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Containers on the app network can use the service name</figcaption><p>The API and a diagnostic client join the same user-defined bridge network, private-app.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Diagnostic container<br>Requests http://api:8000/health</div><div>Network name resolution<br>api identifies the container</div><div>Host exposure<br>Separate published-port choice</div></div><details><summary>Must the API publish port 8000 on the host for this internal request?</summary><p>No. Both containers use the shared network.</p></details><details><summary>Does the same name necessarily resolve from an unrelated network?</summary><p>No. Network membership and DNS scope matter.</p></details>`,
  "0537": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A replacement sees the volume marker, not the old layer marker</figcaption><p>The first container writes V in a named volume and L in its own writable layer.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Replacement mounts same volume<br>Can see V</div><div>Replacement has new layer<br>Does not inherit L</div><div>Access check<br>Verify intended non-root ownership</div></div><details><summary>Does surviving replacement make the volume a backup?</summary><p>No. A separate recoverable copy and restore procedure are still needed.</p></details><details><summary>Can correct mount location alone prove the user can read V?</summary><p>No. Check permissions and ownership as the actual runtime user.</p></details>`,
  "0538": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Waiting for database health does not configure the connection</figcaption><p>Compose starts the API after db becomes healthy. Both join an internal app network.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Startup gate<br>db health passes</div><div>API configuration<br>Still needs host db and credentials</div><div>Later database failure<br>Bounded reconnect needed</div></div><details><summary>Does depends_on fill in the API's database settings?</summary><p>No. Those settings must be supplied explicitly.</p></details><details><summary>Can this internal-only network be assumed to reach a public model API?</summary><p>No. Add a reviewed egress path if the application requires one.</p></details>`,
  "0539": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Unhealthy and exited are distinct container states</figcaption><p>The exercise makes a health check fail while the main process remains alive, then separately ends the process.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Health check fails<br>Main process still running</div><div>Process exits<br>Restart policy can apply</div></div><details><summary>Does standalone Docker restart a container solely because it is unhealthy?</summary><p>No. Health status alone does not trigger that restart.</p></details><details><summary>Does healthy startup remove the need for dependency recovery logic?</summary><p>No. Dependencies can fail later.</p></details>`,
  "0540": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Log volume grows with rate and retention</figcaption><p>Hypothetical bounded output: 1 KiB each second for 60 seconds.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Application output<br>60 KiB before overhead</div><div>Logging driver<br>Collection and rotation policy</div><div>External storage<br>Its own retention policy</div></div><details><summary>Does restarting the container define how long logs are retained?</summary><p>No. Inspect the driver and external retention settings.</p></details><details><summary>Should test logs contain real access tokens to resemble production?</summary><p>No. Use synthetic labels and payloads without secrets or personal data.</p></details>`,
  "0491": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A task definition describes one container's runtime contract</figcaption><p>The fixture requests Fargate with 512 CPU units and 1,024 MiB memory. The API uses port 8000 and a digest-pinned image.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Image identity<br>Exact digest placeholder</div><div>Runtime limits<br>Non-root user 10001<br>Read-only root filesystem</div><div>Shutdown allowance<br>30 seconds</div></div><details><summary>Does this definition alone expose a working public API?</summary><p>No. Networking, service deployment, permissions and traffic integration still need configuration.</p></details><details><summary>Can the application assume it can write anywhere in its root filesystem?</summary><p>No. The root filesystem is read-only. Any required writable storage needs an explicit design.</p></details>`,
  "0492": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">FIFO failure leaves later records unprocessed</figcaption><p>The batch contains IDs 0, 1 and 2. Processing ID 1 raises an error.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Standard mode<br>Visit 0, 1, 2<br>Report failed ID 1</div><div>FIFO mode<br>Visit 0, 1<br>Report IDs 1 and 2</div></div><details><summary>Why report ID 2 in FIFO mode when its handler never ran?</summary><p>It remains unprocessed after the earlier failure and must not be acknowledged as successful.</p></details><details><summary>Does returning this object enable partial batch handling by itself?</summary><p>No. The deployed event-source mapping must enable ReportBatchItemFailures.</p></details>`,
  "0493": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Three failed requests can stop at different layers</figcaption><p>Practice endpoint: POST /answer calls a model after authorization and admission.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>No valid identity<br>Authorizer rejects</div><div>Allowed identity, rate exceeded<br>Throttle rejects</div><div>Admitted request, slow model<br>Integration deadline expires</div></div><details><summary>Does a gateway timeout prove the model call stopped?</summary><p>No. Trace cancellation and backend work separately.</p></details><details><summary>Can one API Gateway configuration be assumed to work for HTTP, REST and WebSocket APIs?</summary><p>No. Verify capabilities for the selected API type.</p></details>`,
  "0494": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Writer loss and accidental deletion need different recovery paths</figcaption><p>Compare a failed writer with a valid transaction that accidentally deletes data.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Writer failure<br>Reconnect to valid writer<br>Resolve uncertain transactions</div><div>Accidental deletion<br>Replicas may contain deletion<br>Recover earlier data from backup history</div></div><details><summary>Does a read replica automatically undo the deletion?</summary><p>No. Replication can carry the same unwanted change.</p></details><details><summary>Does a connection proxy remove uncertainty about an interrupted commit?</summary><p>No. The application still needs a safe retry or reconciliation decision.</p></details>`,
  "0495": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Expiry time and physical deletion are different</figcaption><p>A practice item expires at 12:00. A read at 12:01 still finds the item because asynchronous TTL cleanup has not removed it.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Stored item exists<br>Physical cleanup pending</div><div>Application compares expiry<br>12:01 is after 12:00</div><div>Business decision<br>Treat expired item according to contract</div></div><details><summary>Can presence alone mean the item is still valid?</summary><p>No. Enforce the business expiry rule independently of cleanup.</p></details><details><summary>Will tenant-only partition keys spread one dominant tenant's traffic?</summary><p>Not automatically. Bucketing can distribute writes but may increase query fan-out.</p></details>`,
  "0496": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One expired key can trigger many database reads</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: 100 callers arrive just after a popular tenant-scoped cache entry expires.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>No refresh coordination<br>Up to 100 callers try fallback</div><div>Bounded refresh path<br>Coordinate refresh<br>Limit database admission</div><div>Cache unavailable<br>Fallback still needs a bound</div></div><details><summary>Does eviction mean the business record was deleted?</summary><p>No. The authoritative database determines that state.</p></details><details><summary>Can every failed cache request safely fall through to the database?</summary><p>Not without capacity and admission limits. The fallback can overload the dependency it is meant to protect.</p></details>`,
  "0497": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Visibility hides a message temporarily</figcaption><p>The standard-queue fixture sets visibility to 60 seconds and long polling to 20 seconds.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Receive message M<br>Temporarily hidden</div><div>Worker has not completed<br>Visibility expires</div><div>M can be delivered again<br>Protect the business effect</div></div><details><summary>Should the worker delete M before durable success?</summary><p>No. A crash afterward could lose the work.</p></details><details><summary>Does the dead-letter setting make delivery exactly once?</summary><p>No. The fixture expects duplicates. Redrive handles repeated failure, not effect deduplication.</p></details>`,
  "0498": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Replaying a workflow must recognize its earlier charge</figcaption><p>Practice workflow: charge C succeeds, reservation fails, and the triggering event is replayed.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original run<br>Charge C committed</div><div>Reservation failed<br>Persist recovery decision</div><div>Replay<br>Recognize C<br>Do not blindly charge again</div></div><details><summary>Is compensation an automatic rollback of the original charge?</summary><p>No. It is a new operation that can also fail and needs an owner and identity.</p></details><details><summary>Does replay restore the world to the event's original time?</summary><p>No. It processes history against current systems and must handle prior effects.</p></details>`,
  "0499": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A buffer grows when arrivals exceed processing</figcaption><p>Hypothetical stream: 100 events arrive each second and the consumer processes 80.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Each second<br>100 − 80 = 20 added</div><div>After 60 seconds<br>1,200-event backlog</div><div>Catch-up requirement<br>Drain faster than new arrivals</div></div><details><summary>Does increasing retention stabilize this load?</summary><p>No. It buys time but the backlog continues to grow.</p></details><details><summary>Can restarting from the last committed position repeat work?</summary><p>Yes. Effects completed after that position may be processed again. Design their identity and ordering rules.</p></details>`,
  "0500": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An alarm consumes a metric; it does not compute it</figcaption><p>The command watches the custom ErrorBudgetBurnRate metric with a fixture threshold of 14.4.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Count eligible and bad events</div><div>Compute failure fraction<br>Divide by allowed failure fraction</div><div>Publish custom metric<br>Alarm evaluates it</div></div><details><summary>Does creating the alarm start publishing burn rate?</summary><p>No. The metric-producing path is separate.</p></details><details><summary>Should missing telemetry be treated as zero failures automatically?</summary><p>No. Define low-traffic and missing-data behavior explicitly. This command is not a complete multi-window SLO alert.</p></details>`,
  "0501": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A policy-change alert is the start of a response</figcaption><p>A harmless fixture reports an unexpected policy change by principal P on resource R at time T.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Audit event<br>P, R, T and request identity</div><div>Detection<br>Compare with approved activity</div><div>Reviewed response<br>Contain and preserve evidence</div></div><details><summary>Does the alert prove the principal was malicious?</summary><p>No. Separate observed change from inferred intent and investigate context.</p></details><details><summary>Has containment happened merely because a dashboard is red?</summary><p>No. Assign an owner and verify the approved response actions.</p></details>`,
  "0502": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Review a proposed infrastructure change before applying it</figcaption><p>The commands create and describe change set release-2026-09-01 for evidence-prod, then request drift detection.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Create change set<br>Proposed update</div><div>Describe change set<br>Review replacements and effects</div><div>Detect drift<br>Inspect differences from declared state</div></div><details><summary>Does creating the change set apply the update?</summary><p>No. These commands do not execute it.</p></details><details><summary>Does a drift-detection request prove there is no drift?</summary><p>No. Inspect its completed findings and understand which resources and properties were checked.</p></details>`,
  "0503": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Recovery time includes more than restoring bytes</figcaption><p>Hypothetical drill: data restore takes 8 minutes, secrets and configuration take 3, and application verification takes 4, in sequence.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Data<br>8 minutes</div><div>Configuration<br>3 minutes</div><div>Verification<br>4 minutes<br>Total 15</div></div><details><summary>Would reporting only 8 minutes describe user recovery?</summary><p>No. The service is not verified usable until the later steps finish.</p></details><details><summary>Does backup success prove the data-loss objective was met?</summary><p>No. Compare recovered data with the declared objective and record what is missing.</p></details>`,
  "0504": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Cost can rise without more requests</figcaption><p>A worksheet keeps request count fixed but doubles each response's output bytes.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Requests<br>Unchanged</div><div>Output volume<br>Doubles</div><div>Recalculate<br>Egress and output-related costs<br>Use dated regional prices</div></div><details><summary>Can request count alone explain the bill?</summary><p>No. Include compute, storage, traffic, logs and other priced dimensions.</p></details><details><summary>Is a budget alert a guaranteed spending stop?</summary><p>No. It signals a threshold. Enforced limits require separate controls.</p></details>`,
  "0505": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Completed text is not the same as a verified answer</figcaption><p>The adapter accepts an end_turn response with text-only content. Imagine its blocks contain Hello and world.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Check stop reason<br>Must be end_turn</div><div>Check blocks<br>Only string text fields</div><div>Join exact strings<br>No separator is inserted</div></div><details><summary>Will a tool-use response pass this adapter?</summary><p>No. The adapter deliberately accepts only completed text turns.</p></details><details><summary>Does passing prove the answer is factual or authorized?</summary><p>No. Evaluate evidence and apply access controls separately before publishing.</p></details>`,
  "0506": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Defend one architecture against three separate failures</figcaption><p>The capstone exercise uses a cloud-hosted AI application. Test each failure separately.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Lost zone<br>Trace remaining capacity and state</div><div>Compromised credential<br>Trace reachable permissions</div><div>Model outage<br>Trace fallback user behavior</div></div><details><summary>Does listing cloud services explain recovery?</summary><p>No. Name detection, containment, recovery owner and evidence for the affected user journey.</p></details><details><summary>Can restore evidence substitute for a permission review?</summary><p>No. Recovery and credential containment address different failures.</p></details>`,
  "0507": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A faster build barely changes a long approval wait</figcaption><p>Hypothetical change: a 20-minute build waits two days for approval. Ignore other work for this calculation.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before<br>2,880 + 20 = 2,900 minutes</div><div>Build time halved<br>2,880 + 10 = 2,890 minutes</div><div>Total saving<br>10 minutes</div></div><details><summary>Did halving the build halve delivery lead time?</summary><p>No. The approval queue dominates this example.</p></details><details><summary>Should all approval controls simply be removed?</summary><p>No. Find the smallest safe change at the constraint, assign an owner and measure risk and feedback.</p></details>`,
  "0508": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count handles after the request finishes</figcaption><p>Hypothetical bounded fixture: a process starts with 20 descriptors. A request opens 5 and should close them.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before<br>20 descriptors</div><div>During request<br>25 descriptors</div><div>After cleanup<br>Expected return to 20</div></div><details><summary>What does a rising count after repeated requests suggest?</summary><p>Investigate leaked ownership or unfinished work. Compare with the process limit before blaming host-wide exhaustion.</p></details><details><summary>Does a restart prove the leak is fixed?</summary><p>No. Repeat the bounded workload and verify cleanup.</p></details>`,
  "0509": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A connection can succeed before identity validation fails</figcaption><p>Practice endpoint: DNS resolves and TCP connects, but the certificate does not match the requested hostname.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>DNS<br>Address found</div><div>TCP<br>Connection established</div><div>TLS identity<br>Hostname mismatch<br>Reject</div></div><details><summary>Should certificate verification be disabled to make the check green?</summary><p>No. Correct the identity or configuration problem.</p></details><details><summary>Would a valid TLS session prove the HTTP application is healthy?</summary><p>No. HTTP status and application behavior are later checks with their own deadlines.</p></details>`,
  "0510": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject a tag when a digest is required</figcaption><p>The shell fixture requires an image reference ending in @sha256: followed by 64 hexadecimal characters.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>api:latest<br>Fails syntax check<br>Exit 2</div><div>Valid digest-shaped reference<br>Dry-run adapter</div><div>Approved execution path<br>Deploy, then smoke test</div></div><details><summary>Does passing the regular expression verify the image's signature?</summary><p>No. Syntax is not provenance, signature verification or authorization.</p></details><details><summary>Does the script itself pause for approval after the dry-run?</summary><p>No. The instructions require inspection and approval before running the remaining lines. Do not assume an automatic gate exists.</p></details>`,
  "0511": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Rebasing changes the identity of replayed commits</figcaption><p>In a disposable repository, a private branch contains A then B. Compare merging another branch with replaying A and B onto a new base.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Merge<br>Retain existing commit identities</div><div>Rebase<br>Replayed commits have new identities</div><div>Recovery aid<br>Inspect local reflog</div></div><details><summary>Is a reflog a permanent remote backup?</summary><p>No. It is local and subject to retention.</p></details><details><summary>Should this recovery exercise use a shared branch and force-push?</summary><p>No. Practice on disposable private history so others' work is not rewritten.</p></details>`,
  "0512": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The integration job tests the uploaded candidate</figcaption><p>The workflow's test job runs tests, builds dist and uploads candidate. The integration job depends on test.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Test job<br>Test → build → upload</div><div>Dependency edge<br>integration needs test</div><div>Integration job<br>Download candidate<br>Test dist</div></div><details><summary>Does the integration job build a second candidate in the shown workflow?</summary><p>No. It downloads the uploaded artifact.</p></details><details><summary>Does checkout alone guarantee that an artifact was verified?</summary><p>No. Preserve artifact identity and test results through the later release path.</p></details>`,
  "0513": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Match each defect to the boundary that can expose it</figcaption><p>The checkout exercise introduces three faults separately.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Wrong tax arithmetic<br>Pure rule test</div><div>Duplicate database insert<br>Constraint integration test</div><div>Wrong provider field<br>Payment contract test</div></div><details><summary>Can a mocked payment test prove the provider accepts the field?</summary><p>No. The fake may encode the same mistaken assumption as the application.</p></details><details><summary>Must every arithmetic case use a full browser journey?</summary><p>No. Use the smallest meaningful test for that risk, then cover essential end-to-end behavior separately.</p></details>`,
  "0514": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The same display version can hide different bytes</figcaption><p>Practice release 1.2 has expected digest A. A substituted artifact also says 1.2 but has digest B.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Display version<br>Both say 1.2</div><div>Content identity<br>A differs from B</div><div>Verification policy<br>Bind expected digest to source and builder</div></div><details><summary>Does an SBOM alone establish the artifact's origin?</summary><p>No. Dependency inventory and verified provenance answer different questions.</p></details><details><summary>Should an unexpected builder be accepted because the version label matches?</summary><p>No. The verification policy must check builder identity as well as artifact identity.</p></details>`,
  "0515": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Untrusted pull-request code must not inherit deployment power</figcaption><p>A forked pull request runs code on a runner. Production deployment uses a protected environment and scoped OIDC trust.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Untrusted code<br>Test boundary</div><div>Deployment gate<br>Subject, audience and approval checks</div><div>Cloud role<br>Only approved context receives authority</div></div><details><summary>Are short-lived credentials safe to expose to any runner code?</summary><p>No. They still grant authority while valid.</p></details><details><summary>When should an untrusted branch be denied?</summary><p>Before it obtains deployment authority, not after its code starts using that authority.</p></details>`,
  "0466": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Repeat a transfer without moving money twice</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: A has 10 units and B has 0. Operation T transfers 3 units from A to B.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before T<br>A 10, B 0<br>Total 10</div><div>After T<br>A 7, B 3<br>Total 10</div><div>Replay T<br>A 7, B 3<br>One effect</div></div><details><summary>Would A 4 and B 6 satisfy every invariant?</summary><p>No. The total remains 10, but the repeated operation caused a second effect.</p></details><details><summary>Does a load-test timeout alone show money was lost?</summary><p>No. Check committed state and operation identities separately from overload. Save the failing schedule or random seed.</p></details>`,
  "0467": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A valid identity can still request the wrong document</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: user A belongs to tenant A. Document B belongs to tenant B.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request has valid identity A<br>Authentication passes</div><div>Requested document B<br>Tenant ownership differs</div><div>Object authorization<br>Deny before exposing fields</div></div><details><summary>Would checking only that the user is logged in prevent this leak?</summary><p>No. The API must authorize access to the requested object.</p></details><details><summary>What changes when identity is missing?</summary><p>Authentication fails before object access. Keep this test separate from the valid-identity, wrong-tenant case.</p></details>`,
  "0468": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An approved package name can deliver unexpected behavior</figcaption><p>The harmless practice fixture changes an artifact digest and introduces an unexpected lifecycle script.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Allowed package name<br>Not sufficient evidence</div><div>Resolved artifact<br>Digest differs from expected</div><div>Build boundary<br>Verify artifact<br>Restrict script permissions</div></div><details><summary>Does a clean scanner result prove the script is safe?</summary><p>No. Verify source and artifact identity, isolate installation and limit permissions.</p></details><details><summary>Which identity belongs in the investigation?</summary><p>Record the resolved version, artifact digest and build identity. The package name alone cannot identify what shipped.</p></details>`,
  "0469": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Ten bad requests use the whole budget</figcaption><p>For 10,000 eligible requests and a 99.9% target, the fixed-window allowance is 10 bad requests.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>5 bad<br>Half the budget used</div><div>10 bad<br>Budget exactly exhausted<br>Within target</div><div>11 bad<br>Outside target</div></div><details><summary>What does zero traffic report?</summary><p>No data. It does not prove perfect reliability.</p></details><details><summary>Can you average two instance percentages without their traffic counts?</summary><p>No. Ten requests with one failure plus 990 with none give 99.9% fleet success, not the unweighted 95% average.</p></details>`,
  "0470": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Verify original bytes before accepting a webhook</figcaption><p>A provider sends event E. The receiver reads a bounded raw body and verifies its signature before durable acceptance.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original body and signature<br>Provider SDK verification</div><div>Verified event E<br>Scoped inbox and job commit</div><div>Repeated E<br>Same acceptance<br>No extra job</div></div><details><summary>Does a valid timestamp prevent replay during the allowed window?</summary><p>No. Event identity and durable deduplication still matter.</p></details><details><summary>Should a failed inbox commit return 204?</summary><p>No. Successful acceptance requires the durable commit. Reserializing the signed body also changes the bytes being verified.</p></details>`,
  "0471": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Scan the accepted version, not whatever is newest</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: completion accepts version V1 of an uploaded PDF. The reusable upload URL later writes V2 at the same key.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Accepted record<br>Key plus V1</div><div>Later upload<br>Latest key now V2</div><div>Scan and publish<br>Use exact V1<br>Do not switch to V2</div></div><details><summary>Does a matching content-type header prove the PDF is safe?</summary><p>No. The scanner must inspect the actual content and enforce document limits.</p></details><details><summary>Why store the scan job with the acceptance transaction?</summary><p>It keeps accepted version metadata and the durable work request together.</p></details>`,
  "0472": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An expired attempt cannot delete a new owner's job</figcaption><p>Attempt A pauses beyond its lease. Attempt B reclaims the job with a new attempt UUID.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A held old lease<br>Lease expires</div><div>B claims job<br>New owner UUID</div><div>A resumes<br>Owner predicate fails<br>No acknowledgment of B's work</div></div><details><summary>Does expiry prevent A from making an external call?</summary><p>No. A paused process can resume. Protect effects with idempotency or reconciliation separately.</p></details><details><summary>Can a retry update omit the owner check?</summary><p>No. Retry, heartbeat and dead-letter changes need the same live-owner predicate and affected-row check.</p></details>`,
  "0473": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Changing an audit outcome changes its digest</figcaption><p>The stored payload says actor user-1 disabled a target with outcome allowed.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Exact payload bytes<br>Previous hash<br>Compute digest H</div><div>Change allowed to denied<br>Payload differs<br>Digest differs</div><div>Verification<br>Compare against trusted continuity evidence</div></div><details><summary>Can the local chain detect an administrator who rewrites the whole chain?</summary><p>Not by itself. Independent immutable checkpoints are needed to expose that rewrite.</p></details><details><summary>May verification parse and reserialize the payload first?</summary><p>No. This scheme hashes the exact stored string. Different serialization can change the digest.</p></details>`,
  "0474": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Recheck the target after preview</figcaption><p>An administrator previews disabling user B and confirms B's email. Before execution, the target or permissions can change.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Preview<br>Show target and session count</div><div>Execution transaction<br>Lock and recheck scope<br>Recheck confirmation</div><div>Accepted operation<br>Disable, revoke and audit together</div></div><details><summary>Is typing the correct email sufficient authorization?</summary><p>No. It is a safety check. Current permissions and target scope still apply.</p></details><details><summary>Should the changing session count be part of replay identity?</summary><p>No. Use the stable action, target and reason. Otherwise the same intended operation could appear different after execution.</p></details>`,
  "0475": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Managed hosting does not add a missing tenant filter</figcaption><p>A managed database stores records for tenants A and B. The application accidentally queries both for a tenant-A request.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Provider responsibility<br>Managed infrastructure</div><div>Application responsibility<br>Correct authorization and query</div><div>Missing tenant check<br>Hosting does not repair the leak</div></div><details><summary>Would more database capacity fix the authorization defect?</summary><p>No. Capacity and access control protect different properties.</p></details><details><summary>Who must prove that the application's backup can restore useful data?</summary><p>The team must assign restore-test ownership. A managed backup feature alone is not recovery evidence.</p></details>`,
  "0476": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two zones still share a region</figcaption><p>Paper design: an application spans zones A and B in one region and uses a regional database.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Lose zone A<br>Inspect B's remaining capacity</div><div>Lose the region<br>A and B both unavailable</div><div>Recovery question<br>Where are usable state and dependencies?</div></div><details><summary>Does the second zone prove regional disaster recovery?</summary><p>No. Both zones are inside the affected region.</p></details><details><summary>Is a running recovery server enough?</summary><p>No. It needs reachable credentials, data and other dependencies, with a verified recovery path.</p></details>`,
  "0477": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A guardrail can block an identity's allow</figcaption><p>In the fictional production OU, an identity policy allows action X while an organizational policy explicitly denies X.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Identity policy<br>Allow X</div><div>Organizational guardrail<br>Deny X</div><div>Effective decision<br>Denied</div></div><details><summary>Does removing the organizational deny grant X?</summary><p>It removes that restriction. The request still needs applicable permissions and must pass other controls.</p></details><details><summary>Does a permissive SCP grant X when the identity has no allow?</summary><p>No. An SCP limits permissions; it does not grant them.</p></details>`,
  "0478": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A successful call can reach the wrong account</figcaption><p>The practice deployment intends to use profile training in one region. Another credential source is also present.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Intended destination<br>Training account and region</div><div>Inspect invocation<br>Credential source and caller identity</div><div>Compare before writing<br>Success alone is insufficient</div></div><details><summary>Should the diagnostic print access keys to identify the source?</summary><p>No. Record redacted source metadata and caller identity without secrets.</p></details><details><summary>Can you assume the same precedence for every SDK and CLI invocation?</summary><p>No. Check the exact version and invocation form, including explicit profile behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0479": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An object prefix limits this read permission</figcaption><p>The policy allows GetObject only under evidence-bucket/tenant-42/ and requires secure transport.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>tenant-42/report.pdf<br>Secure transport<br>Matches this allow</div><div>tenant-43/report.pdf<br>Outside resource prefix</div><div>PutObject<br>Outside allowed action</div></div><details><summary>Does matching the allow prove the request will succeed?</summary><p>No. Other policies, explicit denies and service conditions can still prevent access.</p></details><details><summary>Does this policy grant listing the bucket?</summary><p>No. It names GetObject, not a bucket-listing action.</p></details>`,
  "0480": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Role trust and role permissions are separate gates</figcaption><p>A deployment role trusts one repository and its protected environment.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Untrusted branch subject<br>Role assumption denied</div><div>Trusted subject<br>Session can be issued</div><div>Requested cloud action<br>Role and session permissions apply</div></div><details><summary>Does permission to assume a role allow every cloud action?</summary><p>No. The resulting session has its own permission limits.</p></details><details><summary>What changes after session expiry?</summary><p>The expired credentials cannot authorize new requests. Obtain a new permitted session instead of storing long-lived CI keys.</p></details>`,
  "0481": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Encrypted data still needs permission to recover its key</figcaption><p>The envelope-encryption exercise stores ciphertext and an encrypted data key. The application needs the plaintext data key to decrypt the data.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Stored material<br>Ciphertext and encrypted key</div><div>Key service request<br>Caller permission and context checked</div><div>Decrypt denied<br>Application cannot recover plaintext key</div></div><details><summary>Should the encryption context contain a secret?</summary><p>No. It is authenticated metadata, not secret storage.</p></details><details><summary>Does key rotation automatically revoke every caller?</summary><p>No. Rotation and permission revocation are separate mechanisms.</p></details>`,
  "0482": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two subnet names do not provide zone redundancy</figcaption><p>The template places app subnet 10.40.16.0/20 and data subnet 10.40.64.0/20 inside VPC 10.40.0.0/16. Both use us-east-1a.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>App subnet<br>Zone us-east-1a</div><div>Data subnet<br>Zone us-east-1a</div><div>Zone failure<br>Both affected</div></div><details><summary>Does the data subnet's name make it isolated?</summary><p>No. Its route and access policies must establish isolation, including no internet default route for this design.</p></details><details><summary>What is missing for the stated multi-zone plan?</summary><p>Additional subnet placements in other zones and the routing and service configuration that use them.</p></details>`,
  "0483": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Removing NAT affects different destinations differently</figcaption><p>Paper fixture: a private subnet reaches an object store through a configured service endpoint and a public API through NAT.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Object store<br>Endpoint route and policy</div><div>Public API<br>NAT egress path</div><div>Remove NAT route<br>Public API path breaks</div></div><details><summary>Must the object-store path also break?</summary><p>No. Its endpoint path can remain valid if DNS, routes and policy still permit it.</p></details><details><summary>Does a private address prove reachability?</summary><p>No. Trace the required routes, directions and address family.</p></details>`,
  "0484": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The response uses the client's temporary port</figcaption><p>Paper connection: client port 50000 sends to server port 443. A stateless subnet ACL blocks the return destination port 50000.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request<br>50000 → 443</div><div>Response<br>443 → 50000</div><div>Return ACL denies 50000<br>Connection cannot complete normally</div></div><details><summary>Does a stateful security group make the separate ACL stateful?</summary><p>No. Both controls must allow the required traffic under their own rules.</p></details><details><summary>Does one rejected flow-log entry prove the complete application cause?</summary><p>No. Combine filtering evidence with routes, DNS and endpoint checks.</p></details>`,
  "0485": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A DNS change does not move an existing connection</figcaption><p>In a tabletop failover, the authoritative answer changes from primary A to recovery B.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>New lookup without old cache<br>Can learn B</div><div>Cached answer A<br>May still use A</div><div>Existing connection to A<br>Not migrated by DNS</div></div><details><summary>Can the recovery clock start only when the record changes?</summary><p>No. Include health detection, caches, reconnect behavior and readiness of B.</p></details><details><summary>Does resolving B prove the application recovered?</summary><p>No. B must have usable state and dependencies.</p></details>`,
  "0486": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The same URL can have different private answers</figcaption><p>User A and user B request /account. Their balances differ. A local cache uses only the URL as its key.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A requests /account<br>Cache stores A's response</div><div>B requests /account<br>Same cache key</div><div>Unsafe reuse<br>A's data could reach B</div></div><details><summary>Does a signed access URL repair this cache key?</summary><p>No. Authorization and safe response reuse are separate decisions.</p></details><details><summary>What must the cache test establish?</summary><p>Private responses must not cross users. Use an appropriate isolation policy or avoid caching them in the shared cache.</p></details>`,
  "0487": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A listening socket can belong to an unready application</figcaption><p>The practice process accepts TCP connections but its critical database is unavailable.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>TCP check<br>Connection accepted</div><div>Application request<br>Critical dependency unavailable</div><div>Readiness policy<br>Must represent ability to serve intended work</div></div><details><summary>Does the successful TCP check prove a checkout will succeed?</summary><p>No. It tests connection acceptance, not the whole operation.</p></details><details><summary>Can a draining target be killed immediately without affecting long requests?</summary><p>No. Define drain deadlines and treatment of existing connections explicitly.</p></details>`,
  "0488": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A replacement instance must survive incomplete bootstrap</figcaption><p>Paper fixture: instance A is replaced by B. B fails halfway through bootstrap.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Immutable image<br>Known application version</div><div>B bootstrap fails<br>Do not mark ready</div><div>Next replacement<br>Recover using durable state and repeatable inputs</div></div><details><summary>Should B receive normal traffic merely because its process started?</summary><p>No. It must pass the defined readiness criterion.</p></details><details><summary>Where must diagnostic evidence live if B is lost?</summary><p>Store necessary logs outside the disposable instance. Otherwise the failure can erase its own evidence.</p></details>`,
  "0489": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">State survival depends on its storage lifecycle</figcaption><p>Paper fixture: an instance has a scratch file, an attached block volume and access to a shared file store.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Scratch data<br>Disposable instance-local work</div><div>Block volume<br>Inspect deletion and attachment policy</div><div>Shared file<br>Independent service lifecycle</div></div><details><summary>Does terminating the instance answer whether the block volume survives?</summary><p>Not without its configured lifecycle policy. Do not infer retention from the word persistent alone.</p></details><details><summary>Does possessing a snapshot prove recovery?</summary><p>No. Restore it and verify application consistency under the intended recovery procedure.</p></details>`,
  "0490": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A delete marker hides an older version</figcaption><p>In a disposable versioned bucket, an object has version V1. Deleting without a version ID creates a delete marker.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before deletion<br>V1 visible</div><div>After ordinary deletion<br>Delete marker is current<br>V1 still exists</div><div>Remove marker<br>Earlier version can become visible</div></div><details><summary>Can a delete marker restore V1 after V1 itself was permanently deleted?</summary><p>No. Removing a marker cannot recreate deleted bytes.</p></details><details><summary>Should version IDs and retention rules be recorded before the exercise?</summary><p>Yes. They identify the exact disposable targets and any policy that prevents deletion.</p></details>`,
  "0453": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count requests that are in flight</figcaption><p>The estimate combines 2,000 writes and 20,000 reads per second. Mean response time is 0.08 seconds.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Mean arrival rate<br>22,000 per second</div><div>Mean concurrency<br>22,000 × 0.08 = 1,760</div><div>Four-times-load scenario<br>88,000 × 0.08 = 7,040</div></div><details><summary>Can the 300 ms p99 target replace the mean in this calculation?</summary><p>No. Little's Law uses consistent-window means for a stable system.</p></details><details><summary>Is 7,040 a measured capacity limit?</summary><p>No. It assumes mean latency remains 80 ms at peak load. Saturation can invalidate that assumption.</p></details>`,
  "0454": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A hot link and a large file need different paths</figcaption><p>Compare two practice requests: read one popular short URL, and submit one large file for processing.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Short URL<br>Read-heavy key lookup<br>Hot-key and abuse controls</div><div>Large file<br>Durable asynchronous job<br>Byte limits and retry identity</div></div><details><summary>Should the file request hold a synchronous connection through all processing?</summary><p>That conflicts with the durable asynchronous workflow in this case. Separate acceptance from later completion.</p></details><details><summary>Does naming a cache finish the shortener design?</summary><p>No. Explain ownership, invalidation, hot-link behavior and the failure path. The supplied design call is a planning placeholder.</p></details>`,
  "0455": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow one job across three reliability boundaries</figcaption><p>A client submits job J with a stable idempotency key. The response is lost after acceptance.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Database transaction<br>Job and outbox commit</div><div>Client retries<br>Same request identity<br>Recover accepted outcome</div><div>Broker repeats event<br>Inbox and local effect<br>Avoid repeated local outcome</div></div><details><summary>Does the inbox make the model provider part of the database transaction?</summary><p>No. The model call has its own deadline, concurrency and outcome rules.</p></details><details><summary>Does the game-day call prove recovery targets were achieved?</summary><p>No. It lists an exercise. Record measured recovery and invariant checks from an actual drill.</p></details>`,
  "0456": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count the costs of extracting billing</figcaption><p>The discussion fixture gives billing independent changes and owned data. It needs no separate scaling, no cross-boundary transaction and one synchronous call per request.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Benefit score<br>1 + 1 + 0 = 2</div><div>Distributed cost<br>0 + 1 = 1</div><div>Fixture decision<br>Passes extraction threshold</div></div><details><summary>What if extraction adds two cross-boundary transactions?</summary><p>The cost becomes 3, so the fixture returns false.</p></details><details><summary>Does passing the score mean extraction is the right production decision?</summary><p>No. Discuss deployment, ownership and on-call costs. The score is only a discussion aid.</p></details>`,
  "0457": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The word order has two local meanings</figcaption><p>Ordering and Fulfillment use the same noun for different business concepts.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Ordering<br>Order means purchase commitment</div><div>Published boundary<br>Translate agreed information</div><div>Fulfillment<br>Order means warehouse pick instruction</div></div><details><summary>Must both teams share one Order class?</summary><p>No. Each context can keep its own model and translate at the boundary.</p></details><details><summary>Does each noun require a microservice?</summary><p>No. A context map describes model and team relationships. Deployment is a separate decision.</p></details>`,
  "0458": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Place a draft order once</figcaption><p>Order A-42 contains 2,500 USD cents. It starts as a draft.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First place call<br>Status becomes placed<br>One OrderPlaced event</div><div>First pullEvents call<br>Returns event<br>Clears in-memory list</div><div>Second place call<br>Rejected<br>No extra event</div></div><details><summary>Does readonly alone freeze a Money object at runtime?</summary><p>No. This example also freezes the object explicitly.</p></details><details><summary>Can an event drained from memory be assumed durable?</summary><p>No. Persist it atomically with the order. A failed transaction must not discard the only publication intent.</p></details>`,
  "0459": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the order and its publication intent together</figcaption><p>Command C places order A-42. The outbox relay may deliver its event more than once.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Transaction one<br>Claim C<br>Save order and outbox event</div><div>Relay<br>Send event E<br>Possible repeat</div><div>Consumer transaction<br>Claim E and create fulfillment</div></div><details><summary>What if two different command IDs try to change the same order?</summary><p>Command deduplication alone does not serialize them. The order still needs version checking or locking.</p></details><details><summary>May the consumer commit its inbox claim before its local effect?</summary><p>No. They must commit together, or a replay could skip an effect that never happened.</p></details>`,
  "0460": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One failed subscriber does not undo another</figcaption><p>Two in-memory subscribers receive event B-43. One throws. The other records the order ID.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Subscriber one<br>Throws an error</div><div>Subscriber two<br>Records B-43</div><div>Publish result<br>Reports partial fan-out failure</div></div><details><summary>Does publish failure mean no subscriber ran?</summary><p>No. The successful subscriber's effect remains.</p></details><details><summary>After removing the throwing subscriber, does delivery become durable?</summary><p>No. This broker still stores subscriptions and work only in memory. It has no persistent delivery position.</p></details>`,
  "0461": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep one order's events on one partition</figcaption><p>The lab topic has six partitions, three replicas and seven-day delete retention. Events use order_id as the key.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Key A-42<br>Stable partitioner and count<br>Same partition</div><div>Different partitions<br>No global event order</div><div>Seven-day retention policy<br>Delete cleanup<br>Not key compaction</div></div><details><summary>Does adding partitions preserve the existing key placement automatically?</summary><p>No. The partition mapping may change. Plan how ordering crosses that change.</p></details><details><summary>Does this topic retain only the newest event per order?</summary><p>No. Its cleanup policy is delete, not compaction.</p></details>`,
  "0462": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Stop writes when the safe replica set is too small</figcaption><p>The topic has three replicas, minimum ISR 2 and producer acknowledgments set to all.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Three in-sync replicas<br>Wait for current ISR</div><div>Two in-sync replicas<br>Policy still permits writes</div><div>One in-sync replica<br>Policy rejects safe writes</div></div><details><summary>Does acks=all always wait for exactly two replicas?</summary><p>No. It waits for the full current in-sync replica set. The minimum is an admission requirement.</p></details><details><summary>Why not elect an out-of-sync replica just to restore writes?</summary><p>That can lose acknowledged history. The fixture disables unclean leader election.</p></details>`,
  "0463": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Commit the next offset after local effects</figcaption><p>A valid one-partition batch contains offsets 10 and 12. A gap is allowed, for example after compaction.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Offset 10<br>Inbox and effect commit</div><div>Offset 12<br>Inbox and effect commit</div><div>Consumer position<br>Commit 13</div></div><details><summary>What if the process crashes before committing 13?</summary><p>The broker can repeat the batch. Durable inbox claims must prevent repeated local effects.</p></details><details><summary>Would offsets 12 then 10 be accepted?</summary><p>No. The starter checks the full batch and rejects decreasing offsets before applying effects.</p></details>`,
  "0464": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An optional field does not prove compatibility</figcaption><p>The v2 schema permits channel but requires the type value OrderPlaced.v2. It rejects unknown properties.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>v1 event type<br>Fails v2 constant</div><div>v2 event without channel<br>Default is an annotation<br>No automatic insertion promised</div><div>Unknown property<br>Fails strict object schema</div></div><details><summary>Will every validator enforce occurred_at as a valid date-time?</summary><p>Not necessarily. Pin the validator's format-assertion settings.</p></details><details><summary>Can the broker acknowledge a rejected event before quarantine storage succeeds?</summary><p>No. Persist the quarantine record first so the event is not silently lost.</p></details>`,
  "0465": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A passing fake can hide a broken payment request</figcaption><p>In this practice checkout, the price rule is correct but a fake accepts a field that the real payment contract rejects.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Unit test<br>Price rule passes</div><div>Fake payment test<br>Invalid field accepted<br>Test passes</div><div>Contract test<br>Invalid field rejected<br>Boundary defect exposed</div></div><details><summary>Does the passing fake prove integration compatibility?</summary><p>No. It proves behavior against the fake's rules.</p></details><details><summary>Where should a uniqueness constraint be tested?</summary><p>At the database boundary that enforces it. A pure pricing test cannot establish that guarantee.</p></details>`,
  "0443": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A quorum needs a defined replica set</figcaption><p>Three replicas are A, B and C. A write needs two acknowledgments. A read contacts two replicas.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Write: A, B<br>Read: B, C<br>Shared replica: B</div><div>Sloppy write: A, D<br>Read: B, C<br>No shared replica</div></div><details><summary>Do two replies on each side always guarantee overlap?</summary><p>No. The fixed-set argument fails when the write uses a replacement outside that set.</p></details><details><summary>Does overlap alone make this a linearizable register?</summary><p>No. The protocol must also handle versions, concurrent writes and incomplete writes.</p></details>`,
  "0444": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A completed write comes before a later read</figcaption><p>The register starts at 0. These times describe one hypothetical history.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Time 0 to 5<br>Write 1 completes</div><div>Time 6 to 7<br>Read returns 0</div><div>Single-register check<br>Later read misses completed write</div></div><details><summary>Is this history linearizable?</summary><p>No. With no other write, the later read must return 1.</p></details><details><summary>What if the read starts at time 2?</summary><p>The operations overlap. The supplied checker rejects that input as unsupported; its simple non-overlap test cannot decide it.</p></details>`,
  "0445": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An isolated replica cannot learn the new value</figcaption><p>A and B start with 0. A completes a write of 1. A network partition prevents B from hearing about it.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Client reads B<br>B still knows 0</div><div>Linearizable branch<br>Cannot safely return stale 0<br>Read unavailable</div><div>Available-eventual branch<br>Returns 0<br>Stale answer allowed</div></div><details><summary>Does returning an error satisfy CAP availability?</summary><p>No. A response that refuses the operation does not satisfy that requirement.</p></details><details><summary>Does this simulation prove eventual convergence?</summary><p>No. It illustrates the partition choice. A real system still needs a reconciliation protocol.</p></details>`,
  "0446": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Adding one token moves one interval</figcaption><p>The example ring uses points 0 through 99. A owns token 20, B owns 60 and C owns 90. Choose the first token at or after the key, wrapping when needed.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before adding D at 40<br>Point 21 goes to B</div><div>After adding D at 40<br>Points 21 through 40 go to D</div><div>Unchanged boundaries<br>Point 20 goes to A<br>Point 99 wraps to A</div></div><details><summary>How many of the 100 points change owner?</summary><p>Twenty points change owner: 21 through 40 inclusive.</p></details><details><summary>Will the ring spread 1,000 requests for point 21 across all nodes?</summary><p>No. They all target D. Even key placement does not remove a hot-key bottleneck.</p></details>`,
  "0447": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A newer term does not prove a matching log</figcaption><p>The candidate has term 3 and a term-3 entry at index 1. AppendEntries arrives with term 4 but claims that index 1 has term 2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Persist hard state<br>Term 4<br>Clear previous vote</div><div>Step down<br>Become follower</div><div>Compare log prefix<br>Local term 3 differs from 2<br>Reject prefix</div></div><details><summary>Should the node keep term 3 because the prefix failed?</summary><p>No. It has already learned and persisted the higher term.</p></details><details><summary>Does a matching prefix mean replication has succeeded?</summary><p>No. This starter only marks the request ready for log processing. Durable entries and commit rules still need implementation.</p></details>`,
  "0448": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject a paused owner's old write</figcaption><p>Suppose owner A holds fencing token 7. Its lease expires. Owner B obtains token 8 and the protected resource records B's write.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Stored fence: 8<br>A resumes with 7</div><div>Required comparison<br>Stored fence must be less than incoming fence</div><div>8 is not less than 7<br>A changes no row</div></div><details><summary>Does stopping heartbeat messages prove A has stopped running?</summary><p>No. A may be paused or isolated and later resume. The resource must enforce fencing.</p></details><details><summary>Can the starter write twice with token 8?</summary><p>No. Its strict comparison permits one accepted write per token. Repeated writes need a different explicit contract.</p></details>`,
  "0449": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Ignore an idle endpoint that is draining</figcaption><p>The local discovery snapshot contains three endpoints.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A: ready<br>2 outstanding requests</div><div>B: ready<br>0 outstanding requests</div><div>C: ready but draining<br>0 outstanding requests<br>Excluded</div></div><details><summary>Which endpoint does the selector choose?</summary><p>B. If B becomes not ready, it chooses A. C remains excluded while draining.</p></details><details><summary>Does choosing B reserve its capacity?</summary><p>No. The caller must update local accounting before yielding and release it on completion. The snapshot also needs a freshness policy.</p></details>`,
  "0450": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Route the request, then choose a healthy target</figcaption><p>In the example, an API host or a path starting with /api/ selects the API route. Within the local zone, healthy targets A and B have eight and two outstanding requests.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>HTTP route<br>Host or path selects API</div><div>Target selection<br>A: healthy, 8<br>B: healthy, 2<br>Choose B</div><div>Remote C: unhealthy, 0<br>Excluded despite low count</div></div><details><summary>Can an ordinary layer-4 selector inspect the HTTP path to make this choice?</summary><p>No. Path-based routing needs HTTP-aware layer-7 handling.</p></details><details><summary>What happens when no healthy local target remains?</summary><p>The supplied selector considers healthy targets in other zones. It does not make unhealthy C eligible.</p></details>`,
  "0451": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Recovery targets need a measured failover drill</figcaption><p>The plan names ap-south-1 as primary and eu-west-1 as recovery. Replication is asynchronous. Targets are 30 seconds of possible data loss and 15 minutes to restore service.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Normal operation<br>Primary accepts writes<br>Recovery writes disabled</div><div>Approved failover<br>Fence old writer<br>Promote recovery</div><div>Drill evidence<br>Measure lost data and recovery time</div></div><details><summary>Does writing those targets in YAML prove they are met?</summary><p>No. Measure replication lag, dependencies, routing and recovery under a failure drill.</p></details><details><summary>Can both regions accept authoritative writes during promotion?</summary><p>Not under this plan. Fence the previous writer before enabling the new one.</p></details>`,
  "0452": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Rebuild a job from three accepted events</figcaption><p>The in-memory example starts with a missing job at version 0.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>JobSubmitted<br>Queued, version 1</div><div>JobStarted<br>Running, version 2</div><div>JobCompleted<br>Done, version 3</div></div><details><summary>What happens if Complete is requested again?</summary><p>The state rule rejects it. The job remains done at version 3.</p></details><details><summary>Does deciding an event mean it was durably stored?</summary><p>No. The decision does not mutate state. A durable implementation must append with the expected version before applying the event.</p></details>`,
  "0434": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Stop before a later offset skips failed work</figcaption><p>Records arrive in one partition at offsets 10, 11 and 12. Handling 11 fails.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Offset 10 succeeds<br>Commit next offset 11</div><div>Offset 11 fails<br>Route failure<br>Stop partition</div><div>Offset 12<br>Do not continue and commit 13</div></div><details><summary>Does routing the error prove record 11 was durably handled?</summary><p>No. The disposition policy needs durable evidence before advancing past it.</p></details><details><summary>Why use BigInt when advancing offsets?</summary><p>Large decimal offsets can exceed JavaScript's safe integer range.</p></details>`,
  "0435": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Commit local effect before acknowledging delivery</figcaption><p>The consumer claims event E and applies its local effect in one transaction.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Transaction commits<br>Inbox E and effect together</div><div>Crash before acknowledgment<br>Broker can redeliver E</div><div>Redelivery<br>Committed claim already exists<br>Skip repeated local effect</div></div><details><summary>Would acknowledging before the transaction improve safety?</summary><p>No. A crash before commit could lose the effect without redelivery.</p></details><details><summary>Does this transaction protect an unrelated external API call automatically?</summary><p>No. External effects need their own identity and reconciliation contract.</p></details>`,
  "0436": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Changing the partition count can move a key</figcaption><p>Illustrative stable hash value: 5.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Two partitions<br>5 modulo 2<br>Partition 1</div><div>Three partitions<br>5 modulo 3<br>Partition 2</div><div>Migration concern<br>Old and new records can occupy different partitions</div></div><details><summary>Does the modulo change preserve per-key processing order automatically?</summary><p>No. Define an ordering migration plan across the topology change.</p></details><details><summary>Do extra consumers beyond the partition count increase group parallelism?</summary><p>No. Assigned partitions bound that parallelism.</p></details>`,
  "0437": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Choose a failure route before scheduling more attempts</figcaption><p>The classifier receives a structured error code.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>SCHEMA_INVALID<br>Quarantine<br>No automatic retry</div><div>DEPENDENCY_UNAVAILABLE<br>Retry route</div><div>Unknown code<br>Investigate<br>No automatic retry</div></div><details><summary>Does quarantine mean the record was safely stored?</summary><p>No. This function only classifies. Persist the protected record before acknowledging the source.</p></details><details><summary>Can moving one failed record to a retry topic change per-key order?</summary><p>Yes. Later records may overtake it. Decide whether the business contract permits that.</p></details>`,
  "0438": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Emit completion only when the job actually transitions</figcaption><p>Compare a running job with a job already completed.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Running job<br>UPDATE returns row<br>Insert completion event</div><div>Already completed job<br>UPDATE returns no rows<br>No new event</div></div><details><summary>Does a failed outbox insert leave the transition committed?</summary><p>No. Both statements belong to the same transaction.</p></details><details><summary>Does a duplicate inbox claim increment project_stats?</summary><p>No. The claimed CTE returns no row, so the linked UPDATE does not increment it.</p></details>`,
  "0439": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Persist a saga result and its next actions together</figcaption><p>Inventory is reserved, but payment authorization has an uncertain outcome.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Known state<br>Inventory reserved</div><div>Unknown payment outcome<br>Reconcile before compensation</div><div>Durable transition<br>History, state and due actions<br>One transaction</div></div><details><summary>Should a timeout immediately trigger an assumed payment failure?</summary><p>No. The authorization may have succeeded remotely. Resolve that uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Can every compensation restore the original world perfectly?</summary><p>No. Irreversible or failed compensation needs explicit repair handling.</p></details>`,
  "0440": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep a prepared participant in doubt until the decision is known</figcaption><p>Participant A is prepared for tx-42 and asks for the durable coordinator decision.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>COMMIT<br>Commit prepared work</div><div>ABORT<br>Roll back prepared work</div><div>No decision available<br>Remain in doubt</div></div><details><summary>Does a coordinator timeout authorize unilateral rollback?</summary><p>No. The coordinator may already have committed a durable decision.</p></details><details><summary>Why monitor the age of prepared transactions?</summary><p>They can retain locks and resources while recovery waits for a decision.</p></details>`,
  "0441": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Acknowledge only after the required durable replication</figcaption><p>The sketch has leader A and followers B and C.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Append<br>Persist leader entry</div><div>Replicate<br>Obtain required durable matching acknowledgments</div><div>Commit and apply<br>Return term and index</div></div><details><summary>Does the sketch implement election safety and current-term commit rules?</summary><p>No. Those rules are required but omitted from this integration outline.</p></details><details><summary>Does a matching applied index prove a deposed leader may serve linearizable reads?</summary><p>No. It also needs proof of current authority.</p></details>`,
  "0442": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep concurrent name updates as a conflict</figcaption><p>Ada has vector eu 5, us 2. Grace has eu 4, us 3.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Ada<br>Ahead in eu<br>Behind in us</div><div>Grace<br>Behind in eu<br>Ahead in us</div><div>Neither dominates<br>Return both siblings as conflict</div></div><details><summary>Does adding vector components and choosing the larger sum establish causality?</summary><p>No. Compare each component. These histories are incomparable.</p></details><details><summary>What if equal vectors contain different values?</summary><p>The fixture rejects inconsistent payloads for the same version rather than silently choosing one.</p></details>`,
  "0426": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Merge concurrent vector histories before recording receipt</figcaption><p>A has vector {A:2}; B has {B:2}.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A versus B<br>Neither dominates</div><div>Component-wise maximum<br>{A:2, B:2}</div><div>A receives B<br>Increment A<br>{A:3, B:2}</div></div><details><summary>Is equality strict dominance?</summary><p>No. Dominance requires at least one strictly greater component and no smaller component.</p></details><details><summary>Does a larger Lamport number prove one event caused another?</summary><p>No. Causality implies increasing Lamport time, but the reverse implication does not hold.</p></details>`,
  "0427": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Include the delay before a request actually starts</figcaption><p>One synthetic request is scheduled at 0, starts at 90 and completes at 100.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Scheduling wait<br>90 ms</div><div>Service interval<br>10 ms</div><div>Scheduled-to-complete duration<br>100 ms</div></div><details><summary>What would timing only from start hide?</summary><p>It would hide the 90 ms wait before service.</p></details><details><summary>Can the two successful requests establish the all-request p99?</summary><p>No. The fixture also has a timeout and unsent work, and two successes cannot reliably estimate a population tail.</p></details>`,
  "0428": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reuse the remaining deadline budget</figcaption><p>Illustrative local clock: deadline 1,000, current time 700, using the same monotonic units.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Remaining budget<br>1,000 − 700<br>300</div><div>Nested call<br>Keep deadline 1,000</div><div>Already expired<br>Reject before starting operation</div></div><details><summary>Does the helper force an operation that ignores its signal to stop?</summary><p>No. Cancellation is cooperative and each adapter owns cleanup.</p></details><details><summary>Can this monotonic timestamp be sent unchanged to another process?</summary><p>No. Translate the remaining budget at the transport boundary with an explicit timing policy.</p></details>`,
  "0429": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check admission before another retry</figcaption><p>The operation fails temporarily on attempts 1 and 2, then succeeds on attempt 3.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Failure 1<br>Transient and budget available<br>Wait, retry</div><div>Failure 2<br>Same checks<br>Wait, retry</div><div>Attempt 3<br>Return ok</div></div><details><summary>What if budget.take returns false after the first failure?</summary><p>The error is rethrown after one call. The helper does not begin another attempt.</p></details><details><summary>Does this helper implement parallel hedged requests?</summary><p>No. It retries sequentially and assumes the operation is safe to repeat under one identity.</p></details>`,
  "0430": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject the second operation while the only slot is occupied</figcaption><p>Admission limit is 1. Operation A remains pending.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A enters<br>Active count 1</div><div>B arrives<br>At limit<br>503<br>B's work never starts</div><div>A settles<br>Finally restores slot</div></div><details><summary>Does this gate queue B until A finishes?</summary><p>No. It sheds excess work without a waiting queue.</p></details><details><summary>Should a caller timeout release A's slot while A still runs?</summary><p>No. Release on actual settlement, or the active-work bound becomes inaccurate.</p></details>`,
  "0431": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Admit one half-open probe</figcaption><p>The breaker starts open with probeAt 100.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Time 99<br>Reject attempt</div><div>Time 100<br>Admit probe<br>State half-open</div><div>Another attempt at 100<br>Reject while probe pending</div><div>Probe fails at 110<br>Cooldown 100<br>Next probe at 210</div></div><details><summary>Does the fixture permit normal calls during the unresolved probe?</summary><p>No. Only the admitted probe proceeds.</p></details><details><summary>What happens if the probe never settles?</summary><p>The demonstration remains half-open. Add a deadline and one completion path in a real implementation.</p></details>`,
  "0432": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare a repeated maximum with a repeated increment</figcaption><p>Start progress at 40 and counter at 0. Deliver the same update twice.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>max(progress, 60)<br>40 → 60 → 60</div><div>counter + 1<br>0 → 1 → 2</div></div><details><summary>Which operation is duplicate-safe as written?</summary><p>The maximum assignment. Repeating the increment changes the result again.</p></details><details><summary>Does the transfer sketch supply atomicCommit?</summary><p>No. Durable receipt and effect atomicity are required contracts, not implemented by the diagram or function name.</p></details>`,
  "0433": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the event identity stable across redelivery</figcaption><p>job.completed describes the completed job_42 and its result URI.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First delivery<br>Event ID E<br>Job job_42</div><div>Redelivery<br>Same event ID E<br>Same fact</div><div>Trace context<br>Diagnostics<br>Not the deduplication key</div></div><details><summary>Should redelivery generate a new event ID?</summary><p>No. A stable identity lets consumers recognize the same event.</p></details><details><summary>Does exclusive queue delivery guarantee exclusive processing forever?</summary><p>No. Lease expiry and redelivery can overlap a slow worker's processing.</p></details>`,
  "0417": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Let the gateway admit the request and the service authorize the object</figcaption><p>A valid caller submits a small request to /v1/jobs.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Gateway<br>Body and header limits<br>Subject rate policy<br>Route to job-service</div><div>Service<br>Object permission<br>Domain validation<br>Transactional acceptance</div></div><details><summary>Does passing the gateway quota grant project access?</summary><p>No. The service must authorize the requested object.</p></details><details><summary>Should downstream calls receive a new four-second budget at every hop?</summary><p>No. Propagate the remaining caller deadline.</p></details>`,
  "0418": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject an attempted ownership change in a profile update</figcaption><p>The input has name A, visibility private and an extra ownerId attacker.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Allowed fields<br>name and visibility</div><div>Supplied ownerId<br>Unknown writable field</div><div>Parser outcome<br>Reject whole input</div></div><details><summary>Does valid visibility public prove the caller may make the project public?</summary><p>No. Validation and authorization are separate checks.</p></details><details><summary>Does defining outboundPolicy enforce network restrictions?</summary><p>No. The network path must apply the policy to resolution, connection, redirects and response reading.</p></details>`,
  "0419": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate correlation IDs from metric dimensions</figcaption><p>The synthetic observation contains a request ID, trace ID and route template.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Metrics<br>Route template<br>Method, status, tenant class</div><div>Protected logs and traces<br>Request ID<br>Trace ID</div><div>Fixture duration<br>42 ms<br>Illustrative, not measured here</div></div><details><summary>Why omit requestId from metricLabels?</summary><p>Each request would create another label value and unbounded cardinality.</p></details><details><summary>Is a sampled trace a complete audit trail?</summary><p>No. Audit persistence, retention and failure behavior need a separate policy.</p></details>`,
  "0420": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use the ID to continue through timestamp ties</figcaption><p>Two rows share createdAt 10. Their IDs are 2 and 1. Page one returns ID 2.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Timestamp-only next predicate<br>createdAt less than 10<br>Returns no rows</div><div>Timestamp plus ID predicate<br>Same time and ID less than 2<br>Returns ID 1</div></div><details><summary>What does the broken predicate lose?</summary><p>It skips ID 1 despite that row belonging after the first row in the total order.</p></details><details><summary>Does this frozen-array test establish signed-cursor security?</summary><p>No. The actual API needs tenant, signature, expiry and changing-data tests.</p></details>`,
  "0421": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Test the published consumer path in a clean directory</figcaption><p>A new consumer should reach its first authenticated request through one supported setup path.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Clean consumer<br>No repository-local shortcuts</div><div>Released contract and SDK<br>Generate and package</div><div>Consumer test<br>Import artifact<br>Make authenticated request</div></div><details><summary>Are the illustrative CLI commands supplied release tools?</summary><p>No. Replace them with the team's actual tooling.</p></details><details><summary>Does a successful SDK build prove the documentation path is usable?</summary><p>No. Exercise the clean setup and record failures and time to first success.</p></details>`,
  "0422": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep safety separate from eventual progress</figcaption><p>The model has nodes A, B and C and allows one crash failure.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Safety requirement<br>Never commit two outcomes for one command</div><div>Progress condition<br>Stable leader connected to a majority</div><div>Network model<br>Messages may delay, drop, duplicate or reorder</div></div><details><summary>Does lack of progress automatically imply conflicting committed outcomes?</summary><p>No. A system can preserve safety while waiting for the conditions needed to progress.</p></details><details><summary>Does the configuration implement consensus?</summary><p>No. It states assumptions and properties. The transition function remains to be implemented and checked.</p></details>`,
  "0423": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A paused worker can resume after another worker takes over</figcaption><p>The fault plan pauses worker A for five seconds.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A pauses<br>No progress observed</div><div>Recovery path<br>May assign work elsewhere</div><div>A resumes<br>Old work may still execute</div><div>Required invariant<br>One business effect per operation identity</div></div><details><summary>Does an idle timeout prove A crashed permanently?</summary><p>No. The process can be delayed and later resume.</p></details><details><summary>Does listing the fault plan prove the invariant held?</summary><p>No. The injector and assertion implementations must run in a controlled test environment.</p></details>`,
  "0424": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Lose the response after the server performs the effect</figcaption><p>The API commits an operation, then its connection to the client resets.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Authority<br>Effect committed</div><div>Network<br>Response lost</div><div>Client<br>Outcome unknown<br>Reconcile with stable identity</div></div><details><summary>Does the reset prove the operation failed?</summary><p>No. The effect can succeed even though its response is not received.</p></details><details><summary>Does TCP make a retried business operation exactly once?</summary><p>No. Application identity and authoritative deduplication are still required.</p></details>`,
  "0425": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Measure duration when the wall clock moves backward</figcaption><p>Hypothetical local measurement: an operation takes two seconds while the wall clock steps backward by thirty seconds.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Wall-clock subtraction<br>2 − 30<br>Minus 28 seconds</div><div>Monotonic duration<br>Approximately 2 seconds</div></div><details><summary>Did the operation run for a negative duration?</summary><p>No. The wall-clock adjustment invalidated that duration calculation.</p></details><details><summary>Does a later remote timestamp prove a newer causal update?</summary><p>No. Clock skew can misorder events. Choose a version or causality policy appropriate to the data.</p></details>`,
  "0406": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Build the cursor from the last returned row</figcaption><p>With page size 2, the ordered query finds rows A, B and C using limit plus one.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Returned page<br>A, B</div><div>Probe row<br>C<br>Proves another row exists</div><div>Next cursor<br>Boundary from B</div></div><details><summary>What if the cursor used C instead?</summary><p>The next strict-less-than query would skip C even though the client never received it.</p></details><details><summary>Does stable ordering guarantee a fixed cross-page snapshot?</summary><p>No. Concurrent changes can affect later pages unless the API defines snapshot semantics.</p></details>`,
  "0407": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the SQL operator separate from user values</figcaption><p>The client requests duration_ms greater than or equal to 10.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Allowed field<br>j.duration_ms</div><div>Allowed operator<br>&gt;=</div><div>Generated predicate<br>j.duration_ms &gt;= $1</div><div>Parameter value<br>10</div></div><details><summary>May an arbitrary client field name become a SQL identifier?</summary><p>No. The field and operator come from the server-owned allowlist.</p></details><details><summary>Can two compiled predicates simply concatenate while both retain $1?</summary><p>No. Composition must renumber parameters and bound the overall query.</p></details>`,
  "0408": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Exercise both old and new client expectations</figcaption><p>The scripted server returns id and a default priority normal.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>V1 request<br>Name only<br>Requires string id</div><div>V2 request<br>Name and priority<br>Requires priority present</div><div>Candidate missing priority<br>V1 check passes<br>V2 check fails</div></div><details><summary>Does this pair of checks fully validate V2?</summary><p>No. Presence is weaker than a correct type, enum and behavior contract.</p></details><details><summary>Are additive fields always safe for old clients?</summary><p>No. Strict consumers can reject unknown fields. Test the actual clients.</p></details>`,
  "0409": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Apply different freshness lifetimes to private and shared caches</figcaption><p>The response has max-age 60, s-maxage 300 and Age 18. Ignore other age adjustments for this arithmetic example.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Private-cache freshness<br>60 − 18<br>42 seconds remain</div><div>Shared-cache freshness<br>300 − 18<br>282 seconds remain</div><div>Validator<br>catalog-v42 supports revalidation</div></div><details><summary>Does a 304 carry a new full catalog body?</summary><p>No. It lets the client reuse the validated stored representation.</p></details><details><summary>Can these public-cache headers be copied onto personalized responses safely?</summary><p>Only with a proven cache-key and authorization design. Otherwise private information can be shared.</p></details>`,
  "0410": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Offer actions that match the job's current state</figcaption><p>job_42 is awaiting approval. Its representation links to approval and cancellation operations.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Current resource<br>job_42<br>awaiting_approval</div><div>Advertised action<br>PUT approval</div><div>Alternative action<br>PUT cancellation</div></div><details><summary>Does following an old approve link bypass a later state change?</summary><p>No. The authority must recheck the current state and permission.</p></details><details><summary>Does stateless HTTP forbid storing job state?</summary><p>No. Resources can have server-side state. Requests still need the context required for their processing.</p></details>`,
  "0411": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check the request against the published schema</figcaption><p>CreateJob requires a nonempty project_id and forbids other properties.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>project_id: prj_7<br>Matches shown shape</div><div>Missing project_id<br>Required field absent</div><div>Extra admin field<br>Additional property forbidden</div></div><details><summary>Does schema conformance establish access to prj_7?</summary><p>No. Authorization remains an independent requirement.</p></details><details><summary>Does generating a client prove the running server follows the document?</summary><p>No. Run conformance tests against the server as well as schema checks.</p></details>`,
  "0412": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Leave the removed field's tag unavailable for reuse</figcaption><p>Job reserves tag 3 and the old name legacy_result. result_uri uses tag 4.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Tag 3<br>Reserved<br>No new meaning</div><div>Old field name<br>legacy_result reserved</div><div>Tag 4<br>Optional result_uri</div></div><details><summary>Why not reuse tag 3 for a different value?</summary><p>Old encoded data or consumers could interpret it using the earlier meaning.</p></details><details><summary>Should each downstream call receive a fresh full deadline?</summary><p>No. Propagate the remaining caller budget rather than extending the operation at every hop.</p></details>`,
  "0413": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Bound resolver fan-out before it reaches storage</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: a request expands jobs for several projects.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Naive per-parent access<br>One jobs lookup for each project</div><div>Request-scoped batching<br>Collect compatible lookups</div><div>Execution policy<br>Cap pages and complexity<br>Authorize objects and fields</div></div><details><summary>Does declaring maxPageSize 100 enforce the limit?</summary><p>No. Wire the policy into validation before expensive resolver work.</p></details><details><summary>Should a loader cache be shared across users without authorization scoping?</summary><p>No. Request and access boundaries must prevent one user's cached data from leaking to another.</p></details>`,
  "0414": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check both signed bytes and timestamp tolerance</figcaption><p>The fixture's signature covers the timestamp, a dot and the original JSON bytes.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original bytes at signing time<br>Valid signature</div><div>Same signature, changed body<br>Verification fails</div><div>Same body, clock 301 seconds later<br>Outside 300-second tolerance<br>Reject stale</div></div><details><summary>Can the same valid event replay inside the tolerance window?</summary><p>Yes. Use a durable scoped inbox with accepted work or effect.</p></details><details><summary>Should JSON be reformatted before verification?</summary><p>No. Verify the original bounded bytes according to the agreed signing scheme.</p></details>`,
  "0415": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reconnect with the last observed event identity</figcaption><p>The client received event 8472 reporting 65% progress, then reconnects.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Received frame<br>id 8472<br>job_42 at 65%</div><div>Reconnect request<br>Last-Event-ID 8472</div><div>Server responsibility<br>Resume from retained sequence<br>Or define unavailable-history behavior</div></div><details><summary>Does the header alone make the stream durable?</summary><p>No. Retained events and a resume policy must exist behind it.</p></details><details><summary>Should a slow consumer have an unbounded outgoing queue?</summary><p>No. Bound it and define overflow or disconnect behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0416": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Require a reviewer role for restricted projects</figcaption><p>The principal and project share tenant-a, and the principal has project:read.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Ordinary project<br>Read scope sufficient for shown policy</div><div>Restricted project, no reviewer role<br>Deny RESOURCE_POLICY</div><div>Restricted project with reviewer<br>Allow with audit and field obligations</div></div><details><summary>Does allow true automatically filter the response?</summary><p>No. The caller must project the allowed fields and enforce the audit obligation.</p></details><details><summary>Can request JSON supply the authoritative principal?</summary><p>No. It must come from verified authentication.</p></details>`,
  "0397": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Resolve a lost acceptance response at the authoritative service</figcaption><p>The job service accepts SubmitJob, but the client loses the response.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Client<br>Unknown outcome<br>Retains operation identity</div><div>Job-service authority<br>Transaction owns acceptance</div><div>Reconciliation<br>Return accepted resource or stable failure</div></div><details><summary>Should a cache decide whether a second job is safe?</summary><p>No. Resolve the operation against its authoritative record.</p></details><details><summary>Does the boundary object implement these guarantees?</summary><p>No. It documents responsibilities. The service and adapters must implement and verify them.</p></details>`,
  "0398": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Send a validator with the representation request</figcaption><p>The client requests project 42 and supplies its saved project-42-v7 validator.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request<br>GET project 42<br>If-None-Match v7</div><div>Server comparison<br>Current representation validator</div><div>Matching representation<br>Conditional response can avoid resending body</div></div><details><summary>Does negotiating HTTP/2 change GET into a different application operation?</summary><p>No. HTTP semantics and transport version are separate.</p></details><details><summary>Does Traceparent authorize project access?</summary><p>No. It carries tracing context, not proof of permission.</p></details>`,
  "0399": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Repeat a conditional update after its first success</figcaption><p>The first PUT expects v7 and changes the resource to a new version.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First PUT<br>If-Match v7<br>Condition passes<br>Update</div><div>Repeat same request<br>Still expects v7<br>Current version changed<br>Precondition can fail</div></div><details><summary>Does idempotent mean every repetition returns the same status?</summary><p>No. It concerns the intended effect of repeating the method, not identical responses.</p></details><details><summary>Does declaring PUT automatically implement atomic version checks?</summary><p>No. The server must enforce the precondition at the authoritative write.</p></details>`,
  "0400": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep project identity stable while exposing related jobs</figcaption><p>The representation identifies project prj_42 and links to its jobs collection.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Project identity<br>/projects/prj_42</div><div>Related collection<br>/projects/prj_42/jobs</div><div>Submission action<br>POST to jobs collection</div></div><details><summary>Does switching the representation format require a new project ID?</summary><p>No. Resource identity and representation format are different choices.</p></details><details><summary>Does publishing a submitJob link grant permission to submit?</summary><p>No. The target operation must authorize the caller.</p></details>`,
  "0401": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep compressed and uncompressed responses distinct</figcaption><p>The example advertises Brotli encoding and varies by accepted encoding.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Client accepts Brotli<br>Send actual Brotli bytes<br>Content-Encoding br</div><div>Uncompressed variant<br>Send uncompressed bytes<br>No false br label</div><div>Cache selection<br>Respect relevant Vary fields</div></div><details><summary>Does setting Content-Encoding compress a JavaScript object?</summary><p>No. The response pipeline must actually encode the bytes.</p></details><details><summary>Why include language when localized content changes?</summary><p>A cache must distinguish representations selected by that request field.</p></details>`,
  "0402": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use a stable conflict code instead of parsing prose</figcaption><p>A reused key represents a different request.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>HTTP status<br>409</div><div>Stable code<br>IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED</div><div>Human detail<br>Use a new key for a different operation</div></div><details><summary>Should the client branch on the English title?</summary><p>No. Use the documented status, problem type and stable code.</p></details><details><summary>Should an uncertain retry of the original operation invent a new key?</summary><p>No. Preserve its identity while resolving the original outcome. A genuinely different operation needs a different key.</p></details>`,
  "0403": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject a lookalike object host before producing a command</figcaption><p>The allowed origin is https://objects.example.test.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Allowed origin and valid fields<br>Normalize URL<br>Return frozen command</div><div>objects.example.test.evil.test<br>Different origin<br>Reject</div><div>Priority -1<br>Outside 0..9<br>Reject</div></div><details><summary>Does a valid URL prove the caller owns that object?</summary><p>No. Schema validation does not establish object authorization.</p></details><details><summary>Does the origin check alone cover redirects and connection-time addresses?</summary><p>No. The network adapter needs its own SSRF policy and response bounds.</p></details>`,
  "0404": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare intent before replaying a saved response</figcaption><p>Key k1 already records create-job intent A.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Retry k1 with A<br>Return saved status and body</div><div>Request k1 with B<br>Different intent<br>Conflict</div><div>Receipt incomplete<br>Bounded reconciliation<br>No new-write fallthrough</div></div><details><summary>Is hashing arbitrary JSON bytes enough to define equivalent intent?</summary><p>No. Define defaults, field selection and canonicalization before fingerprinting.</p></details><details><summary>What changes after the promised receipt-retention window ends?</summary><p>Deduplication may no longer be available. Document that boundary rather than promising indefinite replay safety.</p></details>`,
  "0405": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Match strong validators rather than splitting blindly on commas</figcaption><p>The current validator is the quoted tag a,b, containing a comma.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Header &quot;a,b&quot;<br>One tag<br>Strong match</div><div>Header W/&quot;a,b&quot;<br>Weak tag<br>No strong match</div><div>Wildcard<br>Tests existence<br>Not a specific version</div></div><details><summary>Does splitting every comma preserve the first header's meaning?</summary><p>No. The comma inside the quoted tag is data.</p></details><details><summary>Does passing this parser prevent a race before the write?</summary><p>No. Enforce the expected version atomically at the authority; a later mismatch must reject the update.</p></details>`,
  "0389": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Verify acknowledged data after a controlled restart</figcaption><p>In an isolated fixture, record an acknowledged write before terminating the process at a controlled point.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before failure<br>Record write and persistence settings</div><div>Restart<br>Load available persistence files</div><div>Verification<br>Check recovered value<br>Measure loss window and load time</div></div><details><summary>Does acknowledgment alone establish the configured crash-loss bound?</summary><p>No. Persistence policy and failure timing matter. Verify the outcome.</p></details><details><summary>Should an AOF repair tool modify the live files?</summary><p>No. Preserve a complete backup and inspect offline copies. Repair can discard data.</p></details>`,
  "0390": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A replication wait timeout does not undo the write</figcaption><p>On one primary connection, SET succeeds and WAIT asks for one replica within 1,000 ms.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>SET<br>Primary accepts value</div><div>WAIT returns 0<br>No required replica acknowledgment within wait</div><div>Write state<br>Not rolled back by WAIT</div></div><details><summary>Does WAIT cover every other client's writes?</summary><p>No. It concerns preceding writes on this connection.</p></details><details><summary>Does one replica acknowledgment turn the service into a linearizable consensus database?</summary><p>No. Validate failover and durability requirements separately.</p></details>`,
  "0391": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A failed lease SET does not guard an earlier cart write</figcaption><p>The lock key already belongs to another owner. Both keys share the user42 hash tag.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>HSET cart sku-1 2<br>Succeeds</div><div>SET lock NX<br>Existing key<br>Returns null</div><div>Cart afterward<br>sku-1 is still 2</div></div><details><summary>Does sharing a slot make the cart write conditional on acquiring the lock?</summary><p>No. It permits colocated operations but does not add that condition.</p></details><details><summary>What must replace this pattern if lease ownership guards the write?</summary><p>Use a validated conditional operation that checks the invariant before changing protected state.</p></details>`,
  "0392": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Recover pending work without assuming the old worker died</figcaption><p>Worker A reads an entry and pauses before acknowledgment. Worker B later claims it after the idle threshold.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A<br>Entry pending<br>May still resume</div><div>B<br>Claims idle entry<br>May process it too</div><div>Business effect<br>Needs atomic deduplication</div></div><details><summary>Does a claim prove A can no longer act?</summary><p>No. Idle time is not proof of death.</p></details><details><summary>Can trimming remove a body whose pending reference remains?</summary><p>Yes. Choose retention from replay needs and test stalled consumers before trimming.</p></details>`,
  "0393": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Distinguish a negative cache hit from a cache miss</figcaption><p>Compare an absent Redis key with a key containing serialized JSON null.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Redis returns null<br>No cache entry<br>Query database</div><div>Redis returns text null<br>Parse to null<br>Return negative cached result</div><div>Database failure<br>Propagate error<br>Do not cache as absence</div></div><details><summary>Can a negative hit hide a newly created version?</summary><p>Yes, during the negative TTL. Define read-after-write requirements separately.</p></details><details><summary>May the database return the latest version under an older version's key?</summary><p>No. The adapter checks exact identity and version before caching.</p></details>`,
  "0394": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject a resumed worker's stale fence</figcaption><p>Worker B writes with fence 11. Paused worker A resumes with fence 10.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Stored fence<br>11</div><div>A proposes 10<br>11 is not less than 10<br>Zero updated rows</div><div>Protected value<br>Keep B's newer write</div></div><details><summary>Is the random lease owner token the same as a fencing token?</summary><p>No. Ownership equality and monotonic ordering are different roles.</p></details><details><summary>Can the shown predicate accept a second write with fence 11?</summary><p>No. It requires a strictly greater fence. This example permits one write per fence.</p></details>`,
  "0395": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check both key scope and command permission</figcaption><p>The probe user can access only the specified tenant42 cache keys with limited commands.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>GET tenant42 project key<br>Allowed</div><div>GET tenant99 project key<br>Wrong key scope<br>Denied</div><div>CONFIG GET<br>Administrative command<br>Denied</div></div><details><summary>Does ACL DRYRUN execute the read?</summary><p>No. It checks permission without executing the command.</p></details><details><summary>Does DRYRUN verify credentials and TLS?</summary><p>No. Test authenticated connections separately in the isolated fixture.</p></details>`,
  "0396": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Retry after commit when the acceptance response is lost</figcaption><p>A job, outbox event and receipt commit together. The process fails before the client receives the response.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Database state<br>One committed job<br>One event<br>Saved job ID</div><div>Client retry<br>Same tenant, key and intent</div><div>Replay branch<br>Return original job ID<br>No second job</div></div><details><summary>What if the crash happened before commit instead?</summary><p>The transaction contract requires no partial records to survive. A later retry can perform a new claim.</p></details><details><summary>Does a mocked replay test establish database concurrency safety?</summary><p>No. Race requests and test failure recovery on the real integration.</p></details>`,
  "0381": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare a list-length request with a full-list reply</figcaption><p>The isolated fixture has 10,000 small entries. Compare LLEN with LRANGE over the entire list.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>LLEN<br>Return one count</div><div>Full LRANGE<br>Process and return many values</div><div>Concurrent latency probe<br>Observe effect on other requests</div></div><details><summary>Does SLOWLOG contain the whole client-perceived wait?</summary><p>No. It measures command execution, not all network and queueing delay.</p></details><details><summary>Does an empty LATENCY DOCTOR report prove zero latency problems?</summary><p>No. Monitoring may be disabled. Check collection settings and client measurements.</p></details>`,
  "0382": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count bytes inside a bulk string</figcaption><p>The text é becomes two bytes in UTF-8.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Text<br>One character</div><div>Encoded value<br>Two bytes</div><div>RESP bulk prefix<br>$2</div><div>Decoder boundary<br>Consume declared byte count</div></div><details><summary>Would a newline inside a binary value end the bulk value early?</summary><p>No. The declared byte length identifies its boundary.</p></details><details><summary>Does one TCP read necessarily contain one complete command?</summary><p>No. Reads can split or combine frames. A parser must retain incomplete bytes.</p></details>`,
  "0383": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Repeat the command batch and compare its counters</figcaption><p>All fixture keys begin absent. Run the batch twice before the session key expires.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First run<br>logins: 1<br>page views: 1<br>Session set</div><div>Second run<br>HSET resets logins, then increment: 1<br>Page views: 2<br>NX leaves session unchanged</div></div><details><summary>Does logins accumulate across these batches?</summary><p>No. HSET resets that field to zero before every increment.</p></details><details><summary>Can a successful EXEC reply be assumed for every command?</summary><p>No. Inspect each result, including the conditional SET outcome.</p></details>`,
  "0384": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow list insertion and movement direction</figcaption><p>LPUSH receives job-1 then job-2 into an empty ready list.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Ready head to tail<br>job-2, job-1</div><div>Move RIGHT to processing LEFT<br>Remove job-1</div><div>After move<br>Ready: job-2<br>Processing: job-1</div></div><details><summary>Does moving job-1 prove it was processed?</summary><p>No. It only changes its list location. Recovery and acknowledgment remain to be implemented.</p></details><details><summary>Which tied leaderboard member comes first in the shown reverse ordering?</summary><p>user:3 precedes user:2 at score 2190 because the reverse member ordering breaks the tie.</p></details>`,
  "0385": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count distinct attendance bits across two days</figcaption><p>Day one sets bit 42. Day two sets bit 99.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Day one<br>Set bits: {42}<br>Count 1</div><div>Day two<br>Set bits: {99}<br>Count 1</div><div>Bitwise union<br>{42, 99}<br>Count 2</div></div><details><summary>Does setting bit 42 again increase its attendance count?</summary><p>No. The bit is already one.</p></details><details><summary>Can HyperLogLog return the two member IDs?</summary><p>No. It estimates unique cardinality; it is not a membership list.</p></details>`,
  "0386": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Preserve a remaining TTL without restarting it</figcaption><p>Suppose a key has 20 seconds remaining when SET KEEPTTL replaces its value.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Key still exists<br>Keep remaining lifetime<br>Do not restart 60 seconds</div><div>Key already expired<br>Recreate without expiry<br>TTL reports -1</div><div>Ordinary SET<br>Remove prior expiry</div></div><details><summary>Does TTL -2 mean the key exists forever?</summary><p>No. It means the key is absent at that observation. -1 means an existing key has no expiry.</p></details><details><summary>Does CONFIG SET affect only this client's fixture key?</summary><p>No. Memory policy changes affect the server. Use a disposable instance and restore recorded settings.</p></details>`,
  "0387": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A Redis transaction can keep an earlier successful write</figcaption><p>balance:42 is 1000. balance:99 contains non-integer text.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>DECRBY first balance 500<br>Success<br>Balance becomes 500</div><div>INCRBY second balance 500<br>Type error</div><div>Final state<br>500 and original text<br>No automatic rollback</div></div><details><summary>Is this a safe transfer implementation?</summary><p>No. The failed credit leaves the successful debit in place.</p></details><details><summary>How is a WATCH conflict different?</summary><p>A watched-key change aborts EXEC before queued operations run. A runtime command error occurs during execution.</p></details>`,
  "0388": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check replies after sending a large batch</figcaption><p>The fixture sends 10,000 SET commands through a pipeline.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Submitted work<br>10,000 commands</div><div>Required evidence<br>10,000 replies<br>Zero command errors</div><div>Performance evidence<br>Latency distribution<br>Throughput and buffer growth</div></div><details><summary>Does writing all bytes prove every SET succeeded?</summary><p>No. Parse and inspect the replies.</p></details><details><summary>Does deeper pipelining guarantee lower per-request latency?</summary><p>No. It can improve throughput while increasing waiting behind queued work.</p></details>`,
  "0375": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A backup file is the start of a restore test</figcaption><p>Restore the logical dump into the separate restore_probe database.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Backup artifact<br>app.dump</div><div>Isolated restore<br>Recreate objects and data</div><div>Verification<br>Constraints and row checks<br>Application smoke checks</div><div>Recovery evidence<br>Measured recovery time<br>Recovered data point</div></div><details><summary>Does a successful dump prove the recovery-time objective?</summary><p>No. Measure the full restore and validation procedure.</p></details><details><summary>Can a physical base backup alone reach every later recovery point?</summary><p>No. The required WAL must also be available through the chosen target.</p></details>`,
  "0376": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Apply the tenant policy with a constrained runtime role</figcaption><p>The trusted application sets transaction-local tenant 42 while connected as app_runtime.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Row tenant 42<br>Matches policy<br>Eligible to read</div><div>Row tenant 7<br>Does not match<br>Hidden by policy</div><div>Proposed write tenant 7<br>Fails WITH CHECK</div></div><details><summary>Should this fixture be tested as the table owner?</summary><p>No. Use the intended constrained role, without owner, superuser or BYPASSRLS privileges.</p></details><details><summary>Does the custom setting protect against a client allowed arbitrary SQL?</summary><p>No. Such a client can change it. Trusted identity assignment and SQL-injection prevention remain essential.</p></details>`,
  "0377": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Total query cost depends on frequency as well as mean time</figcaption><p>Hypothetical statistics: query A averages 2 ms over 10,000 calls; query B averages 100 ms over 10 calls.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Query A<br>Total: 20,000 ms</div><div>Query B<br>Total: 1,000 ms</div><div>Sort by total time<br>A ranks above B</div></div><details><summary>Does the largest mean time always dominate total load?</summary><p>No. Call frequency changes the total contribution.</p></details><details><summary>Do these synthetic numbers constitute measured database results?</summary><p>No. They illustrate the calculation. Collect the actual statistics and workload context before tuning.</p></details>`,
  "0378": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Investigate temporary writes before increasing memory</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: a frequent query shows temporary blocks written while latency rises under concurrency.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Query evidence<br>Temporary writes<br>Actual sort or hash behavior</div><div>Workload evidence<br>Concurrent operations<br>Host memory and I/O</div><div>Candidate change<br>Measure under same workload</div></div><details><summary>Is work_mem a single global memory allocation?</summary><p>No. Multiple operations and sessions can consume work memory. Account for concurrency before increasing it.</p></details><details><summary>Does removing a spill automatically establish safe capacity?</summary><p>No. Check total memory pressure, latency and errors under representative load.</p></details>`,
  "0379": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A displayed keyword score does not change vector ordering</figcaption><p>Candidate A has cosine distance 0.1 and text score 0. Candidate B has distance 0.2 and text score 0.9.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A<br>Vector score 0.9<br>Distance 0.1<br>Ranked first</div><div>B<br>Vector score 0.8<br>Distance 0.2<br>Ranked second</div><div>ORDER BY<br>Vector distance only</div></div><details><summary>Does B win because its text score is higher?</summary><p>No. This query does not combine text and vector scores into its ordering.</p></details><details><summary>What would establish hybrid retrieval?</summary><p>Define and implement candidate fusion or reranking, then compare relevance and latency against a baseline.</p></details>`,
  "0380": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject a stale rename before publishing its event</figcaption><p>The stored project version is 8. A new request asks to rename expected version 7.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Claim request identity<br>Inside transaction</div><div>Conditional UPDATE<br>Expected 7, stored 8<br>Zero rows</div><div>Throw conflict<br>Roll back claim<br>No outbox event or successful receipt</div></div><details><summary>Does replaying a completed identical request perform the rename again?</summary><p>No. The existing matching receipt returns its saved result.</p></details><details><summary>Can the relay publish the committed outbox event twice?</summary><p>Yes. A crash can cause delivery replay. Consumers need atomic inbox-and-effect handling.</p></details>`,
  "0366": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read rows and loops on the same basis</figcaption><p>Hypothetical plan node: estimated rows 5, actual rows 4, loops 100.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Estimate comparison<br>5 versus 4 per loop</div><div>Total repeated output<br>4 × 100<br>400 rows</div><div>Next inspection<br>Buffers, joins and timing</div></div><details><summary>Should estimated 5 be compared directly with total 400?</summary><p>No. First compare estimates and actuals per loop. Then account for repeated work.</p></details><details><summary>Does EXPLAIN ANALYZE merely describe the query?</summary><p>No. It executes it. Use a suitable environment and account for effects that rollback cannot undo.</p></details>`,
  "0367": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A cheap inner lookup can become expensive when repeated</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: a nested loop visits 1,000 outer rows and performs one inner lookup for each.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Outer input<br>1,000 rows</div><div>Inner lookup<br>1 call per outer row</div><div>Total inner executions<br>1,000</div></div><details><summary>Does a fast-looking single lookup prove the whole join is cheap?</summary><p>No. Include its loop count, rows and buffer work.</p></details><details><summary>Should every nested loop be replaced with a hash join?</summary><p>No. Input size, selectivity and available access paths determine the tradeoff. Compare representative plans.</p></details>`,
  "0368": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A retained row version can outlive its update</figcaption><p>An old transaction holds a snapshot while another transaction updates an event.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Old version<br>Still visible to old snapshot</div><div>New version<br>Visible to newer reads</div><div>Cleanup constraint<br>Old snapshot can delay reclamation</div></div><details><summary>Does a recent vacuum guarantee every obsolete-looking version was removed?</summary><p>No. Some versions remain necessary for visibility.</p></details><details><summary>Should a growing table immediately trigger more aggressive vacuum settings?</summary><p>First inspect retaining transactions and slots, update patterns and actual space use. A threshold change cannot remove versions still needed.</p></details>`,
  "0369": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Distinguish cleanup from planner statistics</figcaption><p>The maintenance command combines VACUUM and ANALYZE on events.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>VACUUM work<br>Reclaim eligible dead-tuple space<br>Maintain visibility and freezing</div><div>ANALYZE work<br>Refresh planner statistics</div><div>Diagnostic evidence<br>Dead-tuple estimates<br>Maintenance times<br>Transaction-ID age</div></div><details><summary>Does ANALYZE alone perform dead-tuple cleanup?</summary><p>No. It gathers planning statistics.</p></details><details><summary>Is transaction-ID age just another table-size metric?</summary><p>No. It helps assess freezing and wraparound-related maintenance needs.</p></details>`,
  "0370": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">WAL position advances while work is recorded</figcaption><p>The fixture captures a WAL position, inserts an audit event and commits, then captures another position.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before<br>Record starting LSN</div><div>Transaction<br>Insert probe<br>Commit</div><div>After<br>Measure LSN difference</div></div><details><summary>Does the position difference isolate exactly this row's WAL on a busy database?</summary><p>No. Other concurrent work can also advance WAL.</p></details><details><summary>Does successful CHECKPOINT alone test crash recovery?</summary><p>No. The isolated recovery exercise must restart after an unclean stop and verify committed and uncommitted outcomes.</p></details>`,
  "0371": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Many client connections share fewer server connections</figcaption><p>The sketch permits 500 clients and configures a normal server-pool size of 20.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Client side<br>Many connected clients</div><div>Waiting clients<br>Queue for a server connection</div><div>Transaction pool<br>Reuse connections between transactions</div></div><details><summary>Does max_client_conn 500 mean 500 concurrent database queries?</summary><p>No. Client connections and active server connections are different limits.</p></details><details><summary>Can session state be assumed to stay on one server connection across transactions?</summary><p>No. Transaction pooling can assign a different server connection. Review session-dependent features before adopting it.</p></details>`,
  "0372": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Route timestamps through half-open partition bounds</figcaption><p>The September partition starts at September 1 and ends before October 1, using consistent timestamp-zone interpretation.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>September 10<br>Inside September partition</div><div>September 30<br>Inside September partition</div><div>October 1 boundary<br>Outside September partition</div></div><details><summary>What if no partition covers the October row?</summary><p>The insert cannot route to the shown partition. Create the required partition or define an appropriate default policy.</p></details><details><summary>Does partitioning eliminate the need for an index inside a partition?</summary><p>No. Pruning selects partitions; an index can still help locate rows within them.</p></details>`,
  "0373": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An old replay timestamp can coexist with no new WAL to replay</figcaption><p>The primary has been idle. The standby has replayed the latest available position.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Primary workload<br>No new transactions</div><div>Replay position<br>Caught up</div><div>Time since last replayed transaction<br>Keeps increasing</div></div><details><summary>Does the increasing interval alone prove replication is falling behind?</summary><p>No. Compare positions and workload activity.</p></details><details><summary>Why inspect an inactive replication slot?</summary><p>It can retain WAL while its consumer is absent, increasing storage pressure.</p></details>`,
  "0374": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Prepare the subscriber schema separately from row replication</figcaption><p>The publication includes projects and events. A subscriber receives their data changes.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Publisher<br>Published table changes</div><div>Subscription<br>Transfers changes</div><div>Subscriber prerequisites<br>Compatible tables<br>Independent DDL coordination</div></div><details><summary>Does adding a publisher column automatically migrate the subscriber schema?</summary><p>No. The lesson requires compatible subscriber tables and explicit DDL coordination.</p></details><details><summary>Why avoid creating an extra unused slot before creating the subscription?</summary><p>The subscription normally creates its own slot. An unused additional slot can retain WAL unnecessarily.</p></details>`,
  "0355": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Mark a repeated category before expanding it again</figcaption><p>Suppose category links form a cycle A → B → A.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Seed A<br>Path [A]<br>cycle false</div><div>Visit B<br>Path [A, B]<br>cycle false</div><div>Return to A<br>Path [A, B, A]<br>cycle true</div><div>Next recursion<br>Do not expand the marked row</div></div><details><summary>Is the cycle-marked row absent from the output?</summary><p>No. The query returns it with cycle true. The condition stops its further expansion.</p></details><details><summary>Does a CTE always force materialization?</summary><p>No. The second example explicitly requests NOT MATERIALIZED. Inspect the plan for the actual execution strategy.</p></details>`,
  "0356": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep chronological totals separate from slowest-job ranking</figcaption><p>One tenant has chronological durations 10, 30 and 20 ms, with distinct ordering keys.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First job: 10<br>Running total 10<br>Slowest rank 3</div><div>Second job: 30<br>Running total 40<br>Slowest rank 1</div><div>Third job: 20<br>Running total 60<br>Slowest rank 2</div></div><details><summary>Do both windows use the same ordering?</summary><p>No. One orders by duration; the other by creation time and ID.</p></details><details><summary>Does the window calculation collapse these into one tenant row?</summary><p>No. Each job row remains. Grouped percentile calculation happens separately before the join.</p></details>`,
  "0357": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Move 500 cents within one transaction</figcaption><p>Assume the required account and balance checks have passed. Account 10 starts at 1,000 cents and account 20 at 200.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Debit account 10<br>1,000 → 500</div><div>Credit account 20<br>200 → 700</div><div>Commit both<br>Total remains 1,200</div></div><details><summary>Would rolling back only to the shown savepoint undo the debit?</summary><p>No. The savepoint is after the debit. A transfer failure needs a policy that preserves the whole transfer invariant.</p></details><details><summary>Does a duplicate request-ID error return the earlier result automatically?</summary><p>No. A unique constraint rejects the conflict. Replay needs stored intent and result handling.</p></details>`,
  "0358": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the old balance visible to an older snapshot</figcaption><p>Session A establishes a Repeatable Read snapshot at balance 100. Session B updates the balance to 101 and commits.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>A's existing snapshot<br>Reads 100</div><div>B's committed update<br>New balance 101</div><div>A reads again before commit<br>Still 100</div><div>A commits, then reads<br>Sees 101</div></div><details><summary>Can vacuum remove the old version while A still needs it?</summary><p>No. Cleanup must respect active snapshots that can still see that version.</p></details><details><summary>Should tuple identifiers be treated as permanent business IDs?</summary><p>No. Physical tuple versions can change. Use the declared logical key for business identity.</p></details>`,
  "0359": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Prevent both doctors from leaving the same shift</figcaption><p>Two on-call doctors each read a count of two and try to disable their own row.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Transaction A<br>Sees two doctors<br>Tries to remove A</div><div>Transaction B<br>Sees two doctors<br>Tries to remove B</div><div>Serializable conflict<br>Unsafe combined result rejected<br>Retry with fresh reads</div></div><details><summary>Should a retry repeat only the UPDATE without rereading?</summary><p>No. Rerun the complete transaction and recheck the on-call invariant.</p></details><details><summary>Does Serializable require running every transaction one at a time?</summary><p>No. PostgreSQL can allow concurrent work and reject an unsafe outcome through dependency tracking.</p></details>`,
  "0360": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Skip a job already locked by another worker</figcaption><p>Ready jobs are ordered A, B and C. Worker 1 holds A's row lock.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Worker 2 scans A<br>Locked<br>Skip</div><div>Worker 2 scans B and C<br>Available<br>Can lock and select</div></div><details><summary>Does this preserve strict oldest-job-first processing?</summary><p>No. Skipping A permits later jobs to proceed while it remains locked.</p></details><details><summary>Does the SELECT mark selected jobs as completed?</summary><p>No. The fixture only selects and locks them. A durable worker needs explicit claim, effect and acknowledgment state.</p></details>`,
  "0361": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Retry only the recognized transaction failures</figcaption><p>Compare two first-attempt errors with retry budget remaining.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>SQLSTATE 40001<br>Serialization failure<br>Pause within jitter bound<br>Run fresh transaction</div><div>Other SQLSTATE<br>Rethrow immediately</div><div>Final allowed attempt fails<br>Rethrow without another attempt</div></div><details><summary>Does this helper itself roll back the failed transaction?</summary><p>No. run_transaction must roll back before raising and start fresh on each call.</p></details><details><summary>Can retrying an external payment blindly be made safe by this loop?</summary><p>No. Keep a durable operation identity and a separate policy for uncertain external effects.</p></details>`,
  "0362": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read ready jobs in the index's tenant and time order</figcaption><p>The query asks for tenant 42's ready jobs ordered by creation time and ID.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Partial index contents<br>Ready jobs only</div><div>Leading key<br>tenant_id 42</div><div>Following keys<br>created_at, id<br>Match requested ordering</div><div>Included payload<br>Available in index entries</div></div><details><summary>Does INCLUDE guarantee zero heap visits?</summary><p>No. Index-only scans also depend on visibility information. Inspect heap fetches and the plan.</p></details><details><summary>Does the index guarantee the planner selects it?</summary><p>No. The planner compares estimated alternatives for the actual query and data.</p></details>`,
  "0363": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Match each search shape to its index family</figcaption><p>The example creates indexes for three different data patterns.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Document search values<br>GIN</div><div>Booking time ranges<br>GiST</div><div>Time-correlated event pages<br>BRIN summaries</div></div><details><summary>Should the smallest index always be selected for every query?</summary><p>No. The operator and data distribution determine which access method is useful.</p></details><details><summary>Does a BRIN match prove every row in that page range qualifies?</summary><p>No. Its summaries identify candidate ranges. Rows still need rechecking.</p></details>`,
  "0364": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare ready and completed jobs against a partial index</figcaption><p>The index predicate is state ready.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Query: tenant 42 and ready<br>Predicate matches index subset</div><div>Query: tenant 42 and done<br>Rows absent from this index subset</div><div>Write changes ready to done<br>Index membership must change</div></div><details><summary>Can this index alone supply all completed jobs?</summary><p>No. It omits those rows by design.</p></details><details><summary>Is including payload free?</summary><p>No. Wider entries consume storage and increase maintenance work. Compare that cost with the query benefit.</p></details>`,
  "0365": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Teach the planner that tenant and state may be related</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: one tenant owns mostly ready jobs while another owns mostly done jobs.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Single-column view<br>Overall tenant frequencies<br>Overall state frequencies</div><div>Joint information<br>Tenant/state combinations<br>Dependencies and common values</div><div>ANALYZE<br>Refresh collected statistics</div></div><details><summary>Why can independent estimates misjudge the combined filter?</summary><p>Knowing the tenant changes how likely a state is. Multiplying unrelated overall frequencies can miss that relationship.</p></details><details><summary>Does creating statistics rewrite the table's business data?</summary><p>No. It provides planning information. Verify estimate quality against observed rows in representative plans.</p></details>`,
  "0346": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate object ownership from the login role</figcaption><p>app_owner owns the database and schema. app_runtime is the login role.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>app_owner<br>NOLOGIN<br>Owns app schema</div><div>app_runtime<br>LOGIN<br>Granted schema USAGE</div><div>Name lookup<br>app then pg_catalog</div></div><details><summary>Does schema USAGE grant permission to read every table?</summary><p>No. Table privileges need separate grants.</p></details><details><summary>Does setting search_path create app.projects?</summary><p>No. The lookup function can return null when the relation does not exist. Configuration and object creation are separate.</p></details>`,
  "0347": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Distinguish a waiting backend from an active CPU query</figcaption><p>Practice fixture: inspect two rows from pg_stat_activity while one session waits for another transaction.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Waiting session<br>Inspect wait_event_type and wait_event</div><div>Other session<br>Inspect state and transaction activity</div><div>Evidence boundary<br>Activity snapshot<br>Not a complete execution history</div></div><details><summary>Does an active state alone prove the backend is consuming CPU?</summary><p>No. Inspect wait information and supporting measurements.</p></details><details><summary>Should captured query text be shared without review?</summary><p>No. It can contain sensitive values. Redact diagnostic evidence.</p></details>`,
  "0348": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare heap size with the whole relation footprint</figcaption><p>storage_probe has a primary-key index and 10,000 inserted rows.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Heap measurement<br>Main relation storage</div><div>Index measurement<br>Primary key and other indexes</div><div>Total relation measurement<br>Includes associated storage</div></div><details><summary>Does heap size alone report the complete disk cost?</summary><p>No. Include indexes and associated storage when planning capacity.</p></details><details><summary>Are relpages and reltuples a fresh exact count after every write?</summary><p>No. They are catalog estimates. Compare their meaning with actual size functions and maintenance history.</p></details>`,
  "0349": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare unknown with null-safe equality</figcaption><p>The final query compares null with null in two ways.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>NULL = NULL<br>Result: unknown</div><div>NULL IS NOT DISTINCT FROM NULL<br>Result: true</div><div>WHERE condition<br>Unknown does not pass as true</div></div><details><summary>Does the email domain prove mailbox ownership?</summary><p>No. It checks lowercase text and a minimal shape only.</p></details><details><summary>Would a JSON array satisfy the metadata constraint?</summary><p>No. The check requires a JSON object.</p></details>`,
  "0350": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep SKU uniqueness inside its tenant</figcaption><p>Two tenants both want SKU BOOK.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Tenant 1, SKU BOOK<br>First product accepted</div><div>Tenant 2, SKU BOOK<br>Different composite key<br>Permitted</div><div>Tenant 1, SKU BOOK again<br>Duplicate tenant/SKU pair<br>Rejected</div></div><details><summary>Would a global unique SKU express the same business rule?</summary><p>No. It would prevent different tenants from using the same SKU.</p></details><details><summary>Why store an order-line price snapshot?</summary><p>Later product-price changes must not rewrite the price agreed for an earlier order.</p></details>`,
  "0351": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject overlapping bookings only within the same room and tenant</figcaption><p>Use half-open time ranges. Booking A occupies 10:00 to 11:00 in tenant 1, room 7.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Same room, 10:30 to 11:30<br>Overlap<br>Conflict</div><div>Same room, 11:00 to 12:00<br>Adjacent half-open range<br>No overlap</div><div>Different room, same time<br>Different room key<br>No exclusion conflict</div></div><details><summary>Does deferring the constraint allow conflicting bookings to commit?</summary><p>No. It changes when validation occurs, not the required final invariant.</p></details><details><summary>Does this constraint authorize tenant 1's caller?</summary><p>No. It protects a data invariant. Access control is separate.</p></details>`,
  "0352": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep old and new readers compatible during a backfill</figcaption><p>The migration adds display_name while existing rows may still have null values.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Expand<br>Add nullable column</div><div>Compatible application<br>Read old rows safely<br>Write new shape</div><div>Backfill<br>Bounded committed ID ranges</div><div>Validate and contract<br>Prove non-null before requiring it</div></div><details><summary>Does NOT VALID mean new writes may ignore the added check?</summary><p>No. It defers checking existing rows. Plan writes and backfill before adding the constraint.</p></details><details><summary>Does a two-second lock timeout eliminate DDL locks?</summary><p>No. It limits waiting to obtain them. The migration still needs a safe rollout and retry policy.</p></details>`,
  "0353": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count the filtered jobs before applying HAVING</figcaption><p>Suppose tenant A has ten recent joined jobs, seven done. Tenant B has nine recent joined jobs, all done.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Tenant A<br>Group count: 10<br>Completed: 7<br>Pass HAVING</div><div>Tenant B<br>Group count: 9<br>Completed: 9<br>Fail HAVING</div></div><details><summary>Does tenant B appear because it has more completed jobs?</summary><p>No. Its group fails the minimum-count condition before ordering and limiting the result.</p></details><details><summary>Does the logical order force one physical execution plan?</summary><p>No. The optimizer can rearrange work when the transformation preserves the query's meaning.</p></details>`,
  "0354": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Return a parent once even when two children match</figcaption><p>Project p1 has two failed jobs. Project p2 has no jobs.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>EXISTS failed job<br>p1 appears once<br>p2 absent</div><div>NOT EXISTS any job<br>p2 appears<br>p1 absent</div><div>LEFT JOIN LATERAL latest<br>p2 remains with null child fields</div></div><details><summary>Why include id after created_at in the latest-job ordering?</summary><p>It breaks timestamp ties so the chosen row is deterministic under that ordering.</p></details><details><summary>Does EXISTS multiply p1 by the number of matching jobs?</summary><p>No. It asks whether at least one matching row exists.</p></details>`,
  "0338": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Restore test dependencies even when an assertion fails</figcaption><p>The test replaces current_principal with a principal that has no scopes.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before test<br>Save existing overrides</div><div>Request<br>Fake principal lacks permission<br>Expect 403</div><div>Finally<br>Clear test overrides<br>Restore previous mapping</div></div><details><summary>Does the cleanup run if the status assertion fails?</summary><p>Yes. The finally block restores the mapping on that path too.</p></details><details><summary>Does this fake-principal test verify real token validation?</summary><p>No. It tests authorization behavior with supplied identity. Authentication needs separate tests.</p></details>`,
  "0339": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Race two requests with the same idempotency key</figcaption><p>Both requests submit the same command and same-command key.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request A<br>Concurrent create</div><div>Request B<br>Concurrent create</div><div>Expected responses<br>Same project ID<br>Allowed success statuses</div></div><details><summary>Does matching response identity alone prove exactly one durable effect?</summary><p>No. Also inspect stored rows and transactional behavior, including failure and changed-intent cases.</p></details><details><summary>Does ASGITransport require a real network listener here?</summary><p>No. It calls the app in process. The database and lifespan fixtures remain important integration boundaries.</p></details>`,
  "0340": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep metric labels stable across project IDs</figcaption><p>Requests target projects p1 and p2 through the same route template.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request p1<br>Template /projects/{project_id}</div><div>Request p2<br>Same template</div><div>Metric grouping<br>One route label<br>Different request IDs belong in correlation data</div></div><details><summary>Does live returning alive establish readiness?</summary><p>No. The readiness endpoint separately checks required dependencies.</p></details><details><summary>Does this error counter count every non-success HTTP response?</summary><p>No. It increments when call_next raises. Returned error-status responses need a separate status-based policy.</p></details>`,
  "0341": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Measure after admission, not before it</figcaption><p>The benchmark starts each request timer inside the capacity limiter.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Wait for admission<br>Not inside recorded interval</div><div>Admitted request<br>Start timer<br>Await successful response</div><div>Recorded latency<br>Successful admitted request duration</div></div><details><summary>Do the percentiles include all waiting caused by overload?</summary><p>No. Pre-admission wait is excluded, and closed-loop load has further limits.</p></details><details><summary>What happens if a request returns an error?</summary><p>raise_for_status raises and the task-group experiment aborts. It does not return a complete error-rate benchmark.</p></details>`,
  "0342": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Make the new replica ready only after deployment prerequisites</figcaption><p>The deployment contract separates schema migration, readiness and shutdown.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before serving<br>Run migrations once</div><div>Replica readiness<br>Check required dependencies</div><div>Termination<br>Stop new traffic<br>Drain work<br>Enforce deadline</div></div><details><summary>Does EXPOSE start a server or publish a port?</summary><p>No. The command starts the server; deployment networking decides exposure.</p></details><details><summary>Does running as app make every application operation safe?</summary><p>No. It reduces process privilege. Secrets, network policy, dependencies and request authorization still need controls.</p></details>`,
  "0343": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Adapt one domain object into two public contracts</figcaption><p>Both versioned routes retrieve a project and convert it through different response models.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Domain project<br>Shared source data</div><div>V1 adapter<br>ProjectV1<br>Deprecation and Sunset headers</div><div>V2 adapter<br>ProjectV2</div></div><details><summary>Does marking V1 deprecated disable its route?</summary><p>No. It remains callable until the application actually removes or changes it.</p></details><details><summary>Does the new URL alone prove compatibility for existing clients?</summary><p>No. Check consumer contracts and schema changes before rollout.</p></details>`,
  "0344": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Wrap the original route handler without replacing its behavior</figcaption><p>TimedRoute obtains the framework's handler, then calls it inside try/finally.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before original call<br>Read clock</div><div>Original handler<br>Normal route behavior</div><div>Finally<br>Observe elapsed duration<br>Use route template label</div></div><details><summary>Does the timing run if the original handler raises?</summary><p>Yes. finally runs on that path.</p></details><details><summary>Does this measure the full delivery time of a streamed response body?</summary><p>No. Returning the response object can precede later streaming. Define precisely which interval the metric covers.</p></details>`,
  "0345": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Commit the public result beside the project and event</figcaption><p>The first request creates a project under tenant t1 and key k1.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Claim<br>Tenant, operation, key and intent</div><div>Transactional writes<br>Project<br>Outbox event<br>Serialized response</div><div>Commit<br>Publish them together</div><div>Replay<br>Return saved response</div></div><details><summary>Does the sketch implement concurrent claim isolation?</summary><p>No. The adapter must serialize competing keys and enforce changed-intent checks.</p></details><details><summary>Does the saved response by itself publish the event externally?</summary><p>No. An outbox worker handles delivery separately, with replay and failure policies.</p></details>`,
  "0328": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare the form token with the server-side session</figcaption><p>The authenticated session stores token A. An attacker supplies token B in both the cookie and form.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Cookie B equals form B<br>One comparison passes</div><div>Session A differs from form B<br>Trusted comparison fails</div><div>Combined result<br>Reject with 403</div></div><details><summary>Why is equality between cookie and form insufficient?</summary><p>Both may contain attacker-supplied values. The session-bound token is the trusted reference.</p></details><details><summary>Why can client code read the CSRF cookie but not the session cookie?</summary><p>The example marks the session HttpOnly and leaves the CSRF token readable for the intended submission flow.</p></details>`,
  "0329": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check origin, host and transport separately</figcaption><p>A browser page at app.example.com calls the API at api.example.com over HTTPS.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Origin policy<br>https://app.example.com allowed</div><div>Host policy<br>api.example.com allowed</div><div>Transport policy<br>HTTPS expected</div></div><details><summary>Does an allowed origin authenticate the caller?</summary><p>No. CORS controls browser access to responses. The API still needs identity and permission checks.</p></details><details><summary>Should arbitrary clients be trusted to report the original scheme through proxy headers?</summary><p>No. Trust forwarded headers only from controlled proxy infrastructure.</p></details>`,
  "0330": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Restore request context after an exception</figcaption><p>The client sends request ID req_7, which passes the character and length checks.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Entry<br>Set context to req_7<br>Keep reset token</div><div>Response start<br>Append X-Request-ID</div><div>Exit or exception<br>Reset previous context</div></div><details><summary>What happens to an ID containing a newline?</summary><p>It fails the allowed-character check. The middleware generates a replacement ID.</p></details><details><summary>Does this middleware set request.state.request_id?</summary><p>No. It sets a context variable and response header. Code expecting request.state needs an explicit bridge.</p></details>`,
  "0331": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject a body when actual chunks exceed the limit</figcaption><p>Use a four-byte limit. The request sends chunks of three bytes and two bytes.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First chunk<br>Buffered: 3<br>Accept</div><div>Second chunk<br>Would total 5<br>Exceeds 4</div><div>Result<br>Send 413<br>Do not call endpoint</div></div><details><summary>Does a smaller Content-Length override the measured total?</summary><p>No. The middleware counts received bytes.</p></details><details><summary>Is this the right buffering approach for large uploads?</summary><p>No. It is scoped to small JSON bodies. Large uploads need a streaming policy and upstream limits.</p></details>`,
  "0332": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A documented bearer scheme is not route protection</figcaption><p>The custom schema adds BearerAuth to OpenAPI components.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Schema entry<br>HTTP bearer<br>JWT format hint</div><div>Runtime route<br>Still needs security dependency</div><div>Operation documentation<br>Still needs security requirement</div></div><details><summary>Does adding the scheme reject anonymous requests?</summary><p>No. Documentation does not execute authentication.</p></details><details><summary>What happens on another custom_openapi call after caching?</summary><p>The function returns app.openapi_schema directly. Changes after generation need deliberate cache handling.</p></details>`,
  "0333": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The same signed event can arrive twice</figcaption><p>Event e1 is serialized and signed. Delivery is retried after an uncertain response.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First delivery<br>Event ID e1<br>Signed body</div><div>Retry<br>Event ID e1 again<br>May be validly signed</div><div>Receiver<br>Deduplicate identity and effect atomically</div></div><details><summary>Does a valid signature prove the event has never been processed?</summary><p>No. Signature verification and replay control solve different problems.</p></details><details><summary>Does the webhook decorator deliver events by itself?</summary><p>No. It documents the outbound contract. Delivery is separate executable work.</p></details>`,
  "0334": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Distinguish included routes from a mounted application</figcaption><p>The main app includes project routes and mounts a separate internal app.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Project router<br>Routes included in main app</div><div>/_internal mount<br>Delegate to internal ASGI app</div><div>/assets mount<br>Delegate to static-file app</div></div><details><summary>Does disabling the internal app's docs make its endpoints private?</summary><p>No. Network controls and authorization must enforce access.</p></details><details><summary>Is root_path a substitute for configuring the proxy's path handling?</summary><p>No. It describes the deployment prefix. Verify how the proxy and server pass the actual request path.</p></details>`,
  "0335": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Bound the outgoing queue for a slow WebSocket client</figcaption><p>The connection uses an outgoing stream buffer with capacity 50.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Fast producer<br>Publishes messages</div><div>Buffer<br>Up to configured capacity</div><div>Slow sender<br>Drains toward client</div><div>Disconnect<br>Cancel sibling work<br>Leave room and close streams</div></div><details><summary>Does capacity 50 define whether excess messages are dropped?</summary><p>No. The surrounding manager needs an explicit slow-consumer and overflow policy.</p></details><details><summary>When should room membership be authorized?</summary><p>Before accepting the connection. A parsed room ID does not establish access.</p></details>`,
  "0336": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Frame one event and a separate heartbeat</figcaption><p>Suppose event 7 has type update and JSON on two lines.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Event frame<br>id: 7<br>event: update<br>One data prefix per JSON line<br>Blank line ends frame</div><div>No event before heartbeat threshold<br>Send comment heartbeat<br>Keep stream open</div></div><details><summary>Why reject newlines in the ID and event type?</summary><p>They could change the frame structure. Payload lines are handled separately with data prefixes.</p></details><details><summary>Does an event ID alone implement replay after reconnection?</summary><p>No. The backend needs a retention and resume policy. This generator supplies framing, not durable replay.</p></details>`,
  "0337": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep authorization at each GraphQL resolver</figcaption><p>The same application serves HTML, static assets and a GraphQL endpoint.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>HTML route<br>Template and request context</div><div>Static mount<br>Assets from static directory</div><div>GraphQL route<br>Schema and shared identity context<br>Resolver permissions still required</div></div><details><summary>Does a logged-in GraphQL context authorize every field automatically?</summary><p>No. Each protected resolver must enforce its data-access policy.</p></details><details><summary>Does passing a CSP nonce into a template establish the whole CSP policy?</summary><p>No. The response policy and markup must use it consistently. The template value alone is not enforcement.</p></details>`,
  "0317": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reuse a configured write-scope check</figcaption><p>can_write stores the required scope projects:write.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Principal has projects:read only<br>Missing write scope<br>403</div><div>Principal has projects:write<br>Return principal<br>Continue dependency resolution</div></div><details><summary>Does creating RequiresScope authenticate anyone?</summary><p>No. It configures a callable. PrincipalDep supplies the authenticated principal when the dependency runs.</p></details><details><summary>Should a fake-principal override remain after a test?</summary><p>No. Restore overrides so later tests do not accidentally bypass real dependencies.</p></details>`,
  "0318": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep transport validation outside the acceptance service</figcaption><p>A client submits a command whose value is hello.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>HTTP body<br>value: hello</div><div>Validated Command<br>Dependency supplies service</div><div>Service call<br>await accept(body)</div><div>Route response<br>Declared status 202</div></div><details><summary>Does returning 202 prove the work will survive a restart?</summary><p>No. The service must establish durable acceptance if that is the API promise.</p></details><details><summary>Does this snippet implement get_service?</summary><p>No. The application must supply the dependency and its concrete service adapters.</p></details>`,
  "0319": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Cache settings inside each worker</figcaption><p>Two worker processes call get_settings independently.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Worker A<br>Construct Settings once<br>Cache its instance</div><div>Worker B<br>Construct Settings once<br>Separate cached instance</div></div><details><summary>Does changing the environment refresh an existing cached instance?</summary><p>No. Define a deliberate reload or restart policy.</p></details><details><summary>Does SecretStr make the database credential encrypted storage?</summary><p>No. It helps avoid accidental display. Secret retrieval, access control and safe logging remain separate responsibilities.</p></details>`,
  "0320": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Unwind resources when startup verification fails</figcaption><p>The lifespan creates an engine and HTTP client, then verifies the database.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Acquire engine<br>Register dispose</div><div>Acquire HTTP client<br>Register context cleanup</div><div>Database verification fails<br>Close HTTP client<br>Dispose engine<br>No yield</div></div><details><summary>Does failed verification leave a normally started app?</summary><p>No. Startup does not reach yield. The failure propagates after resource cleanup.</p></details><details><summary>Why register cleanup immediately after acquisition?</summary><p>A later startup step can fail. The exit stack then knows which earlier resources it owns.</p></details>`,
  "0321": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Place waiting work on the correct execution path</figcaption><p>The two routes use different HTTP clients.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>async_io<br>Await async client<br>Loop can run other work</div><div>sync_library<br>Normal def route<br>Framework runs it in a thread pool</div><div>Blocking sleep inside async route<br>Would block the event-loop thread</div></div><details><summary>Does adding async to a function make a blocking library cooperative?</summary><p>No. The called operation must support asynchronous waiting or run behind an appropriate boundary.</p></details><details><summary>Does the broken route demonstrate an actual block as written?</summary><p>No. Its blocking call is commented out. Its fixed response does not measure loop health.</p></details>`,
  "0322": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Load three dashboard sections under one deadline</figcaption><p>The function starts project, activity and permission reads in a task group.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Project read<br>Store project result</div><div>Activity read<br>Store activity result</div><div>Permissions read<br>Store permissions result</div><div>After successful group exit<br>Validate complete Dashboard</div></div><details><summary>What if a read exceeds the surrounding deadline?</summary><p>The timeout cancels the grouped work and prevents the normal Dashboard return. Awaited operations must cooperate with cancellation.</p></details><details><summary>Does this code intentionally return partial results on failure?</summary><p>No. A partial-dashboard policy would need explicit error and missing-section handling.</p></details>`,
  "0323": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Filter by both project and authenticated tenant</figcaption><p>Suppose project p1 belongs to t2 while the principal belongs to t1.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Requested project<br>p1</div><div>Required tenant<br>t1</div><div>Stored row<br>p1, tenant t2</div><div>Combined predicate<br>No match<br>404</div></div><details><summary>Does knowing p1 bypass the tenant predicate?</summary><p>No. Both conditions must match.</p></details><details><summary>Is the configured pool limit a deployment-wide limit?</summary><p>No. Each engine and worker can own a pool. Include all workers and overflow connections when planning database capacity.</p></details>`,
  "0324": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Commit the project and its outbox event together</figcaption><p>The transaction adds a project, flushes to obtain its ID, then adds the event.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First flush<br>Project ID available<br>Transaction still open</div><div>Second flush<br>Outbox row prepared<br>Still uncommitted</div><div>Successful transaction exit<br>Commit both</div></div><details><summary>Does the first flush make the project durable independently?</summary><p>No. The transaction can still roll back.</p></details><details><summary>What delays precede the last two attempts when attempts is 3?</summary><p>The retry loop waits 0.05 seconds, then 0.1 seconds after serialization failures. The operation must create a safe new attempt rather than reuse a failed transaction.</p></details>`,
  "0325": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Return the same receipt after a safe replay</figcaption><p>A tenant submits command C with key k1 twice.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>First request<br>Claim intent<br>Create job and outbox<br>Save receipt<br>Commit</div><div>Replay with same intent<br>Return prior committed receipt</div><div>Changed intent under k1<br>Adapter must reject</div></div><details><summary>Does importing BackgroundTasks make this acceptance durable?</summary><p>No. The shared database transaction and adapter contract provide the intended durability.</p></details><details><summary>Does the endpoint itself deliver the outbox message?</summary><p>No. A durable worker claims and acknowledges that work separately.</p></details>`,
  "0326": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate a token before loading its principal</figcaption><p>The decoder expects the configured issuer, audience projects-api and the permitted EdDSA algorithm.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Token bytes<br>Untrusted input</div><div>Verification<br>Signature, required claims<br>Issuer and audience</div><div>Subject conversion<br>Parse UUID</div><div>Current principal<br>Load account and permissions</div></div><details><summary>Can the token header choose a different accepted algorithm?</summary><p>No. The code supplies the allowed algorithm explicitly.</p></details><details><summary>Should this legacy password-grant fixture be copied as a new login design?</summary><p>No. The lesson labels it a legacy reading exercise and points new user-facing applications to an authorization-server flow with PKCE.</p></details>`,
  "0327": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A read scope does not grant every tenant's project</figcaption><p>A principal has projects:read and requests a project in another tenant.</p><div class="foundation-grid"><div>Scope check<br>projects:read present<br>Pass</div><div>Object-access check<br>Requested tenant and project<br>Still requires authorization</div></div><details><summary>Can the service skip object checks after the scope check passes?</summary><p>No. A general operation scope and permission to a specific object are separate requirements.</p></details><details><summary>What happens when the required scope is absent?</summary><p>The dependency raises 403 before the route calls the service.</p></details>`,
  "0310": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read one extra byte to detect an oversized avatar</figcaption>
<p>The endpoint permits at most 5,242,880 bytes. It requests one byte more than that limit.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>5,242,880 bytes returned<br>Within endpoint limit</div><div>5,242,881 bytes returned<br>Reject with 413</div><div>Both paths<br>Close UploadFile in finally</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an image/png content type prove the bytes form a safe PNG?</summary><p>No. That metadata is untrusted. Inspect the content and decoded dimensions before accepting it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this read limit bound earlier multipart spooling?</summary><p>No. Enforce parsing and upload limits before the endpoint too. Authorize the supplied owner before storage.</p></details>`,
  "0311": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Select public fields at the response boundary</figcaption>
<p>The repository returns a UserRecord with four fields. The route declares UserPublic as its response model.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Repository record<br>id, email<br>password_hash, internal_flags</div><div>Public response model<br>id, email</div><div>Serialized response<br>No password_hash<br>No internal_flags</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the wider return annotation require every record field to be sent?</summary><p>No. The declared response model controls this response's validation and filtering.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does output filtering authorize access to the requested user?</summary><p>No. It limits exposed fields. The route still needs an access policy for whose record may be read.</p></details>`,
  "0312": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Export a name containing a comma as one CSV field</figcaption>
<p>Suppose project 7 has the name Rates, Europe. The export writes the ID and name with csv.writer.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Logical row<br>ID: 7<br>Name: Rates, Europe</div><div>CSV row<br>7,&quot;Rates, Europe&quot;</div><div>CSV reader<br>Two fields<br>Name keeps its comma</div></div>
<details><summary>Does quoting also neutralize a spreadsheet formula?</summary><p>No. Formula injection requires a separate spreadsheet-safe export policy.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the generator assemble the whole export before yielding?</summary><p>No. It yields the header and then each encoded row. The repository iterator must honor its own resource and batching contract.</p></details>`,
  "0313": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Report the location of invalid input without echoing it</figcaption>
<p>Suppose request validation fails at a quantity field.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Validation exception<br>Contains error details</div><div>Selected response fields<br>Location and error type<br>Status 422<br>Request ID when present</div><div>Omitted details<br>Raw submitted value not copied</div></div>
<details><summary>Why select fields from each validation error?</summary><p>It gives clients stable diagnostic information without automatically reflecting potentially sensitive inputs.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the ProjectNotFound handler return the same complete envelope?</summary><p>No. It returns title and status only. A uniform error contract would require deliberately aligning both handlers.</p></details>`,
  "0314": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Observe a disconnect before preparing diagnostics</figcaption>
<p>The route first asks whether the client has disconnected.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Disconnected<br>Return local response object<br>Status 499</div><div>Still connected<br>Set X-Request-ID<br>Return request diagnostics</div></div>
<details><summary>Does returning 499 prove the disconnected client received it?</summary><p>No. The connection is already gone. The response object is not evidence of delivery.</p></details>
<details><summary>Where must request.state.request_id come from?</summary><p>Earlier application code, such as middleware, must set it. This endpoint reads the value but does not initialize it.</p></details>`,
  "0315": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Resolve a tenant only after checking membership</figcaption>
<p>The principal belongs to tenant t1. The request header selects t2.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Authenticated principal<br>Allowed tenants: t1</div><div>Header selection<br>t2</div><div>Membership check<br>t2 is absent<br>Raise 403 before tenant lookup</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a valid tenant header authenticate the caller?</summary><p>No. PrincipalDep must provide verified identity and authoritative memberships.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why inject the resolved tenant into the service?</summary><p>It makes the service's tenant boundary explicit. Repository behavior still needs to enforce the intended data scope.</p></details>`,
  "0316": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Unwind the unit of work before sending success</figcaption>
<p>The unit-of-work dependency uses function scope. Its code after yield commits the work.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Dependency entry<br>Open session<br>Yield unit of work</div><div>Handler succeeds<br>Exit function-scoped dependency<br>Attempt commit</div><div>Exception path<br>Session dependency rolls back<br>Re-raise error</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an exception thrown through yield continue to the next commit statement automatically?</summary><p>No. Without handling that exception, normal execution after yield is skipped.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does cleanup scope matter for commit failure?</summary><p>The commit must complete before success is sent if the response claims persistence. Verify that timing with the application's actual dependency and response paths.</p></details>`,
  "0303": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow the health request to its returned value</figcaption>
<p>The application registers one GET operation at /health.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request<br>GET /health</div><div>Handler<br>Return status: ok</div><div>API response<br>JSON object with status ok</div><div>Contract view<br>Inspect /openapi.json</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this response prove the database is healthy?</summary><p>No. The handler checks no dependencies. It only returns its fixed response.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does naming the application version 1.0.0 create a versioned URL?</summary><p>No. That is application metadata. Routes remain those explicitly registered.</p></details>`,
  "0304": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Send response headers before the body event</figcaption>
<p>The HTTP-only ASGI experiment receives one request event before responding.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Receive http.request<br>Continue response path</div><div>Send http.response.start<br>Status 200<br>JSON content type</div><div>Send http.response.body<br>Status-ok JSON bytes</div></div>
<details><summary>What happens if the received event is http.disconnect?</summary><p>The function returns without sending a response.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does reading one event parse a complete chunked request body?</summary><p>No. This experiment supplies no body parser or multi-event request-body loop. It also does not implement lifespan or WebSocket handling.</p></details>`,
  "0305": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate documentation settings from route identity</figcaption>
<p>The application uses title Projects API, version 2.1.0 and a custom documentation path.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Interactive docs<br>/documentation</div><div>ReDoc setting<br>Disabled</div><div>Operation ID helper<br>Copies each existing API route name</div></div>
<details><summary>Does calling the helper before adding routes rename future routes?</summary><p>No. It loops over the routes present at that moment. Apply the policy after registering the routes it should affect.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can two route names safely become the same client operation ID?</summary><p>Generated clients need unambiguous identifiers. Keep operation IDs unique and stable as part of the API contract.</p></details>`,
  "0306": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use the service result to choose the creation status</figcaption>
<p>service.put returns a project and a created flag.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>created: true<br>Status 201<br>Location points to project</div><div>created: false<br>Status 200<br>Location points to project</div><div>Fixed route /projects/me<br>Registered before /projects/{project_id}</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the decorator force every response to be 201?</summary><p>No. The handler sets response.status_code based on the service outcome.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why register me before the dynamic project path?</summary><p>The fixed segment could otherwise be consumed as a dynamic parameter. Route order affects which operation receives the request.</p></details>`,
  "0307": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate path identity separately from query choices</figcaption>
<p>For a request with a valid tenant UUID, project ID 7 and kind team, the handler receives parsed values.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Path tenant_id<br>UUID</div><div>Path project_id<br>Integer 7<br>Within positive bound</div><div>Query kind<br>team enum member</div><div>Query as_of omitted<br>None</div></div>
<details><summary>Would project ID 0 reach the service through this validated path?</summary><p>No. The positive-value constraint rejects it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a valid tenant UUID prove the caller belongs to that tenant?</summary><p>No. Parsing and bounds do not authorize access. The service needs a trusted tenant-access policy.</p></details>`,
  "0308": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Apply filter defaults, then check explicit values</figcaption>
<p>Compare an empty query with a query that asks for limit 101.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>No filter values<br>limit: 50<br>offset: 0<br>order: created_at<br>tags: empty list</div><div>limit 101<br>Exceeds maximum 100<br>Reject</div><div>Unknown filter field<br>Extra fields forbidden<br>Reject</div></div>
<details><summary>Is X-Request-ID read from the query string?</summary><p>No. The Header declaration reads it from the named HTTP header.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does receiving a session cookie establish a valid session?</summary><p>No. This signature reads a value. Authentication must validate it separately.</p></details>`,
  "0309": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check the order total after checking its fields</figcaption>
<p>Assume a valid payment object. Compare quantity 100 with two integer unit prices.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>100 × 100,000 cents<br>Total: 10,000,000<br>At limit<br>Pass total check</div><div>100 × 100,001 cents<br>Total: 10,000,100<br>Above limit<br>Reject</div><div>Payment kind<br>card selects token model<br>invoice selects purchase-order model</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a valid quantity alone prove the total is allowed?</summary><p>No. The model-level validator checks the relationship between quantity and unit price.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the outer extra-forbid setting automatically impose that same policy on every nested model?</summary><p>No. Review each nested model's configuration. A strict boundary needs deliberate rules at every level.</p></details>`,
  "0293": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate the envelope before accepting its project field</figcaption>
<p>The wire envelope must have exactly schema_version and project. Version must be the integer 1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Version 1, project object<br>Accept envelope</div><div>Version true, project object<br>Reject exact-type mismatch</div><div>Version 1, project list<br>Reject project shape</div><div>Accepted project<br>Domain fields still need validation</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an accepted envelope prove the project has a valid name?</summary><p>No. The decoder checks that project is a dictionary, not its domain fields.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should pickle be substituted for JSON when input is untrusted?</summary><p>No. Unpickling can execute code. Keep the trust boundary explicit.</p></details>`,
  "0294": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Deduplicate before sorting the benchmark input</figcaption>
<p>The input contains numbers 0 through 49,999 twice.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input<br>100,000 entries</div><div>Set conversion<br>50,000 distinct values</div><div>Sorted output<br>0 through 49,999<br>50,000 entries</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the set preserve the original duplicate count?</summary><p>No. Deduplication deliberately removes repeated values.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the example supply a production runtime or memory result?</summary><p>No. It supplies measurement code. Record results in the actual environment and compare representative workloads before optimizing.</p></details>`,
  "0295": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Assert what the service sends to storage</figcaption>
<p>The caller provides spaces around Demo. A spec-constrained mock stands in for Gateway.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Service input<br>Spaces around Demo</div><div>Normalized project<br>name: Demo</div><div>Expected gateway call<br>save once with name Demo</div><div>Returned value<br>name: Demo</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this assertion prove a database row was committed?</summary><p>No. It verifies the service's interaction with a mock gateway.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should a blank-name test also assert?</summary><p>It should assert rejection and that the gateway was not called. Invalid input must not reach the effect boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0296": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Log a failed job without swallowing its exception</figcaption>
<p>Suppose execute runs job J with request ID r1 and process raises.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Start log<br>job_started<br>request_id r1</div><div>Exception log<br>job_failed<br>request_id r1<br>Traceback context</div><div>Caller outcome<br>Original exception re-raised</div></div>
<details><summary>What request ID appears on worker_started outside an adapter?</summary><p>The formatter default supplies a dash, so the record can still format.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a recorded traceback mean the failure was recovered?</summary><p>No. Logging adds diagnostic evidence. This function rethrows for its caller to handle.</p></details>`,
  "0297": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Inspect the installed artifact outside the source tree</figcaption>
<p>The package configuration selects src/interview_evidence for its wheel.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Source package<br>Implementation and optional py.typed marker</div><div>Built wheel<br>Inspect actual archive contents</div><div>Clean environment<br>Install wheel<br>Verify intended imports</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a successful import from the checkout prove the wheel is complete?</summary><p>No. The checkout may expose files absent from the artifact.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does requires-python choose an interpreter automatically?</summary><p>No. It states the compatibility requirement. The chosen environment still needs a suitable interpreter.</p></details>`,
  "0298": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate isolation, resolution and trust</figcaption>
<p>The workflow creates a virtual environment, installs a reviewed hashed resolution and inspects the resulting graph.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Virtual environment<br>Controls interpreter prefix and packages</div><div>Lock and hashes<br>Identify resolved artifacts</div><div>Provenance review<br>Assess where artifacts came from</div></div>
<details><summary>Does installing into a virtual environment make a malicious dependency harmless?</summary><p>No. Environment isolation is not a security sandbox for package execution.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a matching hash prove the dependency is trustworthy?</summary><p>No. It verifies artifact identity against the recorded value. Review the source and provenance separately.</p></details>`,
  "0299": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Pass a numeric buffer and its length to native code</figcaption>
<p>The ctypes array holds four doubles: 1, 2, 3 and 4. The declared function accepts a double pointer and a size.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Buffer<br>[1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]</div><div>Native arguments<br>Pointer to buffer<br>Length 4</div><div>Expected mean<br>10 ÷ 4<br>2.5</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this file supply libstats.so?</summary><p>No. The native library is an external requirement with its own implementation and ABI contract.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would a wrong length be safely checked by Python automatically?</summary><p>No. Native code must honor validated bounds and ownership. A memory error can crash the interpreter.</p></details>`,
  "0300": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate the command before calling the repository</figcaption>
<p>Compare a name with spaces around Demo and a name containing only spaces.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Spaces around Demo<br>Normalize to Demo<br>Call repository.add</div><div>Spaces only<br>Normalize to empty<br>Raise name required<br>No repository call</div></div>
<details><summary>Must the handler import a particular database library?</summary><p>No. It receives a repository through its declared contract. The composition root supplies the concrete adapter.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does returning the project prove the repository is durable?</summary><p>Only if the supplied adapter's contract and implementation establish that guarantee. The protocol alone does not.</p></details>`,
  "0301": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A checked path can change before it is opened</figcaption>
<p>Consider an upload tree that an attacker can modify. The application resolves a path, then passes it to an external decoder later.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Check time<br>Resolved path is under upload root</div><div>Between check and use<br>Attacker replaces a directory or symlink</div><div>Use time<br>Path may resolve elsewhere</div></div>
<details><summary>Does Path.resolve make the later open race-free?</summary><p>No. Control directory ownership or use an appropriate descriptor-relative, no-follow access policy.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does passing subprocess arguments without a shell make image decoding safe?</summary><p>No. It addresses shell parsing, not decoder vulnerabilities, resource use or filesystem races. Use appropriate containment and bounds.</p></details>`,
  "0302": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Replay one committed project result</figcaption>
<p>Submit name Demo with key k1, then repeat the same request within the same authenticated scope.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First claim k1<br>No prior result<br>Add project<br>Save result<br>Commit together</div><div>Same key and intent<br>Return saved result<br>No new project</div><div>Same key, changed name<br>Adapter must reject changed intent</div></div>
<details><summary>What must happen if commit fails?</summary><p>The adapter must roll back uncommitted effects and the receipt together. The service relies on that unit-of-work contract.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a sequential fake prove two concurrent requests cannot both create a project?</summary><p>No. Test atomic claiming, isolation and rollback on the real adapter before claiming concurrent deduplication.</p></details>`,
  "0282": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Match every declared project state</figcaption>
<p>ProjectState permits draft, active and archived. label gives each an explicit branch.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>draft → Draft</div><div>active → Live</div><div>archived → Archived</div><div>Unexpected runtime value<br>assert_never raises</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the Literal annotation block an unchecked caller from passing other text?</summary><p>No. Python annotations do not enforce that boundary at runtime. The fallback still raises if reached.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should happen during static checking if a new state is added without a branch?</summary><p>The value reaching assert_never is no longer impossible. The exhaustive-handling check should reveal the missing case.</p></details>`,
  "0283": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use a reader through its get contract</figcaption>
<p>Hypothetical fixture: a reader returns Ada for p1 and Grace for p2. names receives those IDs in that order.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>get p1<br>Returns Ada</div><div>get p2<br>Returns Grace</div><div>names result<br>[Ada, Grace]</div></div>
<details><summary>Must the reader inherit a particular concrete storage class?</summary><p>No. The protocol describes the required get method and return type.</p></details>
<details><summary>What relationship does ParamSpec preserve in traced?</summary><p>It keeps the wrapped callable's parameter contract connected to the returned wrapper, while R preserves its result type.</p></details>`,
  "0284": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Create a trusted command from checked fields</figcaption>
<p>Input has name spaces around Demo and member_ids [1, 2].</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Name check<br>Nonempty string after trim<br>Demo</div><div>Member check<br>List of exact integers<br>[1, 2]</div><div>Command<br>name: Demo<br>member_ids: tuple (1, 2)</div></div>
<details><summary>Does member_ids [True] pass?</summary><p>No. Each item must have exact type int. The boolean subclass is deliberately excluded.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the frozen dataclass alone establish these checks?</summary><p>No. parse_command validates and normalizes the values before constructing it.</p></details>`,
  "0285": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the previous JSON when serialization fails</figcaption>
<p>The target already contains a valid payload. A later write contains an object that JSON cannot serialize.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Existing target<br>Valid payload</div><div>New temporary file<br>Serialization raises TypeError</div><div>Failure cleanup<br>Remove temporary file<br>Do not replace target</div><div>Target afterward<br>Previous payload remains</div></div>
<details><summary>Why write beside the target before replacing it?</summary><p>The staged file can be completed before the same-filesystem replacement publishes it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the replacement prove crash durability on every storage system?</summary><p>No. Directory synchronization and storage semantics still matter.</p></details>`,
  "0286": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject tokens that do not fit the revision policy</figcaption>
<p>The validator requires a complete conservative token before calling Git.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>abc123<br>Matches policy</div><div>--help<br>Invalid first character<br>Reject</div><div>main;echo injected<br>Invalid punctuation<br>Reject</div><div>abc123 plus newline<br>Full match fails<br>Reject</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an argument list alone eliminate option injection?</summary><p>No. A program can interpret a supplied argument as an option. The token policy and option delimiter address that boundary here.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does passing the regex establish that Git can resolve the token?</summary><p>No. Git can still exit unsuccessfully. check=True turns that outcome into an exception.</p></details>`,
  "0287": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Suspend each task while its timer waits</figcaption>
<p>The program creates five fetch tasks, each of which awaits asyncio.sleep.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Task starts<br>Record start time</div><div>Await sleep<br>Suspend this task<br>Loop can run other tasks</div><div>Gather result<br>item-0 through item-4<br>Input task order</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this create five Python threads?</summary><p>No. These are cooperatively scheduled tasks on the event loop.</p></details>
<details><summary>What changes if fetch uses time.sleep?</summary><p>It blocks the loop's thread during the sleep, preventing the intended overlap of other task progress.</p></details>`,
  "0288": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Account for stop messages as well as work items</figcaption>
<p>Four workers share the bounded queue. After submitting work, the producer puts four None sentinels into it.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Normal item<br>Process under timeout<br>Call task_done</div><div>None sentinel<br>Return from worker<br>Finally calls task_done</div><div>queue.join<br>Wait for every enqueued item<br>Including all four sentinels</div></div>
<details><summary>What if the sentinel path returns without task_done?</summary><p>The queue's unfinished count remains positive, so join can wait indefinitely.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does task_done prove the effect was committed?</summary><p>No. It is queue accounting. This in-memory fixture provides no durable receipt or recovery after restart.</p></details>`,
  "0289": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep read-modify-write inside one lock</figcaption>
<p>For a small trace, start value at 0 and let two threads each increment once.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Thread A owns lock<br>Read 0<br>Write 1<br>Release</div><div>Thread B owns lock next<br>Read 1<br>Write 2<br>Release</div><div>Final value<br>2</div></div>
<details><summary>What can happen if both threads read 0 without the lock?</summary><p>Both may compute 1 and write it, losing one increment. The lock protects the whole operation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the application use the GIL as its counter contract?</summary><p>No. Use explicit synchronization for the invariant rather than relying on interpreter execution details.</p></details>`,
  "0290": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A result timeout does not stop a running process job</figcaption>
<p>Suppose the first submitted prime-counting job is still running when future.result reaches its timeout.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Waiter<br>Timeout raises</div><div>Exception handler<br>Request cancellation of futures</div><div>Already running job<br>May continue</div><div>Pool context exits<br>Waits for running jobs</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the ten-second result timeout guarantee the program exits within ten seconds?</summary><p>No. Running work and executor shutdown can outlast that wait.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why use a real module and the main guard with spawn?</summary><p>Workers import the main module. The guard prevents that import from recursively creating another pool.</p></details>`,
  "0291": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Let restart survival decide before CPU placement</figcaption>
<p>Compare CPU-heavy work that retains the GIL with the same work required to survive a process restart.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>No restart requirement<br>CPU heavy, retains GIL<br>Choose process</div><div>Must survive restart<br>Same CPU characteristics<br>Choose durable queue worker first</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the durable boundary remove the need to choose CPU execution resources?</summary><p>No. A durable worker still needs an appropriate execution model. The helper selects the first boundary, not a complete architecture.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can any blocking I/O function be used directly in an async task?</summary><p>No. The actual library must cooperate with async scheduling or be moved behind an appropriate executor boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0292": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Collect a cycle with no strong application reference</figcaption>
<p>left points to right and right points to left. observed is a weak reference to left.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before deletion<br>Names reach both nodes<br>Nodes point to each other</div><div>Delete both names<br>Cycle remains internally<br>Weak reference does not keep it alive</div><div>Explicit collection<br>Cycle reclaimed<br>observed returns None</div></div>
<details><summary>Must the total collected count equal exactly two?</summary><p>No. Collection may find other eligible objects. The assertion checks that this observed node is gone.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should a file or socket wait for this collection pattern to close?</summary><p>No. External resources need explicit lifecycle management, such as a context manager.</p></details>`,
  "0271": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Import the cached package twice</figcaption>
<p>The example imports projects twice in the same process without removing its cache entry.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First import<br>Load projects<br>Cache module in sys.modules</div><div>Second import<br>Return cached module</div><div>Identity check<br>first is second<br>Both are sys.modules[projects]</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the second import construct an independent package instance?</summary><p>No. It returns the cached module in this example.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does listing ProjectService in __all__ implement the class?</summary><p>No. The package imports it from service. The multi-file example needs that implementation.</p></details>`,
  "0272": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Construct a project through its class method</figcaption>
<p>from_mapping receives a name with spaces around Agent Lab.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Class method<br>Receives cls<br>Trim name</div><div>Construction<br>cls with Agent Lab<br>Allocate and initialize instance</div><div>Instance dictionary<br>name: Agent Lab</div><div>Class attribute<br>category: portfolio</div></div>
<details><summary>Why is category absent from the asserted instance dictionary?</summary><p>It is stored on the class. The instance stores its own name.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does valid_name automatically receive the instance?</summary><p>No. It is a static method and operates on its explicit value argument.</p></details>`,
  "0273": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow save through the method resolution order</figcaption>
<p>LoggedMemorySaver inherits from LoggingMixin before MemorySaver.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>saver.save evidence<br>Find LoggingMixin.save</div><div>Mixin<br>Print saving evidence<br>Call super().save</div><div>MemorySaver<br>Append evidence<br>values: [evidence]</div></div>
<details><summary>Does super always mean a fixed direct parent class?</summary><p>No. It follows the method resolution order for the instance's class. Here it continues to MemorySaver.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if the mixin omitted its super call?</summary><p>The log would occur, but the chain would stop before storing the value.</p></details>`,
  "0274": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Break equal totals with the score tuple</figcaption>
<p>Board A contains [1, 2]. Board B contains [3]. Both totals are 3.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Board A ordering key<br>(3, (1, 2))</div><div>Board B ordering key<br>(3, (3))</div><div>Comparison<br>Totals tie<br>1 is less than 3<br>A is less than B</div></div>
<details><summary>Are the boards equal because their totals match?</summary><p>No. Dataclass equality compares the stored score tuples. These tuples differ.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why include the tuple in the ordering key?</summary><p>It distinguishes different boards with equal totals and keeps ordering consistent with structural equality.</p></details>`,
  "0275": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate size through the descriptor</figcaption>
<p>Construct Batch with label jobs and compare size 3 with size True.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Size 3<br>Exact type int<br>Positive<br>Store 3</div><div>Size True<br>Exact type bool<br>Reject</div><div>summary lookup<br>Fallback returns jobs:3 for valid batch</div></div>
<details><summary>Why is True rejected even though bool is an int subclass?</summary><p>The descriptor uses an exact type comparison, not isinstance. Only actual int values pass.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this class's slots declaration remove its instance dictionary?</summary><p>No. It explicitly includes __dict__, which the descriptor uses for storage.</p></details>`,
  "0276": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Add money only when its currencies match</figcaption>
<p>Use finite Decimal amounts and the Currency enum from the example.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>2 USD + 3 USD<br>Same currency<br>New value: 5 USD</div><div>2 USD + 3 EUR<br>Different currencies<br>Raise currency mismatch</div><div>NaN USD<br>Non-finite amount<br>Reject at construction</div></div>
<details><summary>Does frozen automatically validate the annotated amount type?</summary><p>No. __post_init__ performs the explicit runtime checks.</p></details>
<details><summary>Are the input tags preserved by addition?</summary><p>No. The implementation creates a Money value without tags. A production contract must state how metadata and rounding should work.</p></details>`,
  "0277": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Consume one iterator, then start a fresh traversal</figcaption>
<p>Create an iterator from Countdown 3 and read one value.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First next<br>Returns 3</div><div>Remaining same iterator<br>2, 1</div><div>Fresh Countdown traversal<br>3, 2, 1</div></div>
<details><summary>Does calling iter on the existing iterator restart it?</summary><p>No. It returns that same iterator with its current position.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why reject a negative starting value?</summary><p>The loop decrements while current is truthy. A negative value would keep moving away from zero and would not terminate naturally.</p></details>`,
  "0278": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Pause flattening at a yielded value</figcaption>
<p>The groups are [a, b] and [c]. The caller requests one item and then closes the generator.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First next<br>Enter first group<br>Yield a</div><div>Suspended position<br>b and c not yet yielded</div><div>close<br>Inject GeneratorExit<br>Stop traversal</div></div>
<details><summary>Does creating the generator eagerly build a flat list?</summary><p>No. Values are produced as the caller advances it.</p></details>
<details><summary>How does managed_lines release its file after an early close?</summary><p>Its finally block closes the handle when execution unwinds. The caller must still manage the generator's lifecycle rather than relying on delayed collection.</p></details>`,
  "0279": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep only rows with a kind and value</figcaption>
<p>The source contains five lines. The pipeline splits each at its first comma.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>created,p1<br>Keep created and p1</div><div>missing separator and empty line<br>Skip both</div><div>updated,p2<br>Keep updated and p2</div><div>,bad<br>Empty kind<br>Skip</div></div>
<details><summary>How many event dictionaries remain?</summary><p>Two. The pipeline retains created/p1 and updated/p2.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is silently skipping the other lines always acceptable?</summary><p>No. Count or quarantine malformed rows when losing input is unacceptable. This splitter is also not a quoted-CSV parser.</p></details>`,
  "0280": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Time a parse without replacing its result</figcaption>
<p>The decorated parse receives the text 42.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Wrapper starts<br>Read performance clock</div><div>Wrapped function<br>Convert 42 text to integer<br>Return 42</div><div>Finally<br>Print elapsed measurement<br>Preserve return value</div></div>
<details><summary>What happens when parse receives text that int cannot convert?</summary><p>The finally block still records timing, and the conversion exception continues to the caller.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does parse.__name__ still equal parse?</summary><p>functools.wraps copies the wrapped function's identifying metadata to the wrapper.</p></details>`,
  "0281": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Close earlier files when opening a later file fails</figcaption>
<p>Suppose read_all opens file A, then opening file B raises an error.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Open A<br>Register its context with ExitStack</div><div>Open B<br>Fails before reading</div><div>Stack unwinds<br>Close A<br>Propagate the error</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the list comprehension prevent A from being cleaned up?</summary><p>No. ExitStack already owns A's context and unwinds it when the block exits.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will connected close the client when fetch fails after a successful connect?</summary><p>Yes. The async context manager reaches its finally block and awaits close.</p></details>`,
  "0260": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Identify the interpreter before checking installed packages</figcaption>
<p>Compare a shell's python command with the explicit .venv/bin/python path in the lesson.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Interpreter evidence<br>sys.executable<br>sys.version</div><div>Environment evidence<br>sys.prefix<br>sys.base_prefix</div><div>Package command<br>Use that interpreter's -m pip</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a familiar prompt prove the script uses the intended environment?</summary><p>No. Inspect the executable path. A shell command can resolve to a different interpreter.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the exact disassembly be memorized across Python versions?</summary><p>No. It describes instructions for the inspected interpreter. Preserve version evidence when comparing execution details.</p></details>`,
  "0261": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the multiplier's cells after its outer call returns</figcaption>
<p>multiplier receives factor 2 and returns apply. The caller then invokes it with value 4.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Retained factor<br>2</div><div>Retained calls counter<br>0 → 1</div><div>Current argument<br>4</div><div>Returned value<br>4 × 2 = 8</div></div>
<details><summary>Does calls reset to zero every time double is invoked?</summary><p>No. The closure retains that binding. nonlocal updates the retained counter.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does broken fail before assigning rate 20?</summary><p>The assignment makes rate local to that function block. Its earlier read sees an uninitialized local, not the global rate.</p></details>`,
  "0262": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Trace which member record changes from Ada to Grace</figcaption>
<p>original contains a members list with one member named Ada. Create an alias, a shallow copy and a deep copy.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>original and alias<br>Same outer dictionary<br>Shared member becomes Grace</div><div>shallow<br>New outer dictionary<br>Same members list<br>Member becomes Grace</div><div>deep<br>Copied nested data<br>Member remains Ada</div></div>
<details><summary>Can original and shallow compare equal without being the same object?</summary><p>Yes. Their contents compare equal while their outer dictionary identities differ.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does calling append_member without an argument share one default list?</summary><p>No. The function creates a new list when members is None. Supplying an existing list still permits mutation of that list.</p></details>`,
  "0263": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Round the invoice after calculating tax</figcaption>
<p>The example buys three items at 19.99 and applies tax at 0.0825 using Decimal strings.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Subtotal<br>19.99 × 3<br>59.97</div><div>Tax<br>59.97 × 0.0825<br>4.947525</div><div>Unrounded total<br>64.917525</div><div>Two decimal places<br>64.92</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this prove every invoice should round at the same stage?</summary><p>No. The example rounds the final total. A business contract may require different line-level or tax rounding rules.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why is the float equality check different from the Fraction check?</summary><p>Binary floating point cannot represent these decimal fractions exactly. Fraction represents the stated rational values exactly.</p></details>`,
  "0264": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Replace a character, not its final byte</figcaption>
<p>The UTF-8 bytes for the coffee symbol are E2 98 95. The bytearray operation replaces only 95 with 21, the byte for !.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original symbol bytes<br>E2 98 95<br>Valid coffee symbol</div><div>Partial byte replacement<br>E2 98 21<br>Invalid UTF-8 sequence</div><div>Text-level replacement<br>café !<br>Encode complete characters</div></div>
<details><summary>Will strict decoding silently repair the damaged sequence?</summary><p>No. It raises UnicodeDecodeError.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can two visually identical accented strings compare unequal before normalization?</summary><p>Yes. A composed character and a base character plus combining mark have different code-point sequences. NFC makes the shown pair equal.</p></details>`,
  "0266": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use equal tenant-project values as the same key</figcaption>
<p>owners stores Ada under TenantProject t-1, p-1. A lookup constructs a new instance with those same field values.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Stored key<br>tenant: t-1<br>project: p-1</div><div>New lookup key<br>tenant: t-1<br>project: p-1<br>Equal value and hash</div><div>Lookup result<br>Ada</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the lookup require the identical dataclass instance?</summary><p>No. Equal hashable keys can locate the same dictionary entry.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does an equal hash alone prove two keys are equal?</summary><p>No. Collisions are possible. Key equality still matters.</p></details>`,
  "0267": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Match the command before transforming its data</figcaption>
<p>The function accepts two example command shapes.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Create mapping<br>name: spaces around demo<br>String and nonempty after trim<br>Create with name demo</div><div>Sum sequence<br>[sum, 2, 3] with numeric strings<br>Convert values to integers<br>Return 5</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a create name containing only spaces reach the successful create branch?</summary><p>No. The guard fails. This mapping then matches the type-only fallback and raises ValueError.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the sum pattern prove every remaining value is a valid integer?</summary><p>No. Conversion can still fail. Structural matching and value validation are separate steps.</p></details>`,
  "0268": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Place each search argument in its declared slot</figcaption>
<p>The example calls search with python, senior, limit 10 and region EU.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Positional-only query<br>python</div><div>Extra positional filters<br>Tuple containing senior</div><div>Keyword-only limit<br>10</div><div>Extra keyword metadata<br>region: EU</div></div>
<details><summary>Can the required query be supplied only as a query keyword?</summary><p>No. The slash marks that parameter positional-only. The required positional argument would still be missing.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does remember use a unique missing sentinel?</summary><p>It distinguishes an omitted cache from a supplied object. An omitted cache produces a new dictionary for that call.</p></details>`,
  "0269": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Store validators as callable values</figcaption>
<p>The list contains a minimum-length function with size fixed at 3, and a Between object with bounds 1 and 100.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Username validator<br>Input ada<br>Length 3<br>Returns ada</div><div>Range validator<br>Input 42<br>Within 1..100<br>Returns 42</div><div>Changed input<br>101<br>Range validator raises ValueError</div></div>
<details><summary>Does partial run the validation when username is created?</summary><p>No. It binds the size argument. Validation happens when the resulting callable is invoked.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why can a Between instance appear beside a function?</summary><p>Its __call__ method makes the instance callable. It carries the bounds on the object.</p></details>`,
  "0270": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Split grouped failures by their types</figcaption>
<p>The group contains a profile TimeoutError and a malformed-project ValueError.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>ExceptionGroup<br>Two different failures</div><div>Timeout subgroup<br>Handled by except* TimeoutError</div><div>Invalid-data subgroup<br>Handled by except* ValueError</div></div>
<details><summary>Does handling the timeout mean the malformed project should be retried?</summary><p>No. Each failure needs an operation-specific policy. A type-based subgroup separates handling; it does not decide retry safety.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does raising ProjectUnavailable from RepositoryError preserve?</summary><p>It records the repository exception as the cause while adding the service-level project message.</p></details>`,
  "0254": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Measure a wait without calling it a capacity test</figcaption>
<p>The fixture marks the start, awaits a timeout requested for 60 ms, then marks the end.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Start mark<br>Record work:start</div><div>Idle-wait fixture<br>Await timeout<br>No representative request load</div><div>End evidence<br>Measured duration<br>Loop utilization and delay</div></div>
<details><summary>Must the measured duration equal exactly 60 ms?</summary><p>No. Scheduling can delay completion beyond the requested threshold.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does low utilization during this wait prove the service can handle production traffic?</summary><p>No. Use representative load and request-level measurements to establish capacity.</p></details>`,
  "0255": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A Map entry keeps its request reachable</figcaption>
<p>Call unsafeRemember with request A, then request B under a different ID.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Cache entry A<br>Strong reference to request A<br>Retains its reachable objects</div><div>Cache entry B<br>Strong reference to request B<br>Retains its reachable objects</div><div>No eviction rule<br>Entries can grow with distinct IDs</div></div>
<details><summary>Can garbage collection reclaim A while the cache still holds it?</summary><p>Not merely because the request handler finished. The Map still provides a strong retaining path.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does flat heapUsed rule out growing process memory?</summary><p>No. Inspect RSS and external memory too, including Buffers and native allocations.</p></details>`,
  "0256": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Match a slow request to the deployed build and workload</figcaption>
<p>Incident fixture: requests become slow after a deployment. Collect evidence from that build under the affected input pattern.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Identity<br>Commit, Node version and flags</div><div>Behavior<br>Request latency<br>Loop delay and utilization</div><div>Execution evidence<br>Sampled stacks<br>GC and allocation activity</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a CPU profile from a different input pattern explain this incident automatically?</summary><p>No. Reproduce representative work and correlate the profile with the affected requests.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the inspector be exposed publicly to speed up diagnosis?</summary><p>No. The lesson binds it to loopback and calls for an authenticated local tunnel where appropriate. Diagnostic access needs controlled handling.</p></details>`,
  "0257": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate a native-interface sketch from an implemented addition</figcaption>
<p>The Node-API Add body contains comments only. The WebAssembly example expects an exported add function.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Node-API sketch<br>Validation and conversion still required<br>No completed Add implementation</div><div>WebAssembly call<br>Inputs: 20 and 22<br>Expected result: 42</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the shown C function already implement safe addition?</summary><p>No. It is an outline. Argument checks, computation, result creation and error handling remain to be written.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does moving CPU work into native code automatically stop it blocking the main thread?</summary><p>No. Long synchronous native work can still block it. Choose an appropriate async or worker boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0258": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Bound each import resource separately</figcaption>
<p>The ingestion sketch gives the validation transform a 64 KiB line limit and the persistence sink a concurrency setting of 8.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>One line<br>Limit: 64 KiB</div><div>Active persistence work<br>Configured concurrency: 8</div><div>Whole request<br>Total byte bound still required</div><div>Pending work<br>Admission and queue policy still required</div></div>
<details><summary>Do small individual lines guarantee a small import?</summary><p>No. A request can contain many small lines. The total body needs its own bound.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does passing concurrency 8 prove the unsupplied sink enforces it?</summary><p>No. Implement and test the adapter contract. This sketch specifies a requested limit, not verified behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0259": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An import failure does not undo earlier writes</figcaption>
<p>Capstone fixture: rows A and B commit, then row C fails validation or persistence.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Row A<br>Committed</div><div>Row B<br>Committed</div><div>Row C<br>Fails<br>Pipeline rejects</div><div>Recovery decision<br>Atomic promotion or resumable partial success</div></div>
<details><summary>Does pipeline rejection roll back A and B?</summary><p>No. Pipeline cleanup does not undo prior database commits.</p></details>
<details><summary>What must a retry policy know in the partial-success design?</summary><p>It needs stable record identities and saved outcomes so it can distinguish completed rows from pending work without duplicating effects.</p></details>`,
  "0246": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Repeated shutdown calls share one drain</figcaption>
<p>The coordinator receives two shutdown requests while its first close operation is pending.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First request<br>Mark not ready<br>Start server close<br>Create pending promise</div><div>Second request<br>Return same promise<br>No second close</div><div>Deadline failure<br>Abort controller<br>Force HTTP connections closed</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a successful drain abort the controller in this fixture?</summary><p>No. The successful fake close resolves before the deadline. The abort belongs to the deadline-failure path.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does closeAllConnections close every resource in the process?</summary><p>No. Upgraded connections and non-HTTP resources need explicit ownership and cleanup.</p></details>`,
  "0247": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the revision inside one validated argument</figcaption>
<p>inspectRevision accepts only an entire lowercase hexadecimal SHA-1 abbreviation or full value, 7 to 40 characters long.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>abcdef0<br>Passes syntax check<br>One Git argument</div><div>abcdef0 followed by a newline<br>Full-input check fails</div><div>Text with shell punctuation<br>Fails revision syntax check<br>No shell requested</div></div>
<details><summary>Does valid hexadecimal syntax prove the revision exists?</summary><p>No. Git can still reject an unknown or ambiguous revision. The child-process error must be handled.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does aborting the call prove all child effects were rolled back?</summary><p>No. Cancellation sends a signal. Process-tree containment and rollback are separate concerns.</p></details>`,
  "0248": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Accept one worker outcome and finish cleanup</figcaption>
<p>The worker protocol promises one result. Compare a result message with an exit before any message.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Message first<br>Mark settled<br>Remove abort listener<br>Terminate, then resolve</div><div>Exit first<br>Mark settled<br>Terminate, then reject<br>worker exited before result</div></div>
<details><summary>Does exit code zero count as a successful result without a message?</summary><p>No. This protocol requires a result message. A clean early exit still violates it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can this wrapper be used unchanged for a worker that streams many results?</summary><p>No. It terminates after the first outcome. Streaming needs a different lifecycle contract.</p></details>`,
  "0249": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Cap the cluster's process count</figcaption>
<p>Use two hypothetical machines to trace the minimum calculation.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Available parallelism: 2<br>min(2, 4)<br>Fork 2 workers</div><div>Available parallelism: 8<br>min(8, 4)<br>Fork 4 workers</div><div>Worker exit<br>Log outcome<br>Replace only when not shutting down</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this snippet implement a complete service lifecycle?</summary><p>No. startServer and shuttingDown belong to the surrounding application and must be supplied.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should a platform-managed replica automatically create another replica layer inside it?</summary><p>No. Decide who owns scaling, restart and resource limits. Independent platform replicas may already supply the required behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0250": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A lost acknowledgment repeats the effect</figcaption>
<p>The initial successful job leaves effects at 1. The ambiguous job adds 1, then throws after its effect on each attempt.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before ambiguous job<br>effects: 1</div><div>Attempt 1<br>Add 1, then throw<br>effects: 2</div><div>Attempt 2<br>Add 1, then throw<br>effects: 3</div><div>Attempt 3<br>Add 1, then throw<br>effects: 4</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does the local seen set not prevent these repeats?</summary><p>The key is recorded only after processJob succeeds. Every attempt throws after changing the external effect counter.</p></details>
<details><summary>What happens to the earlier same-key job with amount 100?</summary><p>It is silently skipped because the key was already seen. The queue fails to reject a changed intent under the same key.</p></details>`,
  "0251": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Test cancellation at the adapter boundary</figcaption>
<p>The fetch mock receives the controller's signal. The test aborts it while the first call is pending, then calls again with that already-aborted signal.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First call<br>Register abort listener<br>Reject on test abort</div><div>Second call<br>Mock invoked again<br>throwIfAborted rejects</div><div>Expected mock count<br>2 calls</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the second call prove fetch was never invoked?</summary><p>No. The assertion expects two invocations. The mock checks the already-aborted signal inside the second call.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this test verify real network cancellation?</summary><p>No. It verifies signal propagation and rejection with an adapter fake. Real transport behavior needs separate evidence.</p></details>`,
  "0252": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare exact origins instead of matching text prefixes</figcaption>
<p>The allowlist contains only https://api.example.com.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>https://api.example.com/items<br>Exact allowed origin<br>Accept</div><div>https://api.example.com.evil.test<br>Different host<br>Reject</div><div>https://api.example.com:444<br>Different port<br>Reject</div><div>URL with credentials<br>Reject even on allowed host</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this check make automatic redirects safe?</summary><p>No. Disable redirects or validate each destination in the HTTP adapter.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a lexical allowlist solve DNS rebinding by itself?</summary><p>No. Enforce destination restrictions at connection time or controlled egress as well.</p></details>`,
  "0253": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Correlate start and finish when the operation fails</figcaption>
<p>Suppose observeRequest runs request r1 and the operation rejects.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request context<br>requestId: r1</div><div>Start event<br>request.start<br>Subscriber adds r1</div><div>Failure and finally<br>request.finish with elapsed time<br>Subscriber adds r1</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a finish event mean the request succeeded?</summary><p>No. finally also runs on rejection. The current event contains elapsed time but no explicit success field.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should every request ID become a metric label?</summary><p>No. Unbounded IDs create high-cardinality metrics. Keep correlation in suitable logs or traces and bound metric labels.</p></details>`,
  "0237": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Drain an HTTP/2 session, then enforce a deadline</figcaption>
<p>Suppose one active session has a stalled stream when shutdown begins.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Begin shutdown<br>draining: true<br>Stop accepting new connections</div><div>Existing session<br>Request graceful close<br>Wait for its stream</div><div>After drain deadline<br>Reject shutdown promise<br>Destroy remaining sessions</div></div>
<details><summary>What happens to a session reported after draining starts?</summary><p>The session handler destroys it instead of adding it to the active set.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do two calls to shutdown start two independent drains?</summary><p>No. The cached shutdownPromise gives both callers the same operation and outcome.</p></details>`,
  "0238": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Resolve an internal import through the package map</figcaption>
<p>The package maps #config to ./dist/config.js. Its ESM entry imports config through that name.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Import specifier<br>#config</div><div>Package imports map<br>./dist/config.js</div><div>Loaded module<br>Provides config<br>Has its own module URL</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the mapping create dist/config.js?</summary><p>No. The file must exist in the artifact. Resolution configuration does not build the implementation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why test from the packed package?</summary><p>A source checkout can contain files that are missing from publication. The consumer must resolve the graph actually shipped.</p></details>`,
  "0239": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A cycle can read an export before initialization finishes</figcaption>
<p>Hypothetical CommonJS fixture: A requires B, and B requires A while A is still evaluating.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>A starts<br>Enters require cache<br>Begins evaluation</div><div>A loads B<br>B requires A</div><div>B observes A<br>May see partial exports<br>A has not finished</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the cache prove A has completed initialization?</summary><p>No. CommonJS caches the module before evaluation finishes, which allows cycles to observe partial exports.</p></details>
<details><summary>How does a module replace the object returned to require callers?</summary><p>Assign module.exports. Reassigning the local exports alias does not replace that exported object.</p></details>`,
  "0240": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two package entry points can create two state owners</figcaption>
<p>The package directs import to dist/index.js and require to dist/index.cjs.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>ESM consumer<br>Loads index.js</div><div>CommonJS consumer<br>Loads index.cjs</div><div>Shared-state question<br>Do both reach one implementation<br>or create independent instances?</div></div>
<details><summary>Do matching function names prove both consumers share one cache?</summary><p>No. Separate implementations can hold separate state. Test the intended identity and state behavior.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is testing only the ESM import enough for this package?</summary><p>No. The exports map advertises both consumption paths. Check both from the published artifact.</p></details>`,
  "0241": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Strip annotations, then execute ordinary JavaScript</figcaption>
<p>The example calls label with id p1 and name Demo.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>TypeScript description<br>Project has string id and name</div><div>Runtime operation<br>Join id, colon and name</div><div>Output<br>p1: Demo</div></div>
<details><summary>Does running the file perform the same checks as tsc?</summary><p>No. Type stripping removes supported annotations without type-checking the program. Run the separate compiler check for that evidence.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will a tsconfig paths alias be applied by the runtime just because it type-checks?</summary><p>No. Direct execution ignores tsconfig. Runtime module resolution needs a supported configuration of its own.</p></details>`,
  "0242": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Inspect three different package contracts</figcaption>
<p>The package manifest, lockfile and tarball answer different questions.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Manifest range<br>Which versions are allowed?</div><div>Lockfile graph<br>Which versions and integrity values were resolved?</div><div>Packed artifact<br>Which files reach the consumer?</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a complete lockfile prove required dist files are packaged?</summary><p>No. Inspect the packed file list and test the artifact's public imports.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does ignoring lifecycle scripts mean the package is safe to execute later?</summary><p>No. It limits installation-time script execution. Review provenance and the code used at runtime as separate risks.</p></details>`,
  "0243": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject invalid startup configuration before serving</figcaption>
<p>Suppose PORT is zero, SHUTDOWN_MS is negative and DATABASE_URL is absent.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>PORT check<br>Reject zero</div><div>Shutdown check<br>Reject negative value</div><div>Database check<br>Required value absent</div><div>Startup result<br>Aggregate errors<br>Set exit code 78</div></div>
<details><summary>Why collect these errors before throwing?</summary><p>The operator can see several configuration problems in one startup attempt.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a syntactically valid database URL prove the database is reachable?</summary><p>No. URL parsing checks representation. Connectivity, credentials and allowed destinations need separate checks; do not log the secret URL.</p></details>`,
  "0244": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the original failure inside a service-level error</figcaption>
<p>The dependency fixture throws controlled fixture failure. The catch wraps it as a payments DependencyError.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original error<br>controlled fixture failure</div><div>Wrapper<br>dependency unavailable<br>Code: DEPENDENCY_UNAVAILABLE<br>Service: payments</div><div>Cause link<br>Original error retained</div></div>
<details><summary>Does replacing the message have to discard the original failure?</summary><p>No. Passing cause preserves the lower-level error for diagnosis.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the code retry the payment dependency?</summary><p>No. It throws the wrapper. Retry policy needs a separate decision about safety and failure classification.</p></details>`,
  "0245": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Observe a fatal error without swallowing it</figcaption>
<p>The isolated fixture rethrows a DependencyError. Its uncaughtExceptionMonitor listener only records the failure.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Unhandled failure<br>DependencyError with cause</div><div>Monitor<br>Record error<br>Do not recover application state</div><div>Process outcome<br>Expected nonzero exit<br>Supervisor owns restart policy</div></div>
<details><summary>Is writing the error to a log a recovery strategy?</summary><p>No. Logging supplies evidence. It does not establish that process state is safe to continue using.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why not only set exitCode in a swallowing handler?</summary><p>Open servers can keep the process alive. A crash policy must account for live resources and actual termination.</p></details>`,
  "0231": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Resolve a name against the upload root</figcaption>
<p>The root is /srv/uploads. Compare a nested filename with a parent-directory escape.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>tenant/file.txt<br>Inside /srv/uploads<br>Accepted lexically</div><div>../secret.txt<br>Outside /srv/uploads<br>Rejected</div><div>Root itself<br>Empty relative path<br>Rejected</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an accepted path prove the opened file stays inside the root?</summary><p>No. A symlink or concurrent directory change can alter where the filesystem resolves it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would this check accept an ordinary filename beginning with two dots?</summary><p>No. The startsWith check rejects it conservatively, even when it is not a parent-directory component. The policy is stricter than lexical escape detection alone.</p></details>`,
  "0232": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Preserve a partial line between socket chunks</figcaption>
<p>Suppose the first chunk contains hi followed by a newline and then par. The next chunk contains t followed by a newline.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First chunk<br>Complete line: hi<br>Retain par</div><div>Second chunk<br>Append t and newline<br>Complete line: part</div><div>Echo sequence<br>hi<br>part</div></div>
<details><summary>Can each data event be treated as one application message?</summary><p>No. TCP chunks can contain partial messages or several messages. This protocol uses newline framing.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if write returns false while more complete lines remain buffered?</summary><p>The pump stops and pauses reads. drain resumes processing the pending lines before normal reading continues.</p></details>`,
  "0233": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Ask which resolver path produced the answer</figcaption>
<p>The example makes three different name-service requests for example.com.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>lookup<br>OS resolver path<br>Address results</div><div>resolve4<br>DNS query<br>IPv4 records and TTLs</div><div>resolveMx<br>DNS query<br>Mail-exchange records</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a TTL returned by resolve4 create an application cache?</summary><p>No. The application must implement its cache policy if it needs one.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does setting the default result order after these calls change their already returned results?</summary><p>No. The setting cannot retroactively reorder completed calls. Record the configuration in effect when a lookup runs.</p></details>`,
  "0234": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Negotiate TLS before treating the socket as secure</figcaption>
<p>The client connects to example.com, sends that server name and offers h2 and http/1.1 through ALPN.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Connection options<br>Certificate verification enabled<br>Two application protocols offered</div><div>Secure connection event<br>Inspect authorization<br>Inspect selected ALPN protocol</div><div>Timeout event<br>Destroy socket explicitly</div></div>
<details><summary>Does offering h2 prove it was negotiated?</summary><p>No. Inspect the negotiated value. An offer and the server's selection are different facts.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why destroy the socket in the timeout handler?</summary><p>The timeout notification alone does not perform this cleanup. The handler makes the shutdown policy explicit.</p></details>`,
  "0235": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Distinguish malformed JSON from an invalid project shape</figcaption>
<p>Send requests to POST /projects using the shown echo handler.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Malformed JSON<br>Parsing fails<br>Intended status: 400</div><div>Valid JSON with name 7<br>Shape check fails<br>Intended status: 422</div><div>Name with spaces around Demo<br>Return trimmed Demo<br>Status: 200</div></div>
<details><summary>Does status 200 mean a project was stored?</summary><p>No. This handler validates and echoes a name. It contains no persistence operation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is an oversized-body response guaranteed to reach the client as 413?</summary><p>No. Early async-iterator exit can destroy the request stream. Test actual socket behavior and ingress limits.</p></details>`,
  "0236": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count received bytes rather than trusting the header</figcaption>
<p>For a small fixture, set maxBytes to 8. The response delivers chunks of 5 and 4 bytes.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First chunk<br>Accumulated: 5<br>Within limit</div><div>Second chunk<br>Accumulated: 9<br>Exceeds 8</div><div>Failure path<br>Reject response<br>Attempt body cancellation</div></div>
<details><summary>Would a Content-Length header of 5 make the response acceptable?</summary><p>No. The helper counts the bytes actually exposed by the response body.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the deadline interrupt JSON.parse while it is running synchronously?</summary><p>No. Cancellation is cooperative. Keep the input bounded and account for synchronous processing cost.</p></details>`,
  "0222": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">End periodic work through a shared abort signal</figcaption>
<p>The interval requests ticks every 100 ms. A separate timeout requests shutdown after 250 ms.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Periodic loop<br>Await tick<br>Await runOnce</div><div>Deadline settles<br>Abort controller</div><div>Cleanup<br>Observe task and deadline settlement</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the configuration guarantee exactly two iterations?</summary><p>No. Timer delays are thresholds. Event-loop work and scheduling can delay callbacks.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can slow runOnce calls overlap in this loop?</summary><p>No. Each call is awaited. Interval ticks can still accumulate; a work-then-timeout loop gives different fixed-delay behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0223": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Change a view without changing an earlier copy</figcaption>
<p>The frame contains the UTF-8 payload hello. view shares the payload bytes; copy owns separate bytes.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before mutation<br>Frame payload: hello<br>Copy: hello</div><div>Set view byte 0 to 0x48<br>Shared first byte becomes H</div><div>After mutation<br>Frame payload: Hello<br>View: Hello<br>Copy: hello</div></div>
<details><summary>Does decodeFrame return another shared view?</summary><p>No. Buffer.from copies the selected payload bytes. Later caller mutations do not change that decoded payload.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would a length prefix of 6 match this five-byte payload?</summary><p>No. The exact frame-length check rejects the mismatch.</p></details>`,
  "0224": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read the frame length in the declared byte order</figcaption>
<p>The five-byte hello payload has a four-byte big-endian length prefix.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Prefix bytes<br>00 00 00 05</div><div>Big-endian read<br>Length: 5</div><div>Frame bytes<br>4 prefix + 5 payload<br>Total: 9</div><div>Exact ArrayBuffer copy<br>Slice from byteOffset<br>Take byteLength bytes</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not pass frame.buffer and assume it contains only the frame?</summary><p>A Buffer can occupy only part of its backing ArrayBuffer. Respect its offset and length.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would a little-endian read interpret the prefix as 5?</summary><p>No. It would interpret these bytes as 83,886,080. The protocol's byte order is part of the contract.</p></details>`,
  "0225": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep a UTF-8 character intact across byte chunks</figcaption>
<p>The fixture sends node streams café one byte at a time. The é uses two UTF-8 bytes.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First é byte<br>C3<br>Decoder retains incomplete character</div><div>Second é byte<br>A9<br>Decoder completes é</div><div>Uppercase output<br>NODE STREAMS CAFÉ</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not decode each byte as an independent string?</summary><p>A multibyte character can cross chunk boundaries. Independent decoding loses the incomplete character state.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does StringDecoder reject all malformed UTF-8?</summary><p>No. It substitutes malformed input. Use a separate strict-validation policy where required.</p></details>`,
  "0226": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Stop consuming before producing a million records</figcaption>
<p>The source can yield one million numbered lines, but the consumer aborts and breaks after its first received chunk.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Source<br>Produces numbered byte chunks</div><div>Consumer<br>Read first chunk<br>Abort and break</div><div>Cleanup<br>Source finally runs<br>Readable is destroyed</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the source necessarily generate only one numbered line?</summary><p>No. Buffering can read ahead, and byte chunks need not match logical records. Do not infer exact production counts from one loop iteration.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is a highWaterMark a limit on the total file size?</summary><p>No. It guides buffering. This stream can represent much more data over its lifetime.</p></details>`,
  "0227": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Pause when the writable queue reaches its threshold</figcaption>
<p>The producer writes 1,024-byte chunks into a sink with a 16,384-byte highWaterMark.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>write returns true<br>Producer may continue</div><div>write returns false<br>Chunk accepted<br>Pause further writes</div><div>drain event<br>Resume production</div><div>After end<br>Wait for finish</div></div>
<details><summary>Should a false-returning chunk be written again after drain?</summary><p>No. It was already accepted. Resending it would duplicate data.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why is finish needed after the write loop?</summary><p>Queued writes may still be processing. Ending input and completing the writable are separate events.</p></details>`,
  "0228": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Publish compressed output only after the pipeline succeeds</figcaption>
<p>gzipFile writes into its own staging directory beside the target.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Pipeline running<br>Source → gzip → staged output</div><div>Pipeline succeeds<br>Rename staged output to target</div><div>Pipeline fails<br>Do not publish staged output<br>Remove owned staging directory</div></div>
<details><summary>Can a failed pipeline leave a partially compressed replacement at the target through this rename path?</summary><p>The rename occurs only after pipeline resolves successfully. A pipeline failure skips that publication step.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does successful rename prove survival across power loss?</summary><p>No. Crash durability depends on synchronization and filesystem behavior beyond this example.</p></details>`,
  "0229": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check the boundary before connecting stream APIs</figcaption>
<p>The supplied compression starter uses Node streams throughout. To extend it with a fetch body, first identify that body's Web Stream contract.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Existing source<br>Node file Readable</div><div>Existing middle and sink<br>Node gzip Transform<br>Node file Writable</div><div>Proposed source<br>Web response body<br>Check supported adapter and ownership</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the current starter demonstrate a Web Stream conversion?</summary><p>No. It demonstrates Node pipeline composition. The interoperability extension needs its own implementation and checks.</p></details>
<details><summary>What must a conversion preserve besides the bytes?</summary><p>Check backpressure, cancellation and error propagation across the boundary. An adapter must not leave the original response body consuming resources after failure.</p></details>`,
  "0230": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two calls in one process can collide on the temporary name</figcaption>
<p>Two concurrent calls target state.json in the same process. Both derive the temporary name from that path and the process ID.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Call A<br>Exclusive open succeeds</div><div>Call B<br>Same temporary name exists<br>Exclusive open fails with EEXIST</div><div>Call A continues<br>Write, sync, close<br>Rename to target</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the exclusive flag coordinate both writes into a successful sequence?</summary><p>No. It prevents overwriting the existing temporary file. The losing call fails; this fixture supplies no retry or multi-writer policy.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would checking that the file does not exist before opening solve the race?</summary><p>No. Another call can create it between the check and open. Use the required open flags as the authoritative operation.</p></details>`,
  "0214": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare the executable that each environment actually runs</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: the project works on a laptop but fails in its deployment image. Capture the same evidence object in both places.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Laptop evidence<br>Node version<br>Executable path<br>Platform and architecture</div><div>Deployment evidence<br>Node version<br>Executable path<br>Platform and architecture</div><div>Comparison<br>Locate differences before reproducing</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an engines range prove both machines use the same Node release?</summary><p>No. Record the actual version and executable. A supported range can contain several different releases.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should this evidence object be published without inspection?</summary><p>No. Paths and command arguments can expose environment details or sensitive inputs. Redact before sharing.</p></details>`,
  "0215": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Three asynchronous calls use different underlying resources</figcaption>
<p>The example starts password derivation, a file read and a fetch before awaiting all three.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>pbkdf2<br>Native crypto work<br>Shared worker pool</div><div>readFile<br>File work<br>Shared worker pool</div><div>fetch<br>Network I/O<br>OS readiness machinery</div><div>Promise.all<br>Wait for all results<br>Does not create three JavaScript threads</div></div>
<details><summary>Does using async guarantee these operations cannot delay each other?</summary><p>No. Operations can compete for shared resources, including the worker pool and CPU.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the single elapsed value identify which operation was slow?</summary><p>No. It measures the combined wait. Collect per-operation evidence to locate a bottleneck.</p></details>`,
  "0216": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate the guaranteed pair from the top-level race</figcaption>
<p>The file-read callback schedules both an immediate and a zero-delay timer.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Inside I/O callback<br>Schedule immediate<br>Schedule timer</div><div>Asserted relationship<br>I/O immediate before I/O timer</div><div>Top-level pair<br>No fixed relative order asserted</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the example require the top-level timer to run first?</summary><p>No. Its assertion deliberately avoids that ordering claim.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why keep the I/O scheduling location in the explanation?</summary><p>It is part of the condition supporting the immediate-before-timer relationship. Moving those calls changes the experiment.</p></details>`,
  "0217": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A nextTick chain can keep yielding back to itself</figcaption>
<p>unsafeLoop starts with ticks 0. Each call increments the count and schedules another nextTick while the count is below 100,000.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Initial call<br>ticks: 1<br>Schedule nextTick</div><div>Repeated callbacks<br>Keep adding nextTick work</div><div>Final call<br>ticks: 100,000<br>No further callback queued</div><div>Cost<br>I/O can be delayed while the chain drains</div></div>
<details><summary>Does making each callback short remove starvation risk?</summary><p>No. An unbroken sequence of short callbacks can still delay other work.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should replace this scheduling pattern for a large job?</summary><p>Process a bounded amount of work, then yield through an appropriate scheduler such as setImmediate. Do not assume every microtask or nextTick yield gives I/O a turn.</p></details>`,
  "0218": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Twelve submissions do not imply twelve available workers</figcaption>
<p>The example submits 12 password-derivation jobs to the shared pool.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Application submissions<br>12 jobs admitted together</div><div>Shared pool<br>Available workers run jobs<br>Other jobs wait</div><div>Measurements<br>Total elapsed time<br>Event-loop delay percentile</div></div>
<details><summary>Can requests wait for workers even if event-loop delay looks acceptable?</summary><p>Yes. Pool queueing and event-loop delay are different measurements.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does increasing pool size remove all resource limits?</summary><p>No. CPU and other shared resources still limit throughput. Bound admitted work and measure before changing the pool.</p></details>`,
  "0219": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use only the first callback result</figcaption>
<p>Suppose an operation calls back successfully with 42, then calls back again with an error.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First callback<br>settled: false<br>Set true<br>Resolve 42</div><div>Second callback<br>settled: true<br>Return without changing result</div></div>
<details><summary>What value does the returned Promise keep?</summary><p>42. The later callback is ignored by the wrapper.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does exactlyOnce make the operation's external effects happen once?</summary><p>No. It controls callback settlement at this boundary. It cannot undo repeated writes or other effects inside the operation.</p></details>`,
  "0220": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Register before emitting closed</figcaption>
<p>The example registers a one-time closed listener and creates a Promise with once before emitting closed.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before emission<br>One-time log listener ready<br>Promise listener ready</div><div>Emit closed<br>Synchronous listener dispatch<br>Log once and resolve waiter</div><div>After emission<br>Await can complete</div></div>
<details><summary>Would creating the waiter after emission observe the old event?</summary><p>No. EventEmitter does not replay that past emission to a new listener.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why pass the same onReady function to off?</summary><p>Removal identifies the registered function. A newly created function with the same body is a different listener.</p></details>`,
  "0221": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Carry a request ID through asynchronous work</figcaption>
<p>Call the generated request ID R for this illustration. handle awaits a Promise and a timer before logging.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>context.run<br>Store requestId R</div><div>Async continuation<br>Promise and timer work created in scope</div><div>handled log<br>Read requestId R</div><div>Explicit custom scope<br>Read requestId custom</div></div>
<details><summary>Does log need a requestId argument on every call?</summary><p>Not in this example. It reads the active async context.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a correlation ID establish who is authorized?</summary><p>No. It helps connect observations. Authentication and authorization need their own trusted inputs and checks.</p></details>`,
  "0203": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A render proposal can be replaced before commit</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: a non-urgent results update is in progress when the user types another character.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Results proposal A<br>Render work in progress</div><div>Urgent input update B<br>New work needs attention</div><div>Committed UI<br>Inspect the actual displayed state<br>Do not infer it from render logs alone</div></div>
<details><summary>Can a log inside render prove that version appeared on screen?</summary><p>No. Render work can be restarted or discarded before commit.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the exercise assert an exact number of render calls?</summary><p>No. Observe committed behavior and correctness. Fiber scheduling details do not promise one render per update.</p></details>`,
  "0204": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Match the first client view to the server HTML</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: the server sends count 2, but the client's first render uses count 3.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Server HTML<br>Count: 2</div><div>Initial client view<br>Count: 3</div><div>Hydration requirement<br>Initial content must agree<br>Repair the data mismatch</div></div>
<details><summary>Does suppressing the warning make the two initial states agree?</summary><p>No. Find why the server and client used different values and correct that boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should be checked when unmounting the root?</summary><p>Verify cleanup of owned subscriptions and resources. A removed DOM tree alone does not prove that external work stopped.</p></details>`,
  "0205": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Distinguish delivered HTML from a working control</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: the server can send a page shell while its comments region waits for data.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Shell received<br>Visible page structure</div><div>Delayed region<br>Later streamed content, if supported</div><div>Client interaction<br>Hydration and handlers must be ready</div></div>
<details><summary>Does seeing the shell prove its buttons work?</summary><p>No. Check when the controls become usable in the chosen framework.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can this diagram establish actual response-chunk timing?</summary><p>No. Record the response and client behavior in the exercise. The diagram separates concepts; it supplies no measured timings.</p></details>`,
  "0206": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A local like count does not update the database</figcaption>
<p>Suppose the server reads 4 likes. It passes 4 to LikeButton, and the user clicks once.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Server data<br>article.likes: 4</div><div>Client initial state<br>likes: 4</div><div>After click<br>Local likes: 5<br>No persistence call in this example</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this click prove the article now has 5 stored likes?</summary><p>No. The counter is local state. A real mutation needs persistence and authorization.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why sanitize the rendered article HTML on the server?</summary><p>The HTML enters a raw-HTML rendering path. Keeping work on the server does not make untrusted content safe.</p></details>`,
  "0207": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Authorize the project after validating the form</figcaption>
<p>Suppose the session user is u1 and the requested project belongs to u2. The form fields are otherwise valid.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Session<br>Authenticated u1</div><div>Parsed form<br>Valid project ID</div><div>Ownership check<br>u2 differs from u1<br>Return Not found<br>No update</div></div>
<details><summary>Does use server make every caller authorized?</summary><p>No. The function still checks the session and ownership before writing.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does passing requestId prove retries are deduplicated?</summary><p>No. The database adapter must enforce the intended idempotency contract. This call site only passes the key.</p></details>`,
  "0208": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the visible record aligned with the active route</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: navigate from project p1 to p2 while p1's data request is still pending.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Old route<br>p1<br>Request pending</div><div>Active route<br>p2<br>Load and display p2</div><div>Late p1 response<br>Must not replace p2's view</div></div>
<details><summary>What should Back restore for a URL-backed filter?</summary><p>The filter represented by the restored URL, according to the documented route policy. Test this instead of keeping a conflicting hidden copy.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should hovering one destination preload every project?</summary><p>No. Define a bounded preload policy. Each speculative request consumes resources and may handle sensitive data.</p></details>`,
  "0209": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check the protocol before using the Continue URL</figcaption>
<p>The standalone Continue link accepts only http and https protocols after URL parsing.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>https://example.test/help<br>Protocol https:<br>Accepted by shown check</div><div>javascript:alert(1)<br>Protocol javascript:<br>Rejected</div><div>Relative /help<br>Resolved against location.origin<br>Then protocol checked</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this protocol check restrict links to the same host?</summary><p>No. An external HTTPS destination passes. Add a host policy if the application requires one.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does checking Continue's URL sanitize the separate HTML body?</summary><p>No. The HTML goes through its own sanitizer configuration. Both boundaries need their own checks.</p></details>`,
  "0210": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use the source map for the failing build</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: an error report identifies build A, while the developer's checkout has advanced to build B.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Failure evidence<br>Build A<br>Redacted component stack</div><div>Matching source map<br>Build A<br>Locate the original source</div><div>Candidate fix<br>Reproduce failure<br>Add regression check</div></div>
<details><summary>Can build B's source map be assumed to decode build A correctly?</summary><p>No. Keep map and artifact identities aligned.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the report include all component props for convenience?</summary><p>No. Props may contain sensitive data. Capture the minimum safe context needed to reproduce the interaction.</p></details>`,
  "0211": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Replace the connection when the room changes</figcaption>
<p>The class mounts with room A, receives room B, then unmounts.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Mount A<br>Create and connect A</div><div>Update to B<br>Disconnect A<br>Create and connect B</div><div>Unmount<br>Disconnect B</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an unrelated prop update reconnect the room?</summary><p>Not through componentDidUpdate here. It calls synchronize only when roomId changes.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will the displayed status become connected in this example?</summary><p>No. The state starts as idle and no code updates it. Connection lifecycle and status reporting are separate responsibilities.</p></details>`,
  "0212": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep a server refresh from silently replacing a draft</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: the cached project name is Demo. The user edits a local draft to Demo revised while a server refresh arrives.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Server cache<br>Latest saved project</div><div>Form draft<br>Demo revised<br>Unsaved user work</div><div>Refresh decision<br>Reconcile explicitly<br>Do not silently overwrite the draft</div></div>
<details><summary>Should both values be copied into one global field?</summary><p>That would erase the distinction between saved data and unsaved intent. Give each a clear owner and an explicit reconciliation rule.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should opening two feature instances test?</summary><p>Check whether each draft is independent as intended and whether shared server data updates consistently.</p></details>`,
  "0213": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Turn a working search page into evidence</figcaption>
<p>Capstone fixture: a lesson search page receives slow, empty, failed and out-of-order responses from a controlled backend.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Slow or empty<br>Inspect pending and empty states</div><div>Failure<br>Inspect recovery path</div><div>Out-of-order responses<br>Keep current query visible</div><div>Keyboard and small screen<br>Record usability evidence</div></div>
<details><summary>Can a successful page render establish production quality?</summary><p>No. Supply the relevant tests, interaction evidence and architecture decisions, including known limits.</p></details>
<details><summary>How should a fake backend appear in the portfolio description?</summary><p>Label it as mocked and state what it demonstrates. Do not describe a local fixture as a verified production integration.</p></details>`,
  "0191": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Show a comment before the save completes</figcaption>
<p>Submit the comment Hello. The optimistic updater adds a temporary item to the displayed list.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before submit<br>Authoritative comments</div><div>Pending action<br>Temporary Hello item<br>pending: true<br>Button: Sending…</div><div>After settlement<br>Reconcile with authoritative comments<br>Show an error if supplied by action state</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the temporary item prove the server saved Hello?</summary><p>No. It represents the expected result while the action is pending. The authoritative result must settle the UI.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the fixed pending ID sufficient for multiple simultaneous comments?</summary><p>No. Concurrent optimistic items need distinct identities and a reconciliation policy. This small example does not supply that policy.</p></details>`,
  "0192": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read text state and a selected file from different places</figcaption>
<p>The user types Ada and chooses avatar.png before submitting.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Name input<br>onChange updates state<br>name: Ada</div><div>File input<br>Browser holds selected file<br>FormData supplies avatar</div><div>Submission<br>saveProfile receives name and avatar</div></div>
<details><summary>Does changing the name state select a file?</summary><p>No. The file input is uncontrolled. Its selection comes from the browser's file control.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does accept image/* prove an upload is a safe image?</summary><p>No. It guides file selection. The upload boundary still needs content, size and security checks.</p></details>`,
  "0193": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep search B visible when A finishes late</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: enter A, then B. Configure the fake fetch so B resolves first.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Request A<br>Starts first<br>Still pending</div><div>Request B<br>Starts second<br>Resolves first<br>Display B</div><div>Late A result<br>Older request<br>Do not replace B</div></div>
<details><summary>What should the learner assert after A finally resolves?</summary><p>The UI still displays B's result. This is the expected assertion for the exercise, not an executed browser result.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is aborting A enough to remove every stale-result risk?</summary><p>No. Cancellation and stale-result exclusion are separate concerns. Keep a rule that permits only the current request to update visible results.</p></details>`,
  "0194": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate waiting for a panel from failing to load it</figcaption>
<p>Dashboard tries to render the lazy AnalyticsPanel module.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Module pending<br>PanelSkeleton</div><div>Module loads<br>AnalyticsPanel</div><div>Rendered lazy load rejects<br>PanelError through ErrorBoundary</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a failed pointer-enter preload immediately display PanelError?</summary><p>No. The optional preload catches its own failure. The rendered boundary owns errors encountered when rendering the lazy component.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does Suspense itself provide the retry policy?</summary><p>No. It supplies the waiting fallback. Error recovery needs an explicit boundary and reset strategy.</p></details>`,
  "0195": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Send each failure to its owner</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: a results panel can throw during rendering, and its Retry button can fail inside its event handler.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Render failure<br>Panel error boundary<br>Show panel fallback</div><div>Retry-handler failure<br>Handle in the event/action path<br>Report a usable error</div><div>Unrelated sidebar<br>Keep its current state</div></div>
<details><summary>Should the test expect the boundary to catch the button handler's error automatically?</summary><p>No. Event-handler errors need handling in their own execution path.</p></details>
<details><summary>What must change before resetting a persistently failing panel?</summary><p>Remove or correct the failure condition, or choose a recovery path that does so. A reset that immediately repeats the same throw creates a retry loop.</p></details>`,
  "0196": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep React ownership while moving the dialog in the DOM</figcaption>
<p>The parent mounts Modal while open. The portal places its dialog under document.body.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>React tree<br>Parent owns Modal<br>Context and event ancestry remain here</div><div>DOM placement<br>dialog under body<br>showModal opens it</div><div>Close request<br>onClose updates parent<br>Modal unmounts</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a portal alone prevent interaction with the background?</summary><p>No. This example uses native showModal behavior for modality. The portal changes placement.</p></details>
<details><summary>What happens when Escape produces a cancel event?</summary><p>The handler prevents the default and calls onClose. The parent must update its open state so the component unmounts as intended.</p></details>`,
  "0197": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check a button's types and keyboard behavior separately</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: a Save button exposes a button ref and a click callback.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Correct button ref<br>Accept at compile time</div><div>Input-element ref<br>Reject at compile time</div><div>Disabled Save button<br>Verify no action through user interaction</div></div>
<details><summary>Does casting an input ref to a button ref repair the contract?</summary><p>No. It hides the mismatch. Keep a negative type test that requires the wrong ref to fail.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does passing the type checks prove keyboard usability?</summary><p>No. Exercise focus and activation in the runtime UI as a separate acceptance check.</p></details>`,
  "0198": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep a long search label usable on a narrow screen</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: replace Search with Search all project documents, narrow the viewport and zoom to 200%.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Long label<br>Allow wrapping<br>Retain accessible association</div><div>Search input and button<br>Remain reachable<br>Do not cover each other</div><div>Keyboard focus<br>Visible on each control</div></div>
<details><summary>Is clipping the label a successful responsive result?</summary><p>No. The fixture checks that the full content and controls remain usable when text grows.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a screenshot at normal zoom establish this result?</summary><p>No. Inspect the changed conditions and keyboard focus explicitly. The lesson supplies an acceptance exercise, not a completed visual test.</p></details>`,
  "0199": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Search without moving focus to every status update</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: enter Ada with the keyboard, submit and receive three results.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input<br>Accessible name: Search</div><div>Submit<br>Start request<br>Follow the chosen focus policy</div><div>Status region<br>Announce the result summary<br>Avoid reading every result repeatedly</div></div>
<details><summary>Does adding aria-live prove the announcement is useful?</summary><p>No. Check the actual spoken feedback with a screen reader, including repeated searches and errors.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should the keyboard test record besides whether submission works?</summary><p>Record where focus goes during loading and after completion, whether it remains visible, and how the user reaches the results or error.</p></details>`,
  "0200": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Control when the form's save promise settles</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: submit a form while a fake save dependency remains pending.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Pending save<br>Assert loading feedback<br>Assert intended disabled controls</div><div>Resolve first save<br>Await visible success</div><div>Reject later save<br>Await visible error</div></div>
<details><summary>Why hold the promise instead of resolving immediately?</summary><p>It gives the test a reliable chance to inspect pending behavior.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does act make a simulated DOM equivalent to a browser?</summary><p>No. It coordinates React updates for tests. Layout, focus and browser integration may still need real-browser checks.</p></details>`,
  "0201": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Measure an unrelated input change before and after optimization</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: a page has an expensive list derivation and a separate note input. Keep the list data unchanged while typing in the note.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Baseline<br>Record the note interaction<br>Inspect list work</div><div>Candidate optimization<br>Reuse derivation when inputs match</div><div>Comparison<br>Same data and interaction<br>Check cost and responsiveness</div></div>
<details><summary>Does fewer renders alone prove the page became faster?</summary><p>No. Compare actual work and responsiveness under equivalent conditions.</p></details>
<details><summary>What result should be written before running the measurement?</summary><p>Only the hypothesis and test conditions. Record measured results afterward; this fixture supplies no benchmark numbers.</p></details>`,
  "0202": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep an adoption experiment separate from a purity failure</figcaption>
<p>Practice fixture: enable the compiler for one list component. Use a separate disposable component that mutates its input during render.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Valid list component<br>Compare the same interaction<br>Record measured effect</div><div>Impure fixture<br>Inspect diagnostics<br>Correct the mutation</div><div>Rollout decision<br>Keep or roll back the scoped change</div></div>
<details><summary>Should compilation make input mutation safe?</summary><p>No. Optimization depends on valid component behavior. Correct the impurity itself.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should every manual memo be removed before measuring?</summary><p>No. Make scoped changes and compare results. Preserve correctness and a clear rollback path.</p></details>`,
  "0181": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Expose two actions through the search ref</figcaption>
<p>SearchPanel holds a ref to the handle created by SearchBox.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Parent handle<br>focus method<br>select method</div><div>Child implementation<br>Private inputRef<br>Calls the input's DOM methods</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the parent handle expose the complete input DOM node?</summary><p>No. useImperativeHandle exposes the object with the two supplied methods.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would changing requestRef.current update the displayed input?</summary><p>No. Ref mutations do not request a render. Use state for values that must drive the rendered UI.</p></details>`,
  "0182": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reject a message from the room you left</figcaption>
<p>Open room A, then switch to room B. A late callback from A arrives after its cleanup.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Room A setup<br>active: true<br>Connect A</div><div>Leave A<br>active: false<br>Abort and disconnect</div><div>Room B<br>New keyed message state</div><div>Late A callback<br>active is false<br>Ignore message</div></div>
<details><summary>Why use both the key and the active guard?</summary><p>The key resets displayed message state for the new room. The guard rejects callbacks after the old effect's cleanup.</p></details>
<details><summary>How many messages remain after appending to a full 100-message list?</summary><p>100. The update keeps the last 99, then appends one. Transport buffering still needs its own bound.</p></details>`,
  "0183": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Measure the tooltip before positioning it above the anchor</figcaption>
<p>Suppose the anchor top is 100 pixels and the measured tooltip height is 20 pixels.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Initial height state<br>0<br>Calculated top: 100</div><div>Layout measurement<br>Height: 20</div><div>Updated position<br>100 − 20<br>Top: 80</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does this measurement use a layout effect?</summary><p>The measurement and resulting update need to happen before the browser paints the tooltip position.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the shown dependency list handle every possible later size change?</summary><p>No. It reruns when children changes. Other size changes need a deliberate observation strategy if the component must track them.</p></details>`,
  "0184": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use the latest theme without reconnecting the room</figcaption>
<p>The room stays A while the theme changes from light to dark. A connected event arrives after the dark theme commits.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Connection dependency<br>roomId A unchanged</div><div>Committed theme<br>dark</div><div>Connected notification<br>Effect Event reads dark</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this theme change require a new connection?</summary><p>No. The effect depends on roomId. The notification reads the latest committed theme through the Effect Event.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should roomId also be removed from the dependencies?</summary><p>No. The connection must change when the room changes. This separation follows the behavior each value controls.</p></details>`,
  "0185": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Subscribe to the browser's online indicator</figcaption>
<p>The custom Hook reads navigator.onLine and subscribes to online and offline events.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Subscribe<br>Add online listener<br>Add offline listener</div><div>Browser event<br>Notify subscriber<br>Read current indicator</div><div>Cleanup<br>Remove both listeners</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an Online debug label prove the API server is reachable?</summary><p>No. It displays the browser's connectivity indicator. An individual request can still fail.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the server snapshot a measurement of the client's network?</summary><p>No. The example returns true as its server snapshot. Treat that as an initial rendering choice, not a reachability test.</p></details>`,
  "0186": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A new array reference can invalidate the cache</figcaption>
<p>Search receives items and query. The ranking cache depends on both.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Same items reference<br>Same query<br>Cached ranking can be reused</div><div>New array, same contents<br>Changed items reference<br>Ranking recalculated</div><div>Changed query<br>Ranking recalculated</div></div>
<details><summary>Does memo compare every item deeply by default?</summary><p>No. New prop references can prevent the intended bailout even when their contents look equal.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the UI become incorrect if React recalculates the memo?</summary><p>No. Memoization is a performance optimization. Keep ranking correct and pure, then use profiling evidence to decide whether the cache helps.</p></details>`,
  "0187": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Let the input advance while results catch up</figcaption>
<p>The user changes the search text from a to ab.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Urgent update<br>Input query becomes ab</div><div>Transition update<br>Schedule filter ab</div><div>Deferred results<br>May still show a while new work is pending<br>Eventually use ab</div></div>
<details><summary>Why keep the controlled input update outside the transition?</summary><p>The typed text should update promptly. The expensive result rendering can tolerate lag.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does deferring the value make the ranking computation itself faster?</summary><p>No. It changes scheduling and permits interruption of rendering. Measure the computation separately if it is too expensive.</p></details>`,
  "0188": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Connect one field to its label and error</figcaption>
<p>Call the generated identifier F for this illustration. With an error present, the error identifier is F-error.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Label<br>htmlFor: F</div><div>Input<br>id: F<br>aria-describedby: F-error</div><div>Error paragraph<br>id: F-error<br>Contains the error text</div></div>
<details><summary>What happens to the error reference when error is absent?</summary><p>errorId becomes undefined and the error paragraph is not rendered. The input no longer references that error paragraph.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should this generated ID replace a record's stable list key?</summary><p>No. useId supports relationships within the UI. Use data identity for list keys.</p></details>`,
  "0189": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Reuse a cart snapshot until the store changes</figcaption>
<p>Suppose the cart contains two items. The store exposes an immutable snapshot S1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First read<br>S1<br>Item count: 2</div><div>Read without change<br>Same S1 reference<br>Item count: 2</div><div>After adding an item<br>New snapshot S2<br>Item count: 3</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not return a fresh object on every unchanged read?</summary><p>The snapshot identity would signal change even when the store had not changed. Cache the immutable snapshot until an actual update.</p></details>
<details><summary>What must the initial server and hydration values agree on?</summary><p>They must describe the same initial cart state. Reconstruct the server snapshot from matching bootstrap data.</p></details>`,
  "0190": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read the comments promise through Suspense</figcaption>
<p>Page starts loading comments and passes that promise to the client Comments component.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Promise pending<br>Comments cannot supply its list yet<br>Suspense can show skeleton</div><div>Promise fulfilled<br>use reads comments<br>Render keyed paragraphs</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the skeleton handle a rejected promise as an error screen?</summary><p>No. Suspense handles waiting. A rejected read needs appropriate error handling, such as an error boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can the client directive sit halfway down the same server module?</summary><p>No. The example marks a separate client module. Its directive must be the first statement.</p></details>`,
  "0171": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Find the mount point before creating a root</figcaption>
<p>The entry file looks for the element whose ID is root.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Element exists<br>Create React root<br>Render App inside StrictMode</div><div>Element absent<br>Throw Missing #root mount point<br>Do not call createRoot</div></div>
<details><summary>Will onRecoverableError handle the missing-element throw?</summary><p>No. That throw happens before createRoot receives its options. Inspect the HTML mount point and entry setup.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is reportError supplied by this snippet?</summary><p>No. Connect that call to the application's reporting implementation. The entry file shows where reporting belongs, not a complete monitoring service.</p></details>`,
  "0172": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Describe an image before React commits it</figcaption>
<p>The JSX describes an img with the user's avatar URL, display name and dimensions of 48 by 48.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>JSX expression<br>img and its props</div><div>Element description<br>type: img<br>width: 48<br>height: 48</div><div>React render and commit<br>Use the description to update the UI</div></div>
<details><summary>Does assigning the JSX to avatar immediately insert an image into the page?</summary><p>No. It creates an element description. React must use that description in the rendered tree.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does freezing the element object freeze the browser image?</summary><p>No. The element description and the DOM node are different objects. Freezing the description is not a browser rendering operation.</p></details>`,
  "0173": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Fill the Card's three named places</figcaption>
<p>The caller supplies the title Delete project, a warning paragraph and DeleteActions.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Heading<br>Delete project<br>ID from useId</div><div>Body<br>This cannot be undone.<br>Passed as children</div><div>Footer<br>DeleteActions<br>Passed as actions</div></div>
<details><summary>How does the section get its accessible label?</summary><p>aria-labelledby points to the heading's generated ID. The visible title supplies the label.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the warning make this Card a modal confirmation dialog?</summary><p>No. The component renders a section. Modal behavior also requires appropriate focus and interaction handling.</p></details>`,
  "0174": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Render the story list twice without growing the input</figcaption>
<p>Suppose stories contains A and B. Each render builds a new array with a Create story entry.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input before render<br>[A, B]</div><div>First render result<br>[A, B, Create story]</div><div>Second render result<br>[A, B, Create story]</div><div>Input afterward<br>[A, B]</div></div>
<details><summary>What would happen if rendering pushed into the stories prop instead?</summary><p>Repeated rendering could append repeated Create story entries to the shared input. Copying the outer array avoids that mutation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does Panel call useState even when disabled?</summary><p>Its Hook calls keep the same order across renders. The conditional return comes after the Hook.</p></details>`,
  "0175": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One click queues two increments</figcaption>
<p>The displayed count starts at 0. The button handler queues two functional updates.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Capture phase<br>Toolbar audit runs</div><div>Button handler<br>Queue +1<br>Queue +1<br>Log snapshot 0</div><div>Processed updates<br>0 → 1 → 2<br>Next displayed count: 2</div></div>
<details><summary>Does stopPropagation undo the toolbar's capture audit?</summary><p>No. The ancestor capture handler already ran before the button handler.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does the handler log 0 after queuing the updates?</summary><p>The handler reads the count from its render snapshot. Queuing updates does not replace that captured value.</p></details>`,
  "0176": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Add three without passing the quantity limit</figcaption>
<p>Start quantity at 98. Each queued updater adds one but caps the result at 99.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Starting state<br>98</div><div>First updater<br>min(99, 99)<br>99</div><div>Second updater<br>min(99, 100)<br>99</div><div>Third updater<br>min(99, 100)<br>99</div></div>
<details><summary>What if quantity starts at 1 instead?</summary><p>The queue produces 2, then 3, then 4. Each updater receives the preceding result.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does addTag mutate the existing tags array?</summary><p>No. It constructs a new tags array and a new draft object while retaining the other draft fields.</p></details>`,
  "0177": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Undo the recorded answer, not each navigation step</figcaption>
<p>Start at step 0 with no answers or history. Dispatch answered with yes, then next, then undo.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>answered yes<br>Step 0<br>Answer 0: yes<br>History holds initial state</div><div>next<br>Step 1<br>Same history</div><div>undo<br>Restore initial state<br>Step 0, no answers</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does undo remove the answer as well as return to step 0?</summary><p>Only answered records a snapshot. The stored snapshot predates that answer. next does not create a history entry.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can this history grow without a limit?</summary><p>Yes. Each answered action retains a previous state. Set a retention policy if a product needs bounded memory use.</p></details>`,
  "0178": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Derive the selection from the latest items</figcaption>
<p>Suppose selectedId is b and the current items are a and b. Then the parent removes b.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before removal<br>Items: a, b<br>Selected object: b</div><div>After removal<br>Items: a<br>No item matches b</div><div>Rendered List props<br>selectedId: null</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this render reset the stored selectedId state?</summary><p>No. It derives a null selected object and passes null to List. The stored ID remains b until an update changes it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why calculate the completed count from items each render?</summary><p>The count follows the current data. A separately stored count would require another synchronization rule when items change.</p></details>`,
  "0179": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A stable card key has a parent boundary</figcaption>
<p>Card c1 has a local draft in column A. Compare a reorder in A with a move to B.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Reorder within A<br>Same parent and key c1<br>Draft state preserved</div><div>Move from A to B<br>Different parent<br>Local card state resets</div><div>Shared draft requirement<br>Store draft above both columns</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a globally unique key preserve state anywhere in the tree?</summary><p>No. React matches identity within the relevant parent position. The cross-column move changes that boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why give AccountForm the account ID as its key?</summary><p>A changed account ID intentionally creates a new form identity, resetting the previous account's local form state.</p></details>`,
  "0180": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read tasks from the nearest provider</figcaption>
<p>TasksContext defaults to null. The custom Hook rejects that value.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Consumer under TasksProvider<br>Reads provided tasks</div><div>Consumer without provider<br>Reads null default<br>Hook throws</div><div>Dispatch consumer<br>Uses separate dispatch context</div></div>
<details><summary>Why is an empty task list different from the null default?</summary><p>An empty list is valid provided data. Null signals that this Hook has no usable provider value.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does splitting the contexts stop every render of dispatch-only components?</summary><p>No. It separates their context subscriptions, but parents and other inputs can still cause renders.</p></details>`,
  "0162": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Test the accepted inputs and the actual result</figcaption>
<p>The first function receives the readonly pair [1, 2]. Its contract requires at least one item.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Positive type check<br>Result type is 1 or 2</div><div>Negative type check<br>Empty tuple must be rejected</div><div>Runtime check<br>Returned value must equal 1</div></div>
<details><summary>Could a function returning 2 pass the stated result-type check?</summary><p>Yes. The type permits either literal. The runtime assertion checks the stronger promise that the function returns the first item.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if a later signature starts accepting empty arrays?</summary><p>The expected-error directive becomes unused and the type check fails. It protects the rejected-input contract.</p></details>`,
  "0163": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Choose declarations for the execution environment</figcaption>
<p>The shared configuration supplies strictness rules. Each environment adds its own platform declarations.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Browser<br>ES2023 and DOM</div><div>Node service<br>ES2023 and Node types</div><div>Worker<br>ES2023 and WebWorker</div></div>
<details><summary>Does adding DOM to a Node configuration create browser APIs?</summary><p>No. lib supplies declarations to the checker. It does not install APIs in the running process.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does choosing a newer target polyfill missing library functions?</summary><p>No. target controls syntax transformation. Confirm that the runtime provides the APIs described by the selected declarations.</p></details>`,
  "0164": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the shared domain behind a declaration boundary</figcaption>
<p>The solution lists a domain package, a server and a web app. Consumers use the domain package's declared contract.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Domain project<br>Composite build<br>Produces declarations</div><div>Server consumer<br>Checks the referenced contract</div><div>Web consumer<br>Checks the referenced contract</div></div>
<details><summary>Does listing three projects at the root declare every dependency between them?</summary><p>No. Configure each consumer's actual references. The build needs the dependency graph to order work correctly.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why inspect build-mode output after changing the domain contract?</summary><p>It shows which projects the compiler rebuilds. Consumers may need checking even when their own source files did not change.</p></details>`,
  "0165": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow the tool's exact matching rule</figcaption>
<p>The traversal reports exported function declarations that have no written return type. It skips declaration files.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Exported function declaration<br>No return annotation<br>Report</div><div>Exported function declaration<br>Return annotation present<br>Skip</div><div>Exported arrow in a variable<br>Not a function declaration<br>Not matched by this rule</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a reported function necessarily have an unknown return type?</summary><p>No. The compiler may infer its type. This tool checks whether an annotation is present.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the traversal enforce the rule for every exported callable?</summary><p>No. Its syntax predicate is narrower. Add explicit handling for other declaration forms if the intended policy includes them.</p></details>`,
  "0166": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Locate the slow phase before changing types</figcaption>
<p>Compare two diagnostic runs of the same project: a baseline and a candidate change. Keep compiler version and measurement conditions fixed.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Inputs to compare<br>Effective configuration<br>Included files<br>Compiler version</div><div>Phase evidence<br>Parse time<br>Bind time<br>Check time</div><div>Follow-up<br>Trace expensive relationships<br>Recheck the candidate</div></div>
<details><summary>Does one fast incremental build prove clean builds improved?</summary><p>No. They perform different work. Compare like-for-like runs and keep both measurements where both matter.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why not begin by disabling declaration checks?</summary><p>That can hide a dependency conflict without explaining it. First inspect which files and relationships account for the work.</p></details>`,
  "0167": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Zero diagnostics is one upgrade result</figcaption>
<p>The review object lists several evidence fields. Its true values are checklist examples, not results from an executed upgrade.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Compiler evidence<br>Exact version<br>Strict diagnostics</div><div>Consumer evidence<br>Published declarations<br>Clean package consumer</div><div>Runtime evidence<br>Regression behavior<br>Boundary checks</div></div>
<details><summary>Can an upgrade be approved from editor diagnostics alone?</summary><p>No. Review changed JavaScript and declarations, then check relevant consumers and runtime behavior.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does assigning true to runtimeRegressionSuitePassed run the suite?</summary><p>No. The field records a claim. Attach actual results before treating it as evidence.</p></details>`,
  "0168": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Challenge generated code with a wrong-tenant request</figcaption>
<p>Use a test request whose fields are well formed but whose tenant is not authorized for the authenticated caller.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Shape check<br>Fields have valid types</div><div>Permission check<br>Caller cannot use this tenant</div><div>Expected effect<br>Reject request<br>No unauthorized write</div></div>
<details><summary>Would successful compilation prove this request is safe?</summary><p>No. Types do not establish permission. Review and test the authorization path.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should accompany an AI-generated claim that all checks passed?</summary><p>Actual check results, the reviewed diff and dependency provenance. The checklist object itself does not establish any of these.</p></details>`,
  "0169": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Observe the service through injected dependencies</figcaption>
<p>In a hypothetical test, parsing succeeds, the fake clock returns 100 then 125, and the repository returns a project.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Start<br>Read clock: 100</div><div>Repository<br>Create project<br>Return record</div><div>Finally<br>Read clock: 125<br>Observe duration: 25</div></div>
<details><summary>What lets this duration check avoid waiting for real time?</summary><p>The service receives its clock as a dependency. A test can supply fixed values and inspect the telemetry call.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is telemetry recorded when parsing returns an invalid result?</summary><p>Not by this code. It returns before starting the timed try/finally block. Decide whether validation failures need their own metric.</p></details>`,
  "0170": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A completed write can still have a failed response</figcaption>
<p>Consider the service when the repository completes a write, but telemetry.observe throws in finally.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Repository<br>Write completes<br>Project returned</div><div>Service prepares success<br>ok: true</div><div>Telemetry throws<br>Finally exits with an error<br>Service promise rejects</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the surrounding catch convert this telemetry failure into Result?</summary><p>No. A throw from finally overrides the pending return and escapes this try/catch structure. The caller may see failure after the write succeeded.</p></details>
<details><summary>What must a production design decide before retrying?</summary><p>Define how telemetry failures affect the request and how repeated create attempts are deduplicated. An idempotency-key field alone does not implement durable deduplication.</p></details>`,
  "0152": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Separate the type import from the runtime import</figcaption>
<p>load uses Project to describe its return value and parseProject to check its input.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Project import<br>Used as a type<br>Erased from JavaScript</div><div>parseProject import<br>Called at runtime<br>Retained in JavaScript</div></div>
<details><summary>Would importing only the Project type load a validator?</summary><p>No. The type import disappears. The runtime parser needs its own value import and implementation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does load's Promise of Project validate the input?</summary><p>No. The call to parseProject performs the check. The return annotation describes the result expected by the compiler.</p></details>`,
  "0153": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Both tools must resolve the module</figcaption>
<p>The source imports parseProject from ./project.js under NodeNext settings.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Compiler question<br>Which source or declaration describes ./project.js?</div><div>Runtime question<br>Which emitted JavaScript file can Node load?</div><div>Required result<br>Both refer to the intended module</div></div>
<details><summary>If a paths alias fixes the type error, is runtime resolution fixed too?</summary><p>Not necessarily. paths does not rewrite emitted import strings. The runtime or bundler must support the same module reference.</p></details>
<details><summary>What evidence helps explain why the compiler chose a file?</summary><p>Use the resolution trace and explainFiles output described in the lesson. Inspect the actual selection before changing module settings.</p></details>`,
  "0154": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A declaration promises a parser; JavaScript supplies it</figcaption>
<p>The package declares parseProject in dist/index.d.ts and directs runtime imports to dist/index.js.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Declaration file<br>Input: unknown<br>Output: Project<br>No parser body</div><div>JavaScript file<br>Must implement parseProject<br>Must perform the promised checks</div></div>
<details><summary>Can a declaration file alone validate an incoming record?</summary><p>No. It contains type information. The runtime needs executable parsing code.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does an accepted declaration prove the implementation returns a valid Project?</summary><p>No. Declarations can be inaccurate. Check the implementation and test the package's runtime behavior separately.</p></details>`,
  "0155": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check what a package consumer receives</figcaption>
<p>The package exports its type file and JavaScript file from dist. The published files list includes dist.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Source checkout<br>Source files may be available</div><div>Packed artifact<br>Declared dist files must be present</div><div>Clean consumer<br>Resolve types<br>Load JavaScript<br>Call parser</div></div>
<details><summary>Why can a local source import hide a packaging failure?</summary><p>It may bypass the published exports and use files absent from the package. A clean consumer checks the artifact users actually receive.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if index.d.ts exists but index.js is missing?</summary><p>A type consumer may find the declaration, but the advertised runtime import cannot load the missing implementation.</p></details>`,
  "0156": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Add checks while the file is still JavaScript</figcaption>
<p>The JavaScript parser uses JSDoc to describe an unknown input and a Project output. Its body checks id and name.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input A<br>id: p1<br>name: Demo<br>Returns a project</div><div>Input B<br>id: p1<br>name: 7<br>Throws TypeError</div><div>JSDoc layer<br>Helps static checking<br>Does not execute validation</div></div>
<details><summary>Must the file become TypeScript before these runtime checks work?</summary><p>No. The checks are ordinary JavaScript. JSDoc and type checking can support a gradual migration.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would renaming the file alone reject bad data from a server?</summary><p>No. Keep the parser at the boundary. File extensions do not validate values at runtime.</p></details>`,
  "0157": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Normalize a valid name and report an invalid one</figcaption>
<p>Both requests supply ownerId u1. Their names differ.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Name: spaces around Demo<br>Trimmed name: Demo<br>ok: true</div><div>Name: spaces only<br>Trimmed name: empty<br>ok: false<br>name required</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a successful parse preserve the spaces around Demo?</summary><p>No. The parser returns a new value with a trimmed name.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can the caller read value before checking ok?</summary><p>The failure branch has errors instead of value. Narrow on ok first, then handle the fields for that branch.</p></details>`,
  "0158": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A valid owner field still needs authorization</figcaption>
<p>A decoded request has name Demo and ownerId u1. The parser accepts these field types.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Decode JSON<br>Obtain an unknown value</div><div>Parse fields<br>Nonempty trimmed name<br>String ownerId</div><div>Authorize ownership<br>Check against the authenticated principal</div><div>Persist<br>Only after required checks pass</div></div>
<details><summary>Does ownerId being a string prove the caller may use it?</summary><p>No. That is a shape check. The handler must enforce the ownership policy.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should AI-generated JSON bypass this boundary?</summary><p>No. It is still external data. Decode it, validate its fields and apply permission checks before effects.</p></details>`,
  "0159": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Yield two items, then return the count</figcaption>
<p>Suppose the parser returns one page with items A and B and no next cursor.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First yield<br>Item A<br>Count: 1</div><div>Second yield<br>Item B<br>Count: 2</div><div>Generator completion<br>Return value: 2<br>No next page</div></div>
<details><summary>Is the final count a third yielded item?</summary><p>No. The generator return value and yielded values occupy different protocol slots.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if nextCursor points to another origin?</summary><p>The explicit origin check throws before that next fetch. The generic item type does not enforce this security rule.</p></details>`,
  "0160": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the selected row connected to its type</figcaption>
<p>Use one row with id p1 and name Demo, plus a name column without a custom renderer.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Cell lookup<br>row[name]<br>Demo</div><div>Fallback rendering<br>Convert value to text<br>Display Demo</div><div>Select p1 button<br>Calls onSelect<br>Passes the complete row</div></div>
<details><summary>Does onSelect receive only the row ID?</summary><p>No. The component passes the row itself, preserving the inferred Row type.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this Column type narrow a custom renderer's value to one chosen key?</summary><p>No. Its value parameter is Row[keyof Row], the union of all property value types. A renderer must handle that contract or use a more precise column design.</p></details>`,
  "0161": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Convert environment text before starting the service</figcaption>
<p>loadConfig reads the environment and checks numeric limits.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>PORT absent<br>Default text 3000<br>Number 3000<br>Accept</div><div>PORT: abc<br>Number conversion: NaN<br>Reject</div><div>PORT: 0<br>Integer outside allowed range<br>Reject</div></div>
<details><summary>Does an empty PORT use the default?</summary><p>No. Nullish coalescing keeps the supplied empty string. Number converts it to zero, and the range check rejects it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does receiving SIGTERM force serve to stop correctly?</summary><p>The handler aborts the controller. serve must observe that signal and clean up its resources; the signal alone cannot prove graceful shutdown.</p></details>`,
  "0141": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Infer the allowed directions from the argument</figcaption>
<p>choose receives the readonly pair north, south. Its implementation returns element 0.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Argument<br>[north, south]</div><div>Inferred return type<br>north or south</div><div>Actual returned value<br>north</div></div>
<details><summary>Why is the return type wider than this call's actual result?</summary><p>The signature uses T[number], which includes every element type in the tuple. It does not promise to return only the first element's type.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can the nonempty tuple constraint accept an empty array?</summary><p>No. At least one string is required. That requirement supports reading the first element, while the literal argument preserves the allowed direction names.</p></details>`,
  "0142": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The property name determines the result type</figcaption>
<p>Use a Project with id p1, name Demo and archived false.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Read name<br>Allowed key<br>Result: Demo<br>Type: string</div><div>Read archived<br>Allowed key<br>Result: false<br>Type: boolean</div><div>Read missing<br>Not a Project key<br>Type check rejects the call</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not return string or boolean for every property?</summary><p>The K parameter keeps the selected key connected to T[K]. A known archived key therefore gives a boolean result.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does keyof list keys from an unknown JSON object at runtime?</summary><p>No. It is a type operation. Validate external data before treating it as a Project.</p></details>`,
  "0143": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Derive a patch from the Project fields</figcaption>
<p>Project has id, name and archived. The patch first omits id, then makes the remaining fields optional and writable.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Project fields<br>readonly id: string<br>name: string<br>archived: boolean</div><div>Omit id<br>name: string<br>archived: boolean</div><div>Patch fields<br>Optional name: string<br>Optional archived: boolean</div></div>
<details><summary>Can a patch specify only archived?</summary><p>Yes. The mapped type makes each remaining property optional. It still requires a boolean if archived is supplied under this contract.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this transformation delete id from an existing object?</summary><p>No. Mapped types describe compile-time contracts. Runtime input checks and field selection must enforce the update policy.</p></details>`,
  "0144": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check union members separately or together</figcaption>
<p>The two conditional types deliberately ask different questions.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>ElementOf string[]<br>Extract string</div><div>ElementOf number[]<br>Extract number</div><div>Distributed union result<br>string or number</div><div>Wrapped string-or-number check<br>Whole union is not assignable to string<br>Result: other</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does ElementOf handle both array alternatives?</summary><p>Its checked side is the bare type parameter T. The conditional distributes over the union and combines the results.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does NonDistributive not return text or other?</summary><p>The tuple wrapper checks the union as a whole. Because number is not a string, the whole check selects other.</p></details>`,
  "0145": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Build event names from field names</figcaption>
<p>Apply EventName to the two field names name and archived.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>name<br>Add Changed suffix<br>nameChanged</div><div>archived<br>Add Changed suffix<br>archivedChanged</div><div>Allowed event-name type<br>nameChanged or archivedChanged</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the resulting type accept nameUpdated?</summary><p>No. That text is outside the constructed union. The suffix is part of the contract.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does declaring this type register two event handlers?</summary><p>No. Template literal types calculate allowed strings during checking. Runtime registration still needs executable code.</p></details>`,
  "0146": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Select a public type without selecting runtime fields</figcaption>
<p>The source type has string fields id, name and secret. Pick selects id and name for type D.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Source type<br>id<br>name<br>secret</div><div>Selected type D<br>id<br>name</div><div>Runtime object<br>No fields removed by Pick</div></div>
<details><summary>Is a Pick annotation enough to hide secret in JSON?</summary><p>No. Construct a response with the allowed fields. A type annotation does not change the object being serialized.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does Awaited produce for the nested Promise type in the example?</summary><p>It produces the object type with string id. This calculates a type; it does not execute or wait for a promise.</p></details>`,
  "0147": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow the direction of the value</figcaption>
<p>Every Dog is an Animal. Only Dog promises a bark method.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Producer of Dog<br>Returns a Dog<br>Safe where Animal is expected</div><div>Consumer of Animal<br>Accepts any Animal<br>Safe where a Dog is supplied</div><div>Consumer requiring Dog<br>May call bark<br>Unsafe for an arbitrary Animal</div></div>
<details><summary>Why can the Animal consumer handle a Dog?</summary><p>A Dog has the Animal properties that this consumer needs. The consumer does not require the extra bark method.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why use a readonly array for the wider Animal view?</summary><p>The view permits reads without permitting insertion of an arbitrary Animal through that reference. Such an insertion would break the original Dog-only expectation.</p></details>`,
  "0148": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One repository operation builds on another</figcaption>
<p>A concrete repository implements find. The inherited require method uses its result.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>find returns a record<br>require resolves with that record</div><div>find returns undefined<br>require rejects with not found</div></div>
<details><summary>Must every subclass duplicate the not-found check?</summary><p>No. The base require method supplies that behavior. The subclass supplies the storage-specific find operation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does extending this class create a database connection?</summary><p>No. The abstraction states a contract and provides a method. A concrete implementation still needs its storage and error-handling policy.</p></details>`,
  "0149": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Give checked IDs different compile-time identities</figcaption>
<p>parseUserId checks for a string starting with usr_ before asserting the UserId brand.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>usr_7<br>String with required prefix<br>Accepted as UserId</div><div>prj_7<br>Wrong prefix<br>TypeError</div><div>Runtime accepted value<br>Still the string usr_7</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a UserId brand prove that user 7 exists?</summary><p>No. This parser proves only the shown string and prefix checks. Existence and authorization need separate checks.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will the brand prevent mixing IDs after serialization?</summary><p>The brand is erased at runtime. Validate data again when it returns across an external boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0150": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Watch the decorator around a synchronous call</figcaption>
<p>Call create with Ada surrounded by spaces.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Wrapper starts<br>Log method.start for create</div><div>Method runs<br>Trim spaces<br>Return name Ada</div><div>Finally runs<br>Log method.finish for create</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the finish log still occur if the method throws?</summary><p>Yes. The finally block runs when the call exits through either return or throw.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would the same wrapper wait for an async method to finish?</summary><p>No. It logs after the method returns its Promise. Promise settlement can occur later, so the log is not an async completion measurement.</p></details>`,
  "0151": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Use a runtime catalog to check status text</figcaption>
<p>The ProjectStatus object has three own keys: draft, active and archived. Their values use the same words.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>active<br>String and own key<br>Accept</div><div>deleted<br>String but no own key<br>Reject</div><div>toString<br>Inherited name, not an own key<br>Reject</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does the parser need an object if a union names the same words?</summary><p>The object exists at runtime for the membership check. The type-only union is erased.</p></details>
<details><summary>What assumption would break if active's value changed to enabled?</summary><p>The parser checks keys and returns the input as a value type. That is valid here because keys and values match. A changed catalog would need a matching parser change.</p></details>`,
  "0132": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep, replace or clear Ada's name</figcaption>
<p>Every patch below starts from the same profile: name Ada.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>No name field<br>Keep Ada</div><div>name: Grace<br>Replace with Grace</div><div>name: empty string<br>Set an empty string</div><div>name: null<br>Clear the name</div></div>
<details><summary>What breaks if the update tests whether name is truthy?</summary><p>The empty string and null are both false in a truthiness check. The update would miss two supplied values. Check field presence instead.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a present name with value undefined mean clear?</summary><p>No. The parser rejects it. JSON serialization also drops that property, while a null value remains. Absence and clearing need distinct representations.</p></details>`,
  "0133": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Choose a payment branch, then require a trace</figcaption>
<p>The card payment has amount 10 and token t. The bank payment has amount 20 and account a.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Card branch<br>kind: card<br>token: t<br>traceId: r1</div><div>Bank branch<br>kind: bank<br>account: a<br>traceId: r2</div><div>Shared operation<br>Read amount from either<br>Total: 30</div></div>
<details><summary>Can destination read token before checking kind?</summary><p>No. The bank alternative does not guarantee a token. Checking kind selects the fields available on that branch.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does intersecting a string ID with a number ID choose the number?</summary><p>No. The property must satisfy both types, which makes it impossible to supply an ordinary value. Object spread has different runtime behavior: the later ID overwrites the earlier one.</p></details>`,
  "0134": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Ignore the response from the older request</figcaption>
<p>Request r1 starts first. Request r2 starts before r1 finishes. The reducer uses a latest-start-wins policy.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Start r1<br>loading, requestId r1</div><div>Start r2<br>loading, requestId r2</div><div>Resolve r1 with old<br>Keep loading r2</div><div>Resolve r2 with new<br>success, data new</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does the old response leave the state unchanged?</summary><p>Its request ID does not match the active loading state. Checking only for loading would allow the older response to replace newer work.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does ignoring r1 cancel its network request?</summary><p>No. The reducer controls local state. Cancellation needs a separate mechanism, and remote work may already have occurred.</p></details>`,
  "0135": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow the branch for an empty value</figcaption>
<p>The describe function accepts strings, numbers, Date objects and null.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>null<br>Null branch<br>missing</div><div>Empty string<br>String branch<br>text:</div><div>0<br>Number branch<br>number:0</div><div>Date in 2026<br>Date branch<br>date:2026</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not treat every falsy input as missing?</summary><p>That would discard the valid empty string and zero. The explicit null check keeps their meanings separate.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does finding name with the in operator prove it is an own string property?</summary><p>No. The property may be inherited, and its value may be a number. Check ownership and the value type when the input contract requires both.</p></details>`,
  "0136": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate a command and copy its allowed fields</figcaption>
<p>The input has kind create, name Ada and an extra admin field set to true.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Guard<br>Recognized kind<br>String name<br>Accept shape</div><div>Parser output<br>kind: create<br>name: Ada<br>No admin field</div><div>Later input mutation<br>Original name becomes changed<br>Parsed name remains Ada</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the guard itself remove admin?</summary><p>No. It only returns a boolean. The parser constructs a fresh object with selected fields.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can a predicate that always returns true make null safe?</summary><p>No. TypeScript trusts the declared predicate, but its implementation can lie. Test invalid inputs as well as valid ones before relying on the narrowing.</p></details>`,
  "0137": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One function body, two return contracts</figcaption>
<p>The get overloads accept either one ID or an array of IDs.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input p1<br>String overload<br>Returns p1</div><div>Input [p1, p2]<br>Array overload<br>Returns a new [p1, p2] array</div><div>Append p3 to result<br>Result length: 3<br>Source length: 2</div></div>
<details><summary>Can an unresolved string-or-array argument use either overload automatically?</summary><p>No. Neither visible overload accepts that whole union. Narrow the argument first or provide an appropriate public signature.</p></details>
<details><summary>Must a callback declare the index if visit always supplies it?</summary><p>No. It may ignore a supplied argument. Marking the index optional would instead promise that callers may omit it, which changes the contract.</p></details>`,
  "0138": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Compare object keys with Map keys</figcaption>
<p>The counts object stores 7 under key 1. A separate Map stores both number 1 and string 1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Object access<br>1 and string 1<br>Same property<br>Both read 7</div><div>Map access<br>Number 1: number<br>String 1: string<br>Two entries</div><div>Missing entry<br>Object or Map lookup<br>undefined</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a string index signature guarantee every lookup exists?</summary><p>No. The example enables noUncheckedIndexedAccess so an arbitrary read includes undefined.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the symbol metadata field secret because JSON omits it?</summary><p>No. Reflect.ownKeys can discover it. Serialization behavior is not an access-control boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0139": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Move a readonly coordinate by returning a new pair</figcaption>
<p>Use a small point at x 2, y 3 with the lesson's move function.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input pair<br>[2, 3]<br>Readonly parameter</div><div>Computed pair<br>[2 + 1, 3 + 1]<br>New array</div><div>After the call<br>Input: [2, 3]<br>Result: [3, 4]</div></div>
<details><summary>Does move need to change the input to produce its result?</summary><p>No. It reads each coordinate and returns a new pair. The readonly parameter prevents typed writes through that reference.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a readonly type freeze the JavaScript object?</summary><p>No. The type restriction is erased. Runtime ownership rules may require copying or freezing, depending on what must be protected.</p></details>`,
  "0140": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Index two records with the same ID</figcaption>
<p>The input contains p1 named First, followed by p1 named Last. indexById puts each record into a Map.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First insertion<br>p1 → First</div><div>Second insertion<br>p1 → Last<br>Existing key replaced</div><div>Lookup p1<br>Returns the Last record<br>Full item type retained</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the generic constraint require IDs to be unique?</summary><p>No. It requires a string ID on each item. This implementation keeps the last record for a repeated ID. Enforce uniqueness separately if duplicates are invalid.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a valid key type guarantee a lookup finds a record?</summary><p>No. Map.get can return undefined. The generic relationship preserves the record's fields; it does not prove that a particular entry exists.</p></details>`,
  "0126": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check a missing array element</figcaption>
<p>A project uses the shown configuration. A function promises to return a User but returns element 0 from a User array.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input<br>Empty User array</div><div>Index check<br>User or undefined</div><div>Promised return<br>User only<br>Type check fails</div></div>
<details><summary>Does strict mode alone add this array check?</summary><p>No. The example enables noUncheckedIndexedAccess separately. Record the effective configuration so another developer can reproduce the diagnostic.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will this configuration create JavaScript files?</summary><p>No. noEmit prevents output files. A successful check is evidence about types; it does not run the application.</p></details>`,
  "0127": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow the declaration of answer</figcaption>
<p>The source declares an exported constant named answer, annotated as number, with value 42.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Source tree<br>Declaration of answer<br>Number annotation<br>Literal 42</div><div>Type checking<br>Compare the initializer with number</div><div>JavaScript output<br>Exported constant answer = 42<br>No number annotation</div></div>
<details><summary>Does printing the syntax tree prove the types are valid?</summary><p>No. The visitor shows parsed structure. Full type checking requires more than parsing the file.</p></details>
<details><summary>If the initializer becomes a string, can transpilation still produce JavaScript?</summary><p>Yes. transpileModule is not a full project type check. Do not treat its emitted output or diagnostics as proof that assignments and imports are valid across the project.</p></details>`,
  "0128": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An assertion leaves the payload untouched</figcaption>
<p>The JSON contains id 42 and name null. The program asserts that the parsed object is a User.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>JSON values<br>id: 42<br>name: null</div><div>Compiler view after assertion<br>id: string<br>name: string</div><div>Runtime values<br>id: 42<br>name: null</div></div>
<details><summary>What does the console print for the ID type and name?</summary><p>It prints number and null. The assertion supplies no conversion or validation.</p></details>
<details><summary>What would make the User claim trustworthy at this boundary?</summary><p>Check the parsed value before using it as a User. Reject incorrect field types. Business checks, such as whether the ID exists, need their own rules.</p></details>`,
  "0129": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The same text can have different inferred types</figcaption>
<p>Both status properties start with the text ready. Only the second object uses a const assertion.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Ordinary object<br>status value: ready<br>Inferred type: string</div><div>Const-asserted object<br>status value: ready<br>Inferred type: ready literal<br>Readonly property</div><div>Checked route catalog<br>create.method value: POST<br>Inferred type: POST literal</div></div>
<details><summary>Can the ordinary object's status later hold waiting?</summary><p>Yes. Its property type widened to string. Declaring the object variable with const prevents reassignment of the variable, not mutation of its properties.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does satisfies turn the route catalog into a runtime validator?</summary><p>No. It checks compatibility during compilation while preserving useful inferred types. It does not validate data arriving from a server.</p></details>`,
  "0130": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check the value before reading the project</figcaption>
<p>The parser accepts an unknown value. Compare two incoming records.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Record A<br>id: p1<br>name: Demo<br>Both fields are strings</div><div>Record B<br>id: p1<br>name: 7<br>Name is a number</div><div>Parser outcomes<br>A returns a project<br>B throws TypeError</div></div>
<details><summary>Would an empty string ID pass this parser?</summary><p>Yes. The parser checks field types. Add a separate nonempty-ID rule if the application requires one.</p></details>
<details><summary>The void callback pushes 7 into an empty array. What happens to its return value?</summary><p>The JavaScript call returns 1, the new array length. The void callback type tells typed callers to ignore the result; it does not remove that runtime value.</p></details>`,
  "0131": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A public type does not remove a private field</figcaption>
<p>The internal object has name Ada and admin true. A second variable refers to it with the PublicProfile type.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>internal<br>name: Ada<br>admin: true</div><div>publicView<br>Same object<br>JSON still contains admin</div><div>Explicit projection<br>New object<br>name: Ada only</div></div>
<details><summary>Why can the variable pass when a fresh literal with admin is rejected?</summary><p>The fresh literal receives an excess-property check. Structural compatibility still permits the existing object because it has the required string name.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the strict input parser accept the internal object?</summary><p>No. Its unknown-key check rejects admin. Runtime validation and explicit response projection serve different purposes: one controls accepted input, and the other controls exported fields.</p></details>`,
  "0115": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Decode fields at their declared offsets</figcaption>
<p>The buffer holds 12 bytes. Every field uses big-endian order.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Offsets 0–1<br>Version: 1<br>Bytes: 00 01</div><div>Offsets 2–3<br>Payload length: 8<br>Bytes: 00 08</div><div>Offsets 4–11<br>64-bit ID: 42<br>Bytes end in 00 2A</div><div>Decoded value<br>version: 1<br>id: 42n</div></div>
<details><summary>What happens if the buffer has only 11 bytes?</summary><p>The minimum-length check rejects it before reading the fields.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the decoder reject a thirteenth trailing byte?</summary><p>No. It checks a minimum length. Decide whether the contract permits a stream prefix or requires one exact frame before tightening that condition.</p></details>`,
  "0116": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Publish the value before marking it ready</figcaption>
<p>The shared Int32Array has two slots: status at index 0 and value at index 1. Status starts at 0.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Worker protocol<br>Store computed value in slot 1<br>Store ready status 1 in slot 0<br>Notify waiter</div><div>Reader protocol<br>Wait while not ready<br>Check status<br>Then read value</div></div>
<details><summary>Does any notification prove a completed result is available?</summary><p>No. Check the shared predicate. A notification is a wake-up signal, not the data contract itself.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the supplied fragment handle a worker that never completes?</summary><p>No. The worker and compute function are integration requirements. Add error, termination and deadline handling before relying on them.</p></details>`,
  "0117": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A listener captures a binding that can change</figcaption>
<p>The listener reads owner?.id. owner initially refers to the object with ID project-42.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before cleanup<br>owner binding → object project-42<br>Listener reads that binding</div><div>Explicit cleanup<br>Remove listener subscription<br>Set owner to null<br>The binding no longer identifies the object</div></div>
<details><summary>If the function is still called after owner becomes null, does it read the old ID?</summary><p>No. It reads the binding's current null value. Optional chaining yields undefined.</p></details>
<details><summary>Must the finalizer now run immediately?</summary><p>No. Its timing is not guaranteed. Use explicit cleanup for resources and retaining-path evidence for memory investigations.</p></details>`,
  "0118": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Verify the workload before explaining its speed</figcaption>
<p>The array contains 100,000 points. Point i has x = i and y = 1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Sum x values<br>0 + … + 99,999<br>4,999,950,000</div><div>Sum y values<br>100,000 × 1<br>100,000</div><div>Expected total<br>5,000,050,000</div><div>Then measure<br>20 warmup calls<br>30 timed calls</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a fast timing prove which compiler tier executed the function?</summary><p>No. Record the engine version and relevant engine traces before attributing the behavior to a particular tier.</p></details>
<details><summary>Are those internal tiers a portable JavaScript contract?</summary><p>No. Implementations can change their pipeline while preserving observable language behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0119": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One measured shape cannot establish a shape comparison</figcaption>
<p>Every point in the starter is constructed with x followed by y. There is only one measured data arrangement.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Measured group<br>{x: 0, y: 1}<br>{x: 1, y: 1}<br>… same construction pattern</div><div>Missing comparison<br>Equivalent mixed-shape workload<br>Verified equal output<br>Repeated, order-balanced measurements</div></div>
<details><summary>Can the existing timings quantify a stable-versus-mixed-shape speedup?</summary><p>No. There is no measured mixed-shape comparison. Do not invent a speedup from one group.</p></details>
<details><summary>What must stay equal when adding the comparison?</summary><p>The required computation and result. Record engine evidence and realistic repeated measurements before attributing differences to inline caches.</p></details>`,
  "0120": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A percentile label needs a selection rule</figcaption>
<p>The benchmark sorts 30 measured durations. No timing values are invented here.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Displayed median<br>Index floor(30 × 0.5) = 15<br>Sixteenth sorted sample</div><div>Displayed p95<br>Index floor(30 × 0.95) = 28<br>Twenty-ninth sorted sample</div></div>
<details><summary>Is this median the average of the two middle samples?</summary><p>No. It is the upper-middle order statistic. Label the selected convention when comparing reports.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do thirty warm calls in one process establish production responsiveness?</summary><p>No. Measure representative load and event-loop delay, and report cold-start behavior separately. These samples are not independent production requests or a confidence interval.</p></details>`,
  "0121": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Validate data keys and request destinations separately</figcaption>
<p>The fixture accepts a flat string-valued record and a same-origin HTTPS URL. Neither helper performs network access.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Record input<br>name: Ada → accepted<br>__proto__: blocked → rejected<br>Output has null prototype</div><div>URL input<br>/projects → app.example → accepted<br>evil.example → rejected<br>Embedded credentials → rejected</div></div>
<details><summary>Can safeRecord safely inspect arbitrary hostile objects with executable getters?</summary><p>Not as a general guarantee. Object.entries can invoke getters. Use parsed, bounded data at this boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does same-origin URL acceptance authorize every endpoint or handle redirects?</summary><p>No. Endpoint privileges and redirects need separate controls. This local policy is not a complete SSRF defense.</p></details>`,
  "0122": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Check the result and then apply normalization again</figcaption>
<p>The table has two inputs: Ada with surrounding spaces, and a capital E followed by a combining accent.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First normalization<br>“ Ada ” → ada<br>Decomposed accented E → é</div><div>Second normalization<br>ada → ada<br>é → é<br>Same expected values</div></div>
<details><summary>Do two idempotence checks establish the property for every possible input?</summary><p>No. They check those table cases. A generated property-testing campaign would explore a wider declared input space.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does the pre-aborted fetch assertion demonstrate?</summary><p>That the rejection retains the supplied reason without starting a request. It does not test cancelling an in-flight remote effect.</p></details>`,
  "0123": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Follow the value to the first incorrect transformation</figcaption>
<p>For the input “ Ada ”, inspect the normalization steps around a wrong-result report.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Expected value path<br>Trim → Ada<br>NFC → Ada<br>Lowercase → ada</div><div>Debugging boundary<br>Pause for this failing input<br>Inspect before and after each step<br>Keep the smallest reproduction</div></div>
<details><summary>Would a heap snapshot be the first evidence for a wrong lowercase result?</summary><p>Usually a value-focused breakpoint or minimal test is more direct. Heap retaining paths answer a different question about retained memory.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the passing normalization test establish a CPU hotspot or memory leak?</summary><p>No. Profiles and heap evidence require their own workload and observations. Repeat the original failing workload after the fix.</p></details>`,
  "0124": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The processor awaits one commit before starting the next</figcaption>
<p>Consider a batch containing commands A and B. The injected repository owns the atomic business operation.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Command A<br>Check abort<br>Validate synchronously<br>Await commitOnceWithOutbox<br>Save result</div><div>Then command B<br>Check abort<br>Validate synchronously<br>Await commitOnceWithOutbox<br>Save result</div></div>
<details><summary>Does making validateCommand async preserve this processor's validation boundary?</summary><p>No. The call is not awaited. An async validator needs an explicit awaited contract before persistence can depend on its result.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is this processor a pure function because its dependencies are injected?</summary><p>No. It orchestrates repository effects. Injection makes dependencies explicit without removing those effects or proving atomicity.</p></details>`,
  "0125": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A failed batch can contain a committed prefix</figcaption>
<p>Command A commits successfully. Command B then fails during the serial processor's next iteration.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Command A<br>Business effect committed<br>Result and outbox intent stored by the required adapter</div><div>Command B<br>Fails<br>Batch call rejects<br>No batch-wide rollback of A is supplied</div></div>
<details><summary>Can a caller safely assume that nothing happened because the batch rejected?</summary><p>No. Earlier commits can remain. Define reconciliation and replay for each stable command identity.</p></details>
<details><summary>Which behavior must reuse of a key with changed intent have?</summary><p>The required adapter rejects it instead of replaying an unrelated result. Real concurrent, crash and relay tests are still needed; a memory fake cannot establish durable idempotency.</p></details>`,
  "0104": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An import observes the exporter's binding</figcaption>
<p>Split the labelled code into counter.mjs and feature.mjs. The counter module exports count, initially zero.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>counter.mjs<br>count binding: 0 → 1<br>increment changes that binding</div><div>feature.mjs<br>Imported count → live view<br>After increment(): reads 1</div></div>
<details><summary>Was the initial zero copied into an independent imported variable?</summary><p>No. The import observes the exporter's binding. It is a read-only view for the importer.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this fixture establish that every cyclic module graph initializes safely?</summary><p>No. It contains no cycle. Early reads of uninitialized lexical bindings in a cycle can fail.</p></details>`,
  "0105": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A disabled feature avoids the import</figcaption>
<p>loadAnalytics chooses a path from its enabled argument.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>enabled = false<br>Return a no-op track method<br>No analytics import</div><div>enabled = true<br>Import analytics.mjs<br>Return its analytics export<br>Wrap import failure with a cause</div></div>
<details><summary>Does writing dynamic import prove that the build emits a separate network chunk?</summary><p>No. Inspect the actual build and network behavior. The syntax alone does not establish bundler output or tree shaking.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the dynamically imported module a security sandbox?</summary><p>No. A module boundary organizes code; it does not itself restrict that code's authority.</p></details>`,
  "0106": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The first resolution wins</figcaption>
<p>The supplied thenable tries to resolve 40, reject, then resolve 99.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Resolution attempts<br>40: accepted<br>Later rejection: ignored<br>99: ignored</div><div>Reaction chain<br>Receive 40<br>Add 2 → 42<br>Run finally<br>Fulfill with 42</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does sync:end appear before reaction:40?</summary><p>The registered reaction runs asynchronously after the current synchronous work. Calling the resolving function does not run that reaction inline.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would a throw inside finally preserve the value 42?</summary><p>No. A throwing finally can replace the prior outcome with rejection. The supplied callback does not throw.</p></details>`,
  "0107": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An async function starts before it suspends</figcaption>
<p>operation records async:start, awaits zero, then records async:resume.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Current synchronous work<br>Call operation<br>Record async:start<br>Suspend at await<br>Caller records script:end</div><div>Later continuation<br>Record async:resume<br>Return 42<br>Resolve pending result</div></div>
<details><summary>Does awaiting the plain value zero resume immediately in the same call?</summary><p>No. The continuation runs later even though zero requires no external I/O.</p></details>
<details><summary>Has the CPU work moved to another thread?</summary><p>No. Async suspension is not CPU offloading. Shared state can also change before continuation resumes.</p></details>`,
  "0108": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Queue order explains this particular trace</figcaption>
<p>In the supplied fixture, operation reaches await before the explicit microtask and promise reaction are queued.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Synchronous records<br>async:start<br>script:end</div><div>Queued continuations<br>async:resume<br>queued:microtask<br>promise:reaction</div><div>Later timer record<br>timer:task</div><div>Scope<br>This queue setup<br>Not every browser or Node scheduling situation</div></div>
<details><summary>Can repeatedly queued microtasks delay later tasks?</summary><p>Yes. A checkpoint can continue draining newly queued microtasks. Use bounded experiments rather than an infinite starvation loop.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the timer record prove a browser paint occurred first?</summary><p>No. The fixture records callback order, not rendering evidence.</p></details>`,
  "0109": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A failed item keeps its input position</figcaption>
<p>The pool processes [1,2,3,4] with limit 2. Values double except that 3 throws.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Indexed results<br>0 → fulfilled, 2<br>1 → fulfilled, 4<br>2 → rejected<br>3 → fulfilled, 8</div><div>Concurrency boundary<br>At most two awaited operations active<br>Results remain in input order</div></div>
<details><summary>Does one rejection stop all remaining items?</summary><p>No. Workers record the rejection and continue. The final array includes the result for input 4.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does limit 2 bound total memory and operation duration?</summary><p>No. Inputs and results still occupy linear space, and a hanging operation can prevent completion. Deadlines and streaming need separate controls.</p></details>`,
  "0110": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Either cancellation source can end the local wait</figcaption>
<p>The browser request combines a 1,000-millisecond timeout with a parent signal. The example immediately aborts the parent with reason navigation.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Parent signal: navigation<br>↘</div><div>Timeout signal<br>↙</div><div>Combined signal<br>Passed to fetch</div><div>Wrapper behavior<br>Observed abort → AbortError</div></div>
<details><summary>Does aborting undo an operation the remote server already committed?</summary><p>No. Cancellation is cooperative and cannot establish remote rollback. Reconcile uncertain effects separately.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can the AbortError name alone distinguish timeout from navigation in this wrapper?</summary><p>No. It normalizes observed aborts to that name. Inspect the original signal reason when the distinction matters.</p></details>`,
  "0111": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Disposal unwinds in reverse registration order</figcaption>
<p>The stack first registers an audit subscription, then a deferred callback. Work throws after publishing started.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Registration order<br>1. Audit subscription<br>2. Final callback</div><div>Disposal order<br>1. Final callback runs<br>2. Audit subscription closes<br>Original work failure is handled outside</div></div>
<details><summary>Does cleanup wait for garbage collection?</summary><p>No. using disposes on scope exit. It gives cleanup a defined program boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can a runtime feature guard make unsupported using syntax parse?</summary><p>No. The runtime must understand the syntax before it can execute a guard. Use a supported environment for this example.</p></details>`,
  "0112": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Cloning preserves a cycle in a new graph</figcaption>
<p>The original contains a Date, Map, BigInt and a self reference.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original object A<br>self → A<br>Date and Map values</div><div>Cloned object B<br>self → B<br>Date and Map types preserved<br>B is not A</div></div>
<details><summary>Does cloned.self point back to the original object?</summary><p>No. It points to the cloned object. The cycle is reproduced inside the new graph.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why separate BigInt and cyclic inputs when investigating JSON rejection?</summary><p>Either can cause rejection. Testing them separately identifies the cause instead of attributing one combined failure to the wrong feature.</p></details>`,
  "0113": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One instant can have several display forms</figcaption>
<p>The fixture stores 2026-11-01T05:30:00.000Z and formats it in America/New_York for three locales.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>One stored instant<br>Explicit UTC marker: Z<br>Same timestamp for all formatting</div><div>Display choices<br>en-US<br>en-GB<br>hi-IN<br>Different presentation conventions</div></div>
<details><summary>Does changing the locale change the stored instant?</summary><p>No. Formatting changes the representation. Persist an unambiguous timestamp rather than locale display text.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is one UTC instant enough to define every future occurrence of a recurring local appointment?</summary><p>No. Retain the named zone and calendar policy for that schedule. The fixture does not resolve every daylight-saving ambiguity.</p></details>`,
  "0114": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A full-field check rejects a trailing newline</figcaption>
<p>The identifier grammar accepts a lowercase prefix, a hyphen and one to ten digits. Work is bounded before matching.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>project-42<br>Full matched text equals input<br>Accepted</div><div>project-42 plus newline<br>Dollar anchor may match before newline<br>Matched text differs from full input<br>Rejected</div></div>
<details><summary>What happens to project- followed by 1,000 digits?</summary><p>The length check rejects it before the regular expression runs. The input limit is 64 code units.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the Unicode flag guarantee fast or grapheme-aware matching?</summary><p>No. Unicode pattern semantics are separate from those guarantees. Avoid unbounded catastrophic-pattern demonstrations.</p></details>`,
  "0091": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A default parameter does not forbid arguments</figcaption>
<p>The method read has a default prefix of balance and reads this.balance from account, whose balance is 40.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>account.read()<br>Default prefix: balance<br>Result: balance:40</div><div>account.read("custom")<br>Supplied prefix: custom<br>Result: custom:40</div></div>
<details><summary>Does read.length being zero mean callers cannot supply a prefix?</summary><p>No. The first parameter has a default, so the reported length is zero. That is not an argument-count restriction.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would null select the default like undefined does?</summary><p>No. Undefined selects the default. Null is a supplied value, producing null:40 in this concatenation.</p></details>`,
  "0092": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Detaching a method removes the call-site receiver</figcaption>
<p>detached refers to account.read. The function is the same; the way it is called changes its receiver.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Method call<br>account.read()<br>this is account<br>balance:40</div><div>Explicit receiver<br>detached.call(account, "call")<br>this is account<br>call:40</div><div>Bound wrapper<br>bind account and prefix bound<br>Later bound() → bound:40</div><div>Plain detached call in strict mode<br>this is undefined<br>Reading its balance fails</div></div>
<details><summary>Can call replace the captured this of the fixture's arrow?</summary><p>No. The top-level arrow retains the ES module's undefined this.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is a bound ordinary constructor forced to construct on its bound this object?</summary><p>No. Construction of a constructible bound function uses construction semantics and ignores its bound this. Arrows are not constructors.</p></details>`,
  "0093": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A setter creates an own backing property</figcaption>
<p>Assign the text “ Evidence ” to project.name in the fixture.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Own accessor: name<br>Validate text<br>Trim surrounding spaces<br>Write this._name</div><div>Own data property: _name<br>Value: Evidence<br>Inherited property: kind<br>Value from base: project</div></div>
<details><summary>Does Object.hasOwn(project, 'kind') return true?</summary><p>No. Lookup can find kind through the prototype even though project does not own it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the underscore prevent direct writes that bypass name validation?</summary><p>No. _name is an ordinary property. Use an actual private-state design if that boundary must be enforced.</p></details>`,
  "0094": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Lookup delegates without copying a property</figcaption>
<p>project has base as its prototype. base owns a non-writable property kind with value project.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Read project.kind<br>No own kind<br>↓<br>base.kind → project</div><div>Prototype chain<br>project → base → null<br>Property belongs to base</div></div>
<details><summary>Can ordinary assignment shadow this inherited non-writable kind property?</summary><p>No. In strict mode the attempted assignment throws. It does not create a new own property through that operation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is explicitly defining an own property the same operation as ordinary assignment?</summary><p>No. defineProperty has different semantics. Do not infer its outcome from the assignment rule.</p></details>`,
  "0095": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Private static state belongs to its declaring class</figcaption>
<p>Construct one AuditedLedger and add 4,200 integer cents.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Instance<br>Private entries: [4200]<br>Computed balance: 4200</div><div>Base class Ledger<br>Private created count: 1<br>Ledger.created → 1</div></div>
<details><summary>Does AuditedLedger.created read the same private counter successfully?</summary><p>No. The inherited getter uses this.#created. The subclass does not have the base class's private static brand, so that read fails.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the log inside AuditedLedger.add prove the amount was accepted?</summary><p>No. It logs before super.add validates the amount. It records an attempt, not a successful durable posting.</p></details>`,
  "0096": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Copy the changed branch and share the unchanged branch</figcaption>
<p>The rename-only update changes the project name from Draft to Renamed. The tags do not change.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original state<br>project → object A, name Draft<br>tags → array T</div><div>Renamed state<br>project → object B, name Renamed<br>tags → the same array T</div></div>
<details><summary>Which equality checks should be false and true?</summary><p>The project objects differ by identity, while renamed.tags === state.tags is true. The unchanged branch is shared.</p></details>
<details><summary>Did freezing the outer state automatically freeze the nested containers?</summary><p>No. The fixture freezes them explicitly. Object.freeze does not recursively freeze an arbitrary object graph.</p></details>`,
  "0097": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A hole differs from a present undefined value</figcaption>
<p>The sparse array has length 3: an absent index 0, an explicit undefined at index 1, and 3 at index 2.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original slots<br>0: absent<br>1: present, undefined<br>2: present, 3</div><div>Spread into another array<br>0: present, undefined<br>1: present, undefined<br>2: present, 3</div></div>
<details><summary>Does map call its callback for the absent index in this fixture?</summary><p>No. It skips that hole. That differs from calling the callback with an explicit undefined value.</p></details>
<details><summary>How many own elements does new Array(5) initially have?</summary><p>Zero, although its length is five. Length does not count present element properties.</p></details>`,
  "0098": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Weak metadata does not weaken other owners</figcaption>
<p>The object has ID p-42. The fixture stores it in a Map and also uses it as a WeakMap key.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Strong ownership<br>byId["p-42"] → project object<br>Local project binding → same object</div><div>Weak association<br>project object ⇢ metadata<br>This association alone does not retain its key</div></div>
<details><summary>Does using WeakMap make the object collectible while byId still retains it?</summary><p>No. A strong retaining path remains. Weakness applies to that particular association.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can WeakMap report all its keys for an eviction dashboard?</summary><p>No. It deliberately has no key enumeration or size API. Weak metadata is not a bounded-cache eviction policy.</p></details>`,
  "0099": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One iterator is one traversal</figcaption>
<p>Call range(1,5) once, then call next twice on that returned iterator.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Same iterator<br>First next → 1<br>Second next → 2<br>Position advances</div><div>Fresh range(1,5)<br>New iterator<br>First next → 1<br>Independent position</div></div>
<details><summary>Does asking the original iterator for Symbol.iterator restart it?</summary><p>No. It returns itself. A fresh generator call creates a fresh traversal.</p></details>
<details><summary>What happens to the started generator when the supplied for-of loop breaks?</summary><p>The loop closes it through return, and its finally block runs. It need not generate the remaining values.</p></details>`,
  "0100": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A value sent by next resumes the suspended yield</figcaption>
<p>The supplied generator stores the value sent back into each yield as command.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>next()<br>Yields 1<br>done: false</div><div>next()<br>Resumes and yields 2<br>done: false</div><div>next("stop")<br>command becomes stop<br>Returns stopped<br>done: true</div><div>Cleanup<br>finally runs as the started generator exits</div></div>
<details><summary>What if return is called before the first next?</summary><p>The body has never started, so its finally is not entered. Do not acquire a resource outside the body while assuming this finally always releases it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is this generator a cancellable network paginator?</summary><p>No. It is a synchronous range with a command protocol. Network work and cancellation ownership require additional behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0101": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A symbol description is not its identity</figcaption>
<p>The proxy recognizes one particular Symbol('inspect') stored in the inspect binding.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>inspect binding<br>→ symbol A, description inspect<br>proxy[A] returns the custom hook</div><div>Another Symbol('inspect')<br>→ symbol B, same description<br>B is not A</div></div>
<details><summary>Does accessing the proxy with symbol B select that same custom hook?</summary><p>No. The trap compares the actual symbol identity, not its description.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do symbol keys provide private properties?</summary><p>No. Reflect.ownKeys can reveal actual symbol properties. This custom hook is not a privacy boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0102": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Revoking a proxy does not revoke the target itself</figcaption>
<p>Both target and proxy are held in the fixture. The target contains value 42 and a non-configurable fixed property.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before revoke<br>proxy.value → trap → target.value<br>Result: 42</div><div>After revoke<br>proxy.value → TypeError<br>Separate target reference still exists</div></div>
<details><summary>Can ownKeys hide the non-configurable fixed property?</summary><p>No. That would violate a proxy invariant. The example forwards the target's own keys instead.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does forwarding through Reflect.get make every object transparently wrappable?</summary><p>No. Private brands and built-in internal slots can reject a proxy receiver. A logging wrapper is not a complete security membrane.</p></details>`,
  "0103": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the dependency failure when optional metrics also fail</figcaption>
<p>Two project loads call a gateway that throws the same synthetic connection error. The optional metric recorder also throws.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Each load<br>Gateway throws original<br>Wrap as DependencyError<br>Retain original as cause</div><div>Finally block<br>Metric recorder throws<br>Optional failure is caught<br>DependencyError remains the result</div><div>All-settled result<br>Two rejected loads</div><div>AggregateError<br>Stores both translated errors<br>Does not throw itself</div></div>
<details><summary>What could an uncaught throw in finally do here?</summary><p>Replace the earlier failure. The fixture catches optional metric failure so the original dependency cause remains available.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the full cause chain be serialized to an untrusted caller?</summary><p>Not by default. Map a safe public error separately from internal diagnostic details.</p></details>`,
  "0080": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Observe a value and an exception separately</figcaption>
<p>The observer calls a supplied synchronous operation and records what happens.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Addition operation<br>20 + 22<br>Returned value: 42<br>Type: number</div><div>Throwing operation<br>Throws TypeError<br>Catch records name and message<br>Returns the error object</div></div>
<details><summary>If an operation returns a rejected Promise, does this observer catch that rejection?</summary><p>No. It does not await the Promise. A synchronous catch does not automatically handle later asynchronous failure.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why record the runtime and version alongside the result?</summary><p>They identify the experiment environment. Console presentation is host behavior and should not be mistaken for a language guarantee.</p></details>`,
  "0081": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Conversion changes addition into concatenation</figcaption>
<p>Evaluate the supplied object plus 2. Its valueOf returns an object; its toString returns the text 40.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First conversion attempt<br>valueOf → {}<br>Still not a primitive</div><div>Second attempt<br>toString → "40"<br>A string primitive</div><div>Addition chooses concatenation<br>"40" + 2 → "402"</div><div>Result type<br>string, not number</div></div>
<details><summary>Why is the result not 42?</summary><p>The conversion produces a string. Addition then concatenates after converting the other operand to a string.</p></details>
<details><summary>If valueOf throws, does conversion continue to toString?</summary><p>No. The abrupt failure stops the expression. A partial conversion trace is not evidence of successful completion.</p></details>`,
  "0082": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An alias shares the record; a spread creates another outer object</figcaption>
<p>record starts with name Ada. alias receives record, while copy receives a shallow spread of record.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>record and alias<br>Both → object A<br>alias.name = Grace<br>Object A now holds Grace</div><div>copy<br>→ separate object B<br>Object B still holds Ada</div></div>
<details><summary>Are record and alias identical after the change?</summary><p>Yes. They still identify object A. The mutation changes a property, not the identity of that object.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would a spread also copy a nested mutable object independently?</summary><p>No. It creates a new outer object but preserves references to nested objects. Decide which data may be mutated before choosing a copy strategy.</p></details>`,
  "0083": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Choose a numeric representation before adding money</figcaption>
<p>The examples compare fractional Number arithmetic with integer BigInt arithmetic.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Number fractions<br>0.1 + 0.2<br>Not exactly equal to 0.3</div><div>Integer cents<br>199n + 299n = 498n<br>Exact integer addition</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the integer-cent result define a complete currency policy?</summary><p>No. You still need currency, scale, rounding and serialization rules. The helper only accepts two BigInt operands and adds them.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why can MAX_SAFE_INTEGER + 1 equal MAX_SAFE_INTEGER + 2?</summary><p>Number cannot distinguish every integer at that magnitude. An integer-looking value is not necessarily a safely represented integer.</p></details>`,
  "0084": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One visible accent can have two underlying forms</figcaption>
<p>Compare the supplied composed é with an e followed by a combining accent.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Composed form<br>One accented character code point<br>é</div><div>Decomposed form<br>e + combining accent<br>Visually similar text</div><div>Before normalization<br>Strict string equality: false</div><div>After NFC on both<br>Strict string equality: true</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a length limit mean the same thing in code units, code points and graphemes?</summary><p>No. Choose the unit before writing the limit. The emoji example makes these different counts visible.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does NFC also make case differences and all visually similar identifiers equivalent?</summary><p>No. Normalization is not case folding or a general defense against visually confusable identifiers.</p></details>`,
  "0085": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Parsing a prefix is not validating the whole field</figcaption>
<p>The external count field is the text 42junk. Compare two operations on that exact input.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>parseInt with radix 10<br>Reads numeric prefix 42<br>Returns 42</div><div>Supplied parseCount<br>Requires the entire canonical count text<br>Rejects the junk suffix</div></div>
<details><summary>Would the supplied parser accept the text 042?</summary><p>No. It deliberately rejects leading zeroes except for the single value 0. Change the grammar explicitly if the product needs a different representation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does an empty array's truthiness prove its numeric conversion is nonzero?</summary><p>No. An empty array is truthy while its numeric conversion is zero. Different conversions answer different questions.</p></details>`,
  "0086": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Different equality rules produce different answers</figcaption>
<p>Use NaN and signed zero to distinguish the operations in the fixture.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Searching [NaN]<br>includes(NaN) → true<br>indexOf(NaN) → -1</div><div>Comparing signed zero<br>Set of [-0, 0] → one entry<br>Object.is(-0, 0) → false</div></div>
<details><summary>Why do includes and indexOf disagree here?</summary><p>includes uses SameValueZero, which matches NaN with NaN. indexOf uses strict equality, which does not.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will two separately created empty objects become one Set entry?</summary><p>No. Object identity remains distinct despite equal-looking contents. Stringifying both as labels can hide that distinction.</p></details>`,
  "0087": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The function follows its defining scopes</figcaption>
<p>outer receives input, creates a function-scoped binding, then returns inner from a block with its own binding.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>inner's lookup path<br>Defining block: blockScoped<br>↓<br>Defining function: parameter, functionScoped</div><div>Call result<br>parameter: input<br>Both retained local bindings are readable</div></div>
<details><summary>If the caller has another variable named parameter, does inner use it?</summary><p>No. Lexical lookup follows the defining environments, not the caller's local variables.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the top-level let in this ES module create a globalThis property?</summary><p>No. The fixture uses a module binding. Do not transfer that conclusion unchanged to other script or module environments.</p></details>`,
  "0088": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The call ends before the captured data is used</figcaption>
<p>Trace outer('input') followed by a later call to the returned closure.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First call<br>Enter outer<br>Create bindings and inner<br>Return inner<br>Outer call leaves the stack</div><div>Later call<br>Enter inner<br>Read retained outer bindings<br>Return their values</div></div>
<details><summary>Must outer still be on the call stack for inner to read parameter?</summary><p>No. Captured bindings can remain accessible after the call returns. The current call stack is not the lexical scope chain.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this single-realm example demonstrate cross-realm instanceof behavior?</summary><p>No. It creates no second realm. A separate fixture is needed to observe that boundary.</p></details>`,
  "0089": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An early lexical read is not the same as an early var read</figcaption>
<p>Compare reading temporal before its declaration executes in each version of the experiment.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>let temporal = 1 later<br>Binding exists, uninitialized<br>Early read → ReferenceError</div><div>var temporal = 1 later<br>Binding initialized to undefined<br>Early read → undefined</div></div>
<details><summary>Would the early var read produce the later value 1?</summary><p>No. Initialization to undefined does not execute the later assignment ahead of time.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does const prevent changes to an object's properties?</summary><p>No. It prevents reassigning that binding. Mutability of the referenced object is a separate property.</p></details>`,
  "0090": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two factories create two captured counters</figcaption>
<p>Call createCounter twice. Each returned read and increment pair shares its own call's value binding.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>left.read and left.increment<br>→ binding A, initially 0<br>Two increments: 0 → 1 → 2</div><div>right.read and right.increment<br>→ binding B, initially 0<br>No increments: stays 0</div></div>
<details><summary>Why can a deferred callback read count 1 but message "Count: 0"?</summary><p>The callback reads the current count binding. message is a string built earlier, when count was 0. Updating count does not rebuild that string.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does deleting the registry entry prove the payload was collected immediately?</summary><p>No. It removes that retaining path. Other references and collection timing need separate evidence.</p></details>`,
  "0068": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One place cannot hold two reservations</figcaption>
<p>The capacity counter starts with total 1 and used 0. Calls are serialized.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Reserve once<br>used: 0 → 1<br>Full</div><div>Reserve again<br>used stays 1<br>Raise full error</div><div>Release once<br>used: 1 → 0</div><div>Release again<br>used stays 0<br>Raise nothing-reserved error</div></div>
<details><summary>Why reject total 1.5 in this implementation?</summary><p>used advances by whole numbers and the full check tests equality. It would never equal 1.5. Validate the integer capacity before relying on that invariant.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can this counter identify which customer owns a place?</summary><p>No. Identity is outside its contract. Clarify whether ownership and repeatable retries are needed before treating it as a booking service.</p></details>`,
  "0069": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Equal amounts can represent different money</figcaption>
<p>The value object compares both amount and currency. These are example values, not exchange-rate equivalents.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Money A<br>10.00 INR</div><div>Money B<br>10.00 INR<br>A equals B</div><div>Money C<br>10.00 USD<br>A does not equal C</div><div>Separate objects<br>Value equality does not require<br>the same Python object</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the annotation currency: str enforce recognized currency codes?</summary><p>No. The fixture does not validate that policy. Define accepted codes and scale when the domain requires them.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why reject Decimal NaN, Infinity and negative amounts here?</summary><p>They violate this Money object's finite, nonnegative amount rule. That rule is separate from identity, exchange rates and rounding.</p></details>`,
  "0070": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two floors can still reference one spot</figcaption>
<p>Create one Spot object numbered 101. The supplied add method can place that same object into two different floors.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Floor 1<br>spots[0] → Spot 101</div><div>Floor 2<br>spots[0] → the same Spot 101</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the per-floor duplicate-number check enforce exclusive ownership?</summary><p>No. Each floor checks only its own list. Both can retain the same Spot object.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does removing one floor guarantee that Spot 101 disappears?</summary><p>No. Other references can keep it alive. Define transfer and deletion semantics explicitly before claiming composition in the domain model.</p></details>`,
  "0071": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A fake records a call, not a payment</figcaption>
<p>checkout receives 2,500 integer cents and the supplied FakeGateway.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Checkout boundary<br>Validate integer and nonnegative amount<br>Call charge(2500)</div><div>Fake gateway<br>charges becomes [2500]<br>Returns payment-1<br>No external charge occurs</div></div>
<details><summary>Would a Boolean amount pass this checkout's validation?</summary><p>No. It checks the exact integer type, so True is rejected even though Python treats bool as an int subclass.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does payment-1 establish the real provider's retry behavior?</summary><p>No. The fake proves this call path only. A real integration needs error contracts, idempotency and reconciliation for unknown outcomes.</p></details>`,
  "0072": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Choose a pricing rule for the same stay</figcaption>
<p>The stay is 3 whole hours and the rate is 100 units per hour.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Hourly rule<br>max(1, 3) × 100<br>Fee: 300</div><div>First hour free<br>max(0, 3 − 1) × 100<br>Fee: 200</div></div>
<details><summary>What does each rule charge for zero hours?</summary><p>The hourly rule charges 100 because it has a one-hour minimum. The first-hour-free rule charges zero.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can passing 1.5 hours silently choose a rounding policy?</summary><p>No. The supplied fee function rejects fractional hours. Define partial-hour billing before changing that contract.</p></details>`,
  "0073": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Payment and cancellation lead to different states</figcaption>
<p>The table lists the only accepted commands. Missing state-command pairs are rejected.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Fulfillment path<br>NEW → pay → PAID<br>PAID → ship → SHIPPED</div><div>Cancellation path<br>NEW → cancel → CANCELLED<br>No ship transition exists here</div></div>
<details><summary>What happens if pay arrives again while already PAID?</summary><p>The table rejects it. This example does not implement successful response replay for duplicate commands.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does returning SHIPPED actually ship or persist anything?</summary><p>No. The function only returns a legal next state. Concurrent persistence and external effects require additional boundaries.</p></details>`,
  "0074": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One lock protects the check and the claim</figcaption>
<p>A and B both try to reserve the same Seat instance. The seat begins with owner None.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Whichever caller acquires first<br>Sees None<br>Sets its name<br>Returns True</div><div>Other caller acquires afterward<br>Sees an owner<br>Leaves it unchanged<br>Returns False</div></div>
<details><summary>Can the test require that A always wins?</summary><p>No. Either caller can acquire first. The invariant is exactly one successful claim for this shared instance.</p></details>
<details><summary>If the winner retries, does it get True again?</summary><p>No. The current implementation returns False whenever the seat is owned. Repeatable result replay is a different requirement from avoiding duplicate effects.</p></details>`,
  "0075": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An exception can leave half the work visible</figcaption>
<p>create_booking first adds b-1 to the repository, then appends its event to a separate list. Consider a failing event append.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Step 1 succeeds<br>Repository: {b-1}</div><div>Step 2 fails<br>Event list: []<br>Exception reaches caller<br>Repository still contains b-1</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the raised exception automatically undo repository.add?</summary><p>No. There is no transaction around these effects. The stored booking remains without its corresponding event.</p></details>
<details><summary>What happens if the caller simply retries b-1?</summary><p>The repository rejects the duplicate. It does not replay a successful response or repair the missing event. Define recovery and stable identity explicitly.</p></details>`,
  "0076": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Test the public store contract</figcaption>
<p>Start with a new MemoryStore and interact only through put and get.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Missing key<br>get missing → None</div><div>Overwrite<br>put a = one<br>put a = two<br>get a → two</div><div>Invalid key<br>put empty key<br>Raises ValueError</div><div>Boundary<br>Observed through public methods<br>No private dictionary assertions</div></div>
<details><summary>Would replacing the internal dictionary necessarily require changing these tests?</summary><p>No. A different implementation can preserve the public behavior. Tests of private storage details would make that harder.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do these passing fake-store checks establish another adapter's durability?</summary><p>No. Run the applicable contract against that adapter and add integration-specific failure, consistency and persistence checks.</p></details>`,
  "0077": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">An empty bike spot does not fit a car</figcaption>
<p>The lot has spot 1 for cars and spot 2 for bikes. Both begin empty.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Park KA-01 as a car<br>Spot 1: KA-01<br>Spot 2: empty bike spot</div><div>Try another car<br>No free compatible spot<br>Raise full error</div></div>
<details><summary>After KA-01 leaves, can another car use spot 1?</summary><p>Yes. leave clears that spot's vehicle reference. The next compatible first-fit search can select it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does first fit always choose the lowest spot number?</summary><p>No. It follows input order. Lowest-numbered or nearest allocation would need an explicit policy. This fixture also assumes single-threaded ownership.</p></details>`,
  "0078": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The payer is owed the other participants' shares</figcaption>
<p>A pays an expense with shares of 30.00 each for A, B and C. Use one agreed currency.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>A's posting<br>Paid 90.00; owes own share 30.00<br>Net: +60.00</div><div>B and C<br>B: −30.00<br>C: −30.00</div><div>Balance check<br>60.00 − 30.00 − 30.00 = 0</div><div>Meaning<br>Positive: is owed<br>Negative: owes</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a zero total prove the intended people and shares were correct?</summary><p>No. A wrong set of balanced postings can still sum to zero. Check the participants and input shares too.</p></details>
<details><summary>Has the function settled any debt?</summary><p>No. It returns a proposed posting. Stored history and actual settlement are separate work, with explicit decimal precision and rounding policies.</p></details>`,
  "0079": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A cache read changes who is evicted next</figcaption>
<p>The LRU cache holds two entries. The order below runs from least recent to most recent.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Put a, then b<br>a → b</div><div>Read a<br>b → a</div><div>Put c<br>Evict b<br>a → c</div><div>Result<br>get b → None<br>get a → 1</div></div>
<details><summary>For limit 2 and window 10, why is the request at time 10 allowed after requests at 0, 1 and 2?</summary><p>Times 0 and 1 were admitted; time 2 was rejected. At time 10, the event at 0 expires under the interval (0,10], leaving room for one new admission.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can storing None be distinguished from a miss in this cache?</summary><p>Not with the current return value alone. Use a distinct miss representation if None is an allowed stored value.</p></details>`,
  "0060": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A window limit is not all new capacity</figcaption>
<p>The toy trace reaches congestion window 9. The receiver advertises 6, and 3 units are already in flight.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Outstanding-data limit<br>min(9, 6) = 6</div><div>New-data allowance<br>6 − 3 already in flight = 3</div></div>
<details><summary>Can the sender add 6 new units immediately?</summary><p>No. That would raise outstanding data to 9 and exceed the limit of 6. Subtract in-flight data first.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does limiting socket data keep an upstream job queue bounded?</summary><p>No. Jobs can accumulate before reaching the socket. Application admission and backpressure must also bound queued work.</p></details>
<p>These are toy units and RTT rounds, not measurements from a live TCP implementation.</p>`,
  "0061": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The fragment stays out of the HTTP target</figcaption>
<p>Split https://example.com/search?q=cache#section into the parts used by the request planner.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Server identity<br>Host: example.com<br>DNS and TLS hostname</div><div>HTTP request target<br>/search?q=cache</div><div>Fragment<br>section<br>Not included in that target</div><div>Cold-path plan<br>Resolve → connect → authenticate → request</div></div>
<details><summary>Must a second request repeat every setup step?</summary><p>No. Cached names and an existing connection can avoid some setup. This planner describes a cold path.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does accepting an HTTPS URL establish that it is safe or authenticated?</summary><p>No. The helper only constructs a plan. It does not validate certificates, enforce destination policy or perform a request.</p></details>`,
  "0062": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two descriptors refer to the ends of one pipe</figcaption>
<p>The fixture creates a pipe, writes the five bytes ready and reads those bytes from the other end.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>write_fd<br>Write ready<br>↓<br>Kernel pipe buffer</div><div>read_fd<br>Read up to 5 bytes<br>Fixture receives ready<br>Finally close the reader</div></div>
<details><summary>Why set write_fd to -1 after explicitly closing it?</summary><p>The cleanup block uses that marker to avoid closing it again. A stale descriptor number could later refer to another resource.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this five-byte success establish a general read-exactly helper?</summary><p>No. Larger or interrupted I/O requires explicit handling of partial transfers, errors and message boundaries.</p></details>`,
  "0063": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Share CPU turns with a quantum of two</figcaption>
<p>Both tasks are ready initially. api needs 5 units and worker needs 3. The simulation has one CPU and no I/O.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First round<br>api runs 2; remaining 3<br>worker runs 2; remaining 1</div><div>Second round<br>api runs 2; remaining 1<br>worker runs 1; completes</div><div>Last turn<br>api runs 1; completes</div><div>Total useful work<br>5 + 3 = 8 units</div></div>
<details><summary>Why reject a quantum of zero?</summary><p>No task would make progress. Positive bursts and a positive quantum ensure remaining work decreases.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this prove a smaller quantum always improves real latency?</summary><p>No. The simulation omits context-switch cost, I/O, priorities and other CPUs. Measure the actual scheduler workload.</p></details>`,
  "0064": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Opposite transfers use the same lock order</figcaption>
<p>Accounts A and B each start at 100. One thread transfers 10 from A to B; another transfers 20 from B to A.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Both threads<br>Acquire lower-object-id lock<br>Then higher-object-id lock<br>Transfer while holding both</div><div>Final balances<br>A: 100 − 10 + 20 = 110<br>B: 100 + 10 − 20 = 90<br>Total: 200</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not always lock the source account first?</summary><p>The opposite transfers could each hold one account and wait for the other. A common acquisition order removes that circular wait for this operation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do separately created Account objects for the same business account share protection?</summary><p>No. Their locks are different. This design requires one shared object per account and cooperating readers and writers. It is not a cross-process transaction.</p></details>`,
  "0065": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Translate the page while keeping the offset</figcaption>
<p>The virtual address is 0x1234. Pages are 4096 bytes, or 0x1000. The table maps virtual page 1 to frame 9.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Split virtual address<br>Page: 1<br>Offset: 0x234</div><div>Translate and combine<br>Frame base: 0x9000<br>Keep offset: +0x234<br>Physical address: 0x9234</div></div>
<details><summary>With LRU capacity 2 and accesses [1,2,1,3,1], which page leaves when 3 arrives?</summary><p>Page 2. Accessing 1 refreshed its recency. There are three misses overall: the first 1, the first 2 and the first 3.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does every page fault require reading swap?</summary><p>No. Faults can involve demand-zero, copy-on-write or file-backed pages. This lookup model does not implement a real page-table walk or permissions.</p></details>`,
  "0066": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Visible replacement and durable replacement are separate</figcaption>
<p>The fixture replaces state.bin through a temporary file in the same trusted directory.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Prepare content<br>Write temporary file<br>Flush Python buffer<br>fsync file</div><div>Replace the name<br>Temporary file → state.bin<br>Then fsync directory<br>Clean up temporary path</div></div>
<details><summary>Directory fsync raises after replacement. Does the exception prove the old file is still at the path?</summary><p>No. The replacement may already be visible. Failure at that point does not roll it back.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does reading committed bytes once establish power-loss durability?</summary><p>No. It checks observed content, not survival of a crash. Filesystem and storage guarantees need appropriate crash testing.</p></details>`,
  "0067": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A process snapshot has a limited boundary</figcaption>
<p>The read-only helper reports one process's PID, CPU usage and open-file limit.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Measured scope<br>This process<br>User CPU time<br>System CPU time<br>Descriptor limit</div><div>Not established by this snapshot<br>Container-wide usage<br>Namespace isolation<br>cgroup throttling<br>Cause of an OOM exit</div></div>
<details><summary>Can you call a CPU-throttle incident confirmed from these fields alone?</summary><p>No. Correlate the process behavior with actual controller counters and other relevant evidence.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why does the assertion permit RLIM_INFINITY explicitly?</summary><p>An unlimited limit uses a platform sentinel that need not be a positive number. A simple positive-value test would reject a valid unlimited setting.</p></details>`,
  "0051": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Headers can arrive before the body finishes</figcaption>
<p>The browser fixture records three moments around one fetch. No numerical durations are assumed.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Start → headers available<br>fetch resolves to a Response<br>First measured interval ends</div><div>Headers → body consumed<br>arrayBuffer finishes<br>Second measured interval ends</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the first interval directly measure DNS time?</summary><p>No. It combines work before the response headers become available. Use available resource timing fields to investigate phases, accounting for connection reuse and access restrictions.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does cache: no-store guarantee a new TCP and TLS connection?</summary><p>No. The transport can be reused. A warm connection and a cached response are different sources of saved work.</p></details>`,
  "0052": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A label, an input and a submitted field</figcaption>
<p>The form contains the visible label Search terms and a required input.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Label association<br>label for="query"<br>↓ matches<br>input id="query"</div><div>Submission identity<br>input name="query"<br>↓ supplies field key<br>query = entered text</div></div>
<details><summary>Remove the label's for attribute without nesting the input inside it. What should you inspect?</summary><p>The accessible name and label activation. The page can look unchanged while losing that explicit association.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does required remove the need for server validation?</summary><p>No. Browser validation is one client behavior. The server must validate requests independently.</p></details>`,
  "0053": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A large child z-index stays inside its parent</figcaption>
<p>The child and front sibling overlap. The parent creates a stacking context at z-index 0.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Parent context: 0<br>└ Child inside it: 999</div><div>Front sibling context: 1<br>Painted above the parent context<br>Also above that parent's child</div></div>
<details><summary>Would changing the child from 999 to a larger number let it escape?</summary><p>No. Its ranking remains inside the parent context. Inspect the containing contexts, not just the largest number.</p></details>
<details><summary>What changes when this fixture removes the parent's z-index?</summary><p>The child can compete in the outer context. First confirm that the rectangles overlap; otherwise their visible positions do not test stacking order.</p></details>`,
  "0054": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Known callback order and unknown relative order</figcaption>
<p>One browser task records sync and schedules a microtask, a zero-delay timer and an animation callback.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Guaranteed start in this fixture<br>sync<br>↓<br>microtask</div><div>Later callbacks<br>timer<br>animation callback<br>No portable order asserted between these two</div></div>
<details><summary>Does awaiting an already-resolved promise guarantee a paint before continuing?</summary><p>No. It yields through the microtask mechanism. That does not require input processing or a paint between continuations.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does an animation callback prove the screen has already painted?</summary><p>No. It runs before a rendering opportunity. Observe actual browser behavior when checking input delay or painting.</p></details>`,
  "0055": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Restore the value that existed before a storage probe</figcaption>
<p>The example writes only the synthetic lesson-storage-probe key on a disposable page.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Key initially absent<br>Write synthetic value<br>Read it back<br>Finally: remove the key</div><div>Key initially present<br>Save its old value<br>Write synthetic value<br>Finally: restore the old value</div></div>
<details><summary>Can another tab write the key between the save and restore steps?</summary><p>Yes. Restoration is not atomic across tabs and could overwrite that change. Use an isolated fixture with no competing writer.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a successful storage check establish secure authentication?</summary><p>No. It checks storage mechanics. XSS, CSRF, authorization and session lifetime still require separate design.</p></details>`,
  "0056": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Different addresses answer different delivery questions</figcaption>
<p>The teaching fixture describes a health request sent toward 198.51.100.20. It labels layers rather than serializing real network bytes.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Application<br>GET /health HTTP/1.1</div><div>Transport<br>Source port 53000<br>Destination port 443</div><div>Network<br>192.0.2.10 → 198.51.100.20</div><div>Link<br>Source MAC ending :10<br>Next-hop MAC ending :01</div></div>
<details><summary>Does a TCP receive chunk necessarily contain one whole application message?</summary><p>No. Messages can be split across chunks or combined in one chunk. The receiver needs application framing.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a TCP acknowledgement prove the health request was processed successfully?</summary><p>No. It confirms stream receipt. Application processing and persistence need their own evidence. HTTPS also adds TLS protection beyond this layer-label sketch.</p></details>`,
  "0057": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Resolve the gateway for an off-link destination</figcaption>
<p>The local prefix is 192.0.2.0/27. The fixture uses gateway 192.0.2.1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>192.0.2.18<br>Inside the local prefix<br>Local membership: yes</div><div>198.51.100.8<br>Outside the local prefix<br>Next hop: 192.0.2.1<br>Use the gateway's cached MAC</div></div>
<details><summary>Does this host use ARP to find the remote destination's MAC directly?</summary><p>No. In the fixture it resolves the on-link gateway. The remote address is outside the local prefix.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does membership in this 32-address prefix mean every address is an assignable host address?</summary><p>No. The prefix-membership calculation alone does not establish host assignment rules. IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery rather than ARP.</p></details>`,
  "0058": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The most specific matching route wins</figcaption>
<p>Look up destination 10.42.7.8 in the fixture's routing table.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Matching prefixes<br>0.0.0.0/0<br>10.0.0.0/8<br>10.42.0.0/16</div><div>Choose /16<br>Longest matching prefix<br>Gateway: 10.42.0.1</div></div>
<details><summary>Which route handles 8.8.8.8 in this table?</summary><p>The default /0 route, through 192.0.2.1. Neither more-specific private prefix matches.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does the fixture's reverse NAT entry map?</summary><p>203.0.113.5:40001 maps to 10.42.0.9:53000. One mapping illustrates address translation; it is not a complete connection tracker or firewall.</p></details>`,
  "0059": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The UDP length includes its header</figcaption>
<p>The fixture encodes the five payload bytes in query, with source port 53000 and destination port 53.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>UDP header<br>8 bytes<br>Length field: 13</div><div>Payload<br>5 bytes: query<br>Total datagram bytes: 8 + 5 = 13</div></div>
<details><summary>Remove the last payload byte without changing the header. Does decoding succeed?</summary><p>No. The declared length remains 13 but only 12 bytes are present. The decoder rejects the mismatch.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does passing these checks prove the datagram is valid for every network path?</summary><p>No. The fixture omits checksum validation, and the length-field limit is not a path-MTU budget. IP headers and fragmentation constraints also matter.</p></details>`,
  "0041": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Zero amount and unreachable amount are different</figcaption>
<p>Only coin 2 is available. Fill the minimum-count states for amounts 0 through 3.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Amount 0 → count 0<br>Chosen coins: []<br>A valid empty solution</div><div>Amount 1 → unreachable<br>Amount 2 → count 1, coin 2<br>Amount 3 → unreachable</div></div>
<details><summary>What should the helper return for amount 3?</summary><p>undefined. Infinity in the internal count table means no solution; it must not start reconstruction through an absent predecessor.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why do positive stored coins make reconstruction terminate?</summary><p>Each subtraction strictly reduces the remaining amount until it reaches zero. Zero or negative denominations violate that argument and the stated input contract.</p></details>`,
  "0042": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A dependency cycle has no first task</figcaption>
<p>Task a requires b, and task b requires a. Both directed edges are present and both incoming counts are 1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Dependencies<br>a → b<br>b → a</div><div>Topological queue<br>Zero-indegree vertices: none<br>Processed: 0 of 2<br>Result: cycle error</div></div>
<details><summary>Remove b → a and recompute the counts. Which task starts?</summary><p>a has incoming count 0 and starts first. Processing a lowers b's count to 0, allowing b next.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can you reuse the already-mutated indegree map for another run?</summary><p>No. The sorter consumes the counts. Supply fresh, complete counts for each run; missing vertices are an invalid input fixture.</p></details>`,
  "0043": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Undo one choice before exploring its sibling</figcaption>
<p>Trace the permutation search for [1, 2]. The working path changes during recursion.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First branch<br>[] → [1] → [1, 2]<br>Save a copy<br>Undo to [1], then []</div><div>Second branch<br>[] → [2] → [2, 1]<br>Save a copy<br>Undo to [2], then []</div></div>
<details><summary>What does the same search return for an empty input?</summary><p>One empty permutation: [[]]. The initial empty path already has the required length. That differs from having no solutions.</p></details>
<details><summary>May a branch be pruned simply because its partial result looks unattractive?</summary><p>No. Pruning needs a reason that the branch cannot provide a required solution, or a valid bound for the objective. Otherwise it can discard a correct answer.</p></details>`,
  "0044": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Spread one edge per minute</figcaption>
<p>In [[2,1,0],[0,1,1]], 2 is active, 1 is fresh and 0 blocks spread. Coordinates are row, column.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Minute 0<br>Active: (0,0)</div><div>Minute 1<br>Newly active: (0,1)</div><div>Minute 2<br>Newly active: (1,1)</div><div>Minute 3<br>Newly active: (1,2)<br>No fresh cells remain</div></div>
<details><summary>Why must the current wave end at the saved queue length?</summary><p>Cells added during this wave belong to the next minute. Processing them immediately would let one minute cross multiple edges.</p></details>
<details><summary>What result represents fresh cells that cannot be reached?</summary><p>-1. With no fresh cells at the start, the result is 0 instead. The fixture changes grid cells in place.</p></details>`,
  "0045": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A shared prefix is not automatically a word</figcaption>
<p>Insert cat and car. The trie shares the path through c and a, then branches.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Shared path<br>root → c → a<br>No word-end marker at a</div><div>Branches from a<br>t → word-end marker: cat<br>r → word-end marker: car</div></div>
<details><summary>Why does has('ca') return false?</summary><p>The path exists, but its final node has no word-end marker. Prefix existence and exact word membership are different questions.</p></details>
<details><summary>Would listing every completion take only the time needed to read ca?</summary><p>No. After finding the prefix, listing completions also visits branches and emits output. The supplied helper implements exact membership, not ranked autocomplete.</p></details>`,
  "0046": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two ranges have the same target sum</figcaption>
<p>Count contiguous ranges totaling 3 in [2, -1, 2, 1]. Prefix totals start with 0.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Prefix totals<br>0 → 2 → 1 → 3 → 4</div><div>At prefix 3<br>Need earlier prefix 0<br>Range [2, -1, 2] sums to 3</div><div>At prefix 4<br>Need earlier prefix 1<br>Range [2, 1] sums to 3</div><div>Final count<br>2 ranges</div></div>
<details><summary>Why seed the prefix-frequency map with one zero?</summary><p>It represents the prefix before the first element. Without it, the range starting at index 0 would be missed.</p></details>
<details><summary>For target zero, why count matches before adding the current prefix?</summary><p>Otherwise the current prefix can match itself and count an empty range. Only earlier prefixes should be eligible.</p></details>`,
  "0047": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Walk the outer boundary once</figcaption>
<p>The matrix has rows [1, 2, 3] and [4, 5, 6]. Traverse clockwise from the top left.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Top edge<br>1 → 2 → 3<br>Move top boundary down</div><div>Right edge<br>6<br>Move right boundary left</div><div>Bottom edge, backward<br>5 → 4</div><div>Result<br>1, 2, 3, 6, 5, 4<br>No unvisited row remains</div></div>
<details><summary>Why check whether the bottom edge still exists before walking it?</summary><p>A one-row interior may already have been consumed as the top edge. Walking it again would duplicate values.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this traversal rotate the matrix in place?</summary><p>No. It produces an output sequence. In-place rotation is a separate operation with different constraints.</p></details>`,
  "0048": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Sorting makes overlapping intervals adjacent</figcaption>
<p>Merge closed intervals [8,10], [1,3] and [2,6].</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Sort by start<br>[1,3] → [2,6] → [8,10]</div><div>Merge overlap<br>[1,3] with [2,6] → [1,6]<br>[8,10] stays separate<br>Result: [1,6], [8,10]</div></div>
<details><summary>Would [1,3] and [3,6] merge under this contract?</summary><p>Yes. They share endpoint 3, and the intervals are closed. Do not silently apply this rule to a different occupancy model.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why can the full trace cost quadratic space even when merging is efficient?</summary><p>For disjoint intervals, each step copies a growing output list. Retaining all those snapshots adds more storage than the final merged result.</p></details>`,
  "0049": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A mask encodes a subset, not fewer subsets</figcaption>
<p>Use bit 0 for item A and bit 1 for item B. Two bits describe four possible selections.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>00 → no items<br>01 → A</div><div>10 → B<br>11 → A and B</div></div>
<details><summary>How many subset outputs are needed for n items?</summary><p>2 to the power n. A compact representation does not remove the cost of listing every subset.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does countBits32(-1) count zero set bits because the value is negative?</summary><p>No. It counts the coerced 32-bit pattern, whose 32 bits are all set. The helper does not count an arbitrary mathematical integer's unbounded representation.</p></details>`,
  "0050": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Test the contract before optimizing the search</figcaption>
<p>Return two distinct indices totaling 6, or undefined if no pair exists. Duplicate values are allowed.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Input [3]<br>One value, no distinct partner<br>Expected: undefined</div><div>Input [3, 3]<br>Two separate positions<br>Expected: [0, 1]</div><div>Input [-2, 0, 2], target 0<br>Valid pair: indexes [0, 2]</div><div>Verification<br>Compare existence with a quadratic oracle<br>Check each returned index and sum</div></div>
<details><summary>Is matching the oracle's yes/no result enough to validate a returned pair?</summary><p>No. Also check bounds, distinct indices and the sum. A broken function could claim success with invalid positions.</p></details>
<details><summary>The input is already sorted and extra memory must be constant. What changes?</summary><p>Consider two pointers. Explain whether the contract wants positions in that sorted array or original positions before any sorting.</p></details>`,
  "0029": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Recover the coins, not only the count</figcaption>
<p>Make 6 with coins [1, 3, 4]. Store both the best count and the final coin chosen for each reachable amount.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Best counts for amounts 0…6<br>0, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2</div><div>Reconstruct amount 6<br>previous[6] = 3 → remaining 3<br>previous[3] = 3 → remaining 0<br>Chosen coins: [3, 3]</div></div>
<details><summary>Can the final count 2 alone tell you which coins to return?</summary><p>No. Keep predecessor choices or recover them from enough retained state. A count is not the actual solution.</p></details>
<details><summary>What changes if only one coin of value 3 is available?</summary><p>The unbounded recurrence can no longer be used unchanged. It currently allows the same denomination twice. Limited supply needs a different state or traversal.</p></details>`,
  "0030": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Leave room for the next booking</figcaption>
<p>One room has candidate bookings [0,6), [1,2), [3,4), [5,7). The end time is excluded.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Choose the long booking first<br>[0,6)<br>Other three overlap it<br>Count: 1</div><div>Choose earliest finish first<br>[1,2) → [3,4) → [5,7)<br>Count: 3</div></div>
<details><summary>Why is the earliest finish a useful first choice?</summary><p>Replacing the first booking in an optimal count schedule with an earlier-finishing compatible booking leaves at least as much room afterward.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can one booking start exactly when the previous one ends?</summary><p>Yes, under the half-open interval contract. Changing the endpoint rule changes the compatibility check.</p></details>`,
  "0031": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Clear one set bit at a time</figcaption>
<p>The input bit pattern is 101101. Count the set bits using value AND (value minus 1).</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First two removals<br>101101 → 101100 → 101000</div><div>Last two removals<br>101000 → 100000 → 000000<br>Count: 4</div></div>
<details><summary>Starting with mask 0, what does setting bit 2 produce?</summary><p>Binary 100, or decimal 4. Removing bit 2 returns the mask to zero.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does shifting a Number by 32 create a new flag beyond bit 31?</summary><p>No. JavaScript Number shift counts wrap modulo 32. Use an appropriate wider representation and a defined width policy.</p></details>`,
  "0032": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Look for a partner before storing yourself</figcaption>
<p>Find two distinct positions in [3, 3] whose values total 6.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Index 0, value 3<br>Need 3; map is empty<br>Store 3 → index 0</div><div>Index 1, value 3<br>Need 3; map has index 0<br>Return [0, 1]</div></div>
<details><summary>What can go wrong if you store the current value before looking for its partner?</summary><p>The first 3 can match itself, returning the same index twice. Lookup before insertion enforces distinct positions in this algorithm.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why do arc and car share a group while tap does not?</summary><p>Their sorted-character signatures are acr, acr and apt. Constructing those signatures also costs work; map lookup is not the whole grouping cost.</p></details>`,
  "0033": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Discard impossible pairs using sorted order</figcaption>
<p>Find a pair totaling 19 in [2, 5, 8, 11, 15, 19].</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Outer pairs<br>2 + 19 = 21 → move right left<br>2 + 15 = 17 → move left right<br>5 + 15 = 20 → move right left</div><div>Remaining checks<br>5 + 11 = 16 → move left right<br>8 + 11 = 19<br>Return indexes [2, 3]</div></div>
<details><summary>Why can a sum below 19 move the left pointer?</summary><p>For that left value, every remaining right value is no larger than the current one. None can reach the target. Sorted order supports the discard.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the supplied traced implementation constant-space?</summary><p>No. The two pointers need constant state, but the returned trace stores up to linearly many steps.</p></details>`,
  "0034": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One repeated character moves the window</figcaption>
<p>Scan abcaef for the longest substring with no repeated character.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before the repeated a<br>Window: abc<br>Left index: 0<br>Best: 3</div><div>After the repeated a<br>Move left past old a to index 1<br>Window grows: bca → bcae → bcaef<br>Best: 5</div></div>
<details><summary>Must the scan restart at each possible left position?</summary><p>No. The last-seen map identifies the conflict. Both boundaries move only forward.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this JavaScript string scan count user-perceived characters?</summary><p>Not generally. These indexes count UTF-16 units. Choose a grapheme-aware representation before promising that behavior.</p></details>`,
  "0035": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A later 7 resolves two waiting values</figcaption>
<p>Find each value's next strictly greater value in [4, 2, 7, 5].</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Read 4, then 2<br>Unresolved indexes: [0, 1]<br>Both wait for something greater</div><div>Read 7<br>Resolve 2 → 7<br>Resolve 4 → 7<br>7 now waits</div><div>Read 5; reach end<br>Neither 7 nor 5 is resolved</div><div>Result<br>[7, 7, -1, -1]</div></div>
<details><summary>Would a second 4 resolve the first 4?</summary><p>No. The comparison is strictly greater. Equal values remain unresolved.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why can recording every stack snapshot make this example quadratic?</summary><p>On decreasing input the stack keeps growing. Copying all of it after every item copies 1 + 2 + … + n entries, although each index is pushed only once.</p></details>`,
  "0036": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The old head becomes the tail</figcaption>
<p>Two callers can retain references to the same linked nodes. Reversing the list changes those nodes in place.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before reversal<br>oldHead → node 1 → node 2 → null</div><div>After reversal<br>newHead → node 2 → node 1 → null<br>oldHead still points to node 1</div></div>
<details><summary>What can you reach by following oldHead after reversal?</summary><p>Only node 1 and then null. Retain the returned new head to traverse the reversed list.</p></details>
<details><summary>If another owner needs the original links, is relinking safe for that owner?</summary><p>No. The owner's view changes too. Agree on mutation ownership or construct separate nodes. The reverse function also assumes acyclic input.</p></details>`,
  "0037": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep only the three largest arrivals</figcaption>
<p>The stream is [8, 3, 10, 12, 5]. A min-heap retains the three largest values. The sets below show retained values, not the heap's array order.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>After first three<br>{3, 8, 10}<br>Smallest retained: 3</div><div>After 12<br>Remove 3<br>{8, 10, 12}<br>Smallest retained: 8</div><div>After 5<br>Remove 5<br>{8, 10, 12}</div><div>Third largest<br>Heap root: 8</div></div>
<details><summary>Why is the root the answer, rather than 12?</summary><p>The retained values are the largest three. The smallest of those three is the third largest overall.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if k is zero?</summary><p>The supplied function rejects it. Valid ranks are 1 through the number of values. Retained trace snapshots also use more memory than the heap alone.</p></details>`,
  "0038": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Search for the first feasible capacity</figcaption>
<p>Capacities 1 through 20 are candidates. In this fixture, a capacity is feasible exactly when it is at least 13.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Candidate checks<br>10: false → search 11…20<br>15: true → search 11…15</div><div>Narrow the boundary<br>13: true → search 11…13<br>12: false → search 13…13<br>Answer: 13</div></div>
<details><summary>What makes it safe to discard every value below a failed candidate?</summary><p>The predicate changes from false to true only once. A smaller capacity cannot succeed if the tested capacity fails under this contract.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if even capacity 20 is infeasible?</summary><p>The helper's precondition is violated. Establish a feasible upper bound or explicitly handle the no-solution case before using this search.</p></details>`,
  "0039": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A column of water separates two regions</figcaption>
<p>Only up, down, left and right connect land cells. Here 1 means land and 0 means water.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Grid<br>1 0 1<br>1 0 1</div><div>Regions<br>Left column: one region<br>Right column: one region<br>Total: 2</div></div>
<details><summary>Change the top middle cell to 1. How many regions remain?</summary><p>One. The top row connects the two columns. Marking each cell before recursion prevents repeated visits.</p></details>
<details><summary>In a two-by-two grid with land only on opposite corners, how many regions are there?</summary><p>Two under this four-neighbor rule. Diagonal contact is not a connection. An eight-neighbor rule would be a different contract.</p></details>`,
  "0040": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">More bookings can earn less value</figcaption>
<p>Booking A is [0,4) with value 100. Bookings B and C are [0,2) and [2,4), each with value 1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Maximize booking count<br>Choose B, then C<br>Count: 2<br>Value: 2</div><div>Maximize total value<br>Choose A<br>Count: 1<br>Value: 100</div></div>
<details><summary>Does earliest finish solve both objectives?</summary><p>No. Its exchange argument supports maximum compatible count for this interval model. It does not establish maximum weighted value.</p></details>
<details><summary>What should change when value becomes the objective?</summary><p>Choose an algorithm for weighted interval scheduling, such as the appropriate dynamic program. Do not reuse a proof for a different objective.</p></details>`,
  "0018": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Count comparisons before measuring time</figcaption>
<p>Search the sorted values 0 through 7 for 8, which is absent. Count only the comparisons made by the supplied searches.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Linear search<br>Inspect 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7<br>8 comparisons</div><div>Binary boundary search<br>Inspect indexes 4, 6, 7<br>3 comparisons<br>Insertion position: 8</div></div>
<details><summary>With values 0 through 31 and missing target 32, what are the counts?</summary><p>32 for the linear scan and 5 for this binary search. Each binary step removes the remaining lower part of the search interval.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does that make the complete example logarithmic?</summary><p>No. Constructing the array still takes linear time and space. Count preprocessing and updates when comparing complete workloads.</p></details>`,
  "0019": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One arrival log, three questions</figcaption>
<p>The arrivals are api, web, api. The repeated label is intentional.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Frequency map<br>api: 2<br>web: 1</div><div>Stack: newest first<br>api → web → api</div><div>Queue: oldest first<br>api → web → api</div><div>After two queue reads<br>Head index: 2<br>Next item: last api</div></div>
<details><summary>The first and last labels match. Does the first removal alone distinguish a stack from a queue?</summary><p>No. Both return api here, but they remove different arrivals. Track positions or use distinct labels to expose the ordering difference.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does moving the queue's head index release the consumed array entries?</summary><p>No. The supplied array still holds them. A long-lived queue needs a policy for reclaiming consumed storage.</p></details>`,
  "0020": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Connectivity without a stored route</figcaption>
<p>Start with three separate components: api, db and cache. Join api to db, then db to cache.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Initially<br>{api} {db} {cache}</div><div>After first union<br>{api, db} {cache}</div><div>After second union<br>{api, db, cache}</div><div>Question answered<br>api and cache connected? Yes</div></div>
<details><summary>Can this disjoint set list the edges on the route from api to cache?</summary><p>No. It tracks component membership. Store adjacency and use a traversal when the actual route is required.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can deleting an edge be handled by simply undoing the last union?</summary><p>Not generally. Other routes may exist, and an edge deletion can split a component. The supplied structure does not support that operation.</p></details>`,
  "0021": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The largest coin gives the wrong answer</figcaption>
<p>Available coins are 1, 3 and 4. Make a total of 6 with as few coins as possible.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Greedy choice<br>6 → take 4 → remaining 2<br>Take 1, then 1<br>3 coins</div><div>Better combination<br>6 → take 3 → remaining 3<br>Take another 3<br>2 coins</div></div>
<details><summary>Which final coin does dynamic programming consider?</summary><p>Every usable denomination. For total 6, compare one plus the best count for totals 5, 3 and 2. The route through total 3 gives two coins.</p></details>
<details><summary>With only coin 2, can the algorithm make total 3?</summary><p>No. The fixture returns Infinity for an unreachable total. An amount of zero needs zero coins.</p></details>`,
  "0022": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A rank is not a full sorted result</figcaption>
<p>For values [7, 2, 5, 1], the requested zero-based rank is 2.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Sorted order<br>Index 0: 1<br>Index 1: 2<br>Index 2: 5<br>Index 3: 7</div><div>Quickselect contract<br>Return the value at rank 2<br>Result: 5<br>No fully sorted output required</div></div>
<details><summary>Does selecting 5 prove the intermediate array is sorted?</summary><p>No. Selection needs the requested rank, not a total ordering. Sorting may be useful if many later rank queries need the same unchanged data.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can the supplied last-element pivot give quadratic work?</summary><p>Yes. Ordered or duplicate-heavy inputs can produce repeatedly unbalanced partitions. Do not claim a worst-case linear bound for this implementation.</p></details>`,
  "0023": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Find the first 2, not just any 2</figcaption>
<p>The sorted array is [1, 2, 2, 5]. Lower bound finds the first position whose value is at least 2.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>lo 0, hi 4<br>mid 2 → value 2<br>Set hi to 2</div><div>lo 0, hi 2<br>mid 1 → value 2<br>Set hi to 1</div><div>lo 0, hi 1<br>mid 0 → value 1<br>Set lo to 1</div><div>lo = hi = 1<br>Answer: index 1</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not return immediately when the middle value equals 2?</summary><p>There may be an earlier 2. Moving hi left preserves the first qualifying position.</p></details>
<details><summary>What is the lower bound for target 9?</summary><p>4, the insertion position after the array. A valid boundary answer does not have to be an existing element index.</p></details>`,
  "0024": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep the window's left edge moving forward</figcaption>
<p>Find the longest substring without repeated characters in abba.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Read a, then b<br>Window: ab<br>Left: 0<br>Best length: 2</div><div>Read the second b<br>Previous b at 1<br>Move left to 2<br>Window: b</div><div>Read final a<br>Previous a at 0, outside window<br>Keep left at 2<br>Window: ba</div><div>Result<br>Longest length: 2</div></div>
<details><summary>What goes wrong if the last a moves left back to 1?</summary><p>The window becomes bba, which contains repeated b. An old occurrence outside the current window must not move the left edge backward.</p></details>
<details><summary>For [2, 4, 6], how do prefix sums find the sum over indexes [1, 3)?</summary><p>The prefix array is [0, 2, 6, 12]. Subtract 2 from 12 to get 10. The right endpoint is excluded.</p></details>`,
  "0025": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Save the next link before reversing it</figcaption>
<p>Reverse the acyclic list 1 → 2 → null.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before first rewrite<br>Current: node 1<br>Saved next: node 2<br>Previous: null</div><div>After first rewrite<br>1 → null<br>Current moves to node 2<br>Previous becomes node 1</div><div>After second rewrite<br>2 → 1 → null<br>New head: node 2</div><div>Ownership change<br>The original node links were mutated</div></div>
<details><summary>Why save next before assigning head.next?</summary><p>Overwriting the link can lose the route to the unprocessed nodes. The saved reference preserves that route.</p></details>
<details><summary>Two different nodes both store 1. Does that prove a cycle?</summary><p>No. Cycle detection compares node identity. Equal values in separate nodes do not establish a repeated node.</p></details>`,
  "0026": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A descendant must obey its ancestors' bounds</figcaption>
<p>The valid fixture has root 2, left child 1 and right child 3. Consider an added right child 4 beneath node 1.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Original tree<br>Root 2<br>Left 1; right 3<br>Breadth-first: 2, 1, 3</div><div>Changed left subtree<br>2 → left child 1 → right child 4<br>4 is greater than parent 1<br>But 4 is not less than ancestor 2</div></div>
<details><summary>Is the changed tree a valid binary search tree?</summary><p>No. Everything in the left subtree of 2 must be less than 2 under this strict ordering policy. Comparing only parent and child misses the violation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does the supplied breadth-first queue retain only the current level?</summary><p>No. Its array keeps consumed references as the index advances. Account for storage for all enqueued nodes.</p></details>`,
  "0027": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Distance counts edges in an unweighted graph</figcaption>
<p>The graph has directed edges a → b and b → c. Start the breadth-first search at a.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First layer<br>a: distance 0</div><div>Second layer<br>b: distance 1</div><div>Third layer<br>c: distance 2</div><div>Dependency order<br>a before b before c</div></div>
<details><summary>Add the edge a → c. What distance should c have?</summary><p>1. Breadth-first search reaches c directly in the first layer after a. This minimizes edge count, not arbitrary weighted cost.</p></details>
<details><summary>Add c → a to the original graph. Can all vertices have a topological order?</summary><p>No. The edges form a directed cycle. The supplied topological sorter detects that it cannot process every vertex.</p></details>`,
  "0028": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Repeated values need unique branches</figcaption>
<p>Arrange [1, 1, 2] into distinct permutations. The two copies of 1 have equal values.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>First value 1<br>Remaining 1, 2<br>Leaves: 112 and 121</div><div>First value 2<br>Remaining 1, 1<br>Leaf: 211</div></div>
<details><summary>Why not start another identical branch with the second unused 1?</summary><p>It would produce the same value permutations. The duplicate-sibling rule skips it while still allowing another 1 at a deeper position.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why copy the path when saving a completed permutation?</summary><p>Backtracking changes the working path as it removes choices. Saving the same mutable array would let later changes corrupt earlier results.</p></details>` ,
  "0006": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Read the price calculation in order</figcaption>
<p>A member buys two items at 50 each. The example gives members a 10% discount when the subtotal reaches 100.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Two items<br>50 × 2 = 100</div><div>Member discount<br>100 × 0.1 = 10</div><div>Final total<br>100 − 10 = 90</div><div>Nonmember, same items<br>100 − 0 = 100</div></div>
<details><summary>A member's subtotal is 99. What is the total?</summary><p>99. Membership alone is insufficient: the subtotal must also reach 100. Names such as subtotal and discountFor make both conditions easier to inspect.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can a naming refactor also change rounding?</summary><p>That would change behavior. Review it separately with an explicit currency and rounding policy. This floating-point example teaches decomposition, not a complete money model.</p></details>`,
  "0007": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Similar text operations, different rules</figcaption>
<p>The input has spaces around the name React. Search ignores letter case. Display names preserve it.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Search input: “  React  ”<br>Trim + lowercase<br>Result: “react”</div><div>Display input: “  React  ”<br>Trim + collapse spaces<br>Result: “React”</div></div>
<details><summary>Search becomes case sensitive. Should displayed names change too?</summary><p>No. The rules serve different purposes. Change the search function without changing display-name behavior.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should two callers have separate copies of the same shipping-price rule?</summary><p>No, if they must always follow the same business rule. The supplied domestic 1,200-gram example costs 500 + 2 × 200 = 900 cents. One rule owner prevents the callers from drifting apart.</p></details>`,
  "0008": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Two buyers, one remaining item</figcaption>
<p>Stock is 1. Buyer A and buyer B each request one item. Separate reads can let both buyers see the same available stock.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Unsafe split operations<br>A reads 1<br>B reads 1<br>Both decide “available”</div><div>Required atomic operation<br>First reservation: 1 → 0, succeeds<br>Second reservation: sees 0, fails</div></div>
<details><summary>Does hiding the separate reads and writes behind an interface remove the race?</summary><p>No. The adapter must make the check and decrement atomic. The interface documents the required capability; it does not supply that guarantee.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a sequential fake establish that the database is safe?</summary><p>No. Test concurrent reservations against the real adapter. Exactly one may succeed for this fixture. Either buyer can be first.</p></details>`,
  "0009": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The first matching pricing rule wins</figcaption>
<p>A member has a subtotal of 100. The member rule returns 90. The standard rule always matches and returns the subtotal.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Member rule first<br>Member matches → 90<br>Stop searching</div><div>Standard rule first<br>Standard matches → 100<br>Member rule never runs</div></div>
<details><summary>Can changing rule order change the price without changing either rule?</summary><p>Yes. In this example it changes 90 to 100. Rule precedence is part of the pricing contract.</p></details>
<details><summary>Must a single stable pricing condition become a plugin system?</summary><p>No. Keep a conditional if it fits the actual variation. An extension point also creates ordering and validation responsibilities.</p></details>`,
  "0010": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A replacement must keep the missing-order contract</figcaption>
<p>The caller asks for an order that does not exist. OrderReader promises an order or undefined.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Memory reader<br>find missing ID<br>↓<br>undefined → caller handles absence</div><div>Replacement reader<br>find missing ID<br>↓<br>throws → different failure path</div></div>
<details><summary>Is matching the method signature enough for substitution?</summary><p>No. The replacement must also preserve the agreed behavior for a valid missing-ID lookup. An unexpected exception can break the caller.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a read-only report need the save method?</summary><p>No. Give it OrderReader. Separating reader and writer capabilities avoids requiring an operation the report does not use.</p></details>
<p>The memory adapter retains object references. Readonly types do not freeze those objects at runtime.</p>`,
  "0011": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">The same rules in two orders</figcaption>
<p>Start with 100. Apply a 10% discount and a maximum price of 80.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Discount, then cap<br>100 → 90 → 80</div><div>Cap, then discount<br>100 → 80 → 72</div></div>
<details><summary>Can the two functions be swapped without changing the result?</summary><p>No. Their order changes the result from 80 to 72. Composition makes the pieces reusable but does not decide their order.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does this calculation require a subclass for each combination?</summary><p>No. The example composes pure functions. External effects belong outside these calculations and need separate tests.</p></details>`,
  "0012": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A pattern name is not evidence</figcaption>
<p>The decision record proposes an Adapter for two incompatible payment-provider APIs. Its helper checks that a pattern name, forces and evidence text are present.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Record A<br>Pattern: Adapter<br>Evidence: real contract-test results<br>Shape check: passes</div><div>Record B<br>Pattern: Adapter<br>Evidence: invented claim<br>Shape check: also passes</div></div>
<details><summary>What does the passing helper actually establish?</summary><p>Only that required fields contain values. Inspect the incompatible APIs and actual tests to justify the boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>With one provider and no incompatible second API, what alternative should you compare?</summary><p>A direct function. An Adapter adds a layer; its benefit should come from the real problem, not the pattern's name.</p></details>`,
  "0013": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Building a request snapshot</figcaption>
<p>The request builder starts with a timeout of 5,000 milliseconds. The example sets an HTTPS URL and changes the timeout to 2,000 before building.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Builder before build<br>URL: HTTPS endpoint<br>Timeout: 2,000 ms<br>Headers: mutable builder data</div><div>Built request<br>Timeout: 2,000 ms<br>Copied headers<br>Frozen request and header objects</div></div>
<details><summary>Does the built request use 5,000 or 2,000 milliseconds?</summary><p>2,000. The explicit timeout replaces the default before the snapshot is created.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does requiring HTTPS make every URL safe to request?</summary><p>No. HTTPS alone does not prevent requests to unwanted destinations. SSRF protection and header validation require their own rules.</p></details>
<p>Compare the builder with a plain validated options object before adding construction machinery.</p>`,
  "0014": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Three wrappers, three responsibilities</figcaption>
<p>The supplied classes wrap a text provider. Follow the request toward the provider and the result back to the caller.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Facade<br>Question → “Answer briefly: ” + question<br>Provides a smaller caller interface</div><div>Decorator<br>Calls the inner model<br>Records elapsed time afterward</div><div>Adapter<br>prompt → provider text field<br>provider output → returned string</div><div>Failure boundary<br>Provider error stays an error<br>Optional recorder failure must not replace it</div></div>
<details><summary>The provider succeeds, but the synchronous recorder throws. What reaches the caller?</summary><p>The provider's result. The decorator catches this optional recorder failure. It is not a design for a mandatory audit log.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does forwarding an AbortSignal prove the provider cancels work?</summary><p>No. Forwarding passes the request along. The provider still has to implement cancellation.</p></details>`,
  "0015": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A small workflow with one forbidden shortcut</figcaption>
<p>The state table permits approval before sending.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Allowed path<br>draft → approve → approved<br>approved → send → sent</div><div>Rejected path<br>draft → send<br>No transition exists<br>State stays draft</div></div>
<details><summary>A listener throws after approval. Does the state return to draft?</summary><p>No. State changes before listeners run. It remains approved, and the thrown error prevents later listeners from running.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does reaching sent prove that an external notification was delivered?</summary><p>No. This fixture records a local transition. It does not perform or verify external delivery.</p></details>`,
  "0016": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Keep outputs fixed while changing structure</figcaption>
<p>The old function and its replacement calculate the same two fixture orders. Shipping is added after the discount.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Standard order<br>Subtotal 100 + shipping 5<br>Old: 105<br>New: 105</div><div>Member order<br>100 × 0.9 + shipping 5<br>Old: 95<br>New: 95</div></div>
<details><summary>The new member result is 94.5. Is that a behavior-preserving refactor?</summary><p>No. That result discounts shipping too: 105 × 0.9 = 94.5. The member characterization case should fail.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do these two matching results cover all behavior?</summary><p>No. Check relevant boundaries, invalid inputs and side effects. Separate an intentional behavior correction from restructuring.</p></details>`,
  "0017": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A lost commit reply leaves a question</figcaption>
<p>The use case reads a draft order, saves it as placed, then asks the unit of work to commit.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Known success<br>Save placed<br>Commit succeeds<br>Reply reaches caller</div><div>Ambiguous result<br>Save placed<br>Commit may succeed<br>Reply is lost</div></div>
<details><summary>Does calling rollback after a lost reply prove that the order was not placed?</summary><p>No. The commit may already have completed. Reconcile the stored result and use an idempotent retry design before repeating the action.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does a fake unit-of-work test establish?</summary><p>It can check the caller's save, commit and rollback branches. It does not establish database durability, isolation or safe concurrent placement.</p></details>`,
  "0001": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">One pipeline, two exit statuses</figcaption>
<p>The producer exits with status 7. The consumer, tee, finishes with status 0. Bash must choose one status for the whole pipeline.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Producer<br>exit 7<br>↓<br>Consumer<br>exit 0</div><div>Default Bash policy<br>take the last status<br>↓<br>Pipeline status: 0</div></div>
<details><summary>Enable pipefail. What status does Bash report?</summary><p>7. With pipefail, Bash reports the rightmost nonzero status. The consumer's successful exit does not erase the producer's failure.</p></details>
<details><summary>Now the consumer also fails, with status 3. What changes?</summary><p>The pipeline reports 3 with either policy. By default, 3 is the last status. With pipefail, it is the rightmost nonzero status. The single result does not tell you that both commands failed.</p></details>
<p>Capture the statuses immediately. Another command can overwrite them. Even status 0 does not prove that the intended output is complete.</p>`,
  "0002": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Three versions of one file</figcaption>
<p>A tracked file in HEAD contains quantity 10. You change it to 20 and stage it. Then you change the working file to 30 without staging again.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>HEAD snapshot<br>quantity: 10</div><div>Index: next commit's snapshot<br>quantity: 20</div><div>Working file<br>quantity: 30</div><div>Two comparisons<br>HEAD → index: 10 → 20<br>Index → working file: 20 → 30</div></div>
<details><summary>Without staging again, what does a normal git commit record?</summary><p>20. The commit uses the index. The later value 30 remains an unstaged change in the working file. Staging does not keep a live connection to future edits.</p></details>
<details><summary>Which change appears in git diff --cached?</summary><p>10 to 20. It compares HEAD with the index. Plain git diff shows 20 to 30, comparing the index with the working file.</p></details>
<p>A committed snapshot records content. It does not establish that the code passes tests.</p>`,
  "0003": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">A lockfile cannot fix a mismatched request</figcaption>
<p>In a fictional package, the manifest requires an exact dependency version 1.0.0. The lockfile records that version. You edit the manifest to require exactly 2.0.0 but leave the lockfile unchanged.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Before the edit<br>Manifest: 1.0.0<br>Lock: 1.0.0<br>Versions agree</div><div>After the edit<br>Manifest: 2.0.0<br>Lock: 1.0.0<br>Versions conflict</div></div>
<details><summary>Does npm ci silently change the lockfile to 2.0.0?</summary><p>No. It rejects the mismatch. Update and review the dependency resolution separately, then use the matching manifest and lockfile for a frozen install.</p></details>
<details><summary>Restore both to 1.0.0. Is installation now guaranteed to succeed?</summary><p>No. The package may be unavailable, its platform may be unsupported, or an installation script may fail. Matching versions resolve this particular conflict, not every installation problem.</p></details>
<p>This is a paper exercise. No installation is needed. Runtime, operating system and build tools remain separate inputs when comparing builds.</p>`,
  "0004": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Zero is a cached value</figcaption>
<p>The computation returns 0. Two calls use the key zero. Compare a truthiness check with a membership check.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Broken check: !cache.get(key)<br>First call: missing → compute 0<br>Second call: !0 is true → compute again<br>Total computations: 2</div><div>Membership check: !cache.has(key)<br>First call: missing → compute 0<br>Second call: key exists → reuse 0<br>Total computations: 1</div></div>
<details><summary>Call the fixed function with a different, missing key. How many computations have happened in total?</summary><p>2. The original key computed once. The new key also needs one computation. This check catches a supposed fix that simply stops all computation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do these results prove that simultaneous asynchronous calls are safe?</summary><p>No. These calls are synchronous. Concurrent misses can start duplicate work before either result reaches the cache. Sharing in-flight work needs a separate design and test.</p></details>`,
  "0005": `<figcaption id="mechanism-map-caption">Where a notification can disappear</figcaption>
<p>An order must produce a notification. Compare two designs when the process crashes after the database commit.</p>
<div class="foundation-grid"><div>Direct publication<br>1. Commit order<br>2. Crash before publish<br>Result: order exists; notification intent was not saved</div><div>Transactional outbox<br>1. Commit order + notification intent together<br>2. Crash before relay<br>Result: order and intent exist; relay can resume</div></div>
<details><summary>The relay publishes, then crashes before recording success. Can the notification be sent twice?</summary><p>Yes. On restart, the saved intent still looks unsent. A retry can publish it again. Use stable event identity and an idempotent effect; the outbox alone does not provide exactly-once delivery.</p></details>
<details><summary>The requirement changes: losing an analytics notification is acceptable. Must the outbox remain?</summary><p>No. Direct publication may now be sufficient. Record the accepted loss risk and compare it with the cost of operating, retrying and cleaning up an outbox.</p></details>
<p>A decision record should retain the requirement and tradeoff. Do not describe the preferred design as best for every system.</p>`
};
